@node Answering Emacs Prompts
@section Answer a prompt and return to your work

@cindex minibuffer
@cindex prompts, answering
@cindex completion, first use
@cindex where to type

A command may ask for a file@comma{} a buffer@comma{} a search term or another value. Emacs
reads that answer in the @emph{minibuffer}@comma{} a temporary input area separate from
your document. While the prompt is active@comma{} ordinary typing answers the prompt;
it does not add words to the document. Emacsvox speaks prompts and completion
feedback according to your settings.

For example@comma{} @kbd{M-x} asks for a command name. Type @code{describe-mode}@comma{} including
its hyphen@comma{} then press @kbd{@key{RET}} to run it. Do not type the prompt label or the
letters @code{M-x}. A command may then ask another question: after choosing @code{eww}@comma{}
for example@comma{} its next prompt asks for a web address or search words. Answer
one prompt at a time rather than pasting the whole printed instruction.

Completion helps you enter an existing name. Type a distinctive beginning@comma{}
then press @kbd{@key{TAB}}. Emacs may extend the text or offer several candidates.
With several matches@comma{} type more of the name; you can always type the complete
name and press @kbd{@key{RET}}. Completion interfaces differ between configurations@comma{}
so the guides do not assume that Down Arrow accepts the first suggestion.
Opening a new file permits a name that does not yet exist; selecting an
installed command normally requires a recognised name.

@kbd{@key{RET}} submits the current answer@comma{} including any text already in the prompt.
An empty answer may accept a displayed default. Read that default before
accepting it. For a yes/no question@comma{} use the response requested: some prompts
accept @kbd{y} or @kbd{n}@comma{} while others require @code{yes} or @code{no} followed by @kbd{@key{RET}}.

Use @kbd{C-g} to abandon an unfinished ordinary prompt. If you have already
submitted it and the command has acted@comma{} cancellation does not undo the result.
Likewise@comma{} quitting a settings menu does not undo an adjustment made there.
Follow the recovery instructions for the operation you actually performed.

After the prompt finishes@comma{} notice which buffer has been selected. An open-file
command selects a document; Help may appear in another window; a save normally
leaves you in the document. Press @kbd{C-e m} to identify the current buffer and
mode before editing. Finish or cancel an active prompt before switching buffers
with @kbd{C-x b}; otherwise its answer is still unfinished. See
@ref{Using Online Help, , Using Online Help} for entering and leaving a Help window.

@menu
* Interpret filenames and examples: Filenames And Example Commands.  Resolve paths in the right environment and distinguish input from instructions.
@end menu

@node Filenames And Example Commands
@subsection Interpret filenames and examples

@cindex filenames, entering
@cindex paths, examples
@cindex shell commands, where to run
@cindex WSL, document paths

A @emph{path} identifies a file's location as well as its name. A file prompt often
starts with a directory already filled in. Append a filename to use that
directory@comma{} or replace the editable answer with a complete path. In an ordinary
file minibuffer@comma{} @kbd{C-a C-k} removes the current editable answer: Control-a goes
to its beginning and Control-k removes the text that follows. This edits the
answer@comma{} not a file. Then type the intended path and press @kbd{@key{RET}}.

Like other kill commands@comma{} @kbd{C-k} also puts the removed text in the kill ring.
If you intended to paste a previously copied path@comma{} clearing the prompt this
way can change the next paste. Type the intended path here@comma{} or use
@ref{Choosing Earlier Copied Text,Choose text copied earlier,,emacsvox,} to retrieve the right entry.

The examples use @code{~/} for the home directory understood by the running Emacs.
For example@comma{} @code{~/evox-notes.org} names @code{evox-notes.org} in that home directory.
In WSL this normally means the Linux home@comma{} not Windows Documents. A Windows
C-drive path is normally reached through @code{/mnt/c/} in WSL; native Windows Emacs
uses its own Windows paths. Use a directory that exists and is writable in the
environment running Emacs. A speech server running on another host does not
change where ordinary Emacs document files are stored.

A relative name is resolved against the directory in the prompt@comma{} which may
change with the selected buffer. Do not assume it means the repository root
or the folder used in an earlier task. When an instruction says "from the
checkout"@comma{} it explicitly means that source directory. Replace placeholders
such as @code{/path/to/document.txt} with real names; do not create those directories
merely to copy the example literally. In Emacs file prompts@comma{} type spaces in
names normally rather than adding shell quotes around the whole path.

Keyboard sequences invoke Emacs commands. A shell example belongs at a shell
or terminal prompt in the environment named by the instructions@comma{} not after
@kbd{M-x}. Lisp configuration belongs in the specified Emacs configuration file;
it is not ordinary document text or a shell command. A displayed command's
output is something to check@comma{} not another command to enter. Read the sentence
introducing each example to establish which of these contexts it uses.

When practising@comma{} choose unused names. If a proposed name already contains work@comma{}
cancel and substitute another name consistently. An overwrite warning is not a
routine confirmation to accept. Opening@comma{} copying@comma{} saving and deleting have
different effects; the following task guides explain the effect before the step.

@node Manual Reading Routes
@section Choose a reading route

After @ref{Installation,Installation,,emacsvox,} gives you speech@comma{} work through @ref{Guided Tutorial,Learn the audio desktop,,emacsvox,} for
six progressive lessons using a disposable practice document. It teaches
reading@comma{} editing@comma{} Help@comma{} structure@comma{} temporary settings@comma{} and voice previews@comma{}
with exercises and separate answers. No additional engine is needed.

If Emacs itself is new to you@comma{} @kbd{C-h t} opens its separate interactive tutorial.
Use it for more practice with general editing. @ref{Basic Usage,Basic Usage,,emacsvox,}@comma{}
@ref{Using Online Help,Using Online Help,,emacsvox,}@comma{} and @ref{Audio Desktop,Audio Desktop,,emacsvox,} are references to return to as you work.

If you already use Emacs@comma{} start with @ref{Basic Usage,Basic Usage,,emacsvox,} once speech works.
Keep your usual editing and mode commands. The tutorial's
@ref{Tutorial Temporary Settings,temporary-settings lesson,,emacsvox,} and @ref{Tutorial Voice Experiments,voice experiments,,emacsvox,} provide guided practice
without making its earlier editing lessons prerequisites for experienced users;
prepare its disposable buffer before experimenting. Then read
@ref{Aural Presentation,Aural Presentation,,emacsvox,} for application meaning@comma{} voices@comma{} sounds@comma{} and settings@comma{}
and choose an application guide for your work.

For independent everyday tasks@comma{} use @ref{Working With Files, , Work with files and directories}@comma{}
@ref{Writing An Org Document, , Write a note with headings}@comma{} or @ref{Web Browsing, , Web browsing with EWW}. These guides
explain real files and pages; they do not require completing the tutorial or
changing your voice configuration.

For one question@comma{} use @ref{Finding Tasks, , Find a task}@comma{} Info's index or a text search. For a
familiar action whose key you cannot remember@comma{} use
@ref{Emacsvox Keyboard Commands,Everyday commands and key prefixes,,emacsvox,}. When something is not working@comma{}
@ref{Finding Problem Tasks, , Something is wrong} starts
with symptoms rather than the names of settings. @ref{Speech Backends,Speech Backends,,emacsvox,} contains alternative installation and backend
configuration procedures; it is not required reading between your first
speaking session and everyday editing. The final Developer Guide is for
changing and maintaining Emacsvox@comma{} not for ordinary use.

@node Finding Help In Info
@section Find an answer in Info and return

Info is Emacs's manual reader. A @emph{node} is one navigable topic@comma{} which can have
its own menu of subtopics. These instructions apply to the Info buffer@comma{} not
to Emacsvox's settings menus.

Try this lookup without changing your configuration:

@enumerate
@item
From a working buffer@comma{} press @kbd{C-h i} to open Info@comma{} @kbd{d} for its directory@comma{}
then @kbd{m}. Enter @code{Emacsvox} and press @kbd{@key{RET}} to open this manual.
@item
Press @kbd{i}@comma{} enter @code{ordinary reading voice}@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}. The index
takes you to the voice-task links. Read with your ordinary speech commands.
@item
Use @kbd{@key{TAB}} to reach @emph{Change my ordinary reading voice} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}.
You are now at the named-voice instructions. Reading them changes nothing;
leave the editing commands for when you deliberately want to make a change.
@item
Press lowercase @kbd{l} to return to the task links. Another @kbd{l} returns to
the topic you were reading before the index lookup. This follows your
visiting history rather than the chapter order.
@item
Press @kbd{q} to leave Info. From an Aural interface's contextual manual link@comma{}
@kbd{q} returns to that interface. If you opened other windows or switched
buffers during the exercise@comma{} @kbd{C-x b} also lets you select your original
working buffer by name.
@end enumerate

If the directory has no Emacsvox entry@comma{} the manual is not yet discoverable by
that Emacs session; follow @ref{Installation,Installation,,emacsvox,} rather than guessing a file path.
In an Aural editor@comma{} @kbd{C-c C-i} opens its relevant manual topic@comma{} and @kbd{i} in
that editor's Help opens the same topic.

The following Info commands are useful during later lookups:

@table @kbd
@item i
Look up a subject in the manual's indexes. Use ordinary words@comma{} a
command name@comma{} or a key sequence. @kbd{@comma{}} visits further matching entries.
@item s
Search the manual text@comma{} including other nodes. Enter the words and
press @kbd{@key{RET}}; @kbd{s @key{RET}} continues the same search.
@item TAB@comma{} S-TAB
Move among links. @kbd{@key{RET}} follows the selected link.
@item m
Choose a subtopic from the current node's menu by name@comma{} with completion.
@item u
Go to the containing topic. This is different from @kbd{l}@comma{} which goes
back to the topic you last visited.
@item g
Go to a node by its identifier. For example@comma{} enter @code{Finding Tasks}
to return directly to the task finder.
@end table

For the full reader tutorial and search behaviour@comma{} see the @ref{Top,Info manual,,info,}.
If a key behaves differently in your session@comma{} @kbd{C-h k} followed by that key
explains its effective binding without invoking it.

@node Manual Editions And Formats
@section Recognise the document you are reading

This user manual explains tasks@comma{} feedback@comma{} and recovery. The generated
@emph{Emacsvox Reference Manual} lists commands@comma{} options@comma{} and keymaps from source.
The heritage manual preserves historical Emacspeak material; it is not a
current setup guide. A linked application's own manual remains authoritative
for that application's general operations.

Some stable Info identifiers predate their displayed headings. For example@comma{}
@code{Aural Home} now identifies @emph{Getting started with settings menus}. This keeps
old links and bookmarks valid; it does not make the retired Home menu entry
part of the learning route.

In HTML@comma{} use the contents@comma{} headings@comma{} and task links@comma{} then your browser's Back
command to return from a link. The Info keys above are not browser commands.
The published website may describe a different revision from your installed
manual. Use the manual matching your Emacsvox version@comma{} and check live Help
when a key differs from the documented default.

@node Installation
@chapter Getting Started: Installation and First Speech

@c Generated from docs/manual/emacsvox.org; do not edit.

The aim of installation is a clean speaking session that you can test before
changing your personal Emacs setup. Choose the route for the machine running
Emacs. A Debian package is suitable when your distribution provides the
required Emacs and Transient. The source checkout supports GNU/Linux@comma{} macOS@comma{}
and WSL2. Native Windows uses its own
@ref{Trying Native Windows Emacsvox, , Windows installation procedure}. If speech will play on another workstation@comma{}
use @ref{Installing Remote Emacsvox, , Installing Remote Emacsvox} instead of installing a local speech server.

Use the manual supplied with your checkout or installed package. Older
releases may provide different settings menus. Keep a working installation
available while trying another version.

Commands shown before Emacsvox starts are entered in a terminal. Keys written
with @kbd{C-} or @kbd{M-} are entered inside Emacs. The published installation chapter
is also available before installation at
@uref{https://bartbunting.github.io/emacsvox/Installation.html}; its published version
may describe a different version from your installation.

