@section Debian and Ubuntu package

Emacsvox 2026.9.2 predates Debian packaging. Use a package from a release that
includes one@comma{} or a locally built development package. Omnivox 1.18.0 includes
an amd64 deb for supported Ubuntu systems.

With a downloaded Emacsvox deb@comma{} install it using apt:

@example
sudo apt install ./emacsvox_VERSION_all.deb
emacsvox --diagnose
emacsvox --check
emacsvox
@end example

Replace @code{VERSION} with the version in the downloaded filename. Apt requires
a packaged Emacs 30.2 or newer; an installed terminal-only @code{emacs-nox} also
satisfies this dependency. Settings menus also require Transient 0.13.3 or
newer. Apt installs @code{elpa-transient} when a packaged Emacs 31.1 or newer does
not already supply it. Ubuntu 26.10 supplies both dependencies. Ubuntu 26.04's
stock Transient is too old; use the source installation route below and upgrade
Transient through Emacs's package manager. The package never builds GNU Emacs
or adds an apt repository to resolve missing dependencies. If your distribution
also lacks a supported Emacs@comma{} the source route offers an explicit Emacs 31 build.

Use Omnivox 1.18.0 or newer for the Omnivox route documented here. If it is
unavailable from your apt repositories@comma{} include its downloaded deb in the same
apt command@comma{} or select an existing installation. Confirm both tone and speech
from @code{emacsvox --check} before starting the session.

For remote Emacs or WSL with an existing Windows speech server@comma{} use
@code{sudo apt install --no-install-recommends ./emacsvox_VERSION_all.deb}.
Remote sessions start with @code{emacsvox --remote} after the workstation service@comma{}
token and tunnel are prepared as described in
@ref{Installing Remote Emacsvox, , Installing Remote Emacsvox}. The package does not acquire a Windows speech
runtime; the guided WSL source installer below handles that.

Emacsvox installs under @code{/usr/share/emacsvox}@comma{} with the command
@code{/usr/bin/emacsvox}. It includes Lisp sources@comma{} sounds and offline manuals.
No personal initialisation is changed and ordinary Emacs sessions do not
automatically start speaking. To enable Emacsvox in your own Emacs setup@comma{} add
@code{(load-file "/usr/share/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")} explicitly.
Upgrade the system tree using apt; the source build instructions below apply
to checkouts. Removing the package preserves personal settings and speech
tokens. Package details are in @code{/usr/share/doc/emacsvox/README.Debian}.

@node Obtaining Emacsvox
@section Before You Begin

Install @code{git} to obtain the checkout. Emacsvox requires
@uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/download.html, Emacs 30.2 or newer}. WSL2 users can let the guided installer inspect an
existing Emacs@comma{} suggest a suitable package@comma{} or offer an explicit source build.
It does not begin an Emacs source build by default.

On GNU/Linux and macOS@comma{} install Emacs 30.2 or newer and @code{make}. Omnivox 1.18.0
or newer is the supported speech-server baseline. On macOS@comma{} use
@uref{https://brew.sh, Homebrew} for Omnivox as described in
@ref{Installing An Omnivox Release Binary, , Installing An Omnivox Release Binary}; Emacs and Emacsvox are installed
separately. Manual release-archive installation requires @code{curl}@comma{} @code{tar}@comma{} and
@code{sha256sum} on GNU/Linux or @code{shasum} on macOS@. GNU Texinfo is needed for
building or editing the documentation@comma{} not for an ordinary installation.

Ubuntu 26.04 provides Emacs 30.2 as a distribution package. The same Emacs
minimum applies to native Windows. On Emacs 30@comma{} install @code{lua-mode} separately
if you use Lua; it is built into Emacs 31. Corfu on an Emacs 30 text terminal
also needs @code{corfu-terminal}; Emacs 31 supports its terminal popup directly.

Check the Emacs found by your shell:

@example
emacs --version
@end example

The reported version must be 30.2 or newer. If the command is missing or
reports an older version@comma{} install a supported Emacs or locate its executable.
You can check a specific executable in the same way:

@example
/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs --version
@end example

@subsection Prepare the settings-menu dependency

Emacsvox requires Transient 0.13.3 or newer. Emacs 31 supplies a supported
version; Emacs 30.2's bundled 0.7.2.2 needs an upgrade before building Emacsvox.
Use your selected Emacs and its package manager@comma{} with GNU ELPA enabled:

@enumerate
@item
Run @kbd{M-x set-variable @key{RET} package-install-upgrade-built-in @key{RET} t @key{RET}}.
Answer the variable-name and value prompts separately. This permits package
dependencies to replace older built-in copies in this Emacs session; it
does not save a new startup preference. See @ref{Examining,Examining and setting variables,,emacs,}
in the GNU Emacs Manual for this command.
@item
Run @kbd{M-x package-refresh-contents @key{RET}}@comma{} then
@kbd{M-x package-upgrade @key{RET} transient @key{RET}}. Accept the package's dependencies.
@item
Start a fresh Emacs before building or trying the menus. A running session
can retain the older loaded version after installation.
@end enumerate

An isolated Emacsvox session activates an installed Transient and its
required packages when its bundled version is too old. It does not load your
init file@comma{} activate unrelated packages@comma{} or download dependencies. Install into
the package directory used by the same Emacs profile; a custom package directory
must be on that session's load path. Debian installations also discover the
system ELPA directory without site startup.

If a menu reports an unsupported version@comma{} inspect the version and loaded path
in its error. Upgrade that installation and restart Emacs; restarting only
speech does not replace loaded Lisp. The Emacs minimum remains 30.2.

@subsection Obtain the source checkout

Run the clone from a terminal on GNU/Linux@comma{} macOS@comma{} or WSL@. The following
location works for an ordinary per-user installation:

@example
mkdir -p ~/src
cd ~/src
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox
cd emacsvox
@end example

For an existing checkout@comma{} inspect @code{git status --short} and
@code{git branch --show-current} before changing branches or updating. Do not
replace or discard local work to make the checkout match an example.

The checkout is the installation directory. It builds in place and does not
create a second tree to copy elsewhere. Under WSL@comma{} keep it on the Linux
filesystem@comma{} not below a Windows-mounted path such as @code{/mnt/c}; Windows
filesystem semantics can interfere with Unix executable permissions and build
tools. Clone Omnivox beside Emacsvox only for an Omnivox source build or the
advanced reproducible WSL2 build.

@subsection Use the guided WSL2 installer

For a local WSL2 desktop@comma{} first inspect the machine without changing it:

@example
cd ~/src/emacsvox
./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check
@end example

If Emacs is missing@comma{} the report checks the available apt version and suggests
@code{sudo apt install emacs} only when that candidate satisfies 30.2. Older or
unavailable candidates are identified without being recommended. Refresh apt
metadata and check again@comma{} or choose the pinned source build with
@code{./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --build-emacs}. Add @code{--check} to inspect that
build's prerequisites first.

With Emacs selected@comma{} the report names it@comma{} the matching Omnivox Windows archive@comma{}
and the per-user installation paths. If source-build packages are missing@comma{} it
prints one @code{apt} command for you to review and run. The installer never invokes
@code{sudo} itself. After resolving reported prerequisites@comma{} run:

@example
./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install
@end example

If you chose @code{--build-emacs}@comma{} include that option in the installation command.
It prepares the pinned stable Emacs for your user. The installer then downloads
and verifies the matching Omnivox 1.18.0 Windows archive@comma{} records its path
without editing a shell startup file@comma{} compiles Emacsvox@comma{} runs a diagnostic@comma{}
and plays the final speech-server check. Later runs reuse the Emacs build and
verified downloads.

Routine compiler output is kept below @code{$XDG_CACHE_HOME/emacsvox/logs}@comma{}
normally @code{~/.cache/emacsvox/logs}. If a build fails@comma{} the installer shows its
final log lines and the complete log path. Continue only after hearing both
the final tone and speech. You can then go to the final step in
@ref{Quick Installation, , Quick Installation} and start Emacsvox. Optional engines can wait until
first speech is working.

@node Installing Remote Emacsvox
@section Installing On A Remote Linux Host

Use this route when Emacs runs on a Linux host and speech plays on your
workstation through an SSH reverse forward. It also works for remote-only
Emacs under WSL@comma{} without Windows interoperability. Remote access is a preview
feature. The workstation needs Omnivox 1.18.0 or newer@comma{} including @code{--serve}.

After cloning Emacsvox on the Emacs host@comma{} run:

@example
cd ~/src/emacsvox
./bin/emacsvox-install --role remote-emacs --check
./bin/emacsvox-install --role remote-emacs
@end example

The first command reports prerequisites and paths without changing files.
The second reuses Emacs 30.2 or newer and compiles Emacsvox. If Emacs is missing@comma{}
it checks the local apt candidate and suggests @code{sudo apt install emacs-nox}
when that version is suitable. To choose the source build instead@comma{} run
@code{./bin/emacsvox-install --role remote-emacs --build-emacs}. Add @code{--check} to
inspect build prerequisites first. The checksum-verified GNU Emacs 31.1 source
is built as a terminal-only installation under
@code{~/.local/opt/emacs-31.1-terminal}. Other Linux distributions currently need
an existing Emacs 30.2 or newer. A supported GUI Emacs can also be reused.
Explicit @code{EMACS} and @code{local.mk} selections are preserved; if they conflict or
select an unsupported version@comma{} resolve the reported setting first.

No local Omnivox@comma{} sound device@comma{} GUI build packages@comma{} or Windows speech tools
are required. Missing Debian/Ubuntu build packages are reported as one
@code{apt} command for you to review and run. The installer never invokes @code{sudo}
or changes a shell startup file. Build logs are kept below
@code{~/.cache/emacsvox/logs}@comma{} or the corresponding @code{XDG_CACHE_HOME} location.

Installation can finish before the workstation is connected. Follow
@ref{Remote workstation speech setup, , Remote Workstation Speech} to prepare its service@comma{} private token@comma{} and SSH
reverse forward. Then run on the Emacs host:

@example
./bin/emacsvox --remote --diagnose
./bin/emacsvox --remote --check
./bin/emacsvox --remote
@end example

Diagnosis reports local configuration without connecting or reading the token's
contents. The check connects both speech lanes@comma{} waits for workstation inventory
and voice registration@comma{} and speaks a foreground announcement followed by a
notification announcement. Confirm that both are audible. The last command
starts an isolated terminal Emacsvox session.

The default forwarded endpoint is @code{127.0.0.1:6417}. The token file defaults
to @code{~/.config/omnivox/remote-token}@comma{} respecting @code{XDG_CONFIG_HOME} when set.
To select another forwarded port or private token path@comma{} supply environment
values to the same command:

@example
EMACSVOX_REMOTE_PORT=6418 \
EMACSVOX_REMOTE_TOKEN_FILE="$HOME/.config/omnivox/other-token" \
  ./bin/emacsvox --remote --check
@end example

@code{EMACSVOX_REMOTE_HOST} accepts only @code{127.0.0.1} or @code{::1}. The token path
must be absolute; its contents stay in the private file. Use the same settings
when starting the session. Remote mode selects the Omnivox transport and skips
local speech executable and playback probes; leave @code{TTS_PROGRAM} unset or set
it to @code{omnivox}.

For updates@comma{} keep your speaking session open@comma{} pull the checkout with
@code{git pull --ff-only}@comma{} and rerun the remote installer. It rebuilds byte-code
with the selected Emacs. Perform the connected check while the workstation
service is free@comma{} and test the updated session before closing the known-good
one. A single service admits only one Emacs session at a time: use a separate
test service or explicitly disconnect the old remote session before connecting
its replacement.

@node Updating Emacsvox Under WSL2
@section Updating An Existing WSL2 Installation

The guided installer recompiles the source in the checkout; it does not fetch
newer source. Keep the current speaking Emacsvox session open while updating.
Run these commands from @kbd{M-x shell @key{RET}} in that session or another accessible
WSL terminal:

@example
cd ~/src/emacsvox
git status --short
git branch --show-current
git pull --ff-only
./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check
./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install
@end example

Replace @code{~/src/emacsvox} if you chose another Linux-filesystem location.
Check that this is the intended branch before pulling. The fast-forward-only
pull does not create a merge commit. If Git reports local changes@comma{} divergence@comma{}
or another error@comma{} stop and review the checkout; do not reset or discard files
to complete an update.

Rerunning the installer reuses supported Emacs and verified cached downloads@comma{}
installs a newly pinned Omnivox release when necessary@comma{} rebuilds Emacsvox
byte-code from scratch so files from the previous source revision cannot
survive@comma{} and repeats the launch checks. These operations change files on disk
but do not stop the Emacsvox process already providing speech. If the update
fails@comma{} leave that session running while inspecting the error and named log.

After the installer succeeds and you hear its final check@comma{} start a second@comma{}
isolated process from the updated checkout without closing the old one:

@example
cd ~/src/emacsvox
./bin/emacsvox
@end example

The old session remains the known-good source of speech while you test its
replacement. The new process cannot retain Lisp loaded from the previous
version. Confirm its ready message and ordinary buffer reading. In the new
session@comma{} @kbd{M-x emacsvox-speak-version @key{RET}} reports the Emacsvox version and
@kbd{M-x tts-speak-version @key{RET}} reports the live speech-server version. Only after
these checks pass should you save work in the old session and exit it.

Continue in the isolated session or start Emacsvox through your normal
customised entry point. If the new session does not speak@comma{} close it but leave
the old session running while inspecting the diagnostic and installer logs.

To update Emacsvox without rerunning the guided installer@comma{} replace the two
installer commands after @code{git pull --ff-only} with:

@example
make check-emacs
make bytecode-rebuild
./bin/emacsvox --diagnose
./bin/emacsvox --check
@end example

All four commands must succeed@comma{} and the final check must produce both tone
and speech@comma{} before the second-session test above. This manual procedure
rebuilds Emacsvox but does not download or select a newly pinned Omnivox
release. When Omnivox also needs an independent update@comma{} follow @emph{Manual Release
Installation} in
@ref{Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2, , Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2}@comma{} then repeat the diagnostic
and audible check.

@node Building And Testing The Speech Server
@section Install And Check Omnivox

If the guided WSL2 installer succeeded@comma{} Omnivox is already installed and
checked. Continue to @ref{Quick Installation, , Quick Installation}.

On macOS@comma{} follow the Homebrew instructions in
@ref{Installing An Omnivox Release Binary, , Installing An Omnivox Release Binary}. They install the matching Mac binary
and record its stable executable path for Emacsvox@comma{} including sessions that do
not inherit the shell's @code{PATH}. Homebrew installs Omnivox only; continue below
to build and start Emacsvox with your selected Emacs 30.2 or newer.

On a matching GNU/Linux x86-64 host@comma{} or for a manual Mac installation@comma{} use the
archive instructions in that section. They verify the published 1.18.0 archive@comma{}
keep its runtime data beside the executable@comma{} and add its versioned installation
directory to @code{PATH}. Then run:

@example
omnivox --check
@end example

Use @ref{Installing Omnivox From Source, , Installing Omnivox From Source} on Linux ARM64@comma{} an incompatible distribution@comma{}
or a host whose security policy does not permit the unsigned macOS archive.
No proprietary speech runtime is needed for the GNU/Linux or macOS routes.
For a manual WSL2 installation@comma{} or details of what its guided installer checks@comma{}
see @ref{Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2, , Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2}.

The check plays a tone followed by speech. Hearing both is the acceptance
condition; a successful exit code alone does not prove audible output. If
either is missing@comma{} follow
@ref{Backend Diagnostics And Ownership, , Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities} before continuing.

@subsection When the binary does not match the host

The Linux x86-64 archive requires an Ubuntu 24.04-compatible glibc@comma{} libstdc++@comma{}
libgcc@comma{} and ALSA runtime. The Omnivox source route requires Rust@comma{} Python 3@comma{}
a C and C++ toolchain@comma{} CMake@comma{} and Clang with libclang. It also requires
@code{pkg-config} and ALSA development headers on GNU/Linux@comma{} or Xcode command-line
tools on macOS@. These are not requirements for the recommended WSL2 binary
route. Docker and Windows .NET Framework are needed only for the advanced
reproducible source-build route in
@ref{Building Omnivox For WSL2, , Building Omnivox For WSL2}.

@ref{Speech Backends, , Speech Backends} owns the complete platform matrix@comma{} alternative builds@comma{} and
compatibility servers. Native Windows x64 is supported with Emacs 30.2 or newer;
its PowerShell installer reuses a prebuilt Emacs@comma{} can download a verified Emacs
with @code{-DownloadEmacs}@comma{} or offers an explicit source build. It installs Omnivox
without WSL; follow @ref{Trying Native Windows Emacsvox, , Installing Native Windows Emacsvox}.

@node Quick Installation
@section Build And Start Emacsvox

The guided WSL2 installer has already selected Emacs@comma{} compiled Emacsvox@comma{} and
run the two checks below. Go directly to the final step to start the isolated
session. The full sequence is for GNU/Linux@comma{} macOS@comma{} manual WSL2 installation@comma{}
or later diagnosis.

@enumerate
@item
Enter the Emacsvox checkout:

@example
cd /path/where/you/keep/source/emacsvox
@end example

@item
Select Emacs 30.2 or newer. If it is not the system @code{emacs} and no
@code{local.mk} exists@comma{} create the ignored local settings file:

@example
cp local.mk.example local.mk
@end example

Edit an existing @code{local.mk} rather than replacing it. Set @code{EMACS} there:

@example
EMACS=/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs
@end example

The earlier @code{emacs --version} checks your shell's default. Verify the
executable selected by the build separately:

@example
make check-emacs
@end example

This checks the Emacs selected by @code{local.mk} when present. It can finish
without printing anything when the version is supported. Resolve any
executable or version error before compiling.

@item
Compile Emacsvox:

@example
make bytecode
@end example

A successful build returns to the shell without an error. Compile with
the same supported Emacs selected in the previous step.

@item
Run the non-audible launcher preflight:

@example
./bin/emacsvox --diagnose
@end example

This checks the checkout@comma{} Emacs@comma{} and speech server without playing audio
or opening interactive Emacs. A successful report names Emacs 30.2 or
newer@comma{} the @code{omnivox} speech server@comma{} and an isolated @code{-Q} startup. Correct
any reported path or version problem@comma{} then repeat the command.

@item
Check the complete launch path with sound:

@example
./bin/emacsvox --check
@end example

The launcher prints its selections@comma{} then plays a tone and speech. Confirm
that both are audible before continuing.

@item
Start a clean Emacsvox session:

@example
./bin/emacsvox
@end example

The launcher uses Omnivox and the selected Emacs without loading site
files or your personal init file. It does not change an existing Emacs
setup.
@end enumerate

For explicit paths@comma{} @code{./bin/emacsvox --help} describes the @code{EMACS}@comma{}
@code{TTS_PROGRAM}@comma{} and @code{EMACSVOX_DIR} overrides. Arguments after @code{--} are passed
to Emacs.

@node Confirming First Speech
@section Confirming First Speech

The stable first-speech checkpoint is:

@quotation
Emacsvox is ready.

@end quotation

It normally follows Omnivox's acknowledgement of initial engine inventory@comma{}
routing policy@comma{} and logical voices@comma{} and therefore uses the restored engine
policy. If routing does not finish within five seconds@comma{} Emacsvox speaks it
through the capability-ready fallback. Hearing the checkpoint does not by
itself prove that routing completed or every package integration loaded.
An earlier introduction@comma{} overview key@comma{} version number@comma{} and startup auditory
icon may also be heard; those can change.

Run @kbd{M-x emacsvox-startup-status @key{RET}} to check background preparation of
Emacsvox and its package integrations. The command speaks a summary and opens
a report. Integrations waiting for their packages are normal: speech support
loads when you open those packages. Run the command again to refresh the
report; @kbd{q} closes it.

Before connecting Emacsvox to your personal configuration@comma{} check the live
adapter and everyday feedback:

@enumerate
@item
Begin in an ordinary working buffer. Press @kbd{C-e m} and note its name@comma{}
then run @kbd{M-x tts-speak-version @key{RET}}. The live adapter should speak its
version response.
@item
Use @kbd{C-h v tts-program @key{RET}}. In this isolated launcher session@comma{} the value
should name @code{omnivox}. This opens Help; it is no longer a test in the
original working buffer.
@item
Leave Help with @kbd{q}@comma{} then use @kbd{C-x b} to select the working buffer by name
if necessary. Confirm it with @kbd{C-e m} before moving over its lines with
@kbd{C-n} and @kbd{C-p}. The reached lines should be spoken.
@end enumerate

@kbd{M-x emacsvox-describe-emacsvox @key{RET}} opens the overview. Return to your working
buffer before trying the buffer-local rate adjustments below. See
@ref{Using Online Help,Using Online Help,,emacsvox,} for Help's selection and window behaviour.

For a rate change through the settings interface@comma{} open @kbd{C-e H s} and select
@emph{Rate}; @kbd{@key{RET}} or @kbd{r} enters an exact value. The direct commands remain useful
when first learning the menus: @kbd{C-e d -}@comma{} followed by repeated @kbd{-}@comma{} slows the
current buffer@comma{} and @kbd{C-e d r} accepts an exact rate such as @code{40} for Omnivox.
For individually echoed characters@comma{} @kbd{C-e d f} accepts a multiplier such as
@code{0.7}. A prefix @kbd{C-u} on the exact-rate or character-factor command changes
the global default rather than only a buffer-local setting.

When these checks pass@comma{} continue to @ref{Guided Tutorial,Learn the audio desktop,,emacsvox,} for connected
practice@comma{} or use @ref{Basic Usage, , Basic Usage} as an everyday reference. Connecting Emacsvox
to your normal configuration through @ref{Persistent Startup, , Persistent Startup} is a separate choice;
it is not required to continue learning in the isolated session.

@subsection Add optional engines after first speech

@kbd{C-e H v b} opens the engine browser. The same list is available with
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-omnivox-manage-components @key{RET}}. Select an engine and press
@kbd{@key{RET}} for its details. These distinguish current engine availability from
files installed for a separately managed release.

For supported WSL release management@comma{} choose @emph{Manage installed release} and
check its version and location before choosing @emph{Install engine}. The manager
confirms@comma{} checksum-verifies@comma{} installs@comma{} and validates the selected engine
without @code{sudo}. The installed-release view also offers @emph{Uninstall engine}
for a manager-installed engine. Removal retains its verified download cache;
@emph{Install engine} can reinstall it. Core engines and separately supplied
runtimes cannot be removed through that operation.

The browser distinguishes included engines@comma{} downloadable components@comma{} and
bridges requiring a user-supplied runtime. See
@ref{Optional Omnivox Engine Modules, , Optional Omnivox Engines} for activation@comma{} recovery@comma{} and licensing boundaries.
Optional-engine management is not a prerequisite for ordinary WinRT speech.

@node Recovering From No Speech
@section Recovering From No Speech

Keep the isolated launcher as your known starting point. Find the first stage
that failed rather than adding configuration at several layers at once.

@table @asis
@item @code{./bin/emacsvox --diagnose} fails
No audio or interactive Emacs has started. Under WSL2@comma{} begin with
@code{./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check} and resolve its first reported item.
Otherwise@comma{} correct the selected @code{EMACS}@comma{} the @code{EMACS} value in @code{local.mk}@comma{}
or the installed Emacs version. When the speech server is not executable@comma{}
finish installing Omnivox@comma{} put it on @code{PATH}@comma{} or set @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} to
its absolute executable. The guided WSL2 installer records that selection
in a per-user file; rerunning it repairs a missing installation or selection
without requiring a shell startup edit.
@item The diagnostic passes@comma{} but @code{--check} errors or is inaudible
Diagnose the speech server before Emacs configuration. Follow
@ref{Backend Diagnostics And Ownership, , Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities} until both tone and speech
are audible.
@item The audible check passes@comma{} but ``Emacsvox is ready'' is absent
Run @code{./bin/emacsvox --diagnose} again from the same checkout and shell.
Remove an unintended @code{TTS_PROGRAM} override if it does not select
@code{omnivox}. Request a startup backtrace with:

@example
./bin/emacsvox -- --debug-init
@end example

@code{Could not find speech server executable} means the server visible during
that start cannot be executed. Correct its reported path rather than
adding more init-file configuration. For an Omnivox process error@comma{} inspect
the private log directory described below.
@item The ready message is heard@comma{} but one mode lacks spoken feedback
Run @kbd{M-x emacsvox-startup-status @key{RET}}. It names failed startup work or
package integrations and retains the original error. Select the report and
inspect its lines or paragraphs. @kbd{M-x emacsvox-speak-buffer @key{RET}} can read a
short report; large requests have the limits described in
@ref{Reading Without Moving Point,Short reading and buffer limits,,emacsvox,}. Correct the named package@comma{}
configuration@comma{} or Emacsvox problem@comma{} then verify in a fresh session.
For source edits@comma{} follow the byte-code build procedure before testing.
Repeating startup or restarting the speech server does not retry failed
integrations: an interrupted load can leave some hooks or advice installed.
Keep the original error when requesting help.
@item The ready message is heard@comma{} but speech later stops
If Emacs reports that a speech server exited@comma{} inspect the Omnivox session
log and run @kbd{M-x tts-restart @key{RET}} after correcting the server problem.
Logs are below @code{$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox}@comma{} defaulting to
@code{~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox}. Spoken text is omitted unless explicit
text logging is enabled.
@item The isolated launcher works@comma{} but a customised Emacs start does not
Remove or comment out the Emacsvox environment and @code{load-file} forms from
your init file. This rollback leaves the checkout and known-good
@code{./bin/emacsvox} path intact. Re-add the forms in the order shown in
@ref{Persistent Startup, , Persistent Startup}; server selection must precede loading setup.
@end table

@node Persistent Startup
@section Persistent Startup

After @ref{Confirming First Speech, , Confirming First Speech} succeeds@comma{} add these forms to your init file@comma{}
replacing the example path with the checkout's absolute path:

@lisp
(setenv "TTS_PROGRAM" "omnivox")
(load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")
@end lisp

Keep that order: Emacsvox selects and starts the server while loading setup.
A shell alternative is to export the server name before launching Emacs:

@example
export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox
@end example

Keep the explicit @code{load-file} form for a changing source checkout. Its setup
entry point can warn and prefer source when newer Lisp would otherwise be
hidden by stale local byte-code. A customised startup should speak the same
ready message as the isolated launcher. If it does not@comma{} remove or comment out
the two init forms and return to the isolated launcher before another change.

@node Guided Tutorial
@chapter Learn the audio desktop

@cindex tutorial
@cindex learning Emacsvox
@cindex practice document

This tutorial takes you from reading a short document to exploring its
structure and making a deliberate speech adjustment. Work through the lessons
in order the first time. Each builds on the same practice document; the
exercises ask you to use what you have learned without repeating every step.
Answers are collected at the end so you can try before consulting them.

Start with a speaking Emacsvox session prepared through
@ref{Installation,Installation,,emacsvox,}@comma{} including its settings-menu dependency. No mail account@comma{}
agent service@comma{} downloaded voice@comma{} or terminal package is needed. The first
five lessons work with text and the Org mode supplied with Emacs. The final
lesson uses an already available voice. If your backend cannot preview an
adjustment@comma{} keep the working voice and follow the reported limitation rather
than installing another engine to complete the exercise.

The required exercises do not save speech settings. One clearly marked
optional exercise creates a personal palette; you can finish the tutorial
without doing it. Do not practise in a document@comma{} draft@comma{} or configuration that
you need to preserve.

If key notation is new to you@comma{} read @ref{Emacs Key Notation, , Read the key instructions} before
starting. It explains modifiers@comma{} separate steps@comma{} capital letters and command
names. The short reminders below use that notation.

Keys are the defaults. @kbd{C-n} means hold Control and press n. In @kbd{C-e l}@comma{}
press Control-e@comma{} release it@comma{} then press lowercase l. @kbd{M-} normally means
Alt or Option@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} means Enter@comma{} and @kbd{@key{SPC}} means Space. Capital letters
matter. Use @kbd{M-x}@comma{} type the command name@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{RET}} when a command is
given by name. @ref{Using This Manual,Using this manual,,emacsvox,} explains finding a topic and returning from it.

@menu
* Prepare a practice buffer: Tutorial Practice Buffer.  Make a disposable copy of the practice text without editing repository files.
* Lesson 1 read and locate point: Tutorial Reading.  Distinguish movement, explicit reading, and stopping speech.
* Lesson 2 find@comma{} edit@comma{} and recover text: Tutorial Editing.  Search for a known word, make a small edit, and verify undo.
* Lesson 3 get Help and return to your work: Tutorial Help And Buffers.  Keep two tasks open, inspect a key, and return without losing your place.
* Lesson 4 navigate the document's structure: Tutorial Structured Navigation.  Explore Org headings, links, and a table without evaluating code.
* Lesson 5 change speech in one buffer: Tutorial Temporary Settings.  Compare punctuation levels, inspect scope, and restore the previous policy.
* Lesson 6 preview and keep a voice experiment: Tutorial Voice Experiments.  Compare an unsaved voice draft, resume it, and distinguish saving from selection.
* Exercise answers: Tutorial Exercise Answers.  Check the exercises and the reasons behind their recovery steps.
* Finish and choose your next task: Tutorial Next Steps.  Leave practice safely and choose a task or a deeper explanation.
@end menu

@node Tutorial Practice Buffer
@section Prepare a practice buffer

A buffer is Emacs's working copy of text. It need not have a file behind it.
We will use a new buffer so that editing@comma{} undo@comma{} and temporary settings cannot
change the supplied example or an existing document.

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C-x b}@comma{} enter @code{evox-tutorial}@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}. If that name already
belongs to work you want to keep@comma{} use an unused name such as
@code{evox-tutorial-2} and substitute it throughout these lessons.
@item
Run @kbd{M-x text-mode @key{RET}}. This starts with ordinary text rather than Org's
structural commands. Press @kbd{C-e m} to hear the buffer and mode.
@item
Insert the practice text below. In a source checkout@comma{} you can use
@kbd{C-x i} and select @code{docs/manual/examples/tutorial-practice.org} relative
to that checkout. This inserts a copy; it does not visit or modify that
file. Otherwise copy or type the displayed text into the empty buffer.
@item
Press @kbd{M-<} to move to the beginning. @kbd{C-e l} reads the current line.
It should contain the heading @code{* Practice note}.
@end enumerate

The example is included here so you can use an installed Info or HTML manual
without a source checkout. Preserve its asterisks@comma{} brackets@comma{} pipes@comma{} and line
breaks. Those characters become useful in the structure lesson. The code at
the end is for reading@comma{} not evaluation; do not run it.

@example
* Practice note
The green notebook is on the desk.
This sentence is ready.
Check (A+B)@comma{} then C.

** Packing list
- Notebook
- Headphones
- Cable

** Journey plan
| Day     | Task | Status  |
|---------+------+---------|
| Monday  | Read | Ready   |
| Tuesday | Edit | Planned |

** Reference
[[*Packing list][Return to the packing list]]

** Code to read
#+begin_src emacs-lisp
(+ 2 3)
#+end_src
@end example

Keep the manual and the practice buffer available. @kbd{C-x b} followed by your
practice buffer's name returns to your work without closing the manual.
Finish any active minibuffer prompt first; @kbd{C-g} cancels an ordinary unfinished
Emacs command. In a settings menu or an editor@comma{} use the return procedure
specified in the lesson instead of assuming that every quit key is identical.

If you need a fresh copy@comma{} leave menus and editors first@comma{} then use @kbd{C-x k}
while in the disposable practice buffer. Check the buffer name and discard
only that practice text if asked. Create a new buffer and insert the example
again. Do not use this reset procedure on other work.

@node Tutorial Reading
@section Lesson 1: read and locate point

Emacs calls the editing position @emph{point}. Movement changes point and Emacsvox
speaks what you reach. Short inspections@comma{} such as reading a line or character@comma{}
leave point in place. Learning that distinction makes it easier to recover
when speech is interrupted or you are unsure where the next edit will occur.

@enumerate
@item
In the practice buffer@comma{} press @kbd{M-<}@comma{} then @kbd{C-n}. You reach the line about
the green notebook and hear its text.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e l} twice. Each invocation reads that line again without moving
point. Now press @kbd{C-n}: you reach the sentence about being ready@comma{} not a line
farther down because of the repeated reading.
@item
Press @kbd{C-p} to return to the notebook line@comma{} then @kbd{C-a} to reach its start.
@kbd{C-e c} reads the character at point. Use @kbd{C-f} to move one character
forward and @kbd{C-b} to move back. Point moves only with the movement commands.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e b} to start reading the buffer and @kbd{C-e s} to stop that speech.
Then press @kbd{C-e l}. It still works: stopping an utterance has not switched
speech off or closed the document.
@end enumerate

This small-buffer exercise uses @kbd{C-e b}@comma{} which leaves point in place.
Tracked reading with @kbd{C-e i} is different: on a supporting speech server it
advances point by chunks. Stopping leaves a chunk boundary@comma{} not necessarily
the exact word last heard. See @ref{Reading From Point, , Read from point@comma{} stop@comma{} and start again}
when you are ready for longer reading; it is not required for this exercise.

The exact voice and pronunciation depend on your current settings. Check the
text you reached@comma{} not whether your system sounds like someone else's.
@kbd{C-e m} identifies the buffer and mode whenever your context is unclear.

If @kbd{M-<} is intercepted by your desktop or screen reader@comma{} run
@kbd{M-x beginning-of-buffer @key{RET}} instead. This is an Emacs command@comma{} not a
requirement to change screen-reader configuration.

@subsection Try it

@strong{1A@.} From the start of the buffer@comma{} reach the line beginning @code{Check} using
line movement. Read it twice without changing point.

@strong{1B@.} Start a longer reading@comma{} stop it@comma{} and establish both which buffer you
are in and what its current line contains. Do not restart Emacs or speech.

See @ref{tutorial-answer-1, , Answers for lesson 1}.

@node Tutorial Editing
@section Lesson 2: find@comma{} edit@comma{} and recover text

Searching locates text; it does not change the text. Editing happens at point@comma{}
which is after the match when a forward search succeeds. We will make one
small insertion@comma{} inspect it@comma{} and undo it before attempting a replacement.

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{M-<}@comma{} then @kbd{C-s} and type @code{ready}. When the search finds the word@comma{}
press @kbd{@key{RET}} to finish. Point is after @code{ready} and before its full stop.
@item
Type a space followed by @code{now}. @kbd{C-e l} should read the sentence with
@code{ready now}. The other sentences are unchanged.
@item
In the practice document@comma{} press @kbd{C-g} to deactivate any selection@comma{} then
run @kbd{M-x undo-only @key{RET}}. Read the line with @kbd{C-e l}. If another undo is
needed@comma{} run @code{undo-only} again and inspect again. Undo works in editing
groups@comma{} not necessarily one character at a time. Stop when the sentence
is back to @code{This sentence is ready.}; do not undo the original example.
Use @code{undo-only} for this listen-between-steps pattern: after an intervening
reading command@comma{} ordinary @kbd{C-/} can redo an earlier undo instead.
@item
Search for @code{notebook} from the beginning and finish with @kbd{@key{RET}}. Notice that
searching and reading did not modify the document. You can return to any
known phrase without counting lines.
@end enumerate

If a search does not find its text@comma{} check your spelling. @kbd{C-g} during a
failing search first backs out of the failure; another @kbd{C-g} may be needed to
leave the search. Wait until the search prompt is gone before typing ordinary
text. A successful search is accepted with @kbd{@key{RET}}@comma{} not by typing the edit into
its search prompt.

@subsection Try it

@strong{2A@.} Replace @code{green} with @code{blue}. Use a successful search for the whole word@comma{}
then @kbd{M-@key{DEL}} to remove the word immediately before point@comma{} and type its
replacement. Here @kbd{@key{DEL}} means Backspace; @kbd{M-x backward-kill-word @key{RET}} is an
alternative to @kbd{M-@key{DEL}}. Inspect the complete line and restore @code{green} before
continuing.

@strong{2B@.} What would happen if you typed @code{now} before accepting the search?
Explain the difference between changing the query and changing the document.

See @ref{tutorial-answer-2, , Answers for lesson 2}. Text undo is not a general undo command for settings
menus; we will practise their recovery separately. For more on inspecting
between undo steps@comma{} see @ref{Recovering Text Edits,Recover a text edit before saving,,emacsvox,} and
@ref{Undo,Undo,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual.

@node Tutorial Help And Buffers
@section Lesson 3: get Help and return to your work

A Help buffer is another piece of work you can visit. It need not replace the
document you were editing. We will keep a second text buffer available@comma{} then
inspect a command without running it.

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C-x b}@comma{} enter an unused name such as @code{evox-tutorial-notes}@comma{} and
press @kbd{@key{RET}}. Run @kbd{M-x text-mode @key{RET}} and type
@code{I can return to my document by name.}.
@item
Use @kbd{C-x b} and your original practice buffer's name to return. Its text
and position are still available. @kbd{C-e m} and @kbd{C-e l} confirm where you are.
@item
From the practice document@comma{} press @kbd{C-h k C-e l}. Emacs describes the
current-line reading key instead of executing it as the requested action.
Spoken key Help selects the @code{*Help*} buffer and can remove other windows.
Your practice document is still open. @kbd{C-e h} reads Help again; it selects
Help rather than speaking it from the document's window.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e m} to check which buffer is selected. If it is @code{*Help*}@comma{} read
there directly; otherwise use @kbd{C-x b} to select @code{*Help*} by name. Do not
switch windows merely because Help appeared. Press @kbd{q} while in Help when
finished@comma{} then select the practice buffer with @kbd{C-x b} if necessary.
Check its name and line before editing.
@item
Press @kbd{C-h m} from the practice buffer@comma{} not from Help. It describes that
buffer's modes and commands. Mode Help need not select its text for you;
use the same check-and-select procedure to read it and return.
@end enumerate

The information comes from your running Emacs@comma{} so it is particularly useful
when a package or personal binding differs from this manual. A buffer and a
window are different: switching buffers chooses content@comma{} while @kbd{C-x o}
selects another window. A changed layout does not mean a buffer was closed.
You do not have to recreate a split to finish this exercise: returning by
buffer name is enough. @ref{Using Online Help, , Find help while you work} explains how to read and leave
ordinary Help and the distinct return route used by settings-menu Help.

@subsection Try it

@strong{3A@.} Without closing either text buffer@comma{} visit the notes@comma{} add a second line@comma{}
and return to the practice document. Confirm that each contains its own text.

@strong{3B@.} Discover what @kbd{C-e s} does without stopping speech as the action you
are investigating. Then find the keys for the command name reported by Help@comma{}
using @kbd{C-h w}.

See @ref{tutorial-answer-3, , Answers for lesson 3}. For manual lookup and navigation history@comma{} practise
@ref{Using This Manual,Find an answer in Info and return,,emacsvox,} alongside this buffer-based Help exercise.

@node Tutorial Structured Navigation
@section Lesson 4: navigate the document's structure

The practice text is also an Org document. Changing its major mode gives
Emacs commands that understand headings@comma{} lists@comma{} links@comma{} and tables. The text
remains yours; a folded section is hidden@comma{} not deleted.

@enumerate
@item
Return to the practice buffer and run @kbd{M-x org-mode @key{RET}}. Press @kbd{M-<}@comma{}
then @kbd{C-u C-u C-u @key{TAB}} to show the whole document@comma{} including any folded
sections. Each @kbd{C-u} is a separate prefix keystroke. @kbd{C-e m} now identifies
Org rather than Text mode.
@item
From the first heading@comma{} press @kbd{C-c C-n} to reach @code{Packing list}. This
moves to a heading@comma{} skipping intervening body text. @kbd{C-c C-p} returns to
the previous visible heading. Go forward to @code{Packing list} again.
@item
Press @kbd{@key{TAB}} on that heading to fold its body. @kbd{C-c C-n} reaches
@code{Journey plan}. Return with @kbd{C-c C-p} and press @kbd{@key{TAB}} to expose the list
again. Read down into it to confirm that the items are still present.
@item
Show all content again with @kbd{C-u C-u C-u @key{TAB}}@comma{} then search from the
beginning for @code{Return to the packing list}. Finish with @kbd{@key{RET}} and press
@kbd{C-b} once to leave point inside the link text. @kbd{C-c C-o} follows this
internal link to the heading; no website or external file is opened.
@end enumerate

Heading speech and collapse cues depend on your presentation settings.
What matters is the location and visibility you can inspect. Do not require
one exact spoken phrase or one physical voice to recognise a heading.

@subsection Read the table without editing it

Show the whole document again@comma{} then search from the beginning for @code{Monday}
and finish with @kbd{@key{RET}}. You are in the first data row's Day cell.

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C-e C-t C-r} to enter table reading without moving. Emacsvox
announces the table dimensions and the current cell.
@item
Press @kbd{@key{TAB}} to reach the Task cell@comma{} @code{Read}. @kbd{@key{RET}} reads that cell again.
In this reader these keys do not align the table or insert a row.
@item
Press Down. You remain in the Task column and hear the complete Tuesday
row. Press @kbd{c} to read the column@comma{} or @kbd{r} to repeat the current row.
@kbd{.} reports coordinates; headers and data rows are distinguished.
@item
Press @kbd{q} to leave table reading@comma{} then @kbd{C-e l} to inspect the text line.
The table reader closes@comma{} not the practice buffer. Ordinary Org editing
keys are restored.
@end enumerate

Outside table reading@comma{} Org's @kbd{@key{TAB}} is an editing/navigation command that can
align a table and create rows. A key's meaning belongs to the active mode or
reader@comma{} not to its letter alone. Do not evaluate the source block at the end
of the document: it is present so you can also inspect literal code.

@subsection Try it

@strong{4A@.} Enter table reading at @code{Monday} again. Reach Tuesday's Status cell and
read it without typing into the table. Then leave reading mode.

@strong{4B@.} Fold @code{Packing list}@comma{} read another section@comma{} and bring the list back.
Explain why text undo is not needed to recover the hidden items.

See @ref{tutorial-answer-4, , Answers for lesson 4}. @ref{Browsing Structure,Browsing Structure,,emacsvox,} documents the complete shared table reader;
@ref{Motion,Org heading motion,,org,} and @ref{Visibility Cycling,Org visibility cycling,,org,} explain the underlying document commands.

@node Tutorial Temporary Settings
@section Lesson 5: change speech in one buffer

Now change how one line is spoken without changing its text. This is a live
buffer adjustment@comma{} not a draft preview. Closing the menu keeps it in effect;
restoring it is a separate action. We will not save a default.

@enumerate
@item
In the practice buffer@comma{} leave table reading if active. Show all content@comma{}
then search from the beginning for @code{Check (A+B)@comma{} then C.} and accept the
match. Press @kbd{C-a}@comma{} then @kbd{C-e l} to hear the starting presentation.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e H s p} without a prefix argument. Read the scope and current
punctuation policy. It must name the current buffer@comma{} not global defaults.
Record whether the original policy is automatic@comma{} an explicit level@comma{} or a
named profile. If scope is global@comma{} use @kbd{g} to choose buffer scope before
changing anything.
Practise selecting an item with up/down and changing sections with
left/right. Press @kbd{C-c l} to repeat the selected item@comma{} then @kbd{M-f} to hear
its first word. @kbd{C-a C-f} reviews its first character. These review keys
leave the same item selected and do not change punctuation. In a menu@comma{}
@kbd{C-e} goes to the end of item review; the ordinary speech prefix returns
after you close the menu. See @ref{settings-menu-navigation, , menu navigation} for the full controls.
@item
Press @kbd{? a} for Help on All@comma{} then @kbd{q} to return to the punctuation menu.
Press @kbd{a} to apply All@comma{} followed by @kbd{C-q} to close the menus. Back in the
document@comma{} @kbd{C-e l} lets you compare the same line with punctuation spoken.
@item
Visit the notes buffer and read its line. This operation did not set its
punctuation policy. Return to the practice buffer@comma{} reopen @kbd{C-e H s p}@comma{}
choose @kbd{n} for None@comma{} close with @kbd{C-q}@comma{} and compare the same line again.
@item
Restore the original policy. For an automatic policy@comma{} use @kbd{r} in that
buffer's punctuation menu. For an explicit level or named profile@comma{} use
@kbd{p} and select the recorded choice instead. Close the menu and verify
the line and the reported policy. Do not press uppercase @kbd{S}.
@end enumerate

Punctuation pronunciation varies by engine and configuration@comma{} so judge the
change by the treatment of the line's punctuation rather than an exact sample
transcript. The characters in the buffer never changed. Restoring Automatic
returns control to the mode policy; it is not a synonym for choosing Some.

An explicit global change can affect both the default and the source buffer.
Saving a default adds a persistence step and does not cancel stronger mode
policies or buffer overrides. Those are different tasks from this temporary
experiment; see @ref{Saving And Startup,Choose What Persists,,emacsvox,}.

@subsection Try it

@strong{5A@.} From the practice buffer@comma{} press @kbd{C-e H /}@comma{} search for @code{punctuation}@comma{}
and choose the punctuation control from completion. The first @kbd{@key{RET}} selects
its menu item without activating it. Read its Help@comma{} then leave without making
a change. Check the policy afterward.

@strong{5B@.} After choosing All and then closing the menu@comma{} should the original policy
return? Describe how to restore it and why @kbd{C-/} in the document is not the
right operation.

See @ref{tutorial-answer-5, , Answers for lesson 5}.

@node Tutorial Voice Experiments
@section Lesson 6: preview and keep a voice experiment

An installed @emph{physical voice} is a voice an engine can produce. A palette's
@emph{named voice}@comma{} such as @code{default} for ordinary reading@comma{} chooses physical voices
and adjustments for a purpose. Browsing a physical voice does not replace
that named entry. We will first try a physical voice without selecting it for
ordinary reading or changing startup.

@enumerate
@item
From the practice buffer@comma{} press @kbd{C-e H v b}. Select an available engine
and press @kbd{v} to browse its voices. Expand a language group with @kbd{@key{RET}}
if needed@comma{} then choose an already usable voice.
@item
Press @kbd{P} to hear its sample and @kbd{S} to stop. An unavailable or inactive
downloaded voice is not suitable for this first exercise. Read its details
rather than enabling@comma{} installing@comma{} or applying packages as a workaround.
@item
Press @kbd{t} on the usable voice to open an experiment. If this resumes
someone else's or your earlier unfinished draft@comma{} leave it with Keep and
choose another voice; do not overwrite or discard unrelated work.
@item
In your new editor draft@comma{} find @emph{Average Pitch} and note its value. Use
@kbd{@key{RET}} to enter @code{6}@comma{} or @code{5} if it is already six. Press @kbd{P} to preview and
@kbd{B} to compare the opening and edited versions with the same sample.
@kbd{S} stops. An automatic sample may also follow an adjustment in the full
editor; navigation stops it.
@item
Press @kbd{u} to undo that draft adjustment@comma{} compare again@comma{} then make the
small adjustment once more. This is editor undo@comma{} not text undo. If the
backend reports the adjustment unsupported@comma{} keep that limitation explicit
and continue with browsing; do not infer that saving will fix support.
@item
Press @kbd{q} in the editor and choose to keep the unfinished draft. Return
through the browser's parent views with @kbd{q} until you reach its menu@comma{} then
close the menu sequence with @kbd{C-q}. Ordinary reading is unchanged.
@item
From the practice buffer@comma{} press @kbd{C-e H p d} and select your retained
experiment. Verify the value is still there. To finish without saving@comma{}
leave its editor with @kbd{q} and choose to discard this practice draft only.
Return through the remaining views and menus.
@end enumerate

A retained draft lasts within the Emacs session. It is not a saved palette and
does not survive restart. Discarding it leaves ordinary reading untouched@comma{}
but discarding a draft would not undo a save that had already completed.
The editor's status distinguishes unsaved work@comma{} successful saving@comma{} and failed
or pending application. Never treat a preview as proof of persistence.

@subsection Optional: save an inactive personal palette

This exercise writes personal palette data. Skip it to keep the tutorial
entirely free of saved speech-configuration changes. It deliberately avoids
startup selection and does not require you to restart a working session.

@enumerate
@item
Open @kbd{C-e H v p} and note the active palette and whether selection follows
the standard baseline or is explicit. Press @kbd{N} to create a palette with an unused name such as @code{tutorial-reading}@comma{} a short
description@comma{} and @code{acss-default} as parent. Creation saves the new palette
but does not activate it. Use another name if that one already exists.
@item
Enter your new palette with @kbd{@key{RET}}@comma{} select @code{default}@comma{} and press @kbd{e} to
edit it. Change @emph{Average Pitch} as above and compare with @kbd{B}. This entry
controls ordinary reading@comma{} not every named voice in the palette.
@item
Choose @emph{Save to collection without selecting}. Wait for its result and
check the destination and saved status. Leave with @kbd{q}. The palette now
holds your entry@comma{} but it is still not selected for ordinary reading.
@item
To try live selection deliberately@comma{} return to the palette manager with
@kbd{o}@comma{} select your new palette@comma{} and press @kbd{a}. Return to the practice
document and read the notebook line. Other named voices retain their
own definitions. Rules or unsupported adjustments can make a contrast
subtle; @ref{Voices In Context,Inspect Or Adjust A Voice In Context,,emacsvox,} explains how to investigate.
@item
Reopen the palette manager. If you originally followed the standard
baseline@comma{} use @kbd{f} to follow it again. Otherwise select the original palette
you recorded and press @kbd{a}. Do not use @kbd{U}@comma{} the separate startup action@comma{}
during this tutorial. The inactive tutorial palette remains saved for
later use.
@end enumerate

If saving or application fails@comma{} keep the reported status and follow
@ref{Recovering Aural Changes,Recovering Unfinished Changes,,emacsvox,}. Do not repeat Create@comma{} delete files@comma{} or restart
Emacs to hide the failure. After an accepted save@comma{} text undo and draft discard
are not rollback commands. @ref{Personal Palette Walkthrough,Make a Personal Reading Palette,,emacsvox,} gives the fuller
lifecycle@comma{} including inheritance and startup selection.

@subsection Try it

@strong{6A@.} Create another small preview-only experiment@comma{} keep its draft@comma{} and
resume it without saving. Then discard that practice draft and establish
that your working document is still there.

@strong{6B@.} Explain the difference between Preview@comma{} Keep draft@comma{} Save to collection@comma{}
Activate@comma{} and Use at Startup. Which of them did the required exercise use?
Which additional operations belong to the optional exercise?

See @ref{tutorial-answer-6, , Answers for lesson 6}.

@node Tutorial Exercise Answers
@section Exercise answers

Use these answers to check your approach@comma{} not as a script you must memorise.
Equivalent commands are useful when their scope and result are the same.
In Info@comma{} @kbd{l} retraces the link from an exercise to its answer.

@subsection Answers: lesson 1

@anchor{tutorial-answer-1}

@strong{1A@.} With the unmodified example@comma{} @kbd{M-< C-n C-n C-n} reaches the Check line.
@kbd{C-e l} reads it without moving@comma{} so two repetitions leave the editing position
unchanged. A different number of inserted lines changes the count; search is
more dependable when you no longer know the exact layout.

@strong{1B@.} Use @kbd{C-e b}@comma{} then @kbd{C-e s}. @kbd{C-e m} identifies the buffer and mode;
@kbd{C-e l} reads the line at point. Stopping speech does not delete text@comma{} move
to another buffer@comma{} or disable later speech commands.

@subsection Answers: lesson 2

@anchor{tutorial-answer-2}

@strong{2A@.} @kbd{M-< C-s green @key{RET}} places point after green. @kbd{M-@key{DEL}} removes that
word; typing @code{blue} supplies the replacement. Read the whole line to catch
spacing or punctuation mistakes. Restore it with a search for blue followed
by the same replacement procedure. Alternatively@comma{} use @code{undo-only} and inspect
after each step@comma{} as in the lesson. Deletion and insertion may need separate
undos. Stop at the original line rather than assuming a fixed undo count.

@strong{2B@.} Before @kbd{@key{RET}}@comma{} the letters extend the search query. No document edit
has occurred. Accept a successful search@comma{} then insert the text at point.

@subsection Answers: lesson 3

@anchor{tutorial-answer-3}

@strong{3A@.} Switch to the notes by name@comma{} use @kbd{M->} to reach their end@comma{} add a newline
and a short sentence@comma{} then switch back by name. Neither switch closes the
other buffer. @kbd{C-e m} and line reading distinguish them.

@strong{3B@.} @kbd{C-h k C-e s} describes the effective binding. With the default maps@comma{}
the command is @code{tts-stop}. @kbd{C-h w tts-stop @key{RET}} reports its keys in the
current context. Help describes the binding; it does not invoke it as the
requested action. If you need the keys available in the practice document@comma{}
return there before using @kbd{C-h w}. A Help buffer has its own local bindings.

@subsection Answers: lesson 4

@anchor{tutorial-answer-4}

@strong{4A@.} From Monday's cell@comma{} enter with @kbd{C-e C-t C-r}@comma{} press Down for Tuesday@comma{}
then @kbd{@key{TAB}} twice to reach Status. @kbd{@key{RET}} reads @code{Planned}. @kbd{q} restores normal
Org editing. While table reading is active@comma{} these operations do not realign
the source@comma{} add rows@comma{} or evaluate formulas.

@strong{4B@.} @kbd{@key{TAB}} on the heading changes visibility. Return to it and cycle open@comma{}
or use @kbd{C-u C-u C-u @key{TAB}} to expose the whole document. The hidden items
never left the buffer. Text undo would affect edits@comma{} not provide the intended
folding operation.

@subsection Answers: lesson 5

@anchor{tutorial-answer-5}

@strong{5A@.} Search accepts a result by selecting its menu control. The separate
activation step is what can open an editor or change a value. Reading Help
and leaving without activation does not change the punctuation policy.

@strong{5B@.} Closing keeps the chosen live adjustment. Restore the recorded policy
in the buffer's punctuation menu: @kbd{r} for Automatic@comma{} or @kbd{p} and the recorded
explicit choice. Text undo does not restore speech settings. Uppercase @kbd{S}
would save a default@comma{} so it is deliberately absent from this exercise.

@subsection Answers: lesson 6

@anchor{tutorial-answer-6}

@strong{6A@.} Keep the practice draft on leaving its editor@comma{} return to the document@comma{}
and use @kbd{C-e H p d} to resume that specific draft. Inspect it@comma{} then leave
with Discard. Use the editor and menu return commands@comma{} or select the practice
buffer by name once the menus are closed. Do not discard another draft to
make the chooser empty.

@strong{6B@.} Preview auditions working values. Keep draft retains unsaved work in
this Emacs session. Save to collection writes the destination without selecting
an inactive palette. Activate chooses the palette for this session. Use at
Startup changes its selection in a saved Presentation Profile; it is not the
same as saving the palette. The required exercise uses Preview and Keep draft@comma{}
then discards. The optional exercise additionally creates and saves a palette
and optionally activates it before restoring the original selection. Neither
exercise needs Use at Startup.

@node Tutorial Next Steps
@section Finish and choose your next task

Finish any practice draft in its editor@comma{} close menus@comma{} restore the punctuation
policy@comma{} and restore the original palette if you deliberately activated another.
Keep saved work separate from unsaved practice when choosing what to remove.

To discard the text exercises@comma{} use @kbd{C-x k} on each tutorial buffer after
checking its name. To keep your own text instead@comma{} use @kbd{C-x C-w} and choose
an unused file in your personal documents directory. Review any overwrite
prompt and cancel rather than replacing an existing file. Saving text does not
save voice settings@comma{} and saving a palette does not save the practice document.

A saved tutorial palette can remain inactive. To remove it later@comma{} use the
palette manager's deletion action only on the unused personal palette you
created@comma{} after finishing its drafts and checking known references. Do not
remove a built-in palette or other saved work as part of tutorial cleanup.

You can now read a location twice without moving@comma{} recover a small edit@comma{} visit
Help without abandoning work@comma{} distinguish a document's structure from its
text@comma{} and tell a temporary setting from a preview or a saved resource.
@ref{Finding Tasks,Find a task,,emacsvox,} is the quickest route to a specific goal. @ref{Basic Usage,Basic Usage,,emacsvox,} and
@ref{Audio Desktop,Audio Desktop,,emacsvox,} provide the everyday reference@comma{} while
@ref{Aural Presentation,Aural Presentation,,emacsvox,} explains rules@comma{} palettes@comma{} voices@comma{} and saved setups in depth.
The application guides then apply these habits to mail@comma{} terminals@comma{} and agents.

@node Basic Usage
@chapter Basic Usage

Once Emacsvox is speaking@comma{} ordinary Emacs commands let you read@comma{} edit and
navigate. Emacsvox announces their results. Short speech commands inspect
text at your editing position@comma{} while tracked reading follows a longer passage.
This chapter moves from those reading choices to selecting and editing text@comma{}
then to everyday speech controls. @ref{Audio Desktop, , Audio Desktop} explains how to keep several
tasks available through buffers and windows.

@menu
* How Emacsvox Helps: Overview.  Understand the main kinds of Emacsvox feedback.
* Using Emacs Buffers: Working.  Understand feedback while editing and completing text.
* Reading And Navigating: Reading.  Reading chunks of information.
* Select@comma{} copy@comma{} and move text: Editing Text With Speech.  Check a selection, copy or move it, and recover a mistaken edit.
* Find and replace text carefully: Searching And Replacing Text.  Search deliberately and choose the scope before replacing occurrences.
* Adjust Speech While You Work: Speech System.  Adjust speech for the way you are working.
* Hear Structure With Voice Lock: Voice-lock.  Hear document and program structure through voice changes.
* Find Out Where You Are: Status Information.  Ask about the current buffer, position, and Emacs state.
@end menu

@section Key Notation And Emacs Words

Start with @ref{Emacs Key Notation, , Read the key instructions} if the notation is unfamiliar. It explains
modifiers@comma{} uppercase letters and successive keypresses before asking you to
use them. @ref{Answering Emacs Prompts, , Answer a prompt and return to your work} covers command names@comma{}
completion and filename questions.

A @emph{buffer} holds text or an application view; @emph{point} is the position at which
movement and editing act. The selection guide below introduces the mark and
region when they are needed. For a separate introduction to Emacs editing@comma{}
@kbd{C-h t} opens its interactive tutorial.

@section Your First Few Minutes

Try these in the speaking session started during installation:

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C-n} to move down one line and @kbd{C-p} to move back up. Emacsvox
speaks the destination line.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e l} to hear that line without moving.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e s} to stop speech. Later commands still speak.
@item
Press @kbd{C-h k}@comma{} then a key@comma{} to learn what it does.
@item
Press @kbd{C-h m} to discover the current modes and commands.
@item
Press @kbd{C-h C-e} for the Emacsvox overview.
@end enumerate

When you want to adjust speech@comma{} @kbd{C-e H s} opens Speech settings. Browse with
arrows and ask for an item's Help with @kbd{?} followed by its key. @kbd{@key{RET}} activates
it; @kbd{q} goes back and @kbd{C-q} closes the menu sequence. Browsing does not change
a value@comma{} but leaving does not reverse adjustments already applied. Detailed
scope and saving instructions follow below.

The keys in this manual are defaults. Use @kbd{C-h k} if one behaves differently.
The generated @ref{Emacsvox Keymaps,Emacsvox Keymaps,,emacsvox-reference,} records default bindings; live Help reflects
installed packages and personal customisations. These menu quit keys apply
while an Emacsvox menu is active@comma{} not to every application buffer.

@node Overview
@section Emacsvox Overview

Emacsvox speaks movement and command results. Integrations present information
in meaningful units such as messages@comma{} search results@comma{} tables@comma{} source code@comma{} and
Web pages. Voices can distinguish headings@comma{} links@comma{} comments@comma{} and other text;
auditory icons and tones can identify events without adding words. Pronunciation
dictionaries correct names and terms globally or for a mode or file.

Notice these distinctions before changing them. Compare structural voices in
@ref{Voice-lock, , Hear Structure With Voice Lock}. @ref{Aural Presentation, , Aural Presentation} explains speech@comma{}
sounds@comma{} rules@comma{} and reusable voice settings in detail.

@node Working
@section Using Emacs Buffers

Much editing feedback is automatic. Echo settings can speak each character or
each completed word. Completion announces candidates@comma{} inserted completion@comma{}
and the selected item. Prompts and error messages are also spoken.

Other information is requested explicitly:

@itemize
@item
Reading commands speak a character@comma{} word@comma{} line@comma{} region or larger unit.
Most inspections preserve point; tracked rest-of-buffer reading advances it.
Names usually begin with @code{emacsvox-speak-} and default keys use
@kbd{C-e}.@footnote{The prefix comes from Emacspeak@comma{} where C-e was mnemonic for
the project name.}
@item
Speech-system commands adjust rate@comma{} punctuation@comma{} echo@comma{} and related controls.
Many use @kbd{C-e d} and names beginning @code{tts-}.@footnote{The d originally referred
to DECtalk; this prefix now contains engine-independent controls.}
@item
Help commands explain the active modes@comma{} bindings@comma{} and integrations.
@end itemize

The settings menus provide discoverable routes to these controls; the direct
keys remain useful for frequent actions. @kbd{C-e S} opens focused settings for
the current integration@comma{} or general buffer settings when no focused menu exists.
@kbd{C-e H .} always opens general buffer settings.

Buffers remain available as you change tasks. @kbd{C-e n} and @kbd{C-e p} cycle among
buffers with the same major mode; repeat with @kbd{n} or @kbd{p}. See
@ref{Desktop Objects, , Audio Desktop} for buffer selection and its other navigation controls.

@node Reading
@section Reading And Navigating

Choose the amount of text you need to hear. Use a line or paragraph for a
local check@comma{} a selected region for exact editing boundaries@comma{} or @kbd{C-e i} for
longer reading with a tracked position. Ordinary movement speaks what you
reach; explicit inspection does not require that movement first.

The default keys are collected here. The sections below explain
which preserve point@comma{} how to stop and start again@comma{} and the limits of large
requests. These distinctions matter before you begin selecting text.

