Many reading commands accept a prefix to limit speech before or after point.
Use @kbd{C-h k} before relying on a variant; exact prefix behaviour belongs to
that command's documentation.

@menu
* Read a short passage without moving point: Reading Without Moving Point.  Inspect words, sentences and paragraphs while keeping your editing position.
* Read from point@comma{} stop@comma{} and start again: Reading From Point.  Use tracked reading and understand where interruption leaves point.
* Hear phonetic names after a pause: Delayed Phonetic Reading.  Inspect characters with delayed phonetic names and cancel stale feedback.
@end menu

@subsection Choose source lines or wrapped screen rows

@cindex visual lines, reading
@cindex wrapped lines, continuation feedback
@findex emacsvox-reading-settings
@findex emacsvox-speak-row-position
@vindex emacsvox-reading-lines-defaults
@vindex emacsvox-reading-lines-modes

A source line ends at a newline. A long source line can occupy several screen
rows@comma{} with the breaks changing when you resize the window. Open @kbd{C-e H s R w}@comma{}
@emph{Line reading and wrapping}@comma{} or @kbd{M-x emacsvox-reading-settings @key{RET}} to choose
which unit ordinary Up and Down move through and speak. @emph{Whole source lines}
reads the entire source line. @emph{Wrapped screen rows} enables Visual Line mode
and reads the displayed row. @emph{Native mode behaviour} preserves the major
mode's own defaults. Tables@comma{} terminals and other integrations can own their
navigation and keep their specialised behaviour.

Ordinary long prose does not ask to change this choice. Unusually large lines
and long lines containing control bytes ask before speaking; declining leaves
the reading mode unchanged. @kbd{C-e l} remains a one-shot whole-source-line
inspection in either reading mode. For an occasional position check@comma{} use @kbd{=}
in the menu or @kbd{M-x emacsvox-speak-row-position @key{RET}}. It reports the source
line number and the screen row's position within it without moving point.
The source buffer must be displayed so Emacs can determine its row boundaries.

Wrapped rows can identify their place in the source line. A first row has more
text below@comma{} a middle row has text above and below@comma{} and a last row has text
above. A complete source line has no continuation cue. The menu's @kbd{1}@comma{} @kbd{2}
and @kbd{3} open independent editors for these three types. Each offers Off@comma{} Tone
or Recorded earcon@comma{} its own sound identity and volume@comma{} and Before@comma{} Alongside
or After speech. Before finishes the sound first; Alongside starts it with
the spoken item; After waits for that item to finish. Navigation or Stop
cancels remaining feedback@comma{} including an After sound.

The initial wrapped-row choices are three distinct recorded cues alongside
speech. @kbd{t} stages three distinct tones instead; each row's tone editor
controls pitch@comma{} duration and shaping while retaining the chosen sound type.
Apply or Save in that editor updates the parent draft. @kbd{P} and @kbd{V} stage a common placement
or volume while keeping the three sound identities. Each row's recording
selector chooses a cue from the active sound pack. Its library action lets
you audition recordings and explicitly assign them to @code{wrapped-line-first}@comma{}
@code{wrapped-line-middle} or @code{wrapped-line-last} in a personal sound pack. The
library's @kbd{W} saves pack-wide assignments separately from reading preferences;
it returns to the reading draft when you close the library.

Spoken continuation is independent of sound. Choose Off@comma{} Brief labels@comma{} or
Explicit descriptions of continuation above and below. Descriptions precede
the row text@comma{} and sound timing applies to that complete spoken item. Frequency
can be every wrapped-row visit or only entry into a different source line@comma{}
including a jump into its middle. Changing reading buffers or windows resets
entry tracking. Explicit screen-row rereading can repeat the feedback.

Choices edit a draft. Browsing does not apply them. Start in temporary buffer
scope@comma{} or use @kbd{g} to choose the current major mode or global defaults. Each
preference follows buffer@comma{} mode@comma{} global and factory values in that order.
Apply with @kbd{a} for this session; @kbd{S} saves and applies mode or global choices
through Customize. Buffer scope is not saved per file. Applying wider settings
preserves explicit buffer overrides. @kbd{i} stages inheritance@comma{} @kbd{r} stages factory
values@comma{} and @kbd{d} discards unapplied edits. Leaving keeps applied changes and
discards unapplied edits. Apply or discard before changing scope.

Use @kbd{p} to preview a complete line followed by first@comma{} middle and last rows@comma{}
or Space in an individual editor to preview one type. These previews use
drafts without applying them; @kbd{c} compares the source's applied settings and
@kbd{x} stops playback. Help at @kbd{h} shows applied values@comma{} inheritance and worker
support. Sounds require structured timeline support@comma{} and recorded earcons
follow the auditory-icon switch. Unsupported sounds@comma{} volume or shaping are
identified without silently changing their placement. Listening to the previews
helps you choose levels that leave the words clear.

@node Reading Without Moving Point
@subsection Read a short passage without moving point

@cindex reading without moving point
@cindex proofreading, spoken text
@findex emacsvox-speak-sentence

Use @kbd{C-e l} to check the line containing the next edit@comma{} @kbd{C-e w} to hear its
word@comma{} and @kbd{C-e c} to inspect its character. Repeating @kbd{C-e w} at the same
position spells the word. These are reading commands@comma{} not movement commands.
For a larger unit@comma{} @kbd{C-e [} reads the paragraph and
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-speak-sentence @key{RET}} reads the sentence around point.

A positive prefix changes line@comma{} sentence or paragraph reading to start at
point. For example@comma{} @kbd{C-u C-e l} reads the rest of the line; @kbd{C-u C-e [} reads
the rest of the paragraph. @kbd{C-u -1 C-e l} reads from the line's beginning to
point. Enter the minus sign and digit as part of the prefix argument@comma{} not as
document text. The default form without a prefix reads the whole unit.

Emacs determines sentences@comma{} paragraphs and pages from the mode and text.
A page for @kbd{C-e ]} is an Emacs text page@comma{} not necessarily one screenful or a
printed page. That command also sets a page mark: do not use it while
preserving a selection for an edit. For more detail@comma{} see @ref{Sentences,Sentences,,emacs,}@comma{}
@ref{Paragraphs,Paragraphs,,emacs,} and @ref{Pages,Pages,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual.

For a bounded passage@comma{} set and inspect a region as described in
@ref{Selecting Text With Speech, , Select and check the text first}@comma{} then use @kbd{C-e r}. A short region read
preserves its endpoints and active selection. Speech follows pronunciation
and presentation settings@comma{} so silence is not proof that the selected source
contains no punctuation or whitespace. Inspect the boundaries before editing.

Whole-buffer reading with @kbd{C-e b}@comma{} or the untracked rest-of-buffer form
@kbd{C-u C-e b}@comma{} is useful for short text. Neither supplies the interruption
position tracking described next. With the current default size limit@comma{}
buffer or region requests of 40@comma{}000 characters or more fall back to
windowful reading rather than reading the entire request. That helper removes
other windows and reads a screenful from point. Use smaller passages to retain
your window arrangement@comma{} or tracked reading when the backend supports it.
The limit is recorded in @code{ems--large-text-size}.

These commands read the buffer's accessible text. In Info@comma{} that is the current
node@comma{} not the entire manual; narrowing can also restrict a document. Folding@comma{}
pronunciation and mode-specific presentation affect what you hear. Use the
application's structural reader for tables@comma{} messages or other special views@comma{}
and @ref{Narrowing,Narrowing,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual for restricted editing views.

@node Reading From Point
@subsection Read from point@comma{} stop@comma{} and start again

@cindex continuous reading
@cindex read all from here
@cindex stopping and restarting reading
@vindex emacsvox-tracked-reading-max-chars

@kbd{C-e i} starts reading at point and continues towards the accessible end of
the buffer. Unlike a line or paragraph inspection@comma{} it advances point as
sentence-oriented chunks complete. This requires a speech server supporting
tracked playback completion. An unsupported server reports that limitation;
it does not silently provide equivalent position tracking.

Try it in an ordinary prose buffer@comma{} with no selection you need to preserve:

@enumerate
@item
Find the passage you want and finish any search or prompt. Use @kbd{C-a} for
the beginning of its line and @kbd{C-e l} to check it. For the beginning of
the accessible buffer@comma{} use @kbd{M-<} instead.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e i} and listen without entering another command. The reading
position advances by chunks@comma{} not by each spoken word.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e s} to stop without moving to a different passage. Then use
@kbd{C-e l} to inspect the retained line before editing or moving.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e i} again to start from the retained position. Part of the
last chunk can be repeated; this is not an exact-word pause/resume control.
@end enumerate

Stopping retains the beginning of the current chunk@comma{} not necessarily the last
word you heard. Another user command interrupts tracked reading before
performing its own action. A movement command therefore also changes the
retained position@comma{} and ordinary typing can insert text there. Use the explicit Stop command when you
want to listen@comma{} stop@comma{} and decide what to do next. An existing active region
would change as point advances@comma{} so use @kbd{C-e r}@comma{} not @kbd{C-e i}@comma{} to check a selection.

Chunks prefer sentence boundaries and are bounded by
@code{emacsvox-tracked-reading-max-chars}@comma{} currently 280 source characters. This is
not a word count or an exact acoustic position. Completion reaches the saved
end of the accessible text; failure or interruption ends the reading session.
You can inspect the text and issue a new reading command without restarting
Emacs. On a backend without tracked support@comma{} read paragraphs or short regions@comma{}
or use the untracked buffer form with the size/layout limitations above.

For movement to another starting point@comma{} see @ref{Moving Point,Moving point,,emacs,} in the
GNU Emacs Manual. The speech commands themselves belong to Emacsvox; their
exact interfaces are in @ref{emacsvox-speak,the core speech reference,,emacsvox-reference,}.

@node Delayed Phonetic Reading
@subsection Hear Phonetic Names After A Pause

@findex emacsvox-delayed-phonetic-mode
@vindex emacsvox-delayed-phonetic-delay

@kbd{M-x emacsvox-delayed-phonetic-mode} toggles delayed phonetics. With it enabled@comma{}
character movement speaks the letter normally@comma{} then a pause gives its phonetic
name@comma{} such as "alpha" for "a". These are the English names also used by
@kbd{C-e c}.

The mode starts enabled with a one-second delay. Customize
@code{emacsvox-delayed-phonetic-delay} to another positive number of seconds and
save the mode preference through Customize. The menu at @kbd{C-e H s t} also
exposes delayed phonetics and its global delay@comma{} using a minimum 0.1 seconds
for numeric entry.

Further input@comma{} including the first key of a prefix@comma{} cancels the pending name.
New speech@comma{} stopping speech@comma{} edited source text@comma{} or leaving its buffer/window
also prevents stale feedback. Each pause produces at most one name. Typing@comma{}
deletion@comma{} punctuation@comma{} and explicit phonetic reading do not trigger the delay.

@node Editing Text With Speech
@section Select@comma{} copy@comma{} and move text

@cindex editing through speech
@cindex cut and paste

Reading a passage is not the same as selecting it for an operation. These
procedures make the affected text and destination explicit before changing
anything. They apply to ordinary editable text@comma{} not terminal input@comma{} a settings
editor or a table reader. Keep using the application's own instructions in
those contexts.

For practice@comma{} use @kbd{C-x b} to create an unused buffer named @code{evox-edit-source}@comma{}
run @kbd{M-x text-mode @key{RET}}@comma{} and insert the following text@comma{} including the blank
lines and a final newline after the last sentence. Use another unused name
if that buffer already contains work. No file or speech-setting save is needed.

@example
Keep the opening paragraph.

Move this paragraph.

Keep the closing paragraph.
@end example

Keep the manual open separately and return to this buffer before trying a
step. @ref{Answering Emacs Prompts, , Answer a prompt and return to your work} explains command entry; the
@ref{Killing,Killing and moving text,,emacs,} chapter in the GNU Emacs Manual supplies the wider
editing reference.

@menu
* Select and check the text first: Selecting Text With Speech.  Set point and mark, hear the region, and check its newline boundary.
* Copy a passage into another buffer: Copying Text Between Buffers.  Preserve the source and check what is inserted at the destination.
* Move a paragraph without losing it: Moving Text Safely.  Cut a checked passage, place it elsewhere, and verify both locations.
* Choose text copied earlier: Choosing Earlier Copied Text.  Retrieve a previous kill-ring entry without confusing insertion and replacement.
* Recover a text edit before saving: Recovering Text Edits.  Undo deliberately in the right buffer and distinguish recovery from saving.
* Exchange text with another application: Copying Through The Clipboard.  Distinguish the Emacs kill ring from operating-system clipboard integration.
@end menu

@node Selecting Text With Speech
@subsection Select and check the text first

@cindex region, selecting and hearing
@cindex mark, checking a selection
@cindex newline, including in a selection
@kindex C-SPC
@kindex C-x C-x

Point is the moving end of a selection; the mark holds its other end. The
@emph{region} is the text between them@comma{} whichever comes first. This procedure uses
Emacs's normal Transient Mark mode@comma{} not the Transient settings-menu package.
Do not change either mode just because their names are similar.

@enumerate
@item
In the practice buffer@comma{} press @kbd{M-<}@comma{} then @kbd{C-n} twice and @kbd{C-a}. Check
with @kbd{C-e l} that you reached @code{Move this paragraph.}.
@item
Press @kbd{C-@key{SPC}}: hold Control and press the space bar. This sets the mark
at the line's start. Release the keys@comma{} then press @kbd{C-n} once. Point is
now at the start of the following blank line.
@item
Press @kbd{C-e r} to hear the region. It contains the sentence and its
terminating newline. Reading this short selection does not move its ends
or deactivate it@comma{} so it remains ready for copying or cutting.
@item
Press @kbd{C-b} once to move point back before that newline. The sentence
is still selected@comma{} but its newline is not. @kbd{C-e c} can identify the
character at this boundary; the character at the region's end is excluded.
Press @kbd{C-f} to include the newline again.
@end enumerate

If the operating system intercepts @kbd{C-@key{SPC}}@comma{} @kbd{M-x set-mark-command @key{RET}} is
an alternative. Use a small selection and ordinary unshifted movement for this
exercise. Shift-selection has different deactivation rules.

@kbd{C-x C-x} exchanges point and mark without changing the selected text.
Emacsvox speaks the line at the new point with point indication. You can
inspect that boundary@comma{} move it if necessary@comma{} and exchange again to return to
the other end. Starting a new selection with @kbd{C-@key{SPC}} replaces the boundary;
it is not the command for inspecting the old one.

In the ordinary text buffer@comma{} @kbd{C-g} deactivates the selection without deleting
text. It does not erase the saved mark. Some region commands can still use an
inactive mark@comma{} so always establish a fresh@comma{} checked selection before cutting.
Likewise@comma{} typing over selected text depends on Delete Selection mode; use the
explicit operations below rather than assuming typing will replace it.

More detail: @ref{Mark,The mark and the region,,emacs,}@comma{} @ref{Setting Mark,Setting the mark,,emacs,}@comma{}
@ref{Using Region,Operating on the region,,emacs,} and @ref{Shift Selection,Shift selection,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual.

@node Copying Text Between Buffers
@subsection Copy a passage into another buffer

@cindex copying text between documents
@kindex M-w
@kindex C-y

Emacs calls its remembered copied or cut text the @emph{kill ring}. Copy with
@kbd{M-w} and insert with @kbd{C-y}@comma{} called @emph{yank} in Emacs. The names do not imply
that copying deletes anything.

@enumerate
@item
Select the middle sentence and its newline using the previous procedure.
Hear it with @kbd{C-e r}@comma{} then press @kbd{M-w}. Emacsvox reports the copy; the
source text stays in place and the mark is normally deactivated.
@item
Press @kbd{C-x b}@comma{} choose an unused name such as @code{evox-edit-notes}@comma{} and run
@kbd{M-x text-mode @key{RET}}. Check the buffer with @kbd{C-e m}. Use an empty buffer
for this first copy@comma{} rather than inserting into an existing document.
@item
Press @kbd{C-y}. Emacsvox speaks the inserted text. Point is after it and
the mark is at its beginning@comma{} normally inactive. Immediately press @kbd{C-e r}
to hear that range again; do not set a new mark first.
@item
Return to @code{evox-edit-source} with @kbd{C-x b} and read the middle paragraph.
It is still there. The destination contains an independent text copy.
@end enumerate

For a real document@comma{} check the insertion point and deactivate any unrelated
selection before pasting. A copied newline moves subsequent text to another
line; a copied blank line can preserve paragraph separation. Inspect the
surrounding text as well as the pasted passage before saving.

During the transfer@comma{} @kbd{C-e m} and @kbd{C-e l} are useful checks. Avoid @kbd{C-e f}
for filename inspection and @kbd{C-e a} for message repetition at this point:
those commands also put their result in the kill ring and can change the
next paste. @kbd{M-x emacsvox-speak-current-kill @key{RET}} inspects the current entry
without inserting it. Another application's clipboard copy can also change
what @kbd{C-y} inserts.

If the text goes to the wrong place@comma{} stop and use
@ref{Recovering Text Edits, , Recover a text edit before saving} rather than pasting more copies.
For the general commands@comma{} see @ref{Yanking,Yanking,,emacs,} and @ref{Kill Ring,The kill ring,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs
Manual. @ref{Reference And Draft Windows, , Keep a reference and a draft in separate windows} offers another way to
manage the two working contexts.

@node Moving Text Safely
@subsection Move a paragraph without losing it

@cindex moving paragraphs
@cindex cutting text
@kindex C-w

Cutting with @kbd{C-w} removes the region and stores it for yanking. Practise in
the unchanged three-paragraph source above; copying in the previous task did
not alter it. Do not overwrite an existing buffer to recreate the example.

@enumerate
@item
Select the middle sentence from its beginning. This time move down twice
after setting the mark@comma{} stopping at the beginning of @code{Keep the closing
   paragraph.}. The region contains the middle paragraph and both following
newlines@comma{} but none of the closing paragraph. Check with @kbd{C-e r}.
@item
Press @kbd{C-w}. Emacsvox announces the cut. The source now contains the
opening and closing paragraphs@comma{} and point is where the removed passage
began. Inspect the remaining text without making another cut or copy.
@item
Press @kbd{M->} for the buffer end. With the example's final newline present@comma{}
press @kbd{@key{RET}} once to add a blank line after the closing paragraph. Press
@kbd{C-y} to insert the cut paragraph there.
@item
Read from the beginning using short passages. The order should be opening@comma{}
closing@comma{} then the moved paragraph@comma{} separated by blank lines. Check that
the middle paragraph appears only once.
@end enumerate

In other text@comma{} decide deliberately whether to move a following newline or
blank line. Cutting only the visible words can leave an empty paragraph at
the source and join words at the destination. For a transfer between files@comma{}
verify both buffers before saving either: this is not one atomic file move.

Consecutive kills can combine into one kill-ring entry. Avoid using another
kill command to tidy the destination before yanking the passage; it can alter
what you insert. @kbd{C-k}@comma{} for example@comma{} kills to the line end and can remove the
newline when used there. See @ref{Killing by Lines,Killing by lines,,emacs,} and @ref{Appending Kills,Appending kills,,emacs,} in the
GNU Emacs Manual before using that faster editing pattern.

@node Choosing Earlier Copied Text
@subsection Choose text copied earlier

@cindex paste an earlier copy
@cindex kill ring, earlier entries
@kindex M-y

The kill ring keeps more than the latest passage. At the intended destination@comma{}
@kbd{M-y} after a non-yank command opens a chooser for earlier entries. Use
completion or prompt history to inspect candidates@comma{} then @kbd{@key{RET}} to insert the
chosen text. @kbd{C-g} cancels before insertion. These entries may contain private
text; use a harmless example when practising or sharing your screen or audio.

Immediately after @kbd{C-y}@comma{} the same @kbd{M-y} instead replaces that insertion with
an earlier entry. Further consecutive @kbd{M-y} commands cycle through earlier
entries@comma{} and Emacsvox speaks each inserted candidate. Once you issue a separate
reading or movement command@comma{} do not assume @kbd{M-y} will still replace the last
paste: outside the yank sequence it opens the chooser for another insertion.

To verify a candidate while cycling@comma{} listen to the automatic yank feedback.
When you decide to keep it@comma{} inspect its surroundings normally. If you have
already broken the sequence and want a different passage@comma{} undo the unwanted
insertion@comma{} check the destination@comma{} and choose again. Repeated @kbd{C-y} inserts
more copies; it is not an accept or preview command.

More detail: @ref{Earlier Kills,Yanking earlier kills,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual. Kill-ring
history is useful recovery information@comma{} not a permanent backup of your work.

If you enable Repeatable yank through @ref{managed-muggles, , managed Muggles}@comma{} @kbd{C-y} and @kbd{M-y}
also enter its temporary repeat menu. Leave it with @kbd{C-g} before ordinary
editing. Its additional cycling and chooser keys are described in that guide;
Disable restores the standard bindings when the manager still owns them.

@node Recovering Text Edits
@subsection Recover a text edit before saving

@cindex undo with speech
@cindex pasted into the wrong place
@findex undo-only

Undo belongs to the selected buffer. Finish prompts and leave special menus@comma{}
check the buffer with @kbd{C-e m}@comma{} and press @kbd{C-g} in ordinary text to deactivate
any selection before undoing across the buffer. An active region can limit
undo to that region.

Use @kbd{M-x undo-only @key{RET}} to undo one recorded change@comma{} then inspect with
@kbd{C-e l} or a freshly selected short region. Deactivate that inspection region
before the next undo. Repeat only as far as necessary. Insertion and deletion
can occupy separate undo records; a multi-step move can therefore need more
than one correction.

@kbd{C-/} is the familiar undo key and works for an initial undo. However@comma{} an
intervening reading or movement command breaks its undo sequence@comma{} and a later
ordinary undo can redo what you just undid. @code{undo-only} avoids that reversal
when you pause to inspect through speech. @kbd{M-x undo-redo @key{RET}} deliberately
reapplies undone changes when the buffer's undo history permits it.

For an accidental transfer between buffers@comma{} undo the insertion at the
destination and the cut at the source separately@comma{} inspecting each. Copying
needs no source undo because it left the source unchanged. Neither operation
writes a file until you save. Correcting a saved edit changes the buffer again;
save the verified correction explicitly if the file on disk must change too.
Do not use Revert or close an unsaved buffer as a substitute for inspecting it.

The GNU Emacs Manual's @ref{Undo,Undo,,emacs,} section explains history@comma{} selective undo and
limits. For file destinations and save failures@comma{} see
@ref{Saving A Working Copy, , Save a separate working copy}. Text undo does not reverse a settings-menu
save@comma{} a filesystem operation or an action already sent to a terminal.

@node Copying Through The Clipboard
@subsection Exchange text with another application

@cindex clipboard, exchanging text
@cindex WSL, clipboard boundaries

The kill ring is part of Emacs. The operating-system clipboard can connect it
to other applications when the display session and configuration support that
connection. Under normal graphical clipboard integration@comma{} @kbd{M-w} copies to both@comma{}
and @kbd{C-y} can insert text copied more recently outside Emacs. That is why
checking the pasted result matters even after checking the selection.

Test the boundary with a short@comma{} non-confidential sentence in each direction:
copy from Emacs and paste into a disposable external document@comma{} then copy a
different sentence externally and paste into an empty Emacs buffer. Verify the
actual text rather than assuming that success in one direction proves both.
Native Windows Emacs@comma{} WSLg@comma{} terminal Emacs and remote sessions need not share
identical clipboard behaviour. This guide does not install a bridge or change
your clipboard configuration.

If copying between Emacs buffers works but the external test fails@comma{} investigate
that display/clipboard boundary rather than changing speech settings. Do not
paste test commands into a live terminal. For the general integration and its
options@comma{} see @ref{Clipboard,Using the clipboard,,emacs,} and @ref{Primary Selection,Cut and paste with other window applications,,emacs,}
in the GNU Emacs Manual; a primary selection is not the same as the clipboard.

@node Searching And Replacing Text
@section Find and replace text carefully

@cindex searching, repeated matches
@cindex isearch, finishing before editing

@kbd{C-s} searches forward and @kbd{C-r} searches backwards in the current buffer.
Type the query@comma{} listen to the match feedback@comma{} and repeat the search key to
reach another occurrence. Reverse direction with the other search key when
you overshoot. Searching the current text does not send a web or mail query.

When you find the occurrence you want@comma{} press @kbd{@key{RET}} to end the search before
typing document text. Forward search leaves point after the match; backward
search leaves it before the match. Check with @kbd{C-e l} before editing. With a
failing search@comma{} @kbd{C-g} first backs out of the failed part; another @kbd{C-g} may be
needed to abandon the search. Do not begin an edit while its prompt is active.

For details of repetition@comma{} search history@comma{} case and failure recovery@comma{} use
@ref{Incremental Search,Incremental search,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual. Its rules describe the editor;
Emacsvox adds spoken match context and search cues. The next procedure changes
text@comma{} so it begins by establishing a narrower scope.

@menu
* Review replacements one at a time: Reviewing Text Replacements.  Limit query replacement to a checked region and verify the changed text.
@end menu

@node Reviewing Text Replacements
@subsection Review replacements one at a time

@cindex replacement, controlling scope
@findex query-replace
@kindex M-%

Use a disposable Text-mode buffer for this example. Insert these three lines:

@example
The draft is ready.
Keep this draft for comparison.
The draft outside the selection must stay unchanged.
@end example

@enumerate
@item
From the beginning@comma{} set the mark with @kbd{C-@key{SPC}} and move down two lines.
Point is at the third line's beginning. @kbd{C-e r} should read only the first
two lines. Keep this region active for the next command.
@item
Press @kbd{M-%} to start query replacement. At the first prompt enter @code{draft}
and press @kbd{@key{RET}}; at the second enter @code{version} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}. These are
answers to successive prompts@comma{} not text inserted into the document.
@item
Emacsvox reads the line containing the proposed match. Press @kbd{y} to replace
the first occurrence. At the second@comma{} press @kbd{n} to leave it unchanged.
@item
When the command finishes@comma{} read all three lines. Only the first should say
@code{version}. The second was skipped and the third was outside the selection.
@end enumerate

@kbd{M-x query-replace @key{RET}} is an alternative when Meta-percent is difficult to
enter. The active region limits the operation under normal Transient Mark
mode; without it@comma{} ordinary forward query replacement works from point towards
the accessible buffer end. Do not deactivate or replace the checked mark
between selection and starting the command. For a full-document operation@comma{}
first abandon unrelated selection and deliberately choose the starting point.

At a replacement question@comma{} @kbd{q} or @kbd{@key{RET}} stops without changing further
matches; earlier replacements remain. Comma@comma{} @kbd{@comma{}}@comma{} replaces the current match
and lets you review it before advancing. @kbd{!} replaces the remaining matches
without more questions and is not needed for this guided task. Other editing
keys can leave the replacement loop and then act on the document. Rely on the
automatic match reading@comma{} or stop before issuing ordinary speech/editing keys.

Check the result before saving. Use @ref{Recovering Text Edits, , Recover a text edit before saving} if the
scope or replacement was wrong. @ref{Replace,Replacement commands,,emacs,} and @ref{Query Replace,Query replace,,emacs,} in the
GNU Emacs Manual cover exact matching@comma{} case behaviour and the full set of
answers. Regular-expression replacement is a different@comma{} more specialised task.

@node Speech System
@section Adjust Speech While You Work

@cindex TTS
@cindex speech system
@cindex speech settings

Use @kbd{C-e H s} for rate@comma{} punctuation@comma{} indentation@comma{} capitalisation@comma{} and typing
feedback. It starts with the captured buffer's settings; @kbd{g} switches scope@comma{}
and @kbd{C-u C-e H} starts the menu with global defaults. Individual controls
retain their documented exceptions@comma{} such as global volume or phonetic delay.

Changing a global default is not necessarily saving it for restart. A menu's
Save action records only its documented values@comma{} and local or mode settings can
still override defaults. @ref{Saving And Startup,Choose What Persists,,emacsvox,} explains those distinctions.
The direct keys below are alternatives to menu navigation; use @kbd{C-h k} to
check a command's global prefix form before using it.

@menu
* Choose Typing Echo: Controlling Echo.  Choose character, word, and line feedback while typing.
* Adjust Rate And Spoken Detail: Speech Output Control.  Adjust rate and how textual detail is presented.
* Stop Or Restart Speech: Miscellaneous.  Stop, silence, restart, or identify the speech server.
@end menu

@node Controlling Echo
@subsection Choose Typing Echo

@cindex line echo
@cindex character echo
@cindex word echo

Character echo reads each typed character@comma{} word echo each completed word@comma{} and
optional line echo a completed line. They can be toggled independently. Use
@kbd{C-e H s t} for explained controls@comma{} or the keys below. These direct commands
affect the current buffer; a preceding @kbd{C-u} changes the corresponding default
and applies it to this buffer.

@table @kbd
@item C-e d k
@findex emacsvox-toggle-character-echo

@kindex C-e d k

@code{emacsvox-toggle-character-echo}

Toggle character echo.
@item C-e d w
@findex emacsvox-toggle-word-echo

@kindex C-e d w

@code{emacsvox-toggle-word-echo}

Toggle word echo.
@item C-e d l
@findex emacsvox-toggle-line-echo

@kindex C-e d l

@code{emacsvox-toggle-line-echo}

Toggle line echo.
@end table

@node Speech Output Control
@subsection Adjust Rate And Spoken Detail

Start with rate and punctuation@comma{} then adjust other feedback when the distinction
helps the task. Familiar prose may suit faster speech; exact code inspection
may need full punctuation. Direct commands affect the current buffer unless
their Help specifies a global prefix form.

@table @kbd
@item C-e d r
Enter a rate with @code{tts-set-rate}. The menu alternative is @emph{Rate} in @kbd{C-e H s}.
@item C-e d DIGIT
Choose one of ten predefined rates with @kbd{0} through @kbd{9}.
@item C-e d p
Choose @code{some}@comma{} @code{all}@comma{} or @code{none} with @code{tts-set-punctuations}. A supporting
host also offers named profiles@comma{} described below.
@item C-e d s
Toggle splitting of mixed-capital identifiers into words.
@item C-e d c / C-e d C
Toggle capitalisation indication with the lowercase key. The uppercase
key chooses and previews spoken@comma{} tone@comma{} combined@comma{} personal-rule@comma{} or no
indication. @kbd{C-e H s c} opens the Capitalization menu.
@item C-e d i / C-e d I
Toggle indentation feedback with the lowercase key@comma{} or open its menu with
the uppercase key. @kbd{C-e H s i} reaches the same menu through settings.
@item C-e d T
Choose and preview @emph{Alongside speech} (default) or @emph{Before speech} for
indentation tones. The current buffer changes; a prefix also updates the
default. Save @code{emacsvox-indentation-tone-timing} through Customize for
restart. Timing does not choose a spoken/tonal presentation or enable
indentation. Backends without overlay support play tones before speech
for either setting.
@end table

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Use The Indentation Menu}Use The Indentation Menu


Open @kbd{C-e H s i} or @kbd{C-e d I}. Select spoken columns with @kbd{s}@comma{} pitch tones
with @kbd{p}@comma{} or duration tones with @kbd{d}; uppercase @kbd{P~/~D} combines speech and
tones. @kbd{b} places tones before speech and @kbd{a} alongside. Timing choices are
unavailable for presentations without built-in tones.

@kbd{g} switches @emph{Current buffer} and @emph{Global defaults}. Global defaults leave
existing local choices intact. A prefix starts the menu in global scope;
@kbd{e} toggles indentation feedback in the displayed scope.

Up/down selects a control and left/right changes sections. In @emph{Tone levels}@comma{}
@kbd{l} adjusts the pitch-tone volume in the displayed scope; @kbd{v} adjusts the
shared volume of all main-stream Omnivox tones@comma{} even in buffer scope.
Shift-left/right changes either selected volume by five percentage points@comma{}
bounded to 0–100; @kbd{@key{RET}} enters an exact value. @kbd{-~/~+} adjust the shared
volume directly. These menu controls interrupt obsolete main speech so
repeated adjustments announce the latest value. Pitch-tone shaping and its
shared tone editor are described under @ref{Adjust indentation feedback, , Adjust indentation feedback}.

Changes apply immediately. @kbd{@key{SPC}} previews four-column indentation even when
feedback is off. @kbd{q} leaves changes in effect. In global scope@comma{} @kbd{S} saves
indentation defaults and tone volume; closing without saving keeps defaults
changed only for this session. @kbd{M-x emacsvox-set-indentation-presentation}
remains a direct alternative.

The following punctuation sections are reference material for changing individual
pronunciations. They are not prerequisites for ordinary reading. Reusable
palettes/profiles@comma{} engine-specific tuning@comma{} and the wider output model belong to
@ref{Aural Presentation, , Aural Presentation}. Specialist character-rate and chunking controls are
also available through live Help and the generated reference.

@item
@anchor{Edit Punctuation Pronunciations}Edit Punctuation Pronunciations


A punctuation level decides how much punctuation is spoken; the pronunciation
editor changes how particular characters are read. Open it with @kbd{C-e H s p e}
or @kbd{M-x omnivox-punctuation}.

This interface needs the bundled local launcher and the host's punctuation-editor
capability. Older hosts report that an update is required; remote and
native-direct configuration editing are not provided here. It displays saved
host settings and an unsaved draft separately. Running speech can still use
an earlier configuration.

Rows identify Unicode characters@comma{} pronunciations@comma{} and default/override origin.
The initial level is @emph{some}.

@table @kbd
@item l
Choose none@comma{} some@comma{} or all@comma{} each with independent overrides; supporting hosts also offer named profiles.
@item RET
Speak a name@comma{} preserve the character@comma{} or restore its shipped default.
@item a
Add a character not already listed.
@item d
Restore the selected character's default in this level.
@item s
Save drafts on the host without restarting speech or altering unrelated configuration.
@item r
Restart this session's main and notification workers with saved settings after confirmation; save or discard drafts first.
@item g
Reread the saved file@comma{} asking before discarding edits.
@item q
Return while retaining the draft; reopening the editor resumes it. @kbd{C-e H p d} also resumes retained drafts.
@item ?
Read Help@comma{} including the selected host configuration path.
@end table

@emph{Preserve character} leaves pronunciation and pauses to the engine; it does
not promise silence. Org normally uses @emph{some}. To name apostrophes@comma{} set both
@emph{APOSTROPHE} U+0027 and @emph{RIGHT SINGLE QUOTATION MARK} U+2019 to speak
“apostrophe” in @emph{some}. That includes contractions. Omnivox 1.15.0 names straight@comma{}
curly@comma{} and modifier apostrophes at @emph{all} without a configuration file.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Save And Activate Changes}Save And Activate Changes


Saving rejects a file or target changed since review and retains the draft for
reconciliation. Other configuration is preserved. A named-profile-capable host
writes Omnivox configuration version 4@comma{} unreadable by 1.16.0 and earlier. The
older built-in-only editor writes version 3@comma{} requiring 1.15.0 or newer. Do not
save a shared file with a format another selected executable cannot read.
Capability and configuration-format checks matter even when the main installation
uses a newer supported release.

The editor's @kbd{s} saves pronunciation data; its @kbd{r} activates through worker
restart. By contrast@comma{} @kbd{S} in the parent Punctuation menu saves the selected
level/profile through Customize@comma{} not the host editor's draft.

@item
@anchor{Work With Named Profiles}Work With Named Profiles


With host support@comma{} @kbd{N} creates a profile inheriting @code{none}@comma{} @code{some}@comma{} or @code{all}.
Use a lowercase ID such as @code{prose} or @code{proofreading}. @kbd{l} switches levels and
profiles; @kbd{X} removes the selected profile from the draft. Profiles inherit
base-level edits and override individual characters. @kbd{s} saves all drafts
without changing speech; @kbd{r} loads the same definitions into both workers.

@code{tts-set-punctuations} offers profiles whose definitions agree in current workers.
Explicit selection applies to the buffer. A preference in
@code{tts-punctuation-mode-policy-alist} can use @code{(text-mode profile "prose" some)}@comma{}
retaining @code{some} as fallback for older hosts@comma{} missing profiles@comma{} or inconsistent
worker definitions. Status identifies fallback use. @code{tts-reset-punctuation-mode}
restores mode policy. Explicitly choosing a built-in level overrides a profile.
Character inspection is independent@comma{} and there is no built-in @code{most} level.
@end enumerate
@end enumerate

@node Miscellaneous
@subsection Stop Or Restart Speech

Stopping ends current output without disabling future feedback. Quiet mode
lets you work temporarily without speech. Restart after correcting a backend
problem or changing the selected synthesiser@comma{} not as an assumed cure for every
missing integration.

@table @kbd
@item C-e s
Stop current main and notification speech with @code{tts-stop}.
@item C-e d q
Toggle current-buffer quiet mode; @kbd{C-u} toggles it globally.
@item C-e C-s
Stop and restart the selected speech server.
@item C-e d V
Speak adapter and version information.
@end table

Server selection@comma{} repeated-pattern cleanup@comma{} text splitting@comma{} language selection@comma{}
and direct engine controls have specialist Help and reference entries. For
missing speech@comma{} follow @ref{Recovering From No Speech,Recovering From No Speech,,emacsvox,}.

@node Voice-lock
@section Hear Structure With Voice Lock

Voice Lock starts automatically in supported buffers. It maps faces and
structural text properties to personalities@comma{} making comments@comma{} strings@comma{}
headings@comma{} links@comma{} and other text sound distinct without extra words.

Compare two styled elements in code@comma{} Org@comma{} Info@comma{} or EWW@. Toggle @kbd{C-e d v} locally@comma{}
read the same elements@comma{} then toggle again to restore. @kbd{C-e d C-v} changes
Voice Lock globally. Disabling it removes that layer of voices@comma{} not ordinary
speech@comma{} semantic feedback@comma{} tones@comma{} or icons.

The result depends on engine and presentation settings. Voice Lock@comma{} explicit
face rules@comma{} and semantic rules are separate; see
@ref{Voice Lock, , Voice Lock} in Aural Presentation. Reusable voice@comma{} sound@comma{} and profile choices
belong to that chapter rather than to a Voice Lock toggle.

