@node Using Online Help
@chapter Find Help While You Work

@cindex Online Help

Emacs can explain the key you are about to use@comma{} the modes active in a buffer@comma{}
and the commands and settings available in your session. This live help
reflects installed packages and personal customisations. Use it to understand
an unfamiliar command or resolve a difference between a manual and your
running Emacs.

Most Emacs help commands begin with @kbd{C-h}. Press @kbd{C-h C-h} for
@code{help-for-help} when you need to discover what can follow that prefix.
Settings menus also have their own contextual Help@comma{} described below.

@section Ask About A Key@comma{} Command@comma{} Or Setting

Begin in the buffer whose commands or settings you want to inspect. Help
can select a different buffer@comma{} so return to your working buffer before asking
about another local binding or value. Choose according to what you know:

@itemize
@item
@kbd{C-h k} followed by a key runs @code{describe-key} and explains the command
currently bound to that key.
@item
@kbd{C-h f} runs @code{describe-function}. Enter a command or function name to
read its documentation.
@item
@kbd{C-h v} runs @code{describe-variable}. Enter an option name to inspect its
current value and documentation.
@item
@kbd{C-h w} runs @code{where-is} and reports the keys that invoke a command.
@end itemize

For example@comma{} @kbd{C-h k C-e l} explains the current-line speaking key without
running that command. If you know its command name but not its key@comma{} use
@kbd{C-h w} and enter the name instead.

@section Discover What The Current Buffer Can Do

Start with @kbd{C-h m} after opening Dired@comma{} EWW@comma{} mail@comma{} a terminal@comma{} or another
application. This runs @code{describe-mode} and explains the major mode@comma{} active
minor modes@comma{} and their local commands. Use @kbd{C-h b} (@code{describe-bindings})
when you need the effective key bindings after package maps and personal
customisations have been applied.

For Emacsvox settings that apply to the current task@comma{} press @kbd{C-e S}. It opens
the integration's focused menu when one is available@comma{} otherwise general buffer
settings. @kbd{C-e H .} always opens general buffer settings. The menu captures
the buffer on entry@comma{} so its controls continue to refer to that task while you
navigate its menus and editors.

@subsection Find a setting and read its menu Help

Press @kbd{C-e H /} to find a setting by part of its name. The search is
case-insensitive and includes supported aliases. For example@comma{} @code{cap} finds
capitalisation controls. Space inserts a literal space@comma{} so you can type a
multiword query such as @code{all caps beep}; @kbd{@key{TAB}} completes it. Completion lets
you choose a result; accepting it
opens the appropriate menu and selects the control without changing its
value. Press @kbd{@key{RET}} again to activate or edit it. Cancelling completion
leaves the originating menu open.

The main menu groups its entries in this order: @emph{Everyday settings}@comma{}
@emph{This buffer and item}@comma{} @emph{Rules and saved work}@comma{} @emph{Find and get help}@comma{} and
@emph{Finish}. Use left/right to reach a group@comma{} then up/down to select an item.
Changing sections selects the first available item. Plain arrows navigate;
Shift-left/right adjusts a selected numeric control where offered.
Press @kbd{C-e H n} to choose navigation and announcement preferences; the
same controls remain available through @kbd{C-e H s n}.
@kbd{@key{RET}}@comma{} @kbd{M-@key{RET}}@comma{} or @kbd{C-j} activates the selected item; its displayed shortcut
also works directly.

To hear the selected item again@comma{} press @kbd{C-c l}. This is especially useful
after a section-only announcement: the first item is selected even though
only the heading was spoken. @kbd{M-f~/~M-b} reviews its words and @kbd{C-f~/~C-b}
its characters@comma{} leaving the selection and Enter's target unchanged. These
menu keys differ from ordinary buffer reading; see
@ref{settings-menu-review, , Review an item without changing the selection} for endpoints@comma{} repeats and
menu-specific overrides.

Press @kbd{?} followed by an item's key to hear its effect and scope@comma{} or @kbd{? ?}
for the menu overview and available review keys. The overview is a read-only
snapshot with ordinary text navigation. Its displayed actions run after you
return to the menu. @kbd{q} in Help returns with the selection retained.

Within the menu itself@comma{} @kbd{q} or @kbd{C-g} goes back one level@comma{} while @kbd{C-q} closes
the sequence. Returning from an Emacsvox submenu selects the entry that opened
it@comma{} including when you used its shortcut. The return announcement identifies
that item; @kbd{@key{RET}} opens it again. These navigation commands do not undo changes
already applied.
Choice lists and minibuffer prompts have different roles: arrows browse a
choice list without applying it@comma{} while a prompt uses your normal text editing
and completion. See @ref{settings-menu-navigation, , Navigate menus and read Help} for the complete interaction.
A linked editor keeps its own preview@comma{} saving@comma{} and discard rules. See
@ref{Saving And Startup,Choose What Persists,,emacsvox,} before treating a saved default@comma{} a live adjustment@comma{}
or a retained draft as the same thing.

Use @kbd{C-e H a} to find an action such as browsing voices@comma{} inspecting
diagnostics@comma{} or reading the manual. It uses the same partial-name matching and
two-Enter selection as settings search@comma{} including literal spaces and @kbd{@key{TAB}}
completion. Alternative names lead to one displayed
result for each action: for example@comma{} @code{Feedback for this item} finds
@emph{Explain this item}@comma{} and @code{Recent aural feedback} finds @emph{Recent feedback}.
Editors opened from these menus retain their own Help@comma{}
preview@comma{} save@comma{} and return commands.

@section Read And Leave A Help Window

Ordinary Emacs Help writes its explanation in @code{*Help*}. Some commands@comma{} such
as @kbd{C-h m}@comma{} can display that buffer without selecting it. Spoken descriptions
such as key@comma{} function and variable Help use @code{emacsvox-speak-help}@comma{} also bound
to @kbd{C-e h}. This helper selects Help@comma{} removes other windows in the frame@comma{}
and reads a windowful starting at Help's current point. It does not kill the
buffers those windows displayed@comma{} and it does not necessarily read all of Help.

After requesting Help@comma{} press @kbd{C-e m} to check the selected buffer. If it is
@code{*Help*}@comma{} read there directly. Otherwise@comma{} use @kbd{C-x b} and choose @code{*Help*}
by name@comma{} or select its existing window deliberately. Do not automatically
press @kbd{C-x o}: Help may already be selected@comma{} or there may now be only one
window. @kbd{C-e C-w} describes the arrangement when needed.

Once in Help@comma{} use line or paragraph reading for a local check. @kbd{C-e h} repeats
a windowful from the current Help position; @kbd{M-<} first returns to its beginning.
For longer reading and its effect on point@comma{} see
@ref{Reading,Reading and navigating,,emacsvox,}. Press @kbd{q} while in Help to leave its view.
Then check the selected buffer and use @kbd{C-x b} to return to your document
before editing or requesting buffer-specific Help again. To restore a reference
window@comma{} follow @ref{Reference And Draft Windows,Keep a reference and a draft in separate windows,,emacsvox,}.

Help reached through an Emacsvox settings menu has the menu-return behaviour
explained above. Use its @kbd{q} to return to the originating menu item@comma{} not the
ordinary document-return procedure. Dedicated editors likewise keep their own
Help and return commands.

@section Learn Interactively And Read Manuals

@kbd{C-h t} opens the interactive Emacs tutorial. Emacsvox speaks its text and
your navigation as you work through it. For a separate key-learning exercise@comma{}
@kbd{C-h C-l} runs @code{emacsvox-learn-emacs}: it speaks the function of each key
you press. Leave that loop with @kbd{C-g}.

Press @kbd{C-e H i} to read the offline Emacsvox manual from the settings menu.
It opens at the top in Info@comma{} plays the opening cue@comma{} and reads the first screen
or the whole node according to your Info speech setting. Info's navigation and
index lookup remain available.
Press @kbd{q} to return to the menu with @emph{Read the manual} selected and the same
source buffer and settings scope. @kbd{C-e C-i} opens the manual directly.

@kbd{C-h i} opens Info's general manual reader. Press @kbd{d} for the Info directory@comma{}
then @kbd{m} and enter @code{Emacsvox} to choose this manual.
@ref{Using This Manual, , Using this manual} provides an exercise in index lookup@comma{} following a link@comma{}
and returning to your work. @ref{Finding Tasks, , Find a task} starts from an everyday goal@comma{} and
@ref{Emacsvox Terminology, , Terms used in this manual} explains the names used in the procedures. The
@ref{Top,Info manual,,info,} covers the reader's complete navigation and search commands.

In an Aural interface@comma{} @kbd{C-c C-i} opens the relevant manual topic and @kbd{q} in
Info returns to that interface. In the interface's own Help@comma{} @kbd{i} opens the
same topic.

@section Emacsvox-Specific Help

Use @kbd{C-h C-e} (@code{emacsvox-describe-emacsvox}) for the Emacsvox overview.
@kbd{C-e N} (@code{emacsvox-view-emacsvox-news}) opens project news@comma{} and @kbd{C-e T}
(@code{emacsvox-view-emacsvox-tips}) opens productivity tips.

For unexpected feedback@comma{} leave point on the item and press @kbd{C-e E} to
explain its voices@comma{} sounds@comma{} and other presentation actions. @kbd{C-e H x} opens
Feedback Details through the settings menu. Its report distinguishes retained
output from a simulation; see @ref{Explaining Presentation,Understand Why Something Sounded That Way,,emacsvox,}.

@kbd{C-e H D} opens diagnostics. Use @kbd{d} there for Aural Doctor@comma{} @kbd{s} for spatial
capabilities@comma{} or @kbd{m} and @kbd{n} for retained main and notification diagnostics.
These inspection routes do not imply that speech has been repaired. Run a
repair or restart only when you deliberately choose that action. If there is
no speech at all@comma{} begin with
@ref{Recovering From No Speech,Recovering From No Speech,,emacsvox,} rather than presentation settings.

The generated @ref{Emacsvox Keymaps,Emacsvox Keymaps,,emacsvox-reference,} in the @emph{Emacsvox Reference Manual} records
default bindings. Return to @kbd{C-h k}@comma{} @kbd{C-h m}@comma{} and @kbd{C-h b} when a package or
personal configuration changes those defaults.

@node Audio Desktop
@chapter Work With The Audio Desktop

@cindex Audio Desktop

Emacs uses buffers for documents@comma{} application views@comma{} search results@comma{} and other
work. Each buffer has modes that understand its content. Emacsvox uses that
information to present text and state changes through speech@comma{} voices@comma{} sounds@comma{}
and structured navigation. Together@comma{} these form the @emph{audio desktop}: the same
Emacs environment@comma{} experienced through its commands and auditory feedback
rather than through a separate graphical desktop.

A useful habit is to press @kbd{C-e m} to hear the current buffer and mode@comma{} then
@kbd{C-h m} to discover the commands available there. This approach works in
files@comma{} Web pages@comma{} mail@comma{} shells@comma{} and other Emacs applications.

@menu
* Buffers hold your work: Desktop Objects.  Understand and move between Emacs buffers.
* Work with windows without losing your place: Working With Windows.  Keep several views available while knowing where commands and edits will act.
* Let modes give each buffer its tools: Object Oriented Desktop.  Let major and minor modes interpret each kind of content.
* Hear and navigate the current context: Context-Sensitive Interaction.  Use aural structure, mode commands, buffers, and search.
@end menu

@node Desktop Objects
@section Buffers Hold Your Work

@cindex buffers

A buffer holds the content or application view you are working with. It might
contain a file@comma{} an Info manual@comma{} a Dired listing@comma{} a Web page@comma{} mail search
results@comma{} a shell session@comma{} or Help text. Some buffers visit files; others exist
only within the Emacs session.

The mode line identifies the selected buffer and its major mode. Press
@kbd{C-e m} to hear it. These are the main ways to change buffers:

@table @kbd
@item C-x b
Switch to another buffer by name.  Completion lets you narrow the choices
instead of remembering the complete name.

@item C-x C-b
Open a buffer list.  In that list@comma{} use @kbd{C-h m} to learn its navigation@comma{}
selection@comma{} saving@comma{} and cleanup commands.

@item C-x k
Close a buffer that you no longer need.  Emacs asks before discarding
unsaved file changes.
@end table

Switching buffers does not close the one you left. You can work on a document@comma{}
consult Help or a reference@comma{} visit a shell@comma{} and return to the document without
reopening it.

@subsection Cycle Between Buffers of the Same Kind

@cindex buffer cycling
@kindex C-e n
@kindex C-e p
@kindex C-e .
@kindex C-e ,

When several buffers use the same major mode@comma{} you can cycle among them
without recalling their names. This is useful for comparing Org notes@comma{}
visiting several Dired directories@comma{} or moving between source files.

Press @kbd{C-e n} to select the next buffer with the same major
mode@comma{} or @kbd{C-e p} to select the previous one.  For example@comma{} from
an Org buffer these commands visit other Org buffers@comma{} skipping all buffers
that do not use the same major mode.  Buffers are grouped by major mode@comma{} not
by project or directory.

After the first command@comma{} you only need the final letter: @kbd{n}
continues forward and @kbd{p} moves backward.  You can change
direction at any time@comma{} and cycling wraps around when you reach the end.  For
example@comma{} @kbd{C-e n n p} moves forward twice@comma{} then returns to the
buffer you just left.

To navigate without the major-mode filter@comma{} use @kbd{C-e .} for
next buffer or @kbd{C-e @comma{}} for previous buffer.  You can then repeat
with just @kbd{.} or @kbd{@comma{}}@comma{} in the same way as
@kbd{n} and @kbd{p}.  You can also switch between these
controls while cycling.

The temporary buffer-selection controls also include @kbd{b} to
select a buffer by name@comma{} @kbd{f} to open a file@comma{}
@kbd{k} to kill a buffer@comma{} and @kbd{o} to change windows.

Press @kbd{C-g} when finished to return to normal editing.  A key
outside the temporary buffer-selection controls also ends cycling and
performs its normal action.  To choose a particular buffer by name@comma{} use
@kbd{C-x b}.

@node Working With Windows
@section Work with windows without losing your place

@cindex windows, working through speech
@cindex buffer versus window

A buffer contains your work; a window is a view of a buffer. Emacs calls its
outer application window a @emph{frame}. A frame can contain several Emacs windows@comma{}
but only one is selected for ordinary commands. You can work with those views
by name and spoken context without needing to picture their arrangement.

Switching buffers with @kbd{C-x b} changes the selected window's contents.
Selecting another window with @kbd{C-x o} moves to that view instead. The final
character is lowercase o@comma{} for "other"@comma{} not the digit zero. Emacsvox announces
the selected buffer and mode; @kbd{C-e m} repeats that context and @kbd{C-e l} checks
the current line. @kbd{C-e C-w} describes the window arrangement.

Two windows can show the same buffer at different positions. They are not
independent document copies: edits in either change the same text@comma{} and the
buffer has one mark. For two independent documents@comma{} select different buffers.
The GNU Emacs Manual's @ref{Windows,Multiple windows,,emacs,} section explains this model in detail.

@menu
* Keep a reference and a draft in separate windows: Reference And Draft Windows.  Consult a reference, copy a passage and return to the intended draft.
* Close a window without closing the document: Closing Windows Safely.  Simplify the layout without killing buffers or discarding unsaved text.
@end menu

@node Reference And Draft Windows
@subsection Keep a reference and a draft in separate windows

Begin with an editable draft selected and a reference buffer already open.
Finish prompts and leave settings editors first. For a safe first attempt@comma{}
use the source and notes buffers from @ref{Copying Text Between Buffers, , Copy a passage into another buffer}.
Keep their names available so that you can recover by buffer name at any time.

@enumerate
@item
In the draft@comma{} press @kbd{C-x 4 b}@comma{} enter the reference buffer's name@comma{} and press
@kbd{@key{RET}}. The digit 4 is a separate keypress. Emacs displays and selects the
reference in another window@comma{} creating or reusing a view according to its
display configuration. @kbd{C-x 4 f} does the equivalent for a file pathname.
@item
Check the selected buffer with @kbd{C-e m} and read a line or paragraph. Use
short inspections when you intend to retain point. Tracked reading with
@kbd{C-e i} moves the reference's reading position as explained in
@ref{Reading From Point, , Read from point@comma{} stop@comma{} and start again}.
@item
Press @kbd{C-x o} to select the next window and identify it before typing.
With two ordinary windows this usually alternates between reference and
draft. With more windows@comma{} repeat until the intended draft is selected;
do not assume the next view must be the draft.
@item
To transfer text@comma{} return to the reference@comma{} select and check a short passage@comma{}
then copy with @kbd{M-w}. Use @kbd{C-x o} to return to the draft@comma{} confirm its buffer
and insertion point@comma{} and press @kbd{C-y}. The reference text remains unchanged.
Follow @ref{Copying Text Between Buffers, , Copy a passage into another buffer} for kill-ring and newline checks.
@item
Return to the reference with @kbd{C-x o}. Its position is where you last left
it@comma{} including movement made while selecting or reading. Inspect it rather
than assuming copying restored an earlier reading position.
@end enumerate

If window selection becomes confusing@comma{} @kbd{C-x b} and a known buffer name is
always a useful recovery route from ordinary work. It can replace the current
view rather than restore the old layout; the buffer's text remains available.

Some speech commands intentionally change the layout. @kbd{C-e h} selects and
reads Help using a windowful-reading helper@comma{} which removes other windows.
Whole-buffer and region reading can use that helper for a large request too.
A lost split therefore does not necessarily mean the reference was closed.
Reopen the desired view with @kbd{C-x 4 b}. For a short inspection without that
helper@comma{} use line or paragraph reading as described under
@ref{Reading Without Moving Point, , Read a short passage without moving point}.

For the general display and selection commands@comma{} see @ref{Pop Up Window,Displaying in another window,,emacs,}
and @ref{Other Window,Using other windows,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual. Emacsvox adds speech to the
selection; it does not make the ordinary display rules identical in every setup.

@node Closing Windows Safely
@subsection Close a window without closing the document

Finish active prompts@comma{} then select the view you want to remove and check its
buffer with @kbd{C-e m}. Press @kbd{C-x 0}: Control-x@comma{} then the digit zero. This
removes the selected window@comma{} not its buffer or file. Select the buffer again
with @kbd{C-x b} when you need it.

To keep only the selected view in the frame@comma{} use @kbd{C-x 1} instead. The other
buffers remain available and unsaved text remains unsaved. These layout
commands are neither file saving nor undo. If Emacs cannot remove the last
ordinary window or a specially managed view@comma{} follow its explanation rather
than closing Emacs to force the layout change.

@kbd{C-x k} has a different purpose: it kills a buffer@comma{} with any applicable
unsaved-work prompt. @kbd{C-x 4 0} kills the buffer as well as its window; it is
not a longer spelling of @kbd{C-x 0}. Likewise@comma{} @kbd{q} belongs to the current mode:
in a plain document it usually inserts a letter@comma{} while Help@comma{} Info and Dired
have their own return commands.

See @ref{Change Window,Deleting and rearranging windows,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual for the full
set of layout operations. Keep @ref{Desktop Objects, , Buffers hold your work} as the reference for
buffer selection and closing work itself.

@node Object Oriented Desktop
@section Let Modes Give Each Buffer Its Tools

@cindex major modes

@cindex minor modes

Each buffer has one major mode responsible for its principal editing or
application commands. Programming-language modes work with source code@comma{}
Dired with directories@comma{} EWW with Web pages@comma{} and Org with structured notes.
Minor modes add optional behaviour without replacing the major mode.

The active mode determines which structures Emacs can navigate. In prose you
might move by sentence; in Org by heading; in a list by item. Emacsvox speaks
the result and can announce relevant state changes. To discover the mode's
commands@comma{} press @kbd{C-h m}@comma{} then @kbd{C-h k} followed by a key for more detail.
@ref{Using Online Help, , Using Online Help} explains these help commands.

@node Context-Sensitive Interaction
@section Hear And Navigate The Current Context

@cindex Context-Sensitive Interaction

Because Emacsvox has access to Emacs modes and application state@comma{} it can
announce the kind of object you have reached rather than treating everything
as undifferentiated text. Use the active mode's navigation for the structure
you are working with@comma{} and request more spoken detail as needed.

@subsection Hear Structure Without Extra Words

@cindex audio formatting

Voice changes can distinguish headings@comma{} comments@comma{} strings@comma{} links@comma{} warnings@comma{}
and other meaningful text.  Short sounds and tones can mark events or state
changes without interrupting the spoken content.  Ordinary text remains
available through the usual reading commands.

The exact result depends on the mode@comma{} the Emacsvox integration@comma{} and your Aural
Presentation settings.  @ref{Aural Presentation, , Aural Presentation} explains how the
parts fit together@comma{} including the established Voice Lock compatibility layer;
@kbd{C-e E} explains the presentation chosen for the object at point.

@subsection Move By The Structure Of The Content

@cindex structured navigation

Prefer the navigation supplied by the active mode when it matches your task.
For example@comma{} Info moves among nodes and menu entries@comma{} Org and Outline move
among headings@comma{} Dired moves among files@comma{} programming modes understand
definitions and diagnostics@comma{} and EWW exposes links and document structure.

The mode's Help@comma{} available through @kbd{C-h m}@comma{} describes its navigation
commands@comma{} including those supplied by installed packages and your
customisations. @ref{Browsing Structure, , Browsing Structure} points to guides for common kinds of structured
content.

@subsection Move Between Tasks Without Losing Your Place

@cindex desktop navigation

Use @kbd{C-x b} to return to a known buffer or @kbd{C-x C-b} to browse the current
set. For a reference and draft displayed together@comma{} follow
@ref{Reference And Draft Windows, , Keep a reference and a draft in separate windows}. Select the intended view
and inspect its buffer before editing; @kbd{C-e C-w} describes the arrangement.

Applications often create several related buffers@comma{} such as a search result and
an individual message.  Their mode help explains the commands for moving among
those views.  Treat each buffer as a place you can revisit@comma{} rather than as a
dialog that must be completed before doing anything else.

@subsection Search Wherever You Are

@cindex searching

@cindex finding

In most text buffers@comma{} @kbd{C-s} searches forward and @kbd{C-r} backwards. Type the
query@comma{} repeat the key for another match@comma{} and finish with @kbd{@key{RET}} before typing
an edit. In a failing search@comma{} @kbd{C-g} first backs out of the failed part; a
second cancellation may be needed to leave it. This search works in documents@comma{}
Help@comma{} source code and many application results. @ref{Searching And Replacing Text, , Find and replace text carefully}
explains match positions and scoped replacement; @ref{Incremental Search,Incremental search,,emacs,} in the
GNU Emacs Manual provides the full search reference.

Also look for the mode's semantic search and filtering commands.  A mail
application can search message fields@comma{} Dired can narrow files@comma{} and a browser
can move directly among links or headings.  Use the general search when text
is what matters and the mode-specific command when the kind of object matters.

@node Aural Presentation
@chapter Aural Presentation: Speech@comma{} Voices@comma{} And Sound

@cindex Aural Presentation

A heading@comma{} an unread message@comma{} and a warning need not sound alike. Aural
Presentation lets Emacsvox express those differences through speech@comma{} voices@comma{}
short sounds@comma{} tones@comma{} pauses@comma{} and spatial cues. The application identifies
what happened; presentation rules choose the feedback. The speech server
then produces it with the voices and output capabilities on your machine.

Return to the item whose feedback you want to understand and leave point on
it. Press @kbd{C-e E} to explain that item@comma{} @kbd{C-e H} for the settings menu@comma{} or
@kbd{C-e S} for the current task's focused settings where available. The workflows
below use these inspection and editing tools rather than hand-edited
configuration files. @ref{Emacs Key Notation, , Read the key instructions} explains how to enter the sequences.

@ref{Choosing A Presentation Change, , Choose the change you need} helps you pick the right tool. To listen
without making changes@comma{} start with @ref{Previewing An Installed Voice, , the voice-preview exercise}. The rest of the
chapter explains feedback rules@comma{} voices@comma{} engines@comma{} sounds@comma{} output and saved
setups. Each procedure includes its own menu route. Action search at
@kbd{C-e H a} can also select the relevant control before you activate it.

When something is wrong@comma{} go directly to @ref{Presentation Troubleshooting, , Troubleshooting and recovery}.
In an Aural interface@comma{} @kbd{C-c C-i} opens its manual topic; @kbd{q} in Info returns
to that interface. In the interface's Help@comma{} @kbd{i} opens the same topic.

@menu
* Choose the change you need: Choosing A Presentation Change.  Choose between a voice edit, a matching rule, an engine preference, and output settings.
* Getting started with settings menus: Aural Home.  Try a voice, navigate settings menus, and return from editors.
* How aural presentation works: Presentation Model.  Follow meaning from an Emacs application to audible output.
* Understand why something sounded that way: Explaining Presentation.  Explain current or recently queued feedback.
* Change feedback and manage rules: Adjusting Presentation.  Preview, scope, save, and undo presentation changes.
* Choose And Manage Voice Palettes: Voices And Routing.  Understand named voices, standard inheritance, and personal palettes.
* Edit A Named Voice: Editing Named Voices.  Adjust a voice, compare it, and save the complete result.
* Name Occasional Emoji In Speech: Emoji Speech Names.  Keep occasional decoration from forcing a different speech engine.
* Browse Installed Voices And Choose Engines: Installed Voices And Engines.  Find physical voices and configure shared engine preferences.
* Sounds And Space::             Audition sound packs and inspect spatial fallback.
* Speech Output And Notifications: Aural Speech Output.  Set speech rate and understand foreground and background output.
* Save@comma{} switch and share your setup: Presentation Profiles.  Save selected settings and distinguish profiles from resource backups.
* Troubleshooting and recovery: Presentation Troubleshooting.  Trace unexpected output and use safe diagnostics.
* Voice Lock and advanced configuration: Voice Lock.  Understand the established face and personality voice adapter.
@end menu

@node Choosing A Presentation Change
@section Choose the change you need

@cindex which voice setting to change
@cindex normal reading voice, choosing an operation
@cindex rule or palette

Start with the result you want@comma{} not with the name of an editor. A voice edit
changes how a shared voice sounds. A rule changes which items receive that
voice or other feedback. Output settings control delivery. The distinctions
below help avoid making a wider change than intended.

@subsection Change ordinary reading

Edit the active palette's @code{default} named voice to change normal text and
labels that do not request an explicit voice. Other entries@comma{} such as the
voice used for headings@comma{} keep their own settings. Choose this route for a
more comfortable everyday reading voice@comma{} not for one exceptional heading.
See @ref{Editing Named Voices, , Edit a named voice}.

@subsection Change one kind of item or one context

Use a presentation rule when the change belongs to matching headings@comma{} prompts@comma{}
message fields or events. Inspect a representative item@comma{} choose the criteria@comma{}
and decide how long the rule should last. A rule for the current buffer can
match several items there; it is not automatically limited to the one under
point. The @ref{Heading Voice Walkthrough, , heading exercise} is a reversible starting point.

Use @ref{Choosing A Voice For An Item, , voice mapping} to choose a different named voice for those items. Use
@ref{Guided Feedback Changes, , a guided feedback change} to add@comma{} remove or alter a voice@comma{} sound@comma{} tone or
spoken label. Neither operation requires retuning the shared named voice.

@subsection Retune a voice wherever it is used

Edit a named voice when its other uses in the palette should change too.
For example@comma{} adjusting @code{bolden} affects items that resolve to that entry@comma{}
not only the item from which the editor was opened. Inspect its known uses
before saving. For a change limited to the original context@comma{} use a rule instead.
See @ref{Editing Named Voices, , Edit a named voice} and @ref{Voices In Context, , voice adjustments in context}.

@subsection Prefer one physical voice throughout a palette

Use @ref{Changing A Palette Physical Voice, , the palette-wide review} when you want a physical voice to be preferred
across all its effective named entries while retaining their different
adjustments. Review the proposed result for several entries before saving.
This is a one-time edit@comma{} not a policy automatically inherited by future names.
It is broader than editing @code{default}.

@subsection Change how an engine is chosen

Use @ref{Speech Engine Preferences, , engine preference} when you want to change the shared engine order.
It does not override explicit physical choices already stored in named voices.
If one item still uses another engine@comma{} inspect that voice's physical choices
before changing the engine order again. @ref{Installed Voices And Engines, , Voice availability and activation} are
separate from preference: an installed or allowed voice is not necessarily ready
to play.

@subsection Change delivery or one unwanted sound

For reading speed or punctuation detail@comma{} use @ref{Reading Scope And Punctuation, , Speech settings}. For the place
where notifications play@comma{} use @ref{Notification Routing, , notification routing}; for their loudness@comma{} use
@ref{Output Volumes, , output volumes}. These controls do not decide which text an application
speaks automatically. Its @ref{Integration Speech Settings, , focused speech settings} control that behaviour.

For one unwanted cue@comma{} follow @ref{Changing One Sound, , Silence one cue} instead of disabling all sound.
To change the recordings used by a family of cues@comma{} choose a @ref{Sound Pack Walkthrough, , sound pack}.

After choosing the operation@comma{} decide whether to keep a draft@comma{} use the result
now or retain it for startup. @ref{Saving And Startup, , Choose what persists} explains those choices;
the scope shown in a menu does not make every Save action equivalent.

