@node Previewing An Installed Voice
@subsection Listen without changing settings

This exercise previews an installed voice without changing saved settings.
Start in an ordinary working buffer so the menu has a source to return to.

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C-e H v b} to open the engine browser through @emph{Voices and engines}.
@item
Select an available engine and press @kbd{v} to list its voices. @kbd{@key{RET}} on an
Omnivox engine opens its status@comma{} downloads@comma{} and settings instead.
@item
Move to an available voice and press @kbd{P} to hear a sample. @kbd{S} stops it.
@item
Use @kbd{q} to return through the browser's parent views to @emph{Voices and
engines}. Once back in the menu@comma{} @kbd{C-q} closes the menu sequence. The
original buffer remains your working context.
@end enumerate

Browsing and previewing save nothing. A downloaded but inactive voice may
need enablement and Apply before it can be sampled; choose an already available
voice for this first exercise. If an engine is unavailable@comma{} its details explain
why. If speech itself is absent@comma{} begin with
@ref{Recovering From No Speech,Recovering From No Speech,,emacsvox,}.

@node Returning From Settings
@subsection Choose an entry point and preserve your place

The Emacsvox menu@comma{} @kbd{C-e H}@comma{} groups settings by task. @kbd{C-e S}
opens the current buffer's focused integration settings@comma{} or general buffer
settings when no focused menu exists. @kbd{C-e H .} always opens general buffer
settings. A directly opened focused menu captures the buffer on entry; @kbd{q}
closes it and announces the restored buffer and mode with a close earcon.

The menu captures the source item for contextual actions. In Feedback Details@comma{}
@kbd{q} returns to the menu with the same item and settings scope; another @kbd{q}
closes that menu. Opening Feedback Details directly keeps its usual return
destination. A changed or deleted source item must be selected again by
reopening the menu. If the source buffer has closed@comma{} the menu cannot resume.

Editor and manager @kbd{h} keys open the Transient settings menu with the original
source item. Closing that menu returns to the editor without saving or
discarding its draft.

The menu continues to refer to the item captured when you opened it@comma{} even
if you move point in the source buffer. Use @kbd{C-e H b} to return to that
position. To work on a different item@comma{} close the menu and reopen @kbd{C-e H}
from the new item in an ordinary buffer.

The former Aural Home table remains available through @kbd{M-x emacsvox-aural}
for existing configurations. Its @kbd{C-e H h} entry has been retired; the
workflows below use the settings menus.

Leave an editor using its own @kbd{q}@comma{} @kbd{h} or finish action before using menu
keys. The same letter can mean something else while the editor still has
focus. Returning does not reverse an applied setting@comma{} and keeping an unfinished
draft does not save it. See @ref{Saving And Startup, , Choose what persists} before leaving work that
you need in a later session.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Navigate menus and read Help}Navigate menus and read Help


@anchor{settings-menu-navigation}

The settings menus use Transient: while a menu is open@comma{} its keys act on that
menu. Up/down selects an item; left/right changes sections and selects the
destination's first available item. Section movement stops at the first and
last section. Selecting an item speaks it without activating it or changing
its value. @kbd{@key{RET}}@comma{} @kbd{M-@key{RET}} or @kbd{C-j} activates that item. A displayed shortcut
invokes its action directly@comma{} without first moving the selection to it.

An action can open another menu or editor@comma{} toggle a switch@comma{} or ask for a value.
For a numeric control that offers adjustment@comma{} Shift-left/right decreases or
increases the selected value; @kbd{@key{RET}} opens its exact-value prompt. The item's
Help gives its step@comma{} range and scope. Plain arrows continue to navigate.
Waveform@comma{} envelope and other choice lists use up/down to browse and @kbd{@key{RET}} to
choose; browsing alone does not apply a choice. When a minibuffer prompt asks
for text or a number@comma{} use its ordinary editing and completion keys. Finish
with @kbd{@key{RET}} or cancel with @kbd{C-g} to return to the invoking control. A search
result instead selects the destination control.

In Emacsvox's menus@comma{} @kbd{q} or @kbd{C-g} goes back one menu and @kbd{C-q} closes the
sequence. A child menu normally returns to the item that opened it. Changes
already applied remain in effect; an editor's Back action states whether it
retains or discards an unfinished draft. Other applications using Transient@comma{}
including Magit@comma{} retain their own activation and quit keys. Use @kbd{C-j} to
activate a selected Magit item where @kbd{@key{RET}} has another meaning.

Menu feedback interrupts previous main-stream speech@comma{} including when you
toggle scope or change a setting. Item announcements include the key without
repeating the word "shortcut" or the section name. Under the default
announcement mode@comma{} section names are spoken when you enter a section.
Left/right selects its first selectable item and normally speaks it with the
section name and position.

For menu Help@comma{} press @kbd{?} followed by a choice's key@comma{} or @kbd{? ?} for help on
the current menu. The overview is a read-only snapshot: ordinary text reading
works there@comma{} but its displayed actions run only after you return to the menu.
@kbd{q} in that Help resumes the menu@comma{} including its parent menus and selection.
Opening @kbd{C-e H} announces a short reminder of Help and the @kbd{n} navigation
and speech settings. Moving between items does not repeat that reminder.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Review an item without changing the selection}Review an item without changing the selection


@anchor{settings-menu-review}

@cindex Transient menu review
@cindex menu items, reading by word or character

If an announcement is unclear@comma{} review the selected item's label@comma{} value@comma{}
description and key without leaving the menu. The review position is separate
from menu selection: @kbd{@key{RET}} still activates the same item after you read one
of its words or characters.

@table @kbd
@item C-c l
Repeat the complete item and reset its word and character review.
@item M-f@comma{} M-b
Read the next or previous word. Punctuation stays with its word.
@item C-f@comma{} C-b
Read the next or previous character@comma{} including spaces.
@item C-c w@comma{} C-c c
Repeat the current review word or character.
@item C-a
