@node Presentation Model
@section How Aural Presentation Works

@cindex presentation model

Four questions explain the path from an application event to sound:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.22 0.30 0.48
@item Layer
@tab Question
@tab Example
@item Meaning
@tab What happened or what is this?
@tab A level-two heading was reached during navigation.
@item Presentation
@tab What should convey that meaning?
@tab Speak the heading in a named voice and play a section cue first.
@item Resources
@tab Which voice and sound realise that request?
@tab Find the named voice in the active palette and the cue in the active sound pack.
@item Output
@tab What can this machine produce?
@tab Route speech to an installed engine and apply supported spatial behaviour.
@end multitable

An Emacs mode or integration supplies facts about an object or event. A fact
can identify a role such as a heading@comma{} an event such as completion@comma{} a state
such as unread@comma{} or an attribute such as heading level. Context says whether
you are navigating@comma{} reading continuously@comma{} or receiving a state change. The
same object can therefore receive concise feedback during continuous reading
and more orientation when deliberately reached.

Presentation rules match those facts and the context. A rule can choose a
voice@comma{} add speech or a cue@comma{} insert a pause or tone@comma{} request spatial placement@comma{}
or suppress something unhelpful. Several rules can contribute to the same
result. A stronger rule can replace just one part@comma{} such as pitch@comma{} while
leaving the other contributions in place.

The configuration layers@comma{} from weakest to strongest@comma{} are:

@enumerate
@item
A fixed internal baseline preserving established Emacsvox behaviour.
@item
Automatic integration behaviour supplied by the active mode or package.
@item
Enabled @emph{Presentation Options}@comma{} composed in their stable order.
@item
Personal@comma{} session@comma{} and current-buffer rules for precise overrides.
@end enumerate

A presentation profile is not another rule layer. It records selected options@comma{}
sound pack@comma{} voice palette@comma{} and spatial choices so you can switch them together.

For example@comma{} an Org integration identifies a level-two heading. A rule requests
@code{bolden} and a section cue. The active palette supplies @code{bolden}'s settings and
physical choices; the sound pack supplies the cue. The backend plays what it
supports. Changing the rule changes which headings receive the feedback.
Editing @code{bolden} changes uses of that named entry. Switching sound packs changes
the recording of the cue while preserving its meaning.

When two rules set the same voice field@comma{} the stronger explicit value wins;
values are not added. Personal rules are weaker than session rules@comma{} which are
weaker than current-buffer rules. Rules can also add actions before or after
content. Use Explain to inspect actual contributors rather than inferring
precedence from the order in which you edited settings.

@kbd{C-e H r h} opens the semantic vocabulary without changing settings.
@kbd{C-e H r t} toggles training explanations across all buffers for this Emacs
session; it does not save a startup default.
Use @kbd{C-e H r V} or search for @emph{Training explanation voice} to edit the global
voice assignment@comma{} initially @code{annotate}. The active palette resolves that name;
changing the assignment does not edit the shared named voice or enable training.
The focused Customize editor has separate Apply and Save actions. Outside an
editable field@comma{} @kbd{q} returns to the selected menu item without saving.

@menu
* What you can hear: Presentation Modalities.  Recognize speech, voices, cues, tones, pauses, and spatial output.
@end menu

@node Presentation Modalities
@subsection What You Can Hear

@cindex presentation modalities

Aural Presentation combines these forms of feedback:

@itemize
@item
@emph{Speech} conveys content@comma{} labels@comma{} prompts@comma{} results@comma{} and status. Added speech
should explain meaning not already clear from the content.
@item
@emph{Voice} changes characteristics of spoken content. Named voices make
distinctions such as headings@comma{} annotations@comma{} warnings@comma{} or quoted text
portable across engines.
@item
@emph{Auditory cues}@comma{} also called @emph{earcons} or @emph{auditory icons}@comma{} are short sounds
associated with events or objects. Sound packs supply recordings for cue names.
@item
@emph{Tones} convey compact information using a pitch and duration@comma{} without
requiring sampled sound or a spoken explanation.
@item
@emph{Pauses} separate related parts without adding words.
@item
@emph{Spatial presentation} places speech or cues across supported output. A
backend unable to provide requested placement keeps content audible through
its documented fallback.
@end itemize

These can be ordered around content. Entering a section might play a cue@comma{}
speak its heading in a distinctive voice@comma{} and leave a pause before body text.
The presentation is one result@comma{} not unrelated notifications. Its precise sound
depends on engines@comma{} installed voices@comma{} sound pack@comma{} and hardware. The intended
distinction should remain understandable when a simpler backend offers fewer
voice characteristics or centred output.

@node Explaining Presentation
@section Understand Why Something Sounded That Way

@cindex explaining presentation

Use this workflow when feedback is surprising@comma{} unclear@comma{} or especially useful:

@enumerate
@item
Leave point on the object and press @kbd{C-e E}@comma{} or open @kbd{C-e H x} through
the settings menu. Both open @emph{Feedback Details} with a brief introduction
instead of reading the whole report. The menu route uses the Outline
layout described below.
@item
Inspect the object and occasion@comma{} matching rules@comma{} selected actions@comma{} concrete
voices and sounds@comma{} and any backend fallback or degradation.
@item
Identify the strongest rule changing the part you care about. This tells
you whether to inspect an option@comma{} personal or temporary rule@comma{} named voice@comma{}
sound pack@comma{} or Voice Lock contribution.
@end enumerate

@kbd{C-e H f} groups current and recent feedback actions. @kbd{x} explains the captured
item@comma{} @kbd{c} opens a guided change@comma{} @kbd{v} prepares a voice mapping@comma{} @kbd{t} tunes the
named voice used there@comma{} and @kbd{e} prepares an exact cue remap. @kbd{h} opens recent
feedback@comma{} whose changes use the selected frozen record. Tuning a named voice
affects its other uses; a mapping changes which matching items use a voice.

Opening an editor from this menu does not bypass its preview@comma{} matching
criteria@comma{} scope or Apply/Save steps. On return@comma{} the menu selects the same
entry. A guided draft can be resumed later in this session@comma{} while leaving a
modified rule list still asks before discarding it. If the captured source
item has been replaced@comma{} prepare the change again; the editor does not silently
apply it to a different target.

@subsection Distinguish a record from a simulation

When retained feedback matches the source buffer@comma{} position@comma{} and unchanged
text@comma{} the report describes the exact output queued@comma{} including the source and
resolved resources. It does not reinterpret old output with your latest
settings. Without an exact record@comma{} the report is labelled as a simulation of
what the current rules would produce at point.

A simulation uses captured text and context. @emph{Play simulation} plays that
baseline@comma{} not a recording of earlier sound. It supports field review and draft
previews but is never added to Recent Feedback. A prefix argument with @kbd{C-e E}
selects a simulated occasion explicitly. @kbd{q} returns to the source or menu
item from which the report was opened.

Expand a field and choose @emph{Edit named voice bolden}@comma{} or its reported voice
name@comma{} to open the voice editor. Multiple named voices receive separate links.
This edits the entry in the reported palette and affects its uses there.
@emph{Change field} instead changes the field's feedback rule. Leaving the voice
editor returns to the same link; opening it does not save or apply anything.

@subsection Inspect recent feedback

Press @kbd{C-e H f h} for recent feedback. Each row preserves one recorded
presentation within the history's size limit. Its Content column reads the
preview with captured voice annotations@comma{} including changes within the entry;
other columns and titles use the normal interface voice. @kbd{P} requests replay
with the engine and physical voice recorded for the original delivery.

@table @kbd
@item RET or e
Open Feedback Details for the selected presentation.
@item P
Replay retained feedback with recorded voices without adding history.
@item c
Audition only its auditory cues.
@item r
Prepare a voice remap from the selected record.
@item R
Replace@comma{} suppress@comma{} or restore one exact earcon.
@item L
Change the bounded history limit for this session.
@item i
Include or exclude feedback from Aural interfaces themselves.
@end table

To change the same policy from a menu@comma{} open @kbd{C-e H f H}. Select @emph{Retained
records} and use @kbd{@key{RET}} for an exact nonnegative limit@comma{} or Shift-left/right to
adjust by one. Lowering the limit immediately discards the oldest excess
records; zero clears and disables history. Increasing it cannot restore those
records. The menu's @kbd{i} toggles interface recording@comma{} and @kbd{w} saves both defaults
through Customize. Retained feedback itself is never saved by this action.

Aural interface feedback is excluded by default so it does not displace the
feedback being investigated. This includes their prompts@comma{} completion choices@comma{}
and opening/closing sounds. Ordinary completion elsewhere remains eligible.
Changing the setting affects new recordings@comma{} not entries already retained.

@subsection Read and edit the report's fields

Feedback Details selects a separate read-only buffer and gives a brief
introduction. It stays attached to its presentation as new feedback arrives;
ordinary Help does not replace it. When opened from Recent Feedback@comma{} @kbd{q}
returns to the same row and column.

Where integrations supply semantic fields@comma{} the report groups consecutive voice
spans accordingly. Notmuch results@comma{} for example@comma{} expose @emph{Authors}@comma{} @emph{Subject}@comma{}
@emph{Date}@comma{} @emph{Message count}@comma{} and @emph{Tags}. Headings identify recorded logical voices.
Collapsed fields show only headings. Visibility uses configured cues or optional
spoken labels; @emph{expanded} and @emph{collapsed} are not inserted into heading text.

@kbd{@key{RET}} on a heading exposes its text@comma{} playback/change controls@comma{} voice source@comma{}
sounds@comma{} and limitations. Content uses captured voice annotations. Fields with
multiple spans offer @emph{Individual voice spans} for finer inspection. That heading
announces the section without repeating content. A single-span field shows its
text and controls once and omits a span count. @kbd{@key{RET}} collapses the field again.

Spoken labels such as @emph{indent 2} have separate @emph{Before speech} or @emph{After speech}
headings@comma{} distinct from content spans. @kbd{@key{RET}} on a label heading@comma{} or @kbd{O} within
it@comma{} replays that label using recorded settings. @emph{Edit named voice indent} opens
the palette voice editor with @emph{indent 2} as sample text; it changes every use of
that entry in the recorded palette. The report's recording stays frozen: use
the editor's preview for changes. Labels without a named palette voice remain
playable and explain why an editor link is unavailable.

Fields with unapplied drafts announce @emph{unsaved change}. The report also counts
such fields. These indicators update without replacing buttons or return
positions; @kbd{g} refreshes draft status@comma{} for example after killing a draft buffer.
It does not change the recorded or simulated baseline.

The original report layout uses these controls. Outline changes heading and
button navigation as described in the next subsection@comma{} not playback semantics.

@table @kbd
@item n or p
Move between headings; collapsed fields speak the heading@comma{} expanded fields also speak annotated content.
@item Up or Down
Review lines without reading the whole report.
@item TAB or Shift-TAB
Move between action buttons; RET activates or expands.
@item O
Play the original field@comma{} span@comma{} or label at point@comma{} or its labelled simulation.
@item P
Play the full original presentation or labelled simulation.
@item C
Open or resume a field/span change; on a label@comma{} edit its named voice.
@item r
Choose a named voice for matching items; on a label@comma{} edit its named voice.
@item R
Replace@comma{} suppress@comma{} or restore an exact sound in the field or span.
@item V
Preview the full presentation with linked draft changes.
@item S
Stop playback.
@item g
Refresh draft status.
@end table

For example@comma{} select @emph{Subject}@comma{} press @kbd{C}@comma{} and choose another content voice.
In the editor@comma{} @kbd{O} plays the original field and @kbd{P} the changed field.
@emph{Play whole original}@comma{} also on @kbd{B}@comma{} compares with @kbd{V} for the full presentation
and linked drafts. Both keep you in the editor. The original remains frozen@comma{}
including before a change or lifetime is chosen. A simulated report uses
@emph{Play whole simulation} for its frozen baseline.

Return to the report and choose @emph{Message count} to add a sound @emph{After content}.
It plays once after the complete count@comma{} even if the field contains several
voices. Subject and count keep separate drafts. Their criteria identify field@comma{}
integration@comma{} mode@comma{} and occasion; inspect them before selecting buffer@comma{} session@comma{}
or saved-personal lifetime. Remapping the field does not retune a shared voice.

@emph{Choose another voice for matching items} opens the compact voice editor.
Inspect @emph{Applies to} and choose @emph{How long} before saving. @emph{Change field} opens
the full component editor on the same draft. A draft already changing another
component stays in the full editor so that work is not hidden.

@emph{Sound overrides: replace@comma{} suppress@comma{} or restore} prepares a separate exact-sound
rule draft restricted to the field. It is not included in linked-field previews.
@emph{Restore inherited behavior} removes an override made by that workflow in the
selected scope. @kbd{q} returns to the report.

@subsection Use the Outline report from the menu

@kbd{C-e H x} opens the experimental Outline version of Feedback Details. It is
read-only@comma{} with a visible summary and initially folded fields. @kbd{@key{TAB}} on a
heading cycles visibility@comma{} @kbd{S-@key{TAB}} cycles the whole report@comma{} and @kbd{n~/~p} moves
between visible headings. Standard Outline minor-mode commands are available
under @kbd{C-c @@}@comma{} including @kbd{C-c @@ C-u} for the parent. Arrows read visible lines.
@kbd{@key{RET}} folds a heading or activates an action. Away from a heading@comma{} @kbd{@key{TAB}} and
@kbd{M-n} move to the next visible action. Playback and editing use the report
commands above.

@kbd{o} compares Outline with the original layout. Refreshing draft status with
@kbd{g} preserves folds and position; returning from an editor preserves its
button. The report still describes the captured feedback. Reports opened
directly start in the original layout; @kbd{o} switches their style.

@subsection Understand playback evidence and limitations

Original replay uses the recorded engine and physical voice@comma{} including
notification voices and fallbacks@comma{} with captured tuning even after a palette
change. It synthesises speech again at the current reading speed and host
pronunciation settings. History stores no audio. Cue and pause order remains@comma{}
but timing may differ from the first delivery.

Replay requires a completed presentation with complete Omnivox voice receipts.
Older@comma{} interrupted@comma{} truncated@comma{} or unsupported records remain inspectable but
report replay unavailable rather than guessing a voice. Cues positioned inside
speech@comma{} or voice changes that cannot be aligned after host text processing@comma{}
also prevent original replay. An unavailable recorded voice stops playback
instead of substituting another. Navigate over the source to capture a new
example.

A simulated report labels its baseline throughout the report and change editor.
Proposed playback uses current rules@comma{} captured buffer context@comma{} and linked drafts
without applying them. A span can be targeted only when its semantic field@comma{}
distinct named face@comma{} or explicit voice annotation identifies it. Truncated
history cannot supply complete playback or a comparable preview. Records
missing preview inputs remain readable; capture a new example before previewing.

Each field explains the sources of its voices and sounds@comma{} such as a face or
personal override@comma{} using the contributors actually captured. Unsupported voice
families and other limitations appear beside the affected field.

@emph{Debug details} opens a separate read-only raw snapshot of facts@comma{} rule IDs@comma{}
resolved voices@comma{} and backend diagnostics. Opening speaks only a short
announcement. @kbd{q} returns to the same report location with fields and drafts
retained.

@kbd{C-e H r t} toggles @emph{Explain feedback as you navigate} for this session. It adds
concise semantic explanations after normal presentations@comma{} useful for learning
an integration's vocabulary. It is deliberately more verbose than normal use;
turn it off when no longer needed.

