@node Heading Voice Walkthrough
@subsection Walkthrough: Change Headings In One Buffer

This experiment changes headings in a temporary Org buffer without altering
palette definitions or saved personal rules. Use an unused buffer name; if
@code{*aural-practice*} already contains work@comma{} choose another name throughout the
exercise. Do not erase existing text to recreate the example.

@enumerate
@item
Use @kbd{C-x b} to create that new buffer. Run @kbd{M-x org-mode @key{RET}} and insert:

@example
* Reading notes
An ordinary paragraph.
* Next steps
Another ordinary paragraph.
@end example

@item
Move onto the first heading and press @kbd{C-e E}. Identify its named voice
and heading facts@comma{} noting whether the report describes recorded output or
a simulation. Press @kbd{q} to return to the source before opening a new menu.

@item
Press @kbd{C-e H f c} for the guided change. Press @kbd{c}@comma{} select @emph{Content voice}@comma{}
and choose @code{annotate} with @kbd{@key{RET}}@comma{} or @code{bolden} if @code{annotate} is already used.
@kbd{O} plays the original and @kbd{P} previews the proposed voice. Only the draft
has changed.

@item
Press @kbd{m} for @emph{What should match}. Keep the Org module@comma{} heading role@comma{} and
heading level if shown. Leave other criteria as shown for this experiment.
Press @kbd{l} and select @emph{Current buffer@comma{} until it is killed}. This matches
every qualifying heading in the buffer@comma{} not only the original heading.

@item
Review the summary and press @kbd{a} to apply it. Open settings with @kbd{h}@comma{}
then press @kbd{b} in that menu to return to the captured item. Move between
the two headings using the same navigation action. Both matching headings
should use the chosen voice; ordinary paragraphs should keep their
previous presentation.

@item
Press @kbd{C-e E} on a heading again. Check that the buffer rule supplies the
selected voice. If the sound is too similar to distinguish@comma{} the report
still shows whether it matched. Compare voices or consult
@ref{Presentation Troubleshooting,Troubleshooting,,emacsvox,}. Return to the source with @kbd{q}.

@item
Open @kbd{C-e H r o} for @emph{Your feedback rules}. Find the rule in buffer scope
and inspect its target. @kbd{t} disables it and @kbd{d} removes it. Revisit the
heading to check the result. Killing the practice buffer also ends its
buffer rules; none survives an Emacs restart.
@end enumerate

For a lasting change@comma{} repeat on a representative item and choose @emph{Saved
personal rule} at step 4. Review the criteria carefully: that rule can affect
future matching buffers.

@node Guided Feedback Changes
@subsection Guided Change This Feedback

@enumerate
@item
Move to an item and press @kbd{C-e H f c} to capture @emph{Current item}. For earlier
feedback@comma{} open @kbd{C-e H f h}@comma{} select a record@comma{} and press @kbd{C}. A multipart
record expands numbered parts in playback order. Move with @kbd{n~/~p} to
hear text in captured voices@comma{} @kbd{O} to replay the part@comma{} and @kbd{@key{RET}} to choose
it. Truncated history cannot provide a complete original/proposed comparison.

@item
Press @kbd{c} to expand component changes and @kbd{@key{RET}} on a choice. @emph{Content
voice} expands named voices@comma{} identifies original and replacement@comma{} and lists
the original first when available@comma{} then alternatives alphabetically.
Selecting a voice with @kbd{@key{RET}} keeps the panel open for comparison. Changes
can add a cue@comma{} tone@comma{} or spoken label@comma{} replace a sound@comma{} change its timing@comma{}
change a tone@comma{} or suppress a component. A cue can be added when none existed.
Choices depend on integration facts; they cannot create missing semantic
distinctions.

@kbd{@key{RET}} on @emph{Parts} reopens the parts; each retains its unfinished choices.
@kbd{q} goes up one level@comma{} for example from a voice to @emph{Content voice} and
then @emph{Change}. The section closes and its parent's name and state are
announced through the configured cues and labels. At the top@comma{} @kbd{q} leaves
the panel while preserving its draft.

@item
Press @kbd{P} to preview before choosing a lifetime. @kbd{O} replays the original
and @kbd{S} stops. Proposed preview resolves the example with current rules
and captured context; it does not apply the draft to live feedback.

@item
Press @kbd{m} or @kbd{@key{RET}} on @emph{What should match}. Facts@comma{} states@comma{} events@comma{} and
occasions are marked @emph{Included} or @emph{Excluded}. All included conditions must
match; excluded conditions are ignored@comma{} not forbidden. The captured or
simulated example's facts are not editable@comma{} but can be selected as conditions.
Opening criteria preserves existing selections; extra facts start excluded.

A mail action of @emph{select} describes the operation producing feedback@comma{} not
selection in this editor. Include that action@comma{} or an event such as
@emph{operation failed}@comma{} to restrict the rule. @kbd{@key{RET}} toggles each criterion.
Excluded criteria remain available to restore. Excluding @emph{with state unread}
while retaining @emph{with role field}@comma{} @emph{with field kind authors}@comma{} and the
Notmuch integration matches authors in both read and unread messages.
Keep at least one criterion. The parent row summarises the match; @kbd{m}
collapses the criteria. These conditions govern matching items@comma{} not one
document object.

A shell example may match "Items from the shell integration@comma{} in shell
mode@comma{} during navigation@comma{} and with text style comint highlight prompt."
The text style names the Emacs face used to display that text.

@item
Press @kbd{l} for @emph{How long}: source-buffer lifetime@comma{} this Emacs session@comma{} or
a saved personal rule. A buffer rule affects every matching item in that
buffer. Review target@comma{} change@comma{} criteria@comma{} and lifetime together.

@item
Press @kbd{a} or @kbd{C-c C-c} to apply or save. @kbd{e} opens the prepared rule in
the Advanced editor. At the top@comma{} @kbd{q} hides the guided draft. Resume it
through @kbd{C-e H p d}@comma{} which opens the retained draft in its owning editor.
Aborting a choice or leaving before Apply does not change live rules.
Failed saves retain the draft and report failure.
@end enumerate

To change an existing earcon's timing@comma{} choose @emph{Change sound timing} and
select the sound. @emph{Before speech} finishes the sound before the selected
content starts. @emph{Alongside speech} starts it at the content boundary and
overlaps speech. @emph{After speech} starts it when that content finishes.
The completion list marks the current timing and explains each choice;
browsing applies nothing. The sound keeps its volume and spatial settings@comma{}
and other feedback keeps its order. Preview@comma{} matching criteria@comma{} lifetime
and Apply work as described above.

@emph{Before speech} requires structured speech delivery@comma{} supported by Omnivox.
If a delivery cannot preserve that timing@comma{} ordinary speech continues with
the cue omitted and a warning. Preview reports the limitation instead of
playing a different timing. Exact overlap also depends on the speech backend.

Replacing or suppressing a sound targets its identity in a before- or
after-content phase while retaining other components. Existing rule data can
insert only at a phase's beginning or end@comma{} so replacing a middle component
asks which edge to use and includes that placement in the summary. Ambiguous
components require the Advanced editor. Current-item changes recheck their
captured source; a changed or killed target must be inspected again. Historical
changes remain tied to the selected record.

@node Choosing A Voice For An Item
@subsection Remap One Item At Point

@enumerate
@item
Move to a representative object and press @kbd{C-e E} to identify what
controls it. Return to the source with @kbd{q} before starting a new action.

@item
Press @kbd{C-e H f v} to choose a voice for matching items. To tune the named
voice already used there instead@comma{} use @kbd{C-e H f t}. Tuning changes that
voice wherever the palette entry is used; remapping changes which voice
a face or item receives. For a shell prompt@comma{} you can assign a different
voice to the prompt face or tune the current prompt voice. It need not
have been spoken before you begin.

In the remapping editor@comma{} move with @kbd{n~/~p} or arrows. @kbd{@key{RET}} on @emph{Voice}
chooses a voice@comma{} @emph{Preview original} plays the captured item@comma{} and @emph{Preview
change} plays the draft. @kbd{O~/~P} provide the same previews; @kbd{S} stops.
Previewing does not save or retune the named voice. Use @kbd{C-e H f e} for
an exact earcon remap.

@item
Choose @emph{How long}: personal persists across Emacs sessions@comma{} session lasts
until Emacs exits@comma{} and buffer affects only the captured live buffer.

@item
Review @emph{Applies to}@comma{} @emph{Voice}@comma{} and @emph{How long}@comma{} then @emph{Save} or @kbd{w}. A face
mapping affects matching uses within its module/mode and lifetime.
@emph{Advanced} opens the full editor for finer criteria. At the top@comma{} @kbd{q} hides
the compact editor and retains its draft. After a voice has been chosen@comma{}
@kbd{C-e H p d} resumes it directly in the original editor.

@item
Return to the source@comma{} repeat the action@comma{} and press @kbd{C-e E} to confirm
which rule wins.
@end enumerate

@node Managing Feedback Rules
@subsection Review And Remove Your Rules

@kbd{C-e H r o} opens @emph{Your feedback rules}@comma{} combining personal@comma{} session@comma{} and
captured-buffer changes. @kbd{q} returns to the selected menu item. @kbd{P} previews
the complete result@comma{} @kbd{t} temporarily enables/disables a rule@comma{} and @kbd{d} removes
it. Removing an override restores the next weaker contribution rather than
leaving a copied default.

In the rule list@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} or @kbd{e} opens the selected rule's panel. Other rules
stay in the working draft. @kbd{n~/~p} moves through settings and @kbd{@key{SPC}} reads the
selected value. @kbd{@key{RET}} expands @emph{What should match}@comma{} @emph{Content}@comma{} @emph{Before content}@comma{}
or @emph{After content}. Matching criteria toggle included/excluded states.

Within @emph{Content}@comma{} @emph{voice} and @emph{speak} expand choices in place with the current
selection shown. Other fields ask for the selected value. Existing actions
expand into settings; @emph{Change operation} chooses an operation for that phase.
@emph{Inherit} removes a voice or speech override and @kbd{D} removes another selected
setting. Each change preserves other settings and remains in the draft.

@kbd{P} in the panel previews the working rule and @kbd{S} stops. @kbd{w} or @emph{Save} writes
to its original scope and announces whether it was saved or temporarily
applied. @kbd{q} closes a section and focuses its parent@comma{} retaining edits: voice
choice to @emph{voice} to @emph{Content}@comma{} then panel to rule list. In the list@comma{} @kbd{N} adds
a rule and @kbd{q} asks before discarding an unfinished draft.

@node Presentation Options
@subsection Try A Presentation Option

Presentation Options supply maintained additions such as heading feedback or
integration-specific cues. Open them with @kbd{C-e H r f}:

@enumerate
@item
Expand a collection with @kbd{@key{TAB}} or @kbd{@key{RET}}.
@item
Preview an option with @kbd{P} composed with current settings@comma{} or @kbd{C-u P}
in isolation.
@item
Toggle it with @kbd{t}. @kbd{a} reads enabled options in weakest-to-strongest order.
@item
Return to the source and repeat the original task. Personal@comma{} session@comma{}
and buffer overrides remain stronger than enabled options.
@end enumerate

Do not reorder options to solve one exceptional item when a narrow override
expresses the intended change more precisely.

@node Voices And Routing
@section Choose And Manage Voice Palettes

@cindex voice palettes
@cindex acss-default
@cindex palette inheritance
@cindex voice aliases

A @emph{named voice}@comma{} also called a @emph{logical voice} in detailed views@comma{} gives a
reusable name such as @code{bolden} or @code{annotate} to a way of speaking. A @emph{voice
palette} contains these entries. Each includes shared adjustments@comma{} ordered
physical choices@comma{} and adjustments for individual choices.

A @emph{physical voice} comes from an installed speech engine. The palette expresses
intended choices; availability and shared engine preferences determine which
can be used. A presentation rule determines when to use a named voice. Editing
that entry changes every use resolving to it; changing a rule changes which
items select it.

This section manages the collection. @ref{Editing Named Voices,Edit a Named Voice,,emacsvox,} covers its physical
choices and adjustments; @ref{Installed Voices And Engines,Installed Voices and Engines,,emacsvox,} covers browsing and
engine preferences.

Open @emph{Voice Palettes} with @kbd{C-e H v p}. @kbd{@key{RET}} on a palette browses its voices@comma{}
@kbd{P} auditions its sequence@comma{} and @kbd{a} activates it. Built-in palettes remain
intact; editing can open an unsaved personal child in the common editor.

@strong{Upgrading personal palettes.} This version accepts schema 3 only. Before
upgrading older personal palettes@comma{} back up palette/routing data and arrange a
reviewed conversion of entries and physical choices. Opening or previewing does
not convert them. Preserve names and profile references@comma{} with each personal
parent chain ending at @code{acss-default}. Shared per-voice routing@comma{} migration@comma{}
and preset screens have been removed; use the common editor for named-voice
choices and adjustments. Shared engine preference remains separate.

Opening the manager announces its selected palette; entering a palette
announces its name@comma{} voice count@comma{} selected voice@comma{} and main keys. @emph{Kind}
distinguishes personal@comma{} built-in@comma{} and standard-default palettes. @emph{Parent}
names the inherited source. Read-only @code{acss-default} is the root: chains such
as @code{a -> b -> c -> acss-default} are supported. It provides personalities from
Emacsvox and loaded integrations@comma{} not a fixed list of all custom names. Only
the root has no parent.

A palette inherits the nearest complete definition of a named voice. Its
style@comma{} ordered physical choices and individual tuning come from that one
entry; they are not assembled from several parents. The @emph{Source} column names
the supplying palette@comma{} and capital @kbd{O} opens it.

Press @kbd{u} for @emph{Where Used} before editing a shared entry. This lists known
loaded references@comma{} aliases and affected descendant palettes. It cannot inspect
configuration that has not been loaded. Editing a shared entry asks you to
review those known uses and descendants.

An @emph{alias}@comma{} such as @code{voice-bolden} for @code{bolden}@comma{} is a declared alternative
spelling of the same entry. Integrations maintain aliases; the manager does
not edit them as separate voices. Use Copy Voice for an independently
adjustable entry.

@kbd{N} in a voice list creates New Voice and @kbd{c} copies one into an unsaved
editor draft. Copy preserves the full effective entry@comma{} fallback order@comma{} and
individual tuning. Editing or copying a built-in voice requests a personal
child palette; saving adds only the requested direct entry. Opening or previewing
writes nothing.

@kbd{R} resets a personal definition to its inherited entry. Confirmation names
the source that will take over and rejects a source changed during confirmation.
Other saved settings remain. @kbd{d} deletes a direct custom voice with no inherited
replacement; built-in and inherited entries cannot be deleted. Remap known
references first if deletion would leave them unresolved. Finish or discard
voice drafts before deletion. @kbd{o} returns to the palette manager.

New Palette@comma{} @kbd{N} in the manager@comma{} creates a child of the chosen parent. Copy
Palette@comma{} @kbd{c}@comma{} saves all effective entries independently under @code{acss-default};
local physical choices belong to the new palette. The summary and parent action
@kbd{E} inspect or change a personal parent; inheritance cycles are rejected.

To delete a personal palette@comma{} select it and press @kbd{d}. Change children and
known references@comma{} including saved profiles@comma{} and finish/discard its voice drafts
first. Deleting an active palette returns to the standard baseline.

In Voice Palettes@comma{} @kbd{a} activates the selected palette for this session.
To return to following the standard baseline@comma{} press @kbd{f}. That clears an
explicit session selection without deleting palette data or changing startup.

Capital @kbd{U}@comma{} @emph{Use at Startup}@comma{} changes the palette named by the selected
saved Presentation Profile and preserves that profile's other fields. It does
not apply the profile now. With no startup profile@comma{} the operation proposes
one captured from the current setup; review it before saving. Its checks cover
saved rules and profile options@comma{} not session or buffer rules. An open Profiles
view refreshes to show the saved choice.

