* Walkthrough Make A Personal Reading Palette: Personal Palette Walkthrough.  Create, edit, save, activate, and optionally select a palette for startup.
* Walkthrough Inheritance@comma{} Aliases@comma{} And Reset: Palette Inheritance Walkthrough.  Follow an entry to its parent and distinguish an alias from a copy.
* Rename Voices And Palettes: Renaming Voices And Palettes.  Rename personal entries while checking their known uses.
@end menu

@node Personal Palette Walkthrough
@subsection Walkthrough: Make A Personal Reading Palette

Use an unused name; @code{reading-demo} is an example. Note the active palette and
whether selection is explicit or follows the standard baseline. The experiment
saves a personal palette and briefly activates it@comma{} but leaves startup selection
unchanged. Restore the selection policy as well as the audible result at the end.

@enumerate
@item
Open @kbd{C-e H v p}. Press @kbd{N}@comma{} enter @code{reading-demo}@comma{} describe its purpose
as ``My reading experiments''@comma{} and choose @code{acss-default} as parent.
Creation saves but does not activate the palette. Its standard voices
are inherited.
@item
Press @kbd{@key{RET}} to enter it. Find @code{bolden} and inspect @emph{Source}@comma{} which should
name @code{acss-default}. @kbd{.} repeats the column title/value@comma{} @kbd{@key{SPC}} reads the
row@comma{} and @kbd{P} previews the voice.
@item
Press @kbd{e} to edit @code{bolden}. Check destination and inherited source@comma{} then
set @emph{Average Pitch} with @kbd{@key{RET}} to @code{6}@comma{} or @code{5} if already six. @kbd{B} compares
opening and edited voices using one sample. Unsupported adjustments remain
reported; consult @ref{Voice Adjustments,Voice Adjustments,,emacsvox,} if the contrast is unclear.
@item
Choose @emph{Save to collection without selecting}. After the save result@comma{}
@kbd{q} returns to the voice list. @emph{Source} should now name @code{reading-demo};
other inherited entries retain their providers. This is a complete local
entry@comma{} not a pitch-only patch on its parent.
@item
Use @kbd{o} to return to the manager and @kbd{a} to activate @code{reading-demo}.
Check status and revisit content using @code{bolden}. This changes every use
resolving to that entry. Explain at the source distinguishes palette
settings from contextual rules.
@item
Leave startup selection unchanged for this experiment. Capital @kbd{U} is
the separate @emph{Use at Startup} action@comma{} not the next step required to hear
your saved palette.
@item
In the palette manager@comma{} use @kbd{f} if you originally followed the standard
baseline. Otherwise select the previous explicit palette and press @kbd{a}.
The saved demo remains for later experiments. To remove only its @code{bolden}
override@comma{} follow @ref{Palette Inheritance Walkthrough,Inheritance@comma{} aliases and reset,,emacsvox,}.
@end enumerate

For a deliberate lasting change@comma{} @kbd{U} changes the palette in the saved profile.
If it proposes a new profile@comma{} review its other settings too. Re-selecting an
old palette does not by itself undo creating or editing that startup profile.
See @ref{Saving And Startup,Choose what persists,,emacsvox,} before taking this separate step.

@node Palette Inheritance Walkthrough
@subsection Walkthrough: Inheritance@comma{} Aliases@comma{} And Reset

Use @code{reading-demo} from the previous exercise or another personal palette
with @code{acss-default} as parent.

@enumerate
@item
In the manager@comma{} create @code{reading-child-demo} with @kbd{N} and choose
@code{reading-demo} as parent. The chain becomes
@code{reading-child-demo -> reading-demo -> acss-default}.
@item
Inspect @code{bolden}'s @emph{Source} in the child. A direct entry in @code{reading-demo}
supplies the whole voice; otherwise @code{acss-default} does. @kbd{O} opens its
provider and @kbd{u} shows known uses@comma{} aliases@comma{} and descendants.
@item
@code{voice-bolden} is an alias of @code{bolden}@comma{} not a separate adjustable entry.
Aliases are not created or edited in this view.
@item
Copy @code{bolden} in the child with @kbd{c} to an unused name@comma{} such as
@code{reading-heading-demo}. Change pitch@comma{} compare@comma{} and save to the collection
without selecting. A rule must choose this name before ordinary content
uses it; copying does not remap headings.
@item
Edit the child's inherited @code{bolden}@comma{} change a field@comma{} and save to the
collection. Its Source becomes the child. Back in the list@comma{} press @kbd{R}.
Confirmation names the parent entry that will take over. After confirming@comma{}
@code{bolden} remains but its Source returns to the provider.
@item
Delete @code{reading-heading-demo} with @kbd{d} when it has no inherited replacement
or known use. Remove/remap a referencing rule first. Reset restores an
inherited entry; Delete removes a removable custom name.
@end enumerate

The root keeps standard voices available after Reset and cannot be deleted
or edited here. Palettes can contain custom names@comma{} and loaded integrations can
register more standard names. Copy Palette differs from New Palette: it copies
effective entries and stops following edits to the source while still inheriting
new standard names from @code{acss-default}. Definitions referring to an existing
personality still follow that personality's settings. A copy is not a sound
recording.

@node Renaming Voices And Palettes
@subsection Rename Voices And Palettes

@kbd{r} in the voice list renames a direct custom voice in a personal palette.
Entering the new name saves it with its settings@comma{} physical choices@comma{} and tuning
intact. Finish/discard voice drafts first. Standard names cannot be renamed;
copy one for a custom entry. An inherited voice must be copied or renamed in
its owning palette.

Renaming updates personal@comma{} session@comma{} and buffer rules@comma{} face mappings@comma{} and loaded
global/buffer face maps. Personal rules and the voice are saved together; a
failure leaves the old name and mappings usable. Update face mappings written
in your Lisp configuration there too for future sessions. Maintained integration
rules@comma{} Presentation Options@comma{} and other voice definitions can block a rename
until their references are updated. Another palette directly defining either
name also blocks it because mappings are shared.

In @emph{Voice Palettes}@comma{} @kbd{r} renames a personal palette and saves immediately.
It retains settings and fallback tuning@comma{} updates children and personal
Presentation Profiles@comma{} and keeps an active palette selected under its new name.
Finish/discard unfinished voice edits first. Built-ins cannot be renamed@comma{} and
maintained integration references can block the change until their owner
updates them.

@node Editing Named Voices
@section Edit A Named Voice

@cindex voice editor
@cindex voice adjustments

Open a palette through @kbd{C-e H v p}@comma{} select a voice@comma{} and press @kbd{e}. To tune
the voice used by the captured source item@comma{} use @kbd{C-e H f t}. This changes the
shared named entry@comma{} not a rule for that one item. @kbd{q} offers to retain unfinished
work and returns to the menu.

Saving an inherited voice creates a complete entry in the destination palette.
Editing a built-in palette requests a personal child. Opening and previewing
do not change live feedback or save settings.

@code{default} controls ordinary reading and labels without an explicit voice.
Choose it to change normal text's physical voice@comma{} fallbacks@comma{} or tuning. Each
palette has an effective @code{default}@comma{} inherited until locally edited. Switching
palettes changes it too. Other entries such as @code{indent} and @code{bolden} retain
their own settings; editing @code{default} is not a whole-palette voice change.

@menu
* Use one physical voice across a palette: Changing A Palette Physical Voice.  Review a preferred physical voice across named entries before saving.
* Understand Voice Adjustments: Voice Adjustments.  Understand the voice controls and their defaults.
* Choose And Tune Fallback Voices: Voice Fallbacks.  Order physical choices and customize their individual adjustments.
* Inspect Or Adjust A Voice In Context: Voices In Context.  Compare a voice with contextual rules and edit a rule rather than its base entry.
@end menu

@subsection Adjust and compare one voice

The editor reads field labels in the normal navigation voice. Move between
fields with @kbd{n}@comma{} @kbd{p}@comma{} the up/down arrows or @kbd{@key{TAB}}. Left/right adjusts a
numeric field and moves between other fields. Press @kbd{@key{RET}} to activate a
control or enter a value.

Lowercase @kbd{d}@comma{} or blank numeric input@comma{} requests the adapter's default for
that adjustment. Entering zero instead requests an explicit value@comma{} such as the
lowest pitch or leftmost pan. A default and zero may sound alike for a
particular effect@comma{} but they remain different settings.

@emph{More adjustments and effects} exposes filters@comma{} gain@comma{} pan@comma{} and optional controls.
Point stays on its button and speech says ``More adjustments shown below'' or
``More adjustments hidden''. Move down or use @kbd{@key{TAB}} to enter exposed controls@comma{}
and activate the button again to hide them. Changed fields state earlier values;
unsupported settings stay visible and retained. Field navigation stops at its
ends without wrapping@comma{} and numeric limits announce minimum or maximum.

@kbd{P} previews the edited voice; @kbd{B} compares captured original and edited versions
with the same sample and spoken labels. @kbd{T} changes sample text and @kbd{S} stops.
An automatic sample follows adjustments unless @emph{Automatic sample after adjustment}
is off. The value precedes it@comma{} for example ``1. The quick brown fox…''.

That toggle announces on/off with distinct cues. @emph{More adjustments and effects}
and @emph{Fallbacks} use open/close cues and optional state labels on both toggling
and navigation. Navigation stops a sample. @kbd{u} undoes an edit@comma{} including reset.
@kbd{q} returns to the originating view@comma{} offering to retain or discard unsaved work.

@emph{Physical voice} searches engine or voice names. Audition with working adjustments
before accepting; cancellation keeps the earlier choice and returns to the field.
@emph{Fallbacks} allows replacement@comma{} reordering@comma{} removal@comma{} individual auditions@comma{}
appending@comma{} or returning to Automatic. Unavailable@comma{} engine-default@comma{} and property
choices are retained. Shared adjustments supply inherited fields;
@emph{Customize this voice} provides optional per-choice settings.

@subsection Tune through the focused menu

From @kbd{C-e H v p}@comma{} enter a palette with @kbd{@key{RET}}@comma{} select a voice and press @kbd{e}
to open its editor. Press @kbd{m} there for focused tuning. This menu edits the
same retained draft. Up/down
selects controls@comma{} left/right changes sections@comma{} and Shift-left/right adjusts
relative rate@comma{} pitch@comma{} pitch range@comma{} or gain by one unit. @kbd{@key{RET}} enters an exact
value. @kbd{d} restores the selected adjustment to adapter default@comma{} or asks which
one to reset. Zero remains explicit.

@kbd{f} selects an existing fallback@comma{} @kbd{h} returns to shared settings@comma{} and the title
identifies scope. For a fallback@comma{} @kbd{i} inherits the shared value and @kbd{d} requests
adapter default. Play and Compare use the selected scope.

@kbd{P} previews@comma{} @kbd{B} compares original/edited with one sample@comma{} @kbd{S} stops@comma{} and @kbd{T}
changes text. These previews are private and exclude contextual rules.
Adjustments speak values without automatic samples here; the full editor's
automatic-sample preference is retained. @kbd{u} undoes the last draft change.

@kbd{e} opens engine-specific settings@comma{} waiting for a qualified runtime catalogue
before permitting edits. Its @kbd{q} returns to the common editor; @kbd{m} reopens
focused tuning on the same draft and scope. Physical experiments@comma{} individual
fallbacks@comma{} and engine controls remain available in the full editor.

@kbd{w} saves and applies; @kbd{c} saves to the collection without selecting. Existing
destination prompts@comma{} inheritance@comma{} and recovery remain. @kbd{q} stops playback and
returns to the editor with the draft. Leaving that editor still offers keep@comma{}
discard@comma{} or continue choices.

@subsection Save the draft and check application

@anchor{voice-saving-procedure}

@kbd{w} or @kbd{C-c C-c} saves and applies a complete named-voice draft. Save buttons
follow settings@comma{} previews@comma{} and change details. @emph{Save to collection without
selecting} saves an inactive palette without changing selection. The header
separates unsaved changes@comma{} partial saves@comma{} applying@comma{} failures@comma{} and saved/applied.

Retry keeps the proposal and records which saving steps already succeeded.
If you make more edits while an earlier Apply is still waiting for a reply@comma{}
that reply does not save the later edits. They remain unsaved. If a file or
destination has changed@comma{} review the proposal again rather than expecting the
editor to merge those changes automatically.

Complete-choice previews require Omnivox's complete-voice-preview capability.
They do not select the draft or change live settings. Older hosts report this
unsupported; individual physical auditions remain available. Playback evidence
identifies accepted audio when reported@comma{} including unsupported adjustments@comma{}
without inferring a successful fallback from inventory or claiming exact native
parameter measurements.

@kbd{q} returns to the originating row and column. Keep@comma{} discard@comma{} or continue with
unsaved work. Retained drafts can be resumed through @kbd{C-e H p d} during
this Emacs session only. They do not survive restart@comma{} and leaving does not
undo a completed save.

@emph{Save and apply} selects the palette for this session. @kbd{U}@comma{} @emph{Use at Startup}
in Voice Palettes@comma{} retains its startup selection separately. Saving can involve
multiple files; partial failures retain the proposal for retry. See
@ref{Recovering Aural Changes,Recovering Unfinished Changes,,emacsvox,}. Physical experiments first request a
destination; see @ref{Keeping A Voice Experiment,Keep an Installed Voice Experiment,,emacsvox,}.

@node Changing A Palette Physical Voice
@subsection Use one physical voice across a palette

Select a physical voice in @emph{Browse voices} or eSpeak @emph{Voice variants} and press
@kbd{V}. Choose an existing personal palette or create one. Creation copies entries@comma{}
adjustments@comma{} and fallbacks from your chosen source. All effective names@comma{}
including @code{default} and inherited entries@comma{} are reviewed.

The review starts at @code{default}. Up/down selects rows@comma{} @kbd{O} plays original@comma{}
@kbd{P} proposed@comma{} @kbd{B} compares both@comma{} and @kbd{A} compares representative voices.
@kbd{S} stops; @kbd{x} details descendants@comma{} retained fallbacks@comma{} and playback results.
An unavailable selected physical voice may cause fallback.

If an entry already contains the exact physical choice@comma{} the review moves
that choice to the front and preserves its tuning. When several choices match@comma{}
only the first moves; the others keep their order. A newly added choice follows
that entry's shared adjustments@comma{} while existing choices keep their individual
tuning. Saving makes inherited entries complete local entries.

This review changes the entries once. Later edits are independent@comma{} and names
inherited in the future do not automatically acquire the chosen physical voice.

@kbd{w} saves the review and applies the palette to both streams; @kbd{c} saves without
selection or application. Startup is separate. @kbd{C-c C-k} cancels an unsaved
review without writing; @kbd{q} returns to the browser but retains it. Its actions
menu offers @emph{Resume palette voice review}.

Use @kbd{r} to resolve other unsaved voice editors before continuing@comma{} then
refresh this review with @kbd{g}. Refresh it after saved inputs change as well.
If only some saving steps succeed@comma{} the original proposal remains available
for retry with the same action.

A failure on one speech stream does not undo a successful palette save.
Retry and inspect the result for each stream. Selecting another palette ends
that pending retry; to use the saved palette later@comma{} select it deliberately
through Voice Palettes. @emph{Close saved result} closes a completed review without
undoing its data or changing the selection.

@node Voice Adjustments
@subsection Understand Voice Adjustments

Change one field at a time and compare with @kbd{B}. Portable controls are not
measurements in Hertz or decibels@comma{} and equal values may sound different on
different engines. Most numeric fields use integer levels zero through nine;
Relative Rate is the exception.

@itemize
@item
@emph{Relative Rate} ranges from -20 to 20. Negative requests slower speech@comma{}
positive faster@comma{} and zero leaves base rate unchanged. Engine-relative steps
are not words per minute. A stronger contextual value replaces rather than
adds to this one.
@item
@emph{Average Pitch} requests lower or higher overall pitch. Start with a small
contrast between voices for related content.
@item
@emph{Pitch Range} requests less or more pitch variation; supported low settings
can sound monotone and higher settings more varied.
@item
@emph{Stress} requests emphasis according to the engine's implementation.
@item
@emph{Richness} changes tonal character or fullness where supported. Compare
samples rather than treating it as volume.
@item
@emph{Gain} changes output level: five is unchanged@comma{} lower reduces@comma{} higher raises.
@item
@emph{Pan} requests left at zero@comma{} centre at five@comma{} and right at nine where supported;
output arrangement also matters.
@item
@emph{Low-pass Amount} and @emph{High-pass Amount} remove high and low frequencies@comma{}
respectively. Zero is neutral and nine strongest.
@item
@emph{Reverb} adds reverberation@comma{} @emph{Echo} delayed repeats@comma{} and @emph{Chorus} a doubled
voice. Zero disables each; higher values increase it.
@item
@emph{Portable Fallback Family}@comma{} where offered@comma{} is a compatibility hint. Use
@emph{Physical voice} for an installed choice. Family is outside complete Omnivox
voice preview.
@end itemize

In shared settings@comma{} @kbd{d} or blank input requests @emph{adapter default} and @kbd{u} undoes
it. For a physical choice@comma{} @kbd{i} means @emph{use the shared value}. Zero is explicit
in either context. The standard-default palette chooses the collection@comma{}
Automatic delegates physical selection@comma{} and adapter default delegates one
adjustment: they answer different questions.

Filter amount scales apply to arrows and numeric input in the common editor.
Zero is the neutral cutoff; blank requests adapter default. Existing saved
styles retain their sound and stored low-pass cutoff values keep their original
meaning.

Neutral values are named: gain five @emph{unchanged}@comma{} pan five @emph{centre}@comma{} and zero
reverb@comma{} echo@comma{} or chorus @emph{disabled}. Chorus adds bounded doubled-voice modulation
without changing speech duration or marker positions. Omnivox applies it to
buffered audio and reports unsupported routes. Portable levels are translated
into adapter-specific values.

The standard palette includes @code{telephone}@comma{} combining high-pass and low-pass
filtering for a narrow-band sound on buffered Omnivox engines without replacing
the physical voice or changing duration/markers. Preview it in Voice Palettes
or Workbench before assignment. Adapters without post-synthesis filtering keep
the style but cannot apply those filters.

For overall Omnivox pitch contrast (@code{omnivox-average-pitch-contrast})@comma{} open
@kbd{C-e H v o} and select @emph{Omnivox pitch contrast}. Shift-left/right adjusts by @code{0.1}; @kbd{@key{RET}} enters an exact
value from zero to two. The default @code{0.5} gives gentler distinctions@comma{} @code{1.0}
restores historical contrast@comma{} and zero neutralises average pitch. This scales
all average-pitch levels without changing individual named-voice definitions;
other backends are unaffected.

Changes refresh logical-voice registration for subsequent previews@comma{} structured
speech and legacy inline voices. Compare familiar content@comma{} then restore the
previous value if needed. The menu's @kbd{w} saves both contrast and comparison
labels through Customize; it does not save a voice or palette.

@node Voice Fallbacks
@subsection Choose And Tune Fallback Voices

Shared settings provide a base for the whole voice. Physical choices are tried
in displayed order@comma{} subject to availability@comma{} engine policy@comma{} and synthesis
failure. An empty explicit list means @emph{Automatic}@comma{} using shared engine policy.
Availability predicts a possibility; the last sample's result identifies what
played.

Palette inheritance supplies the whole entry. Within that entry@comma{} each
physical choice can either inherit a shared adjustment or supply its own.
For example@comma{} suppose shared pitch is six@comma{} this physical choice specifies four@comma{}
and a matching contextual rule requests seven. The resulting request is seven
because the rule overrides the choice. Without that rule it is four. Selecting
@emph{Use shared value} for the choice then makes it six.

Expand @emph{Fallbacks} to distinguish rows using shared settings from customised
ones. A row's menu auditions or compares it; comparison follows the same row
after reordering@comma{} even where two rows use the same physical voice. A newly
added row has no original yet: audition it or compare the whole voice. Replacing
a customised physical choice asks whether to keep tuning or return to shared.

@emph{Customize this voice} opens individual tuning. Each field distinguishes shared@comma{}
custom@comma{} and explicit adapter default. @kbd{@key{RET}} offers all three@comma{} left/right sets
numbers@comma{} @kbd{d} requests adapter default@comma{} and @kbd{i} inherits. Zero remains explicit.
Later shared changes affect inherited fields only. @emph{More adjustments and effects}
reveals additional controls. Automatic samples name the value/state and audition
that row; @emph{Play whole fallback chain} tests normal selection. @emph{Return to shared
settings} leaves individual tuning within the same draft and Save operation.
Removing a row disables its controls until undo or return to shared settings.

With per-choice support@comma{} complete previews retain the chain and each row's
tuning. @emph{Last sample playback started on …} identifies the engine/voice whose
audio started. Accepted audio or spoken @emph{Original} and @emph{Edited} labels do not
prove a start. Stop@comma{} text changes@comma{} draft changes@comma{} or context changes retire
pending previews; retained evidence is labelled @emph{Earlier preview}. Saving and
application status are separate from playback evidence.

@emph{Details and last playback evidence} explains shared@comma{} row@comma{} and contextual
contributions to the confirmed sample. The context view offers @emph{Where the last
sample's settings came from}. These explanations use captured settings despite
later edits@comma{} identify original versus edited@comma{} and distinguish inherited values@comma{}
defaults@comma{} zero@comma{} and unsupported requests. They describe requested settings@comma{}
not measured native parameters or sound.

Older servers provide shared-only previews. Customised complete previews need
per-choice support; individual auditions are available only if tuning can be
represented faithfully@comma{} including defaults. Saving remains possible when a
preview is unsupported.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Work with engine-specific parameters}Work with engine-specific parameters


@emph{Engine-specific settings} opens parameters described by the selected worker
within the same voice draft. A worker that can describe but not apply them
shows them read-only. Engine-wide and startup controls also remain read-only
here@comma{} with scope identified by Help.

Actions precede expandable parameter groups with value@comma{} source@comma{} and range.
@kbd{@key{RET}} offers @emph{Set value}@comma{} @emph{Follow common settings}@comma{} @emph{Engine voice default}@comma{}
and @emph{Parameter help}. Units and limits belong to the engine. Help explains
default source@comma{} relevant common controls@comma{} and reported side effects. @kbd{?} opens
and reads parameter Help; @kbd{q} returns to the row. Left/right steps a set numeric
value within limits. For inherited or unknown defaults@comma{} first choose a value
with @kbd{@key{RET}}; the editor does not guess current native state.

@kbd{i} removes an override@comma{} @kbd{d} requests engine voice default@comma{} and @emph{Reset
engine-specific settings} removes native overrides without altering common
adjustments or other choices. @kbd{u} undoes these edits.

Automatic samples name the parameter/value and share the common editor's
@emph{Automatic sample after adjustment} preference. Opening@comma{} searching@comma{} refreshing@comma{}
and Help do not audition. @kbd{P} previews@comma{} @kbd{B} compares@comma{} and the normal Save keeps
the draft. @kbd{q} returns to common settings. @emph{Search controls} searches names@comma{}
identifiers@comma{} and Help across folded groups; empty input clears the filter.
@emph{Adjusted controls only} shows overrides. @kbd{g} refreshes without restarting;
a busy engine is retried quietly for up to five seconds. Leaving cancels retry.
If still busy@comma{} finish speech and retry @kbd{g}. Unrecognised saved fields stay
visible for explicit removal.

@emph{Explain planned settings} describes the captured draft without speech playback.
Where supported@comma{} last-playback details also explain applied sample settings.
Planned@comma{} applied@comma{} and engine-read-back values are labelled separately. An expired
sample plan cannot be reconstructed by these queries.
@end enumerate

@node Voices In Context
@subsection Inspect Or Adjust A Voice In Context

In the voice editor@comma{} choose @emph{Effective sound / Where settings came from}.
It uses the attached source item captured through the entry route or voice list.
If none is attached@comma{} choose an ordinary buffer at its current point. The item
must provide inspectable facts or valid retained feedback. @emph{Choose another
source context} deliberately selects a replacement.

This simulates captured facts and rules@comma{} showing the selected voice@comma{} matching
rules@comma{} requested fields@comma{} and their sources. One rule can set pitch and another
richness. A stronger pitch rule can mask a base edit; a rule choosing a different
named voice can make the open entry irrelevant. Competing relative rates also
replace@comma{} not add.

@kbd{P} previews the edited voice in context and @kbd{B} compares original/edited with
the same facts@comma{} rules@comma{} and text. These voice-only previews omit cues and spatial
placement. Family remains a portable fallback outside full preview. Per-choice
support applies the actual fallback's tuning before context@comma{} and an explicit
context value overrides even when numerically equal to shared tuning. Requested
values remain distinct from confirmed starts@comma{} accepted audio@comma{} and unsupported
adjustments. Changed/deleted sources are rejected. After saving a rule@comma{} refresh
facts and rules from the source to capture its contribution. @kbd{q} returns to
the base editor.

@emph{Adjust for this context} presents matching criteria and personal@comma{} session@comma{} or
buffer lifetime. Choose one adjustment and value to prepare the ordinary rule
editor. A richness-only change does not select a different voice. Review criteria
with @kbd{@key{RET}}@comma{} use @kbd{V} for another voice field@comma{} and save the chosen scope with
@kbd{w}. The palette is unchanged. Finish existing rule-editor work before preparing
another contextual rule.

@emph{Inherit} removes a field so weaker contributions can supply it; removing the
last voice field removes the patch. Existing explicit defaults are shown and
preserved when another field is edited. Zero is not inheritance. Some older
default forms cannot reset underlying tuning on every backend; inspect preview
and explanation. Context changes are saved as rules@comma{} not as a palette entry
containing the combined result. The
@ref{Integration Maintenance,Developer Guide,,emacsvox,} describes representation and compatibility.

@node Emoji Speech Names
@section Name Occasional Emoji In Speech

An emoji can require fallback from a voice such as Eloquence. Naming transforms
speech text before engine selection: @code{Forecast 🔮} becomes @code{Forecast crystal
ball}@comma{} while source text stays intact. This can keep the preferred voice when
the remaining text is compatible; normal repertoire checks still decide.

Naming is enabled by default. Use @kbd{M-x customize-group @key{RET} emacsvox-emoji @key{RET}}.
Disable @code{emacsvox-emoji-naming-enabled} globally or only in a source buffer:

@lisp
(setq-local emacsvox-emoji-naming-enabled nil)
@end lisp

For Org buffers in your configuration:

@lisp
(add-hook 'org-mode-hook
          (lambda () (setq-local emacsvox-emoji-naming-enabled nil)))
@end lisp

All four options support buffer-local values@comma{} which override global defaults.
Aural previews use the remembered source policy@comma{} and contextual previews its
captured policy. Changes during playback do not change an already submitted
sample.

@code{emacsvox-emoji-approved-sequences} defaults to @code{t} for every exact name in
Emacs's emoji table@comma{} including seedling @code{🌱}@comma{} flags@comma{} keycaps@comma{} and joined emoji.
You need not approve each separately; an explicit list restricts eligible
complete sequences. Unknown joined sequences remain intact even if a component
is named.

Arrows and harpoons also receive names in all directions@comma{} including diagonal@comma{}
double@comma{} and curved forms. @code{← ↑ → ↓} becomes @emph{left arrow up arrow right arrow
down arrow}. Symbols such as @code{⇒} and @code{⟶} outside the emoji table use Unicode
names. Text/emoji selectors are supported; unknown joined or accented sequences
remain unchanged. Box-drawing characters use Unicode names too: @code{│} becomes
@emph{box drawings light vertical}. Light@comma{} heavy@comma{} double@comma{} rounded@comma{} dashed@comma{} and
junction forms follow the same naming settings and limits; custom names can
shorten frequent ones. Foreign-language letters and unrelated symbols retain
normal fallback@comma{} and exact character inspection is unchanged.

@code{emacsvox-emoji-maximum-count} defaults to 64 occurrences@comma{} counting repetition.
@code{🤔Dinner? 🍕🍕🍕} becomes @emph{thinking face Dinner? pizza pizza pizza} without
fallback caused by those four emoji. Above the limit@comma{} naming is skipped for
the whole item. One captured presentation can contain several voice runs;
a standalone preview is one item. Transport chunk sizes do not change this
limit. Sequences crossing style or positioned-cue boundaries remain unchanged.

Names come from Emacs's bundled emoji/Unicode data and may vary with its version.
Arrows prefer emoji names where available; the four plain arrows share their
emoji equivalents' names. Missing data leaves the sequence to normal fallback;
nothing is downloaded. Names are English and apply even to engines capable of
speaking the original.

Explicit pronunciation entries take precedence. For eligible sequences@comma{}
@code{emacsvox-emoji-custom-names} overrides a bundled name:

@lisp
(setq-local emacsvox-emoji-custom-names '(("🔮" . "prediction")))
@end lisp

Custom names must contain 1–80 characters with no control characters. Empty
names are invalid; use explicit pronunciation to omit content. Restriction and
custom-name lists allow at most 64 entries@comma{} with sequences up to 32 characters;
full-table mode has no entry limit. The occurrence limit accepts 0–64.
Invalid settings skip automatic naming with an explanation rather than stop
speech.

Feedback Details (@kbd{C-e E}) shows original sequences and names or reasons for
skipping. Voice-editor playback details report sample substitutions. These are
prepared-text evidence@comma{} not confirmation of what you heard. @emph{Play original}
retains captured speech text after settings change. Rule proposals change
presentation while retaining that text; capture new source feedback to try
another naming policy.

@node Installed Voices And Engines
@section Browse Installed Voices And Choose Engines

@cindex Voice Workbench
@cindex physical voices
@cindex speech engine preference

Open @kbd{C-e H v} for @emph{Voices and engines}. @kbd{b} browses engines and voices@comma{} @kbd{p}
palettes@comma{} and @kbd{w} resumes Voice Workbench. Browser @kbd{q} commands unwind child
views and return to the originating menu item. Filters@comma{} selection@comma{} and staged
work survive; unsaved engine-policy changes are still announced. A directly
opened browser retains its ordinary return destination.

The main Workbench views are:

@table @kbd
@item e
Engines@comma{} for trying voices or inspecting availability.
@item v
Physical voices@comma{} including engine voices and Omnivox downloads; on an engine row@comma{} restrict to that engine.
@item l
Named voices@comma{} labelled Logical voices@comma{} such as bolden and annotate.
@item s
Styles and effects for those entries@comma{} including supported parameters.
@end table

From the engine browser at @kbd{C-e H v b}@comma{} @kbd{v} opens voices. @kbd{@key{RET}} on an Omnivox
engine opens status@comma{} downloads@comma{} and settings; other adapters use it to browse
voices directly. @kbd{@key{RET}} or @kbd{P} on a voice auditions@comma{} @kbd{x} shows details@comma{} and @kbd{t}
opens an experiment. @kbd{q} returns through the engine's details and list as
appropriate before leaving Workbench.

Filters set with @kbd{F} and each view's row/column survive navigation through
settings menus. The browsed engine is a temporary limit@comma{} removed when switching
to logical@comma{} style@comma{} or engine views@comma{} or pressing @kbd{v} from a voice list. Explicit
filters remain; @kbd{C} clears them to show voices across engines. Direct view
shortcuts remain available.

@menu
* Experiment With An Installed Voice: Physical Voice Experiments.  Try physical voices without saving, then keep a useful experiment.
* Walkthrough Keep An Installed Voice Experiment: Keeping A Voice Experiment.  Place an experiment in a named voice and verify the saved combination.
* Set Shared Engine Preferences: Speech Engine Preferences.  Choose engine order and distinguish it from explicit palette choices.
@end menu

@subsection Find and filter installed voices

A language with one voice shows it directly; several voices form an expandable
heading with count and state. @kbd{@key{RET}} toggles it. Initial sorting ignores
capitalisation. Regions form a tree@comma{} for example English then United Kingdom
then English (West Midlands). Redundant single-child levels are omitted;
private language-tag codes do not create extra headings. Unknown codes stay
visible and missing language is grouped as @emph{Unknown language}.

A one-language list starts expanded. Each engine scope remembers folds;
refresh preserves voice and column as library data arrives. Sorting keeps
headings with their voices. @kbd{A} samples usable visible voices@comma{} including
single-voice languages@comma{} in display order. Expand a group to include hidden
voices.

@kbd{/} searches names across collapsed languages and disabled downloads within
the current engine and explicit filters. Choosing expands its language and
selects it. A hiding quick filter switches to @emph{All voices}. Search neither
plays a sample nor changes enablement.

Omnivox quick filters are available through @kbd{Q} with counts@comma{} or directly:

@table @asis
@item 1
All reported engine and installed library voices.
@item 2
Catalogue downloads@comma{} including disabled ones; imports and built-ins remain in All voices.
@item 3
Library voices enabled for the next Apply.
@item 4
Needs Apply: desired enablement differs from either worker's eligibility. Unknown or stale worker evidence cannot establish a pending change and is counted separately.
@end table

Counts include collapsed groups and respect engine/explicit filters. Each
quick filter remembers row/column. After Apply empties @emph{Needs Apply}@comma{} @kbd{2} returns
to downloads for previewing or editing. Engine overrides may prevent confirmation;
@emph{Active} and engine details still report health and usability. @kbd{C} clears the
quick filter as well.

@subsection Distinguish installation@comma{} enablement@comma{} and active speech

A voice can be installed without being ready to speak. @emph{Installed} means
it belongs to the library or is supplied by an engine. @emph{Enabled} records
whether it should be available after the next Apply. @emph{Active} identifies
eligibility on the main stream@comma{} notification stream or both; it does not say
that a model is currently resident in memory.

Library information arrives asynchronously. When worker evidence is missing
or inconsistent@comma{} the view reports @emph{Unknown} rather than guessing whether the
voice is active.

@kbd{+} enables/disables a managed voice@comma{} @kbd{a} reviews Apply@comma{} and @kbd{d} opens downloads.
Downloads start disabled; enable and Apply before auditioning. Engine-managed
voices retain their own selection mechanism. Other adapters retain their
physical-inventory view.

To remove a downloaded Piper@comma{} Flite@comma{} or MBROLA package@comma{} disable its voices@comma{}
Apply@comma{} then press @kbd{C-c u} on one voice. Review all speakers sharing it@comma{} size@comma{}
known palette references@comma{} and retained-file reasons. Confirmation removes only
reviewed managed files. Built-in voices@comma{} imports@comma{} and runtimes remain@comma{} as do
saved palette choices for a later redownload. The backend guide describes
supported managed-engine download and removal boundaries in more detail.

Another session may still need the package; files remain until retirement is
confirmed@comma{} and older session records may need inspection. Results distinguish
removed bytes@comma{} remaining files@comma{} and unconfirmed amounts. @kbd{C-c U} or
@code{omnivox-library-resume-uninstall} reviews interrupted cleanup on hosts supporting
uninstallation.

Check an engine's @emph{Availability} before changing its routing policy. A
missing runtime remains listed as unavailable@comma{} with @emph{Last failure} explaining
the problem. @emph{Routing policy: allowed} permits selection; it does not install
the runtime. An engine being disabled@comma{} unhealthy or temporarily failed are
also distinct conditions.

@subsection Compare samples and interpret the result

@kbd{A} auditions available matching voices in displayed order@comma{} skipping unavailable
rows. @kbd{B} uses the selected voice as A and asks for B; @emph{Search all engines}
can compare outside current filters. Both use one sentence. @kbd{T} edits it@comma{}
@kbd{F} filters@comma{} @kbd{C} clears filters@comma{} and @kbd{R} refreshes inventory.

Use @kbd{C-e H v o l} to choose comparison labels: engine and voice names@comma{}
letters or sequence numbers@comma{} or no labels. The option is
@code{emacsvox-aural-preview-label-verbosity}. The list starts on the current
choice. Arrows browse without applying; @kbd{@key{RET}} applies and returns@comma{} while @kbd{q}
cancels. This global option changes labels@comma{} leaving sample text and order
intact. With no labels@comma{} the sequence no longer identifies its voices aloud.
Press @kbd{w} in Voice presentation to save labels and pitch contrast through
Customize.

Labels and samples form one sequence: @kbd{S} or foreground navigation cancels
remaining playback. Exact-preview failure is reported rather than accepting
a substitute as the requested voice. Capabilities describe adapter claims@comma{}
not a listening test of every parameter.

@node Physical Voice Experiments
@subsection Experiment With An Installed Voice

@kbd{t} on a physical voice opens an experiment with that exact engine/voice@comma{}
adapter-default characteristics@comma{} current base rate@comma{} and neutral audio effects.
Opening@comma{} adjusting@comma{} and comparing save nothing. Controls match
@ref{Editing Named Voices,Edit a Named Voice,,emacsvox,}@comma{} but @kbd{w} must first choose a palette destination.

To compare pitch:

@enumerate
@item
Open @kbd{C-e H v b}@comma{} select an available engine@comma{} and press @kbd{v}. Select a
physical voice and press @kbd{P}; @kbd{B} can compare with another available voice
using the same sentence.
@item
Press @kbd{t}@comma{} set @emph{Average Pitch} to @code{3}@comma{} listen@comma{} then set @code{7} and listen
again. An unsupported pitch control remains identified.
@item
@kbd{B} compares the captured starting voice with the edited one@comma{} not the
immediately previous change. @kbd{u} undoes; @kbd{d} restores adapter default.
@item
Finish with @kbd{q} and discard@comma{} or keep the experiment using the next walkthrough.
@end enumerate

@kbd{w} chooses a destination palette and entry. The default keeps @emph{Physical voice
and adjustments — this choice}. Alternatives keep only the physical voice@comma{}
only one row's adjustments@comma{} or shared settings for inheriting choices. Physical
voices can replace a row@comma{} become preferred@comma{} or append as fallback. A row-level
keep replaces that row's tuning with the experiment's values and defaults@comma{}
preventing unrelated destination shared tuning from changing the base sound.
Use @kbd{i} afterward for fields intended to inherit.

Other rows/shared settings survive a row-level keep. Replacing only the
physical voice asks whether to keep/reset its tuning. Shared adjustment keeps
affect inherited fields; custom rows override them. An existing destination
draft can accept the changes or resume untouched@comma{} retaining other work and
pending save recovery. The destination preview uses the proposed combination@comma{}
which can differ because of scope/fallback order. Choosing it has not saved.

@node Keeping A Voice Experiment
@subsection Walkthrough: Keep An Installed Voice Experiment

Create @code{reading-demo} as in @ref{Personal Palette Walkthrough,Make a personal reading palette,,emacsvox,}@comma{} or deliberately
choose an existing personal destination. Note the active palette and whether
selection follows the standard baseline or is explicit. Saving into an
existing destination changes that resource; it is not preview-only practice.

@enumerate
@item
In the voice browser@comma{} open @kbd{t}@comma{} adjust a field@comma{} and preview with @kbd{P~/~B}.
Press @kbd{w} to keep it.
@item
Select the personal destination and named voice @code{bolden}. Choose @emph{Physical
voice and adjustments — this choice}@comma{} then @emph{Add as preferred}. This places
the experiment first while retaining alternatives.
@item
The common editor opens an unsaved proposal. Expand @emph{Fallbacks}: the new
first choice is customised@comma{} preceding previous explicit choices. @kbd{P} plays
the whole proposed voice; inspect its actual reported engine/voice because
an unavailable preference may fall back.
@item
@kbd{w} saves and applies. Wait for its result and follow
@ref{Recovering Aural Changes,Recovering Unfinished Changes,,emacsvox,} after partial saving or failed application.
Success selects the destination for this session. Use @kbd{U} in Voice
Palettes only to choose it for startup too.
@item
Before saving@comma{} undo or discard to reverse. After saving@comma{} reopen the voice@comma{}
expand fallbacks@comma{} choose @emph{Remove} in the added row's @kbd{@key{RET}} action menu@comma{}
preview@comma{} and save. Other choices and shared tuning remain. To restore
session selection@comma{} use @kbd{f} in Voice Palettes if you originally followed
the standard baseline; otherwise activate the recorded explicit palette.
@end enumerate

For independent fallback tuning@comma{} open its menu and @emph{Customize this voice}.
Change one field@comma{} audition that row@comma{} then test @emph{Play whole fallback chain}.
The custom field overrides shared tuning only for that choice. @kbd{i} restores
inheritance; reordering carries adjustments with the row.

@node Speech Engine Preferences
@subsection Set Shared Engine Preferences

Engine preference is global@comma{} not buffer-local. In @kbd{C-e H v}@comma{} @kbd{e} chooses a
session preference@comma{} @kbd{S} chooses and saves one@comma{} and @kbd{r} restores saved or initial
order. Availability and unsaved-policy checks remain. The speech-settings
scope does not change these saving rules.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Edit Saved Engine Configuration}Edit Saved Engine Configuration


From engine details choose @emph{Engine configuration}@comma{} or run
@kbd{M-x omnivox-engine-configuration}. This needs an updated local Omnivox host.
Files belong to the speech host@comma{} including Windows under WSL; entered paths
must make sense there.

@kbd{@key{RET}} edits a row: enabled engines@comma{} preferred/backup order@comma{} or automatic-selection
permission for a helper. @emph{Advanced startup settings} exposes literal arguments
and wait limits. @kbd{l} changes engines while retaining drafts. @emph{Add installed
helper} registers a compatible program disabled; it neither downloads nor
executes it. Enable@comma{} Save@comma{} then Apply to check runtime support.

@kbd{s} saves without changing speech. A file conflict retains the draft for fresh
review. @kbd{g} rereads saved settings@comma{} asking before discarding. @kbd{q} returns while
retaining the draft. Saved/draft states are distinct from worker availability@comma{}
and explicit environment overrides remain visible.

@kbd{A} reviews and applies saved engine/library settings to both workers through
preflight and rollback. Save all edits first. @emph{Check saved settings} validates
files only. @kbd{P} opens live voices in Workbench@comma{} so saved changes are audible
there only after successful Apply.

@item
@anchor{Choose The Shared Preferred Engine}Choose The Shared Preferred Engine


@kbd{C-e d e} chooses an available engine for this session; @kbd{C-u C-e d e} saves
the preference. With no saved policy@comma{} an ordinary first invocation uses live
Omnivox policy as an in-memory baseline without writing. The prefix saves that
baseline and preference. Explicit named-voice physical choices outrank it.

Workbench policy is shared across palettes. @kbd{O} toggles preferred engines and
@kbd{[~/~]} reorders them; @kbd{f} toggles fallbacks and @kbd{@{~/}@}~ reorders them. @kbd{D}
enables/disables and @kbd{K} requests a recovery probe. These are staged changes;
@kbd{w} saves and applies. Physical choices for an individual entry are saved in
its voice editor.

Names appear first in tables; @kbd{x} details capabilities@comma{} native IDs@comma{} and
registration. Availability@comma{} failure@comma{} predicted selection@comma{} and observed playback
are distinct. Saving engine policy is separate from voice or profile saving.
For unconfirmed application@comma{} see
@ref{Recovering Aural Changes,Recovering Unfinished Changes,,emacsvox,}. Installation belongs to
@ref{Optional Omnivox Engine Modules,Optional Omnivox Engines,,emacsvox,}.
@end enumerate

@node Sounds And Space
@section Sounds@comma{} Tones@comma{} And Spatial Cues

@cindex sound packs
@cindex spatial presentation

Rules request cues by semantic name. Sound packs map names to audio assets
and can inherit missing cues. This lets you change the sound of a family of
cues without rewriting rules. A recording library holds available sounds;
assigning one to a cue changes its sound in that pack. To change when the cue
plays@comma{} use its feedback rule.

To enable or disable earcons in the captured buffer@comma{} use @kbd{C-e H r l e}.
In that submenu@comma{} @kbd{E} changes the global default for buffers without a local
override; @kbd{i} removes the source buffer's override so it follows that default.
Speech remains available when earcons are off@comma{} but menu cues also disappear
where the off setting applies. These switches last for the session or buffer
and have no Customize Save action. Toggle again or restore inheritance to
reverse an experiment.

@kbd{C-e H o p} opens @emph{Sound Packs}. Its @kbd{q} returns to @emph{Sounds and output} with
the same settings scope@comma{} and another @kbd{q} returns to the main menu. Directly
opened browsers keep their usual returns.

@table @kbd
@item P
Audition a representative cue without activating the pack.
@item RET
Browse individual cues and their source or fallback.
@item P in the cue browser
Audition the selected concrete cue; RET shows details.
@item v
Validate resources and report missing/invalid entries.
@item a in the pack manager
Activate the selected pack.
@item a in the cue browser
Compare library recordings for an explicit assignment.
@end table

Spatial requests describe placement portably@comma{} not a particular device.
@kbd{C-e H D s} reports capabilities and fallbacks; @kbd{C-e H o n} controls output@comma{}
separation@comma{} direction@comma{} speech@comma{} and cues. Unsupported or mono output retains
audible content at the centre. Named tones are separate compact resources@comma{}
independent of sampled files and the active pack.

For waveform@comma{} pitch sweep and envelope adjustments@comma{} use the
@ref{spelling-tone-editor, , shared tone editor}. Spelling@comma{} capitalisation beeps and rising-pitch
indentation each open it from their own menu@comma{} as do the five line-condition
cues. The waveform and envelope controls are shared; scope and saving belong
to the destination. General personal-rule editing retains its own controls.

@menu
* Walkthrough Try A Sound Pack: Sound Pack Walkthrough.  Preview, activate, retain, and restore a sound pack.
* Walkthrough Silence One Cue: Changing One Sound.  Suppress a cue while retaining speech and restore it afterward.
* Change A Blank-Line Tone: Changing Tones.  Change a recorded tone and distinguish it from a sampled cue.
* Adjust Spatial Presentation: Spatial Presentation Controls.  Check stereo support, adjust placement, and restore centered output.
* Inspect And Maintain A Personal Sound Pack: Personal Sound Packs.  Understand pack metadata, discovery, and the limits of the manager.
@end menu

@subsection Tune line and boundary cues from the menu

@kbd{C-e H o c} opens @emph{Line and boundary cues}. Blank@comma{} whitespace-only@comma{} separator@comma{}
decorated@comma{} and unspeakable-line tones have pitch and integer-duration controls.
Shift-left/right adjusts by 25 Hz or 5 milliseconds; @kbd{@key{RET}} enters exactly.
Their @kbd{E} opens the shared tone editor for volume@comma{} waveform@comma{} sweep and envelope.
Boundary/warning and large movement instead choose replacement earcons.
Changing those named earcons affects every use@comma{} including warnings elsewhere.

Each cue retains a global draft. @kbd{@key{SPC}} auditions it@comma{} @kbd{s} stops@comma{} @kbd{n} silences
in the draft@comma{} @kbd{r} restores bundled values@comma{} and @kbd{D} discards unapplied edits.
@kbd{A} applies a temporary session rule. @kbd{S} saves the conditional personal rule
and removes its temporary session override. Unrelated rules remain intact and
other presentation rules may override the result. To undo an applied change@comma{}
choose the desired values or bundled restoration@comma{} then Apply or Save again.

Inside the shared editor@comma{} @kbd{A} applies for the session and @kbd{W} saves the personal
rule. Preview leaves the parent's draft and live rules unchanged; @kbd{q} discards
unapplied editor adjustments and returns to the same @emph{Edit tone} control.
The parent menu's @kbd{q} retains its draft@comma{} including an applied change. Line cues
use the presentation-rule store because they match line conditions. These
controls change cue output@comma{} not classification of separators or decorated lines.

@node Sound Pack Walkthrough
@subsection Walkthrough: Try A Sound Pack

@enumerate
@item
Open @kbd{C-e H o p} and note the active pack. Select another of kind @emph{sound}
and press @kbd{P} to preview without activating.
@item
Enter with @kbd{@key{RET}} and inspect @emph{Availability} and @emph{Provider}. Cues may come
from the pack@comma{} parent@comma{} or registered fallback. @kbd{P} previews and @kbd{S} stops.
@item
Press @kbd{s} to return to the manager@comma{} then @kbd{v} to validate. Resolve missing
files or cues before relying on the pack. Activate with @kbd{a}@comma{} return through
the browser/menu@comma{} and repeat a familiar action such as entering a list.
@item
To retain the selection@comma{} open @kbd{C-e H p p} for profiles. @kbd{N} captures a new
setup and @kbd{w} deliberately updates the selected profile. Review all fields:
this saves more than the sound-pack selection. See
@ref{Presentation Profiles,Save@comma{} Switch@comma{} and Share Your Setup,,emacsvox,}.
@item
Restore the original pack by activating it. If the experiment was saved
in a profile@comma{} restore/update that profile too. Preview alone needs no undo.
@end enumerate

For a different recording on one cue@comma{} assign from the library below. To
suppress the cue or change when it plays@comma{} use its feedback rule.

@node Changing One Sound
@subsection Walkthrough: Silence One Cue

@enumerate
@item
Repeat the action producing the cue@comma{} then open @kbd{C-e H f h}. Select the
record and @kbd{@key{RET}} to explain it. Identify cue name and before/after phase.
@item
Return to the list and press @kbd{C} for @emph{Change this feedback}. Select the
relevant part if needed@comma{} then @kbd{c}@comma{} @emph{Suppress one component}@comma{} and the cue.
@item
Compare @kbd{O~/~P}: speech and other components should remain. Inspect criteria
with @kbd{m}@comma{} then choose @emph{This Emacs session} through @kbd{l}.
@item
Apply with @kbd{a} and repeat the source action. Matching feedback should omit
that cue; other identities or unmatched contexts remain unchanged.
@item
Open @kbd{C-e H r o}@comma{} find the session rule@comma{} and disable with @kbd{t} or delete
with @kbd{d}. It also expires when Emacs exits.
@end enumerate

To add a missing cue@comma{} choose @emph{Add a sound}@comma{} @emph{Before content} or @emph{After content}@comma{}
then a registered cue. Preview and review criteria/lifetime. Adding and later
removing use the same rule workflow. For an existing cue@comma{} @emph{Change sound timing}
offers playback before@comma{} alongside or after speech; see
@ref{Guided Feedback Changes,Make a guided feedback change,,emacsvox,}.

@node Changing Tones
@subsection Change A Blank-Line Tone

This changes a tone actually present in recorded feedback@comma{} not every visually
blank line regardless of its presentation.

@enumerate
@item
Navigate onto a blank line and open @kbd{C-e H f h}. Select its record@comma{} press
@kbd{C}@comma{} and choose a part if necessary.
@item
Press @kbd{c}@comma{} @emph{Change a tone}@comma{} select the tone@comma{} and choose @emph{Pitch and duration}.
Try @code{500} Hertz and @code{80} milliseconds. If it originally lay between other
components@comma{} choose @emph{Before the other feedback} when asked about placement.
Review that movement in the summary as well as pitch/duration.
@item
Compare @kbd{O~/~P}. Other content should remain. A named tone can be selected
instead of numeric values.
@item
Review criteria with @kbd{m}@comma{} choose session lifetime with @kbd{l}@comma{} then @kbd{a}.
Visit another matching blank line to hear the live result.
@item
Disable/delete the session rule through @kbd{C-e H r o} to restore the earlier
behaviour. It also expires when Emacs exits.
@end enumerate

If the record contains a sampled cue@comma{} @emph{Change a tone} is unavailable. Explain
it@comma{} then suppress that cue and add a tone with separate rules if appropriate;
remove both to restore. If no record exists@comma{} check the history limit and repeat
the action. The interface cannot infer a missing component from appearance.

@node Spatial Presentation Controls
@subsection Adjust Spatial Presentation

Open @kbd{C-e H D s} to inspect reported speech/cue capabilities@comma{} then return to
the menu. @kbd{C-e H o n} opens @emph{Notifications and spatial output}. Enable
presentation@comma{} speech@comma{} and cues independently. Output follows capabilities or
can be declared mono. Separation ranges from zero (centred) to one (full);
direction can be normal@comma{} reversed@comma{} or centred for some/all requests.

@kbd{o} opens the Output list and @kbd{m} opens Direction mapping. Each focuses and
marks the current choice. Arrows browse without applying; @kbd{@key{RET}} applies and
returns to the same parent control@comma{} ready to reopen. @kbd{q} or @kbd{C-g} cancels.
If direction mapping uses a custom function@comma{} its selected entry lets you keep
it; choosing a built-in mapping replaces it. Use Customize to edit the function.

These spatial controls apply immediately and are global. Shift-left/right
adjusts selected separation by 0.1; @kbd{@key{RET}} enters an exact value. @kbd{@key{SPC}} requests
left then right cue previews; mono or limited capabilities may centre them.
@kbd{x} stops. @kbd{S} saves spatial and routing defaults without restarting speech@comma{}
separately from output-volume saving.

For a gentler layout@comma{} note the current settings@comma{} select maximum separation@comma{}
and enter @code{0.3}. Repeat feedback that requests spatial placement. Stereo
headphones help compare it. If everything stays centred@comma{} Explain the source
to check its placement request and inspect backend capability. A sample without
a placement request cannot demonstrate separation.

For comparison@comma{} select mono and repeat the action; content remains audible.
Restore the original values afterward. Save deliberately with @kbd{S} or capture
spatial choices in a Presentation Profile. A voice's Pan effect and
notification-device routing are separate from presentation placement.

For a mapping beyond the built-in choices@comma{} use @kbd{M-x customize-option @key{RET}
emacsvox-aural-spatial-remapping @key{RET}} and select @emph{Custom remapping function}.
The Lisp function receives a balance from @code{-1.0} (fully left) through @code{0.0}
(centre) to @code{1.0} (fully right)@comma{} and returns a balance in the same range.
For example@comma{} @code{(lambda (balance) (* 0.5 balance))} brings each requested
position halfway towards the centre. Maximum separation then limits the
result; spatial enablement and output capability still apply. Use Customize's
Apply or Save actions deliberately. To return to built-in behaviour@comma{} choose
@emph{Normal} in Direction mapping and save if you also want to replace a saved
custom function.

@node Personal Sound Packs
@subsection Inspect And Maintain A Personal Sound Pack

In the manager@comma{} @kbd{o} opens the directory and @kbd{g} rescans. For a discovered
personal pack@comma{} @kbd{e} edits summary@comma{} parent@comma{} coverage profiles@comma{} and default
spatialisation. Built-ins are read-only. Editing metadata saves immediately;
this is not a retained voice-editor draft.

Coverage profiles declare cue sets for validation@comma{} not working Presentation
Profiles. Native assets belong to the pack; effective ones include inherited
and fallback cues. Validation and details explain those sources.

Use the cue browser's @kbd{a} to replace a cue from the recording library. Its
@emph{Availability} distinguishes explicit assignments from native files and
inherited sounds. Assigned recordings show their names in @code{Recording / file};
row speech includes the name and provider. The manager does not record or edit
audio@comma{} or create a pack through a wizard. For a new personal pack directory@comma{}
see @ref{Authoring Sound Packs,Authoring Sound Packs,,emacsvox,} in the Developer Guide.

@subsection Browse recordings and assign them to cues

Open @kbd{C-e H o l} for the recording library@comma{} or run @kbd{M-x emacsvox-sound-library}.
The default directory is @code{~/.emacsvox/sounds/library/}; change it through
@kbd{M-x customize-option @key{RET} emacsvox-sound-library-directory @key{RET}}. Put Ogg@comma{} WAV@comma{}
MP3 or FLAC files there@comma{} optionally in collection subdirectories@comma{} then press
@kbd{g} to rescan. @kbd{i} imports a copy into a named collection and leaves its source
intact. Adding files does not create cues or assign them to events.

Up and Down move between recordings and@comma{} by default@comma{} play each recording
alongside its row announcement. Left and Right read names@comma{} collections@comma{}
durations and known assignments without replaying the recording. @kbd{@key{SPC}} or @kbd{P}
replays the current recording and @kbd{S} stops.

To change automatic playback@comma{} open @emph{Library preview} with @kbd{C-e H o L}@comma{} or
choose it from the library's @kbd{C-c C-a} Actions menu. Choose @emph{Off}@comma{} @emph{Before
announcement}@comma{} @emph{Alongside announcement} or @emph{After announcement}. Arrows
explain choices without applying them; @kbd{@key{RET}} applies the selected choice to
all library buffers. @kbd{S} saves it across restarts and @kbd{q} returns. Off still
allows explicit replay with @kbd{@key{SPC}} or @kbd{P}. Before and After require Omnivox
timeline support: Before finishes the recording before speaking; After
finishes speaking before playing the recording. Moving again cancels the
previous sequence. Alongside also supports external audio players@comma{} which
control their own playback.

@kbd{/} filters names and collections; an empty search restores all rows.
@kbd{u} filters recordings without saved assignments; it also keeps pending
choices visible so you can review them. Duration is shown
when the optional @code{ffprobe} program is available. Playback still depends on
your audio backend. Identical file bytes appear once@comma{} even under several names.

To assign several sounds in a writable personal pack:

@enumerate
@item
Select a recording in the library and press @kbd{a} to choose the destination
pack and cue. This queues the recording without saving. Alternatively@comma{}
open @kbd{C-e H o p}@comma{} select the pack@comma{} then @kbd{@key{RET}} for its cues. Find the cue
and press @kbd{a} to open the library@comma{} then @kbd{@key{RET}} to queue a recording.
@item
The row shows and speaks @emph{Pending} with the pack and cue. @kbd{@key{SPC}} previews the
row; @kbd{C} plays the saved sound for the current cue. Browse and press @kbd{@key{RET}}
to replace that cue's pending choice. Saved cue assignments stay unchanged.
@item
To assign another cue@comma{} select another recording and press @kbd{a} again. Choose
the cue; the pack stays the same. You stay in the same buffer@comma{} and earlier
choices remain pending. Repeat for as many cues as needed. The header shows
the current cue and the number of pending changes.
@item
Press @kbd{W} to save all pending changes together. Their status becomes @emph{Saved}.
Speech confirms the number of assignments@comma{} the pack and whether it is active.
An active pack uses the saved sounds; an inactive pack stays inactive.
@item
Press @kbd{q} to return to the opening menu or cue row. This discards any changes
still pending and announces how many were discarded. Saved assignments stay
in place. Reopen the library to choose a different pack.
@end enumerate

A recording can serve several cues. Its row lists each pending and saved
assignment separately. Replacing one pending choice leaves the other cues'
choices intact. Until Save@comma{} an old recording still shows its saved assignment
because that is the sound currently used.

To restore inheritance@comma{} open that cue's assignment and press @kbd{R}. This queues
Reset to parent alongside any other pending changes. @kbd{W} saves them together@comma{}
skipping that cue's local assignment and revealing its parent or fallback while
retaining original audio files. Select and save another recording to replace
it again. If the pack or a candidate changes while pending@comma{} Save refuses the
stale edit and leaves the whole batch unsaved. Cancel@comma{} refresh and reopen it.

Saved assignments copy audio into the pack. Renaming or removing the library
source cannot break that copy. To move a pack@comma{} copy its whole directory and
retain its parent packs; the library is not required for playback. Older
filename-based packs continue to work. A saved assignment takes precedence
over their local file for that cue.

@kbd{o} opens the library directory for file management. @kbd{d} deletes the selected
library file after confirmation@comma{} refusing recordings assigned in known packs
or pending in the current buffer.
Assignment reporting covers configured packs@comma{} not copies elsewhere. Original
imports outside the library and audio copied into packs remain intact.

This browser does not upload audio to a remote speech host. Personal library
paths outside the configured workstation sound root cannot be previewed through
the remote resource route. Use local playback for those recordings; remote
resource installation remains a separate task.

@node Aural Speech Output
@section Speech Output And Notifications

These settings control reading detail and delivery. They do not replace the
rules choosing content or the palette shaping its voice. Start with rate and
punctuation for reading@comma{} routing for where notifications play@comma{} or volumes for
how loud the components are. Each control has its own scope and saving action;
a capital @kbd{S} in one menu does not save everything described in this section.

@menu
* Set the reading scope@comma{} rate@comma{} and punctuation: Reading Scope And Punctuation.  Set rate or punctuation for the current buffer and understand saving.
* Understand the main and notification streams: Notification Routing.  Choose where notifications play and distinguish selection from restart.
* Adjust output volumes: Output Volumes.  Adjust speech and cue levels without confusing volume with routing.
* Adjust an integration's speech settings: Integration Speech Settings.  Find focused controls and their integration-specific saving boundaries.
@end menu

@node Reading Scope And Punctuation
@subsection Set the reading scope@comma{} rate@comma{} and punctuation

@kbd{C-e H s} opens Speech settings for the captured buffer. @kbd{g} switches to global
defaults; @kbd{C-u C-e H} starts there. Indentation shares this scope with its
parents@comma{} including changes made inside its own menu. Opening/closing alone
saves nothing.

Select @emph{Rate} and adjust with Shift-left/right by the configured step@comma{} or use
@kbd{@key{RET}~/~r} for an exact value. Speech must be running; Omnivox uses 0–100.
Global rate changes also affect the current buffer. A named voice's relative rate
modifies this base as described in @ref{Voice Adjustments,Voice Adjustments,,emacsvox,}.

In Speech settings@comma{} press @kbd{p} for the punctuation menu. Choose @kbd{n} for
none@comma{} @kbd{s} for some or @kbd{a} for all punctuation. That menu's @kbd{p} offers the
built-in levels and available named profiles. In buffer scope@comma{} @kbd{r} restores
automatic mode policy. Press @kbd{q} to return to Speech settings.

The punctuation menu's @kbd{e} opens the Omnivox pronunciation editor. In that
editor@comma{} lowercase @kbd{s} saves overrides on the speech host and lowercase @kbd{r}
restarts speech after confirmation. Its @kbd{q} returns to the same punctuation
menu item@comma{} retaining the draft and selected character for reopening.

Capital @kbd{S} in the punctuation menu has a different purpose: it saves the
selected level or profile through Customize. It does not save the pronunciation
editor's host-side draft.

@kbd{S} in Speech settings saves displayed rate and punctuation as startup defaults.
Rate uses the backend's startup option. Saving from buffer scope promotes those
displayed choices to defaults. Mode punctuation policy and explicit buffer
settings still override the saved punctuation default.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Use Emacs word and phrase dictionaries}Use Emacs word and phrase dictionaries


For word or phrase substitutions managed by Emacs@comma{} open @kbd{C-e H s p d}.
This menu is separate from the Omnivox pronunciation editor on @kbd{e}. Its
dictionaries are stored in an Emacs dictionary file@comma{} not Customize or the
speech host@comma{} and changes need no speech restart. Opening the menu writes
nothing and leaves the source's pronunciation table unchanged.

Press @kbd{d} to define an entry at the captured source item. Choose buffer@comma{} file@comma{}
directory or major-mode scope@comma{} then enter the term and spoken replacement.
The existing prompt can load saved dictionaries first. Buffer entries last
only in that buffer's current pronunciation table. Other scopes update the
in-memory dictionary collection and need a separate @kbd{w} Save to persist.

Use @kbd{e} to choose an existing dictionary and open its widget editor. @kbd{@key{TAB}}
moves between fields and buttons; @kbd{@key{RET}} activates them. Field edits update
the in-memory collection. Its Save button writes dictionaries to a chosen
file. Return with @kbd{C-c C-c} from anywhere@comma{} or @kbd{q} outside editable fields;
inside a field@comma{} @kbd{q} inserts text. Returning retains edits without saving or
rolling them back@comma{} and reopening resumes the same editor buffer.

Back in the menu@comma{} @kbd{r} rebuilds and enables the captured source's table from
its dictionaries. This replaces temporary buffer-only entries. @kbd{t} turns
substitution off or composes the table again when turning it on; turning off
also loses buffer-only entries. Changes to file@comma{} directory and mode entries
remain in the collection. To reverse an entry change@comma{} edit it back and refresh
the source; save again if you changed the saved dictionary.

Finish and close existing widget editors before loading replacements: their
retained fields are not reloaded. The menu's @kbd{l} loads a dictionary file into
the collection; refresh the source afterward to use those entries. @kbd{w} saves file@comma{} directory and mode dictionaries@comma{}
excluding buffer-only entries. Both actions prompt for a file; Load offers
@code{emacsvox-pronounce-dictionaries-file}@comma{} while Save offers its basename in the
Emacsvox user directory. Saving elsewhere does not change the configured
startup file. Use @kbd{?} followed by an item's key
for its scope and effects before applying it.

@item
@anchor{Adjust indentation feedback}Adjust indentation feedback


Open @kbd{C-e H s i} or directly @kbd{C-e d I}. In global-default scope@comma{} @kbd{S} saves
indentation defaults and tone volumes through Customize. In @emph{Tone levels}@comma{}
@kbd{l} changes @emph{Pitch-tone volume} in the selected buffer/global scope; @kbd{v} changes
@emph{Shared tone volume} globally. Adjust either by five percentage points with
Shift-left/right@comma{} or enter with @kbd{@key{RET}}. The shared level scales all main-stream
tones. The individual level affects rising-pitch indentation only;
duration-coded tones still use the shared level. Plain left/right changes
sections and @kbd{@key{SPC}} previews indentation.

@kbd{t} opens pitch-tone settings: base pitch@comma{} rise per column@comma{} maximum pitch@comma{}
requested duration and individual volume. Shift-left/right adjusts; @kbd{@key{RET}}
enters exactly. @kbd{@key{SPC}} previews four columns without changing presentation or enabling feedback.
@kbd{s} stops. Short durations show the effective blank-line-tone minimum.
@kbd{R} restores these five bundled values in scope. Global changes preserve local
overrides; @kbd{S} saves global defaults and tone volume. @kbd{q} retains live changes.

From pitch-tone settings@comma{} @kbd{E} opens the shared base-tone editor@comma{} also reached
with @kbd{C-e H s i t E}. Its draft includes base pitch@comma{} requested duration@comma{}
individual volume@comma{} waveform@comma{} sweep and envelope. @kbd{@key{SPC}} previews the first
column's tone; @kbd{P} reads a four-column sample. Both retain the blank-line
minimum duration. The starting pitch still rises with indentation; an enabled
sweep ends at its absolute End pitch. Duration-coded tones are unchanged.

@kbd{A} applies the draft to the captured buffer or global defaults@comma{} as shown in
the title. @kbd{W} saves only the base-tone fields through Customize and requires
global scope; return to the parent to change scope. Global changes preserve
local overrides. Rise per column@comma{} maximum pitch@comma{} timing@comma{} enablement and shared
volume remain in the parent menu. Base pitch cannot exceed Maximum pitch;
adjust that parent control first if needed. @kbd{q} discards unapplied editor
changes and returns. See @ref{spelling-tone-editor, , the shared controls} for presets and envelopes.

@item
@anchor{Set capitalisation feedback and its beeps}Set capitalisation feedback and its beeps


@kbd{C-e H s c} opens @emph{Capitalization}. @kbd{i} toggles indication and @kbd{c} independently
toggles camel-case splitting. These and the presentation choice share
buffer/global scope on @kbd{g}. @kbd{@key{SPC}} previews reading; @kbd{S} saves these three
startup defaults. Buffer overrides still apply. The reading sample contains
several camel-case identifiers. Capital cues and a voice's own pronunciation
can make splitting on and off sound similar; compare with indication off to
hear splitting without capital cues.

Press @kbd{p} to browse Spoken@comma{} Tone@comma{} Spoken and tone@comma{} Personal rules or None.
The current choice is focused and marked selected. Up/down browses without
applying; @kbd{?} followed by a choice's key explains its effect. @kbd{@key{RET}} applies
in the parent menu's scope@comma{} announces the choice and returns to @emph{Presentation}.
Press @kbd{@key{RET}} again to try another choice@comma{} or @kbd{@key{SPC}} to preview reading. @kbd{q} or
@kbd{C-g} cancels and returns to the same control. Choosing a presentation neither
enables indication nor saves startup defaults.

Within Capitalization@comma{} @kbd{l} edits the capital-letter beep and @kbd{a} the all-caps
beep. Each has separate global defaults saved through Customize. You can
preview edits before applying or saving them; these unsaved edits are what
the editor calls a @emph{draft}.

Select pitch@comma{} duration or @emph{Beep volume} (@kbd{v}) and adjust by 25 Hz@comma{}
5 milliseconds or 5 percentage points with Shift-left/right@comma{} or use @kbd{@key{RET}}.
The individual volume affects only that beep and is scaled by shared tone
volume. @kbd{@key{SPC}} previews the beep alone. @kbd{P} reads two sentences with several
examples of the selected kind@comma{} so you can judge repeated beeps against speech.
@kbd{s} stops. Samples privately enable tone indication without changing your
reading settings; contextual presentation rules still apply.

In a beep menu@comma{} @kbd{A} applies that cue's global defaults for this Emacs session;
@kbd{S} also saves them through Customize for future sessions. These defaults
apply to tone/combined presentation@comma{} and presentation rules can override them.
@kbd{r} prepares factory values for preview; apply or save to use them. @kbd{D}
discards preview changes and rereads current defaults. @kbd{q} returns and @kbd{C-q}
closes menus@comma{} retaining unsaved preview edits for this session. Applied changes
remain effective. If defaults change elsewhere while you are editing@comma{} discard
the preview edits and reopen before applying or saving.

Press @kbd{E} inside either beep menu for the shared tone editor: the full routes
are @kbd{C-e H s c l E} and @kbd{C-e H s c a E}. @kbd{@key{SPC}} previews the edited tone alone;
@kbd{C} compares the current cue and @kbd{P} reads the same repeated-beep sample.
@kbd{A} applies the global defaults and @kbd{W} also saves them through Customize.
@kbd{q} discards unapplied changes and returns to the parent. @kbd{R} prepares factory
values for preview. Waveform and envelope controls follow
@ref{spelling-tone-editor, , the common editor instructions}.

The Customize options are @code{emacsvox-capitalization-capital-tone} and
@code{emacsvox-capitalization-all-caps-tone}. Each holds pitch@comma{} duration@comma{} individual
volume and shape. Conditional rules@comma{} profiles and palettes remain in the
separate presentation store.

If you previously saved these beeps as menu-generated personal rules@comma{} opening
the beep menu imports their values into its draft without writing anything.
Save once with @kbd{S}@comma{} or @kbd{W} in the shared editor@comma{} to migrate that cue into
Customize. Apply asks you to save first while these legacy rules remain.
Migration removes only rules that retain the generated matches and action
structure for that cue. Rules with different matches or action structures remain
intact. Finish any unsaved personal-rule editor before migrating. If the rule-store write fails@comma{} the old rules remain
effective and the menu reports that Customize was saved; correct the write
problem and retry Save to finish migrating.

@item
@anchor{Choose spelling feedback}Choose spelling feedback


@kbd{C-e H s f} opens @emph{Spelling feedback}. These settings are global. Toggle
@kbd{v} for voice changes@comma{} @kbd{t} for a tone@comma{} and @kbd{e} for the help earcon. You can
combine them or turn all three off without disabling Flyspell checking and
highlighting. The default is voice plus earcon.

When you read text already marked by Flyspell@comma{} tones and earcons occur at
the flagged word. Voice feedback uses the existing spelling face mappings.
Tone and earcon feedback also report errors detected while editing. Character
movement cues entry into a flagged word: its first character moving forwards@comma{}
or its last character moving backwards. Moving within the same word or onto
an adjacent space does not repeat the entry cue. Opening
a mail reply checks and highlights inserted text without a burst of cues.

Use @kbd{m} to choose tone timing. @emph{Alongside speech} is the default: it plays
the tone while the word is spoken without adding a tone pause. @emph{Before speech}
finishes each tone before its flagged word@comma{} adding a pause. Previously saved
choices remain in effect. Arrows browse without changing the setting; @kbd{@key{RET}}
applies and returns to Timing@comma{} while @kbd{q} cancels. Timing leaves voice and
earcon feedback unchanged. Compare it with @kbd{@key{SPC}} or by reading flagged text:
a tone-only preview or editing cue has no accompanying speech to overlap.

Select @kbd{p} for pitch@comma{} @kbd{d} for duration@comma{} or @kbd{l} for spelling tone volume.
@kbd{@key{RET}} enters an exact value; Shift-left/right adjusts by 25 Hz@comma{} 5 milliseconds@comma{}
or 5 percentage points. Spelling tone volume ranges from 0 to 100 percent:
zero silences the tone@comma{} and the default of 100 preserves its original level.
@kbd{V} changes the shared volume of all main-stream tones@comma{} including indentation
and spelling. The shared volume still scales the spelling tone after its
individual adjustment; it is also available in Sounds and output.

These controls change the current session immediately. @kbd{b} previews the tone
alone@comma{} even when tone feedback is off. @kbd{@key{SPC}} reads a sample containing one
flagged word with the selected combination. Capitalisation follows the captured
source buffer's active reading settings and presentation rules. A local Spoken
choice can therefore say “cap” even when global defaults use Tone. Open
Capitalization for that buffer to inspect or change its reading settings.
@kbd{S} saves the spelling settings@comma{}
including tone timing and shape@comma{} and the shared tone volume through Customize.
@kbd{q} returns and @kbd{C-q} closes the menus without saving.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Use the shared tone editor}Use the shared tone editor


@anchor{spelling-tone-editor}

@cindex spelling tone editor
@cindex shared tone editor
@cindex tone presets

Spelling feedback@comma{} each capitalisation beep menu@comma{} indentation's pitch-tone
settings and the five line-condition menus open this editor with capital @kbd{E}. The title identifies the cue and
scope. Waveform@comma{} pitch sweep and envelope controls accompany pitch@comma{} duration
and individual volume. Adjustments affect only a private draft until you
apply or save it using the destination's labelled actions.

The exercise below uses global spelling at @kbd{C-e H s f E}. Its timing stays
in Spelling feedback at @kbd{C-e H s f m}; voice@comma{} earcon and tone enablement also
remain there. Shared main-tone volume controls all main tones from Sounds
and output. Indentation's buffer/global scope follows its saving instructions
above; capitalisation and spelling tones use global defaults. Line tones use
conditional session or personal rules as described in
@ref{Sounds And Space,Sounds@comma{} Tones@comma{} and Spatial Cues,,emacsvox,}.

The sections follow the editing task: the cue's basic controls@comma{}
Pitch movement@comma{} Envelope@comma{} Preview@comma{} Presets@comma{} Apply and save@comma{} then Finish.
Left/right changes sections; up/down selects an item. Shift-left/right adjusts
only a selected number. @kbd{@key{RET}} enters an exact number@comma{} toggles Pitch sweep@comma{}
or opens the selected choice list. In a list@comma{} up/down browses without changing
the draft; @kbd{@key{RET}} chooses and returns@comma{} while @kbd{q} cancels. Waveform and Envelope
are choices@comma{} so shifted arrows do not adjust them. @kbd{?} followed by an item's
key explains its effect@comma{} units and dependencies. Use @kbd{C-c l} or the other
@ref{settings-menu-review, , menu review keys} to inspect an announcement without activating it.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Try a tone without changing live feedback}Try a tone without changing live feedback


@enumerate
@item
Open @kbd{C-e H s f E} and press capital @kbd{C} to hear the currently applied
tone. Press @kbd{@key{SPC}} to hear the draft@comma{} initially the same sound. Both play
the tone alone@comma{} interrupting previous main speech.
@item
Move to @emph{Volume} and lower this draft's individual level with Shift-left
if wanted. This leaves other tones and the live spelling cue unchanged.
@item
Press @kbd{w} to open Waveform. Browse a choice@comma{} read its Help@comma{} then press
@kbd{@key{RET}} to return with that waveform in the draft. Press @kbd{@key{SPC}} to hear it.
For Pulse@comma{} adjust @emph{Pulse width} back in the editor@comma{} then preview again.
@item
Alternate @kbd{C} for the applied tone and @kbd{@key{SPC}} for the draft. Capital @kbd{P}
reads a spelling sample with the draft tone@comma{} using the parent's current
timing@comma{} voice and earcon settings. Use this to judge whether the tone
obscures the word. Capital @kbd{S} stops; navigation also interrupts playback.
@item
Press @kbd{q} to return and discard unapplied edits. Reopening starts from
the currently applied tone. To keep the experiment instead@comma{} use the
Apply@comma{} Save or preset actions described below before leaving.
@end enumerate

These previews privately enable the tone even if live spelling tones are
off. They do not turn the live cue on@comma{} apply adjustments or save defaults.
A tone alone cannot demonstrate @emph{Before speech} versus @emph{Alongside speech};
use capital @kbd{P} for that comparison. Shared tone volume still scales playback@comma{}
so a shared volume of zero silences these previews too. After Apply@comma{} @kbd{C}
compares against the newly applied tone@comma{} not the editor's opening value.

@item
@anchor{Shape the pitch and waveform}Shape the pitch and waveform


@kbd{p} sets pitch; Shift-left/right changes it by 25 Hz. Choose @kbd{w} for waveform:
sine is a smooth pure tone@comma{} triangle adds a softer harmonic character@comma{} and
square sounds brighter.
Pulse offers adjustable width; Sawtooth has a bright@comma{} buzzy character. Their
perceived loudness can differ; compare at a comfortable individual volume.

For Pulse@comma{} select @kbd{u} for @emph{Pulse width}. This is the percentage of each cycle
spent high: 50 percent sounds like Square. Shift-left/right changes it by
five percentage points within 5–95 percent@comma{} and @kbd{@key{RET}} enters an exact value.
Only Pulse uses width. Trying another waveform keeps the width in this draft@comma{}
so you can return to Pulse and continue comparing.

Toggle @kbd{t} for @emph{Pitch sweep} to make pitch rise or fall during the tone. The
ordinary Pitch control sets the start; @kbd{f} sets End pitch. Shift-left/right
changes the end by 25 Hz and @kbd{@key{RET}} enters it exactly. Equal start and end
values keep constant pitch. Turning the sweep off retains End pitch in the
draft. The sweep occupies the existing duration and does not change timing
relative to speech.

Pitch and End pitch range from 50 to 10000 Hz. Duration ranges from 5 to
1000 milliseconds and steps by 5; individual Volume ranges from 0 to 100
percent and steps by 5 percentage points. Zero volume silences this tone.
Waveform changes can alter perceived loudness even at the same volume.

@item
@anchor{Shape loudness with an envelope}Shape loudness with an envelope


@cindex tone envelope, ADSR
@cindex attack, decay, sustain and release

Choose @kbd{e} for the envelope@comma{} which describes how loudness changes over the
life of the tone. @emph{Classic} uses the original short fades. @emph{ADSR} uses four
controls@comma{} with times in milliseconds and sustain in percent of peak level:

@itemize
@item
@kbd{a}@comma{} Attack: time to rise from silence to peak level.
@item
@kbd{c}@comma{} Decay: time to fall from peak to the sustain level.
@item
@kbd{s}@comma{} Sustain: the level held for the remaining time@comma{} rather than a duration.
@item
@kbd{r}@comma{} Release: time to fade from sustain to silence.
@end itemize

For example@comma{} with Duration 100 milliseconds@comma{} Attack 10@comma{} Decay 20 and Release
30@comma{} the tone holds its Sustain level for the remaining 40 milliseconds.
A Sustain value of 40 means 40 percent of peak loudness during that hold@comma{}
not a 40-millisecond duration. A longer Attack softens the onset; a short
Attack followed by a longer Decay can make a plucked sound.

Shift-left/right changes each timed stage by 5 milliseconds and Sustain by
5 percentage points. @kbd{@key{RET}} accepts stage times from 0 to 60000 milliseconds
and a Sustain level from 0 to 100 percent. The duration of the whole tone
remains the limit@comma{} even when a stage requests a much longer time.

Total duration includes the whole envelope. If attack@comma{} decay and release
would exceed it@comma{} they shorten proportionally and leave no sustain hold.
Zero skips a timed stage; endpoint smoothing still limits clicks. Classic
ignores these four controls@comma{} retaining their values while this draft remains
open. The initial waveform is sine with Classic fades.

With ADSR@comma{} @kbd{k} chooses the envelope curve. @emph{Linear} uses straight ramps and
preserves the original ADSR behaviour. @emph{Exponential} starts the attack more
slowly and starts decay and release more quickly@comma{} while retaining the same
stage durations and endpoint levels. Arrows browse the choices; @kbd{@key{RET}} selects
and returns@comma{} and @kbd{q} cancels. Classic ignores the curve but retains its choice
in the draft.

Preview after each small change with @kbd{@key{SPC}} or its equivalent@comma{} @kbd{b}. Capital @kbd{S}
stops immediately without waiting for the Release stage. Compare a short tone
with the spelling sample too: a shape that is clear alone can be masked by
speech or make the word harder to hear.

@item
@anchor{Reuse a tone through presets}Reuse a tone through presets


Capital @kbd{L} opens presets. Arrows browse without changing the draft; @kbd{@key{RET}}
loads a copy and returns@comma{} while @kbd{q} cancels. Soft tick@comma{} Rounded beep and Short
pluck are starting points with different pitches and envelopes@comma{} each at
35 percent individual volume. Use @kbd{?} followed by a preset's displayed key
for its character. After loading it and returning to the editor@comma{} press @kbd{@key{SPC}}
to hear the draft. The preset list does not preview while you browse.
Further adjustments leave the preset itself unchanged.

Capital @kbd{N} saves the draft under a name in your personal preset collection
through Customize. This does not apply the tone or save the spelling cue
defaults. Reusing a personal name asks before replacing it; bundled names
cannot be replaced. Saved presets appear alongside the bundled choices at @kbd{L}.

@item
@anchor{Apply save or discard the draft}Apply@comma{} save or discard the draft


For spelling@comma{} use @kbd{A} to apply the draft for this session. Capital @kbd{W} saves and applies
its tone defaults through Customize. These actions leave timing@comma{} voice@comma{} earcon@comma{}
tone enablement and shared volume unchanged. @kbd{R} resets only the draft to factory
defaults for preview and deliberate application. @kbd{q} returns to Spelling
feedback and discards unapplied edits; applied and saved changes remain.
Reopening starts from the current tone. To reverse an applied adjustment@comma{}
edit it back and apply again@comma{} saving too if you changed the saved defaults.

Apply and Save store the active tone shape. Unused Pulse width@comma{} End pitch and
ADSR values remain available while editing this draft@comma{} but reopening reads the
applied shape; it need not recover those inactive values. A named preset stores
the complete draft for later reuse.

The keys have different jobs in the two menus: capital @kbd{S} stops playback in
the tone editor@comma{} but saves settings in the parent Spelling feedback menu.
Capital @kbd{W} in the editor saves only tone defaults. To keep a choice such as
voice off and tone on@comma{} return with @kbd{q} and save with @kbd{S} in Spelling feedback.
That parent Save also includes timing@comma{} earcon selection and shared tone volume.
Saving a preset with @kbd{N} stores neither those choices nor the live cue defaults.

@item
@anchor{When a tone cannot be played}When a tone cannot be played


Individual volume below 100@comma{} harmonic waveforms and ADSR require an Omnivox
worker with richer-tone support. Pulse@comma{} Sawtooth@comma{} sweeps and exponential
curves require extended-tone support. Older workers omit an unsupported tone
while retaining speech and earcons@comma{} and the feedback explanation records the omission.
A preview reports missing support instead of playing an inaccurate substitute.
Your settings remain intact. Use an updated worker@comma{} or restore volume 100@comma{}
sine and Classic and turn off Pitch sweep to hear the tone on an older worker.
@end enumerate
@end enumerate

@item
@anchor{Choose typing and point feedback}Choose typing and point feedback


@kbd{C-e H s t} opens @emph{Typing and interaction}. Character@comma{} word@comma{} completed-line
echo@comma{} point indication/presentation@comma{} and spoken messages share buffer/global
scope; global changes also affect the source buffer. Delayed phonetics and
delay are global. Shift-left/right changes delay by 0.1 seconds; exact entry
through @kbd{@key{RET}} accepts at least 0.1 seconds.

@kbd{p} opens Point presentation with the current choice focused and marked.
Choose animated voice@comma{} tone@comma{} earcon@comma{} voice with tone or earcon@comma{} spoken point@comma{}
personal rules@comma{} or none. Arrows browse without applying; @kbd{@key{RET}} applies in the
displayed scope and returns to Point presentation. Press @kbd{@key{RET}} again to try
another choice@comma{} or cancel the list with @kbd{q} or @kbd{C-g}. Choosing a presentation
leaves point indication's on/off setting unchanged; each choice's Help explains
its effect.

@kbd{@key{SPC}} previews the captured line with point settings@comma{} @kbd{P} speaks its character's
phonetic name immediately@comma{} and @kbd{s} stops. @kbd{S} saves displayed settings through
Customize@comma{} promoting buffer choices to startup defaults. Closing retains live
changes without saving.
@end enumerate

@node Notification Routing
@subsection Understand the main and notification streams

@cindex speech streams
@cindex notification stream
@vindex tts-notification-device

The @emph{main stream} presents current interaction such as movement@comma{} buffer entry@comma{}
and message opening. Navigation interrupts obsolete main speech. The
@emph{notification stream} presents asynchronous events without stopping main speech;
normal navigation does not stop a separate notification stream. Both can be
heard concurrently@comma{} with each notification's cue and speech delivered together.
Integrations may deliberately route particular asynchronous actions differently:
Notmuch's explicit refresh@comma{} for example@comma{} uses interruptible main speech.

Open @kbd{C-e H o n} for routing. @kbd{r} opens a list of automatic@comma{} left@comma{} right@comma{}
both channels@comma{} main output@comma{} and other device. The configured choice is focused
and marked. Arrows browse without applying; @kbd{@key{RET}} selects the route for the
next restart and returns to the Route control. @kbd{q} or @kbd{C-g} cancels the list.
Other device asks for an existing device name; cancelling that prompt stays on
Other device and retains the previous route. Selecting does not restart speech.

@kbd{N} previews active output@comma{} which may still use the old route; @kbd{R} restarts
after confirmation. @kbd{h} inspects a notification-history snapshot@comma{} returning
with @kbd{q}. @kbd{S} saves routing and spatial defaults without a restart.

With Omnivox@comma{} Automatic normally uses an independent notification process;
left/right/both choose its route. @emph{Use main output (notifications remain
audible)} uses the main process instead@comma{} where navigation can interrupt it.
It is not a mute option. Other backends use their configured device or fall
back to main speech. In Lisp@comma{} @code{default} or an empty string means main output;
use descriptive labels for ordinary configuration.

After changing the route@comma{} @kbd{C-e d C-n} also initialises the arrangement.
Use notification history to read an announcement you missed. Ordinary
obsolete-main-speech cancellation is local to main; explicit @code{tts-stop} stops
both streams.

@node Output Volumes
@subsection Adjust output volumes

@kbd{C-e H o} provides five global Omnivox volumes: main tones and earcons@comma{} and
notification speech@comma{} tones@comma{} and earcons. Each is an integer percentage 0–100;
100 is normal@comma{} zero silences that component. For example@comma{} notification speech
35 and earcons 70 emphasise brief cues over messages.

Shift-left/right adjusts the selected volume by five; @kbd{@key{RET}} enters exactly.
Changes apply to subsequent audio without restarting; already prepared audio
keeps its old level. @kbd{S} saves these five volumes@comma{} not routing or spatial
settings.
Replacement and reconnected Omnivox processes receive current levels.

Independent notification levels need the separate process selected by Automatic@comma{}
Left@comma{} Right@comma{} or Both channels. Main-output routing/fallback shares main-process
levels. Earcon volume applies to Omnivox-played sounds@comma{} not external players or
other speech backends. Zero volume does not disable notification recording.

@node Integration Speech Settings
@subsection Adjust an integration's speech settings

@anchor{Integration speech settings}

@kbd{C-e S} opens focused settings directly where available. Alternatively use
@kbd{C-e H j} or @kbd{C-e H . j}. In that integration menu@comma{} @kbd{s} invokes a local Agent
Shell speech-level chooser or EAT/comint autospeak control@comma{} @kbd{c} opens the
source integration's settings@comma{} and @kbd{a} chooses among loaded integrations.
Settings search uses the same rule: installed packages appear only after you
load them@comma{} for example by opening their application. Discovery does not load
packages to reveal their settings. Cancelling returns to the same entry.

The selected integration determines where a change applies and what its
Save action retains. For example@comma{} a terminal-local setting is not automatically
saved as the default for future terminals. The summaries below identify those
boundaries; the application chapters give the complete workflows.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Shell/comint and EAT}Shell/comint and EAT


Shell/comint's focused menu toggles automatic speech with @kbd{s} and background
monitoring with @kbd{b}. Background reading needs autospeak enabled. Both values
belong to the captured buffer for its lifetime; there is no Save action. Arrows
announce values and @kbd{@key{RET}} activates. @kbd{q} returns to the entry@comma{} or closes a
direct focused menu. Search at @kbd{C-e H /} finds @code{comint} and @code{monitoring}.
Choosing Shell/comint from another buffer explains the required context and
leaves its controls unavailable.

EAT's @kbd{s} toggles foreground speech@comma{} @kbd{b} content-free background cues/counts@comma{}
@kbd{p} prompt feedback@comma{} and @kbd{d} automatic detail. Background monitoring is
independent of foreground autospeak and never reads background content.
Changes are local to the terminal@comma{} including from frozen review. @kbd{w} saves all
four displayed defaults; terminals with overrides keep them. @kbd{A} opens advanced
Customize and @kbd{q} returns. All controls are searchable. Choice lists start on
the current value; arrows browse@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} applies@comma{} and @kbd{q} cancels. See
@ref{EAT Terminal Access,EAT Terminal Access,,emacsvox,} for prompt integration and safety boundaries.

@item
@anchor{Agent Shell Org and EWW}Agent Shell@comma{} Org@comma{} and EWW


Agent Shell's menu covers temporary session speech@comma{} global feedback defaults@comma{}
and reading limits. @kbd{w} saves displayed globals@comma{} excluding the session override.
See @ref{Agent Shell,Agent Shell,,emacsvox,}.

Org's @kbd{c} chooses cell@comma{} header@comma{} or combined table-editing feedback and @kbd{w} saves
that global default. Column headers come from the first row@comma{} row headers from
the first column@comma{} and local overrides take precedence. @kbd{t} opens Org table
options and @kbd{A} advanced Emacsvox Org settings in Customize@comma{} returning with
@kbd{q}. This editing feedback is separate from the shared table reader described
in @ref{Browsing Structure, , Browsing Structure}.

EWW's @kbd{s} controls section-navigation autospeak and @kbd{i} suppresses images on
subsequent rendering. It also updates the shared SHR image default while
preserving local overrides; it does not reload the page. @kbd{w} saves the two
Emacsvox defaults and @kbd{A} opens advanced settings. The @kbd{o} outlining choice
applies to the captured page@comma{} while @kbd{m} selects the HTTP identity default for Emacs URL
requests. Both are temporary and excluded from @kbd{w}; see
@ref{Web Browsing,Reading web pages with EWW,,emacsvox,} for their scope and effects.

@item
@anchor{Notmuch and shared choice-list navigation}Notmuch and shared choice-list navigation


Notmuch has the same focused menu in Hello@comma{} Search@comma{} Show@comma{} and Tree. @kbd{c} chooses
adaptive@comma{} count-only@comma{} cue-only@comma{} or silent successful completion; failures remain
audible. @kbd{f~/~b} set field character/UTF-8 byte limits and @kbd{t~/~B} total limits.
Shift-left/right steps by 100@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} accepts a positive integer or @code{none} for
that limit only. @kbd{w} saves those four limits and completion policy: five
values@comma{} not every mail setting.

Org and Notmuch choice lists start on the current global value. Arrows browse
without applying@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} applies/returns@comma{} and @kbd{q} cancels. @kbd{?} plus a choice key
explains effect/scope; @kbd{q} returns to that browsed choice.

Notmuch's @kbd{s~/~m} open ordered search/message fields and @kbd{i~/~I} their status
maps. These Customize editors preserve custom formatters and use their own
Apply/Save/Reset. @kbd{p} opens Presentation Options: enabled
@code{mail-message-status-cues} owns standard cues@comma{} while maps still suppress spoken
status tags. @kbd{A} exposes advanced options such as separators. @kbd{q} returns;
opening or returning does not save. @ref{Notmuch Mail,Notmuch Mail,,emacsvox,} explains privacy and full
inspection. Integrations without focused entries remain accessible through
major-mode settings and advanced Customize.
@end enumerate

@node Presentation Profiles
@section Save@comma{} Switch@comma{} And Share Your Setup

@cindex presentation profiles

A Presentation Profile records enabled options and their order@comma{} sound pack@comma{}
voice palette@comma{} and portable spatial settings. It switches a coordinated setup
for focused work@comma{} leisure reading@comma{} or different audio environments. It is not
an Emacs startup configuration profile or a backup of every setting.

@kbd{C-e H p p} opens profiles. The parent @kbd{C-e H p} also links palettes and
unfinished drafts; each editor retains its own Save/Apply actions. Palette
@kbd{w} exports portable voices@comma{} @kbd{W} backs up full local choices@comma{} and @kbd{i} imports
an independent palette without activation. See
@ref{Sharing Voice Palettes,Export@comma{} Back Up@comma{} and Import Palettes,,emacsvox,}.

@enumerate
@item
Configure the live options@comma{} sound pack@comma{} voice palette@comma{} and spatial choices.
@item
Open @kbd{C-e H p p} and inspect the fields to be captured.
@item
@kbd{N} saves the live selection as a new named profile.
@item
Later@comma{} @kbd{a} applies the selected profile's saved values.
@item
To update it from deliberately changed live settings@comma{} use @kbd{w}. To discard
live differences instead@comma{} apply with @kbd{a} and review confirmation.
@end enumerate

Only one profile is selected at a time. @emph{Active} means its saved values match
the live configuration. @emph{Diverged} means it is still selected@comma{} but one or more
live values now differ from its saved values. That status is not a warning
about an unsaved editor draft.

Applying a profile also saves its selection for future sessions. This differs
from activating an individual palette@comma{} which selects it only for this session.

Profiles reference palettes and sound packs by name@comma{} not private copies.
Editing a shared palette affects every profile using it. Profiles exclude
engine policy@comma{} speech rate@comma{} notification output@comma{} rules themselves@comma{} buffer-local
Voice Lock@comma{} and current-buffer rules. Those remain in their owning settings
or rule stores.

@menu
* Walkthrough Switch Reading And Working Setups: Presentation Profile Walkthrough.  Save two selections, switch between them, and understand shared resources.
* Choose what persists: Saving And Startup.  Distinguish previews, drafts, saved resources, activation, and startup.
* Export@comma{} Back Up@comma{} And Import Palettes: Sharing Voice Palettes.  Share portable voices or retain local choices in a backup.
@end menu

@node Presentation Profile Walkthrough
@subsection Walkthrough: Switch Reading And Working Setups

Use unused names and note the selected profile first.

@enumerate
@item
Open @kbd{C-e H p p}. For a restore point@comma{} @kbd{N} saves @code{before-demo} with a short
purpose and selects it. This captures resource/option choices@comma{} not rule@comma{}
resource-definition@comma{} or engine backups.
@item
Use @kbd{C-e H v p} and @kbd{C-e H o p} to activate your reading palette and sound
pack. Choose Presentation Options at @kbd{C-e H r f} and spatial settings at
@kbd{C-e H o n}. Return through each view's own navigation before opening the
next. In profiles@comma{} save @code{reading-setup-demo} with @kbd{N}.
@item
Choose a different palette or pack and save @code{working-setup-demo}. @kbd{@key{RET}}
describes each profile's saved fields.
@item
Apply the reading profile with @kbd{a} and repeat familiar feedback. Inspect
active resources in their managers@comma{} then apply the working profile to
compare.
@item
Change a live option without updating the profile. @emph{Diverged} indicates the
difference. @kbd{a} restores saved values and @kbd{w} replaces them with current
choices; read confirmation before discarding either intention.
@item
Finish on the profile wanted at startup@comma{} because applying saves its
selection too. Restore the original or @code{before-demo} when finished.
Delete unwanted demo profiles with @kbd{d} only after selecting the retained one.
@end enumerate

If both profiles use @code{reading-demo}@comma{} edits to that palette affect both. For
independent voices@comma{} Copy Palette and select different resources. A profile's
Copy action copies selections only. To change startup palette while preserving
other profile fields@comma{} use @kbd{U} in Voice Palettes rather than recapturing everything.

@node Saving And Startup
@subsection Choose What Persists

@cindex saving aural settings
@cindex startup palette

Decide what you want to keep before choosing a Save action. @emph{Scope} tells
you where a value applies; @emph{persistence} tells you whether it survives a
restart; @emph{activation} tells you whether it is in use now. These are separate
questions.

A current-buffer value applies to the captured buffer. A global default is
live@comma{} but buffer overrides and mode policy can still take precedence. Changing
it does not automatically save it@comma{} and only the command's documented behaviour
tells you whether existing buffers are updated too. Saved startup settings
are retained for later sessions.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Try a change or leave it unfinished}Try a change or leave it unfinished


Preview an example to hear a proposal without saving or selecting it. Keep a
retained draft when you intend to resume it during this Emacs session.
@ref{Recovering Aural Changes, , Recover unfinished changes} explains how to return to its editor.
A draft is not a backup: it is lost when Emacs exits@comma{} and discarding it does
not undo an earlier completed save.

@item
@anchor{Store a voice without changing today's setup}Store a voice without changing today's setup


Use @emph{Save to collection without selecting} in the voice editor to store a
voice for later use without selecting its destination palette. The detailed
@ref{voice-saving-procedure, , voice-saving procedure} explains the editor's result and partial-save states.
This is appropriate for an alternative you want to keep@comma{} not yet adopt.

@item
@anchor{Use a saved voice or setup now}Use a saved voice or setup now


@emph{Save and apply} stores a voice and selects its palette for this session.
@emph{Activate} in the palette or sound-pack manager instead selects an existing
resource. After applying@comma{} check the result and repeat the original task.
Some host changes need a separate confirmed speech restart@comma{} and a successful
save does not establish that both speech workers acknowledged the application.
See @ref{Unconfirmed Speech Changes, , An unconfirmed Apply result} when a result is incomplete.

@item
@anchor{Start the next session with it}Start the next session with it


In Voice Palettes@comma{} @emph{Use at Startup} changes the palette in the saved profile
without applying that profile now. A Presentation Profile stores coordinated
options@comma{} palette@comma{} sound pack and spatial choices@comma{} not copies of their resources.
Applying a profile also saves its selection for future sessions. Use
@ref{Presentation Profiles, , Presentation Profiles} to switch such a group of choices.

For an individual control@comma{} use its own documented Save action. Speech settings
saves the displayed rate and punctuation; Sounds and output saves five volumes;
a focused integration menu saves only its documented defaults. Linked editors
retain their own Save/Apply actions. No one menu action saves all Emacsvox
settings.

Personal rules persist. Session rules expire when Emacs exits@comma{} and buffer
rules expire with their buffer. Saving a profile cannot make temporary rules
or drafts permanent. If a choice disappeared after restarting@comma{} follow
@ref{Settings Lost After Restart, , the persistence checks} before recreating or overwriting it.

@item
@anchor{Share a palette or make a backup}Share a palette or make a backup


Choose @emph{Export} for effective portable entries or @emph{Full backup} when palette
ancestry and local voice choices matter. Neither includes engines@comma{} sound-pack
audio or every Emacsvox setting. Import creates an independent palette; it
is not an overwrite or automatic activation. Follow
@ref{Sharing Voice Palettes, , Export@comma{} back up and import palettes} for the exact contents and checks.
@end enumerate

@node Sharing Voice Palettes
@subsection Export@comma{} Back Up@comma{} And Import Palettes

@kbd{w} in Voice Palettes exports effective portable entries@comma{} asking before omitting
machine-local choices. @kbd{W} backs up full ancestry and referenced local choices.
@kbd{i} imports either as an independent personal palette under an unused name;
it neither replaces ancestry nor activates the result. Missing installed voices
are reported and retained for replacement. Partial import retains its proposal
for Retry or Discard.

For practice@comma{} export @code{reading-demo} with @kbd{w}@comma{} review omitted choices@comma{} and import
as @code{reading-import-demo}. Enter and preview before activating. An unavailable
retained choice is a review request@comma{} not proof of audio. Delete the imported
copy when no profile@comma{} child@comma{} or rule needs it; the original is unchanged.

Use @kbd{W} when local choice data and ancestry matter. Import still creates a
new independent palette@comma{} not an overwrite. Neither export contains engines
or sound-pack audio@comma{} and neither is a backup of every Emacsvox setting.

@node Presentation Troubleshooting
@section Troubleshooting And Recovery

@cindex Aural Doctor

Start by describing the symptom and the last action that produced it.
Avoid deleting personal data@comma{} resetting all settings or reinstalling as a
first diagnostic step. Those changes can remove both useful configuration
and the evidence needed to explain the problem.

If speech has stopped altogether@comma{} start with
@ref{Recovering From No Speech,Recovering from no speech,,emacsvox,}. The presentation checks below assume a working
speech route. @ref{Backend Diagnostics And Ownership, , Backend diagnostics} covers further server investigation.
For the keys in a particular Aural interface@comma{} use its @kbd{?} Help.

For a problem with otherwise working speech@comma{} choose the closest symptom:

@itemize
@item
@ref{Saved Voice Change Not Audible, , I saved a voice change but cannot hear it}. Check selection@comma{} the named
voice@comma{} overrides and engine support in that order.
@item
@ref{Settings Lost After Restart, , A setting disappeared after restarting}. Establish what was saved and
what was only selected for the previous session.
@item
@ref{Feedback Differs Between Buffers, , Only one buffer or application sounds different}. Inspect its local
settings and rules before changing global defaults.
@item
@ref{Unconfirmed Speech Changes, , I cannot tell whether Apply succeeded}. Separate a completed save from
worker acknowledgements before retrying.
@item
@ref{Recovering Aural Changes, , I left an editor and need my unfinished work}. Resume its own draft
without changing unrelated drafts.
@end itemize

For other unexpected feedback@comma{} inspect the current item with @kbd{C-e E} or
an earlier record through @kbd{C-e H f h}. Use a Presentation Option for unwanted
broad optional behaviour@comma{} or inspect a narrow exception in @emph{Your feedback
rules} at @kbd{C-e H r o}. For a missing or inappropriate cue@comma{} validate the sound
pack and inspect overrides. For a saved setup that no longer appears active@comma{}
compare its @emph{Active} or @emph{Diverged} status. Voice distinctions that disappear
in some buffers can also come from Voice Lock or explicit face presentation.

A missing custom voice name is a configuration error. Ordinary speech keeps
the text by using an unstyled voice and records a diagnostic; it does not
invent the missing palette entry. Explain and Aural Doctor can locate that
unresolved reference.

@menu
* A saved voice change is not audible: Saved Voice Change Not Audible.  Trace a saved voice from palette selection to the original spoken item.
* A setting disappeared after restarting: Settings Lost After Restart.  Check the resource and startup action before recreating a missing choice.
* Feedback differs between buffers: Feedback Differs Between Buffers.  Compare local settings, integration policy and matched rules safely.
* Inspect the system with Aural Doctor: Aural Doctor Inspection.  Read configuration and backend evidence without automatic repairs.
* Review changed settings before editing again: Inspecting Changed Settings.  Compare buffer, global and saved values without changing them.
* Prepare a useful problem report: Reporting Speech Problems.  Collect a minimal reproduction and review private data before sharing.
* Recover unfinished changes: Recovering Aural Changes.  Resume drafts and distinguish saved settings from confirmed application.
* An Apply result is unconfirmed: Unconfirmed Speech Changes.  Interpret timeouts and partial results without treating them as rollback.
@end menu

@node Saved Voice Change Not Audible
@subsection A saved voice change is not audible

@cindex saved but unchanged
@cindex wrong voice
@cindex voice change, not audible

Use one representative item and repeat the same navigation or reading action
for each comparison. A preview and a different action may use different
context@comma{} so changing several things at once makes the result harder to explain.

@enumerate
@item
Read the editor's save and application status first. A retained draft has
not been saved; a partial or unconfirmed application needs
@ref{Unconfirmed Speech Changes, , its own recovery}@comma{} not another speculative voice edit.
@item
Open Voice Palettes with @kbd{C-e H v p} and check which palette is active.
Saving to the collection does not select its destination. Activate that
palette only if you intend to use it now; otherwise its unchanged live
sound is expected. Return through the manager and menu to the source.
@item
Explain the item with @kbd{C-e E}. Check that it resolves to the named entry
you edited. A rule may choose a different entry@comma{} or this kind of text may
have an explicit voice rather than using @code{default}.
@item
Inspect the physical choice and last sample result. An explicit choice
outranks the shared preferred-engine policy@comma{} and that choice's individual
tuning can mask a shared adjustment. Then check availability@comma{} disablement
and failures rather than repeatedly changing the engine order.
@item
In the voice editor@comma{} inspect @emph{Effective sound / Where settings came from}.
A contextual rule may override the edited value. Compare samples and
reported unsupported fields to distinguish an override from an engine
that cannot express the adjustment.
@end enumerate

After correcting the identified cause@comma{} return to the original item and repeat
the same action. Confirm both the explanation and what you hear. If the cause
remains unclear@comma{} keep the working settings and @ref{Reporting Speech Problems, , prepare a problem report}
instead of removing unrelated rules or voices.

@node Settings Lost After Restart
@subsection A setting disappeared after restarting

@cindex settings, lost after restart
@cindex forgot my settings

First establish whether the change was ever meant to survive restarting.
A retained draft@comma{} session rule or buffer rule does not. Activating a palette
for a session is also separate from choosing it at startup.

@enumerate
@item
Identify the resource you changed: a named voice@comma{} rule@comma{} profile@comma{} speech
control or host setting. Reopen its own editor or manager and check its
saved state. Do not assume it is absent merely because it is not active.
@item
For a saved palette@comma{} inspect the selected startup profile. Use @emph{Use at
Startup} only when you intend to change that profile's palette. Applying
a Presentation Profile also retains its selection; a profile does not
contain speech rate@comma{} engine policy@comma{} notification output or temporary rules.
@item
For an individual control@comma{} check exactly what that menu's Save records.
The @ref{Inspecting Changed Settings, , changed-settings snapshot} can compare values known to Customize@comma{} but
it is not a complete inspection of voice@comma{} rule or host-side files.
@item
If the saved value exists but is not effective@comma{} check a mode policy or
buffer override before saving again. For host-side settings@comma{} use the
owning editor's application or restart procedure@comma{} not a voice-palette Save.
@end enumerate

Customize saves need a normal initialisation destination. If Emacsvox reports
"Settings were not saved" because normal initialisation was skipped@comma{} possibly
with the @code{-q} startup option@comma{} restart with your usual configuration and save
again. A temporary change in that session is not a successful saved default.
For spelling@comma{} the tone editor's @kbd{W} saves the tone itself; use @kbd{S} in its
parent Spelling feedback menu to save voice@comma{} tone and earcon enablement too.

Use @ref{Saving And Startup, , Choose what persists} to select the intended action. Check the result in
the current session first. A later restart tests startup selection; finish
or deliberately save needed work beforehand because retained drafts will end.
Do not use a restart as the first way to investigate an unfinished draft.

@node Feedback Differs Between Buffers
@subsection Feedback differs between buffers

@cindex only one buffer sounds different
@cindex too much talking
@cindex application feedback, unexpected

Different applications deliberately announce different information. Compare
the same kind of item and the same action before treating that difference as
a fault. An explicit request for detail can also speak more than automatic
feedback without ignoring the saved automatic policy.

If the problem is too much speech@comma{} first identify what was added. Automatic
application content belongs to its focused speech settings. Training adds
explanations across buffers; inspect its current state in @kbd{C-e H r} before
changing it. Extra labels can come from Presentation Options or rules. Speech
that arrives independently may be a notification; use
@ref{Notification Routing, , notification routing} to understand that stream rather than treating it as
part of the line you are reading.

@enumerate
@item
In the affected buffer@comma{} use @kbd{C-e S} to inspect its focused settings@comma{} or
general buffer settings when no focused integration menu is available.
Read the current values before toggling anything.
@item
Use @kbd{C-e H d} to compare captured-buffer values with global defaults and
saved values known to Customize. Return from the snapshot before choosing
a different buffer and opening a fresh comparison.
@item
Explain the affected item with @kbd{C-e E}. Inspect matching buffer or session
rules@comma{} Presentation Options@comma{} Voice Lock and explicit face presentation.
These contributions need not be the same in another buffer.
@item
Change only the setting or rule that explains the difference. Return to
the original item@comma{} repeat the action@comma{} and check an unaffected buffer too.
Restore the earlier value if the change does not address the symptom.
@end enumerate

@ref{Integration Speech Settings, , Integration speech settings} explains the different saving boundaries.
Use the application's own chapter for automatic-speech limits and privacy;
do not assume the same key or background setting has the same effect in EAT@comma{}
Shell/comint@comma{} Notmuch and Agent Shell.

@node Aural Doctor Inspection
@subsection Inspect the system with Aural Doctor

Open Aural Doctor with @kbd{C-e H D d}. It checks live bindings@comma{} loaded source
and byte-code@comma{} the compatibility baseline@comma{} options@comma{} profiles@comma{} personal data@comma{}
resources@comma{} spatial fallback and backend. Opening the report neither starts
speech nor changes settings. A row that offers a reviewed safe repair can be
acted on explicitly with @kbd{r}; read the proposed effect first.

Worker rows report current capabilities and the last inventory. Agreement
between configuration identities means the startup identities were acknowledged;
it does not prove that saved files are in use. Missing or retired connections
leave the evidence unknown. A running process or available engine does not
establish audible playback.

In the diagnostics menu@comma{} @kbd{s} reports spatial capabilities and policy.
Lowercase @kbd{m} and @kbd{n} open retained local main and notification output@comma{}
reporting an absent buffer where appropriate. Press @kbd{q} in those views to
return to the selected menu item. For reporting and logging precautions@comma{}
see @ref{Reporting Speech Problems, , Prepare a useful problem report}.

@node Inspecting Changed Settings
@subsection Review changed settings before editing again

Press @kbd{C-e H d} to inspect changes without making another one. The snapshot
separates captured-buffer values@comma{} global defaults and saved startup values
known to Customize. It includes loaded Emacsvox@comma{} TTS and Omnivox options that
are saved@comma{} buffer-local or nonstandard. Saved expressions appear as recorded.
Press @kbd{@key{RET}} to read a complete row with its scope@comma{} @kbd{g} to refresh the
snapshot@comma{} and @kbd{q} to return.

Editor and cue drafts appear separately. The snapshot does not reread files@comma{}
query the speech host@comma{} establish that saved data is active or include options
from unloaded integrations. Voice@comma{} rule and host-pronunciation stores retain
their own saving rules. Return to the owning editor or menu to change@comma{} save@comma{}
apply or restart anything.

@node Reporting Speech Problems
@subsection Prepare a useful problem report

@cindex problem report
@cindex diagnostic logging, privacy
@cindex speech logs, sensitive text

Record the application and buffer type@comma{} the exact keys or action@comma{} what you
expected@comma{} what you heard and whether the problem repeats. Note the platform@comma{}
Emacs and Emacsvox versions and speech backend. Distinguish a reported
success or timeout from an audible result; neither is a substitute for the
other. Prefer a short@comma{} non-confidential example that reproduces the problem.

In Aural Doctor@comma{} @kbd{y} copies the report. It does not copy spoken text or log
contents@comma{} but it can contain personal paths and configuration. Review that
copy before sharing it. The log row identifies paths without opening files.

Enable detailed logging only for a reproduction whose spoken content you are
prepared to handle as sensitive data. In the diagnostics menu@comma{} lowercase @kbd{l}
toggles logging for this session@comma{} including complete spoken text. Note its
previous state and restore that state after the reproduction. Capital @kbd{A}
opens log-file@comma{} size@comma{} retention and buffer-visibility options in Customize.
Apply and Save there are explicit; buffer-naming changes take effect after
speech restarts.

Review logs separately before sharing excerpts. Remove credentials@comma{} message
content@comma{} personal paths and unrelated text; do not assume a diagnostic file
contains only technical metadata. State which checks you tried and their
results so another person can continue the investigation without repeating
unsafe or inconclusive steps.

@node Recovering Aural Changes
@subsection Recover Unfinished Changes

Press @kbd{C-e H p d} to resume a retained draft directly in its owning editor.
When several drafts exist@comma{} choose one by name; cancelling keeps the menu open.
Opening the draft preserves its source and working settings and saves nothing.
Finishing returns to the same menu item. Each editor retains its own save and
leave choices: the voice editor offers Keep@comma{} Discard@comma{} or Continue editing;
a rule editor can ask before discarding an unsaved draft.

In the common editor@comma{} @kbd{u} undoes and @kbd{q} offers keep@comma{} discard@comma{} or continue.
Guided feedback @kbd{q} first goes up through components@comma{} then hides/retains the
draft at the top. To reverse an applied rule@comma{} use @kbd{C-e H r o} and disable or
delete it in the correct scope with @kbd{t~/~d}.

Drafts last only until Emacs exits. Discard abandons unsaved edits@comma{} not completed
saves. The voice editor distinguishes stored settings from successful worker
application; use that distinction when deciding to retry or make another edit.

If saving finished but application did not@comma{} keep the result separate from
an unfinished editor draft. Use the following procedure before retrying.

@node Unconfirmed Speech Changes
@subsection An Apply result is unconfirmed

@cindex timeout, applying speech settings
@cindex Apply, unconfirmed result
@cindex did my change succeed

Apply waits for acknowledgements from each speech stream. After an Omnivox
timeout@comma{} Workbench reports @emph{server outcome unconfirmed}@comma{} stops that
transaction and sends no later steps. Requests already sent may still take
effect. A timeout does not undo the saved settings@comma{} and it does not tell you
that Omnivox rejected them.

@enumerate
@item
Read the result for each stream. Partial results retain acknowledged
streams and identify timeouts; do not describe both as failed when only
one is unconfirmed.
@item
Check for newer staged edits before retrying. Save them deliberately or
cancel them; do not mistake an older reply for acceptance of newer work.
@item
In Workbench@comma{} @kbd{r} reapplies saved engine settings and requests a new result.
This is an engine-settings recovery action@comma{} not a universal retry key in
every editor. For a palette review or voice save@comma{} use that view's own
retained proposal and retry action.
@item
Inspect the new result and repeat the original speaking task. If the result
remains unconfirmed@comma{} keep that distinction in your problem report rather
than claiming a rollback or a successful application.
@end enumerate

Do not delete the saved resource or discard unrelated drafts merely because
a worker did not answer. @ref{Reporting Speech Problems, , Prepare a useful problem report} describes the evidence
and privacy precautions for further investigation.

@node Voice Lock
@section Voice Lock And Advanced Configuration

@cindex Voice Lock

Voice Lock is one compatibility input to Aural Presentation@comma{} not the entire
system. It maps Emacs faces and @code{personality} text properties to voices.
Faces are visual style labels that Emacsvox can use without requiring sight.

@multitable @columnfractions 0.28 0.42 0.30
@item Policy
@tab What it controls
@tab How to inspect or change it
@item Semantic presentation
@tab Integration roles@comma{} events@comma{} states@comma{} and attributes
@tab Explain@comma{} Presentation Options@comma{} and feedback rules
@item Explicit face presentation
@tab Rules deliberately matching an Emacs face
@tab emacsvox-aural-toggle-face-presentation or Aural Doctor
@item Voice Lock
@tab Historical buffer face/personality mapping
@tab C-e H r l v locally; C-e H r l V globally
@end multitable

To identify Voice Lock's contribution:

@enumerate
@item
Move across differently styled elements@comma{} such as a comment/string or Org
heading/link.
@item
Toggle @kbd{C-e d v} in that buffer and compare the same elements.
@item
Explain with @kbd{C-e E} to inspect Voice Lock/face state and contributing rules.
@end enumerate

Turning it off removes only that compatibility contribution. Semantic
presentation@comma{} explicit face rules@comma{} cues@comma{} tones@comma{} and spoken status remain.
The global command updates existing and new buffers; a mode can still opt out
on startup. The same controls are available at @kbd{C-e H r l v} for the captured
buffer and capital @kbd{V} for global policy. That submenu's @kbd{w} saves only global
Voice Lock through Customize. Buffer-local Voice Lock is not captured in
Presentation Profiles.

To silence or restore the personality or mapped face at the captured item@comma{}
choose @kbd{s} in that submenu. This uses the existing local suppression command;
it can affect other text using that mapping in the same buffer. It does not
retune the named voice@comma{} and a globally inaudible mapping cannot be restored
there. The change lasts for the buffer and is not saved. Close and reopen the
menu from the intended item if its captured source has changed.

For new changes@comma{} prefer explanation@comma{} settings menus@comma{} palettes@comma{} and Workbench.
Legacy named voices can still use @code{-settings} options such as @code{voice-monotone-settings}. Customise
the option@comma{} not a generated @code{acss-...} symbol. Historical background is in
@ref{Audio Formatting And Aural CSS,Audio Formatting And Aural CSS,,emacsvox-heritage,} in @emph{Emacspeak Heritage}.

Named formatting voices such as @code{bolden} and @code{annotate} follow the active
palette. An inherited standard entry may be implemented by a personality's
settings option within the explicit root@comma{} not a hidden second fallback.
Explicit styles retain their own settings and rules can change the resulting
voice.

The @ref{Integration Maintenance,Developer Guide,,emacsvox,} covers voice metadata@comma{} rule authoring@comma{} and integrations;
@ref{Speech Server Protocols,Speech Server Protocols,,emacsvox,} covers delivery. The generated
@ref{Emacsvox Commands And Options,Emacsvox Commands And Options,,emacsvox-reference,} records exact commands@comma{} options@comma{} and keymaps.
These complement the tasks here rather than being prerequisites for them.

@node Emacs Packages
@chapter Applications And Integrations

@cindex applications
@cindex integrations
@cindex packages, speech support

Start an application with its normal Emacs command. Emacsvox prepares speech
support as the relevant libraries load@comma{} then presents movement@comma{} results@comma{} and
state changes through that application's structure. Emacs or the third-party
package still owns its editing@comma{} files@comma{} network operations@comma{} and other actions.

Use @kbd{C-h m} to learn the active mode@comma{} @kbd{C-h b} to inspect its effective keys@comma{}
and @kbd{C-h k} to explain one binding. For Emacsvox's feedback controls@comma{} @kbd{C-e S}
opens the current integration's focused settings when available@comma{} otherwise
general buffer settings. @kbd{C-e H j} also provides integration settings and a
chooser. These controls adjust speech; they do not replace the application's
own setup or preferences. @ref{Using Online Help, , Using Online Help} explains menu Help and search.

This chapter is a guide to maintained workflows@comma{} not a claim that every module
has a complete task guide. The generated
@ref{Emacsvox Commands And Options,Emacsvox Commands And Options,,emacsvox-reference,} in the @emph{Emacsvox Reference Manual} records
public commands and options. Consult both that reference and the application's
manual when diagnosing a problem.

The everyday guides below cover opening and copying files@comma{} writing and reviewing
notes@comma{} and reading web pages. They use existing speech settings. Start with
@ref{Answering Emacs Prompts, , Answer a prompt and return to your work} if filename entry@comma{} completion or
switching from the manual to a working buffer is unfamiliar.

Earlier surveys of W3@comma{} Dismal@comma{} and scanner/OCR setup remain in the
@ref{Emacs Packages,Historical Application Survey,,emacsvox-heritage,} in @emph{Emacspeak Heritage}. They describe their
original period and are not current installation instructions.

