@node Working With Files
@section Work with files and directories

@cindex files, everyday work
@cindex directories, browsing
@cindex buffer versus file

A file is stored on disk. Visiting it in Emacs opens a @emph{buffer} containing its
working text; editing changes that buffer until you save. Switching to another
buffer leaves the work open. Closing a buffer does not delete its file.

These tasks use ordinary text files and existing writable directories. They
do not convert Word documents or PDFs into text. Start with speech working and
finish any open menu or prompt. @ref{Answering Emacs Prompts, , Answer a prompt and return to your work}
explains filename entry and the distinction between Emacs and shell input.

@menu
* Open a document and check its identity: Opening A Document.  Visit the intended text file and distinguish a new file from an existing one.
* Save a separate working copy: Saving A Working Copy.  Preserve the original saved file and make later saves go to the intended copy.
* Find and inspect files with Dired: Browsing Files With Dired.  Navigate filenames, request metadata and return without changing files.
@end menu

@node Opening A Document
@subsection Open a document and check its identity

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C-x C-f}. At the file prompt@comma{} choose the existing text file you
want to read. Complete or type its path@comma{} then press @kbd{@key{RET}}. Emacs selects
the file's buffer; use @kbd{C-e m} to hear its name and mode.
@item
Press @kbd{M-<}@comma{} then @kbd{C-e l} to read its first line. Check that the content
is what you expected before editing. A missing filename can instead open
an empty buffer for a new file. Opening that buffer alone has not created
a saved document: check the name rather than assuming your text was lost.
@item
If you need to confirm the full destination@comma{} use
@kbd{C-h v buffer-file-name @key{RET}}. This Help shows the pathname visited by the
source buffer; @code{nil} means that buffer is not visiting a file. Read Help
as described in @ref{Using Online Help, , Using Online Help}@comma{} then return to the document by
its buffer name with @kbd{C-x b}. Do not edit while Help is selected.
@end enumerate

For reading without accidental text edits@comma{} use @kbd{C-x C-r} in place of
@kbd{C-x C-f}. That opens the file read-only. A read-only buffer is different from
a document with unsaved changes; do not remove the protection merely to make
an unfamiliar command work.

When you follow this task from Info or HTML@comma{} leave the manual available and
switch to the document before trying its editing commands. In Emacs@comma{} @kbd{C-x b}
selects an existing buffer by name; @kbd{C-x C-f} selects a file by its path.
Those are different questions even when the buffer and file have similar names.

To close a document buffer@comma{} use @kbd{C-x k} and check the name and any unsaved-work
prompt. That is not a file deletion. Unlike a Dired or Help buffer@comma{} an ordinary
text buffer does not use @kbd{q} to quit: typing it normally inserts a letter.

@node Saving A Working Copy
@subsection Save a separate working copy

Use this route before editing a document whose original you want to preserve.
It writes the current buffer text@comma{} including unsaved edits@comma{} to a new filename
and makes that new file the buffer's saving destination.

@enumerate
@item
Select the source document and check its identity. Press @kbd{C-x C-w}@comma{}
replace the proposed filename with an unused path such as
@code{~/evox-notes-copy.txt}@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}. Keep the appropriate extension
for the document@comma{} for example @code{.org} for Org notes. Cancel an unexpected
overwrite prompt and choose another name.
@item
Read the save result and check the new destination with
@kbd{C-h v buffer-file-name @key{RET}} when necessary. A permission or write error
is not a completed save. Keep the buffer open while correcting the problem;
do not discard the only copy of unsaved text.
@item
Return to that document@comma{} make the intended changes@comma{} and press @kbd{C-x C-s}.
This saves to the new destination. The old saved file remains at its old
path. You can open it separately with @kbd{C-x C-f} to compare it with the copy.
@end enumerate

@kbd{C-x C-w} does not keep the buffer attached to both files. After it succeeds@comma{}
later @kbd{C-x C-s} saves update the new file. The old file retains its previously
saved content@comma{} not any unsaved changes that were written only to the copy.
Changing the filename is also not a format converter: naming plain text with
@code{.docx} does not create a Word document.

To duplicate the last saved file while leaving the working buffer's destination
unchanged@comma{} use @kbd{M-x copy-file @key{RET}} instead. Answer its source and destination
prompts with paths. It copies the file on disk@comma{} not unsaved buffer edits@comma{} and
does not switch you into the new copy. Open the destination separately to work
on it. See @ref{Save Commands,Save commands,,emacs,} and @ref{Copying and Naming,Copying and naming files,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs
Manual for other saving and copying operations.

If Emacs reports that another program changed the original@comma{} do not force a
save or revert simply to clear the warning. Keep your buffer@comma{} preserve its
text under an unused filename if needed@comma{} and compare the versions before
deciding which changes to keep. @ref{Simultaneous Editing,Simultaneous editing,,emacs,} explains that conflict.

@node Browsing Files With Dired
@subsection Find and inspect files with Dired

@cindex Dired
@cindex file size, hearing
@cindex file modification time
@findex dired
@findex emacsvox-dired-speak-file-size
@findex emacsvox-dired-speak-file-modification-time

Dired is Emacs's directory browser. Its ordinary listing is not document text:
letters such as @kbd{n}@comma{} @kbd{p} and @kbd{q} invoke commands rather than being inserted.
Emacsvox normally speaks filenames as you move@comma{} leaving detailed metadata for
inspection on request. Voices and cues can distinguish directories@comma{} links and
marked entries according to your presentation settings.

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C-x d}@comma{} enter an existing directory such as @code{~/}@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}.
In the Dired buffer@comma{} press comma@comma{} @kbd{@comma{}}@comma{} to hear the listing header.
@item
Move with @kbd{n} and @kbd{p} to the next or previous entry. To reach a known name
in a long listing@comma{} use @kbd{C-s}@comma{} type part of the filename@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}
to finish searching. Check the filename you reached before acting on it.
@item
On an ordinary file@comma{} press @kbd{z} to hear its size@comma{} lowercase @kbd{c} for its
modification time@comma{} and @kbd{/} for permissions. These inspect the selected
entry without opening it. Uppercase @kbd{C} is Dired's Copy command@comma{} not a
second way to hear the time. Size inspection on a directory instead uses
the external @code{du} program and may take longer or be unavailable; file-type
inspection with apostrophe@comma{} @kbd{'}@comma{} similarly requires the @code{file} program.
@item
Press @kbd{@key{RET}} on the file to visit it in Emacs. On a directory@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} opens
that directory's listing. Opening announces the destination; @kbd{C-e m} lets
you check the resulting buffer and mode. Use @kbd{@key{RET}} here@comma{} not @kbd{C-j}:
Emacsvox assigns @kbd{C-j} and Control-Enter to a separate@comma{} file-type-aware
opener which may preview or play a file instead of visiting its text.
@item
To return from the document@comma{} use @kbd{C-x b} and select the directory buffer.
In Dired@comma{} @kbd{^} goes to the parent directory and @kbd{g} refreshes the listing
from disk. Refreshing Dired does not save or discard an open document's
edits. Press @kbd{q} in Dired to leave the listing; your files remain on disk.
@end enumerate

Looking at a file is not the same as marking it for an operation. @kbd{m} marks
an entry and then moves to the next; its action cue precedes the newly selected
filename. @kbd{u} unmarks an entry and also advances. If you are inspecting a
listing with existing marks@comma{} leave them alone. Dired operations can apply to
marked files rather than the row you are hearing.

This guide does not require deletion@comma{} renaming@comma{} changing permissions or running
commands on files. In particular@comma{} @kbd{d} flags deletion and @kbd{x} executes flagged
deletions; do not use either as a way to close a listing. Cancellation after a
file operation and ordinary text undo are not promises of filesystem recovery.
Use @ref{Dired,Dired,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual before performing those operations@comma{} and
@ref{emacsvox-dired,emacsvox-dired,,emacsvox-reference,} for the complete speech-command reference.

@node Document Authoring
@section Document Authoring

@cindex document authoring

Open or create a document in its normal Emacs mode. The mode supplies the
format's editing commands; Emacsvox presents movement@comma{} edits and structure
through speech. Voice Lock can distinguish useful faces supplied by the mode.
The following task writes a small Org note@comma{} then reviews it without relying
on visual formatting. Org comes with Emacs; no mail account or additional
speech engine is required.

For copying an existing document before editing@comma{} use
@ref{Saving A Working Copy, , Save a separate working copy}. Outlines and template fields are covered
under @ref{Structured Editing, , Structured editing and templates}@comma{} and tables under
@ref{Reading Markdown And Org Tables, , Reading Markdown and Org tables}.

@menu
* Write a note with headings: Writing An Org Document.  Create a real text file and organise a short note with Org headings.
* Review structure and correct text: Reviewing An Org Document.  Read headings, reveal their contents, search and verify an edit before saving.
@end menu

@node Writing An Org Document
@subsection Write a note with headings

@cindex notes, writing
@cindex documents, creating
@cindex Org, writing a document

Unlike the tutorial's disposable buffer@comma{} this task creates a file you can
return to. Use an unused name and a writable directory. The name
@code{~/evox-notes.org} is an example@comma{} not a file the package supplies.

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{C-x C-f}@comma{} enter @code{~/evox-notes.org} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}. If it already
contains work@comma{} choose another name before typing. The @code{.org} extension
normally selects Org mode; check with @kbd{C-e m}. If a personal configuration
selected another mode@comma{} run @kbd{M-x org-mode @key{RET}} in this new note.
@item
Type or insert the following text in the document buffer@comma{} not in the
minibuffer or the manual. Start each heading at the beginning of its line.
One asterisk and a space introduce the main heading; two asterisks and a
space introduce a heading beneath it.

@example
* Project notes
The draft is ready for review.
** Questions
Check the delivery date.
** Decisions
Keep a separate review copy.
@end example

@item
Press @kbd{M-<} and read with @kbd{C-n} and @kbd{C-e l}. Verify the heading words
and body text. Org heading feedback can identify structure without reading
every leading asterisk; exact character inspection remains available.
@item
Press @kbd{C-x C-s} and read the save result. That writes this Org text to
the selected file. It does not export a formatted document or change speech
settings. Keep the file open for the following review@comma{} or reopen it later
with @kbd{C-x C-f} and its path.
@end enumerate

Choose your own heading titles when writing real notes. Org stores the title
and body as plain text@comma{} so the structure remains in the file even when parts
of it are folded in the current view. Do not delete a folded heading to try
to reveal its body: folding is a visibility operation@comma{} not missing content.

@node Reviewing An Org Document
@subsection Review structure and correct text

Start in the Org document@comma{} not in an Info example. Use @kbd{C-e m} to check the
selected buffer. If the note was reopened with content folded@comma{} first press
@kbd{M-<}@comma{} then @kbd{C-u C-u C-u @key{TAB}}: Control-u three times@comma{} followed by Tab.
This shows all content without rewriting it. See
@ref{Global and local cycling,Global and local cycling,,org,} in the Org manual.

At the first heading@comma{} @kbd{C-c C-n} moves to the next visible heading and
@kbd{C-c C-p} to the previous one. In the example@comma{} these let you review Questions
and Decisions without reading every paragraph. Emacsvox announces the heading
you reach. Use @kbd{C-e l} to repeat it and @kbd{C-n} to begin reading its body.

On a heading@comma{} @kbd{@key{TAB}} cycles that subtree's visibility. Listen to the state
feedback before moving into it. This is different from Tab in a table or at a
completion prompt. The show-all sequence above provides a known starting view
if the current folds become confusing. @ref{Motion,Motion,,org,} documents the other heading
commands@comma{} while @ref{Browsing Structure, , Browsing Structure} explains the speech detail for Org headings.

For a focused text correction in the example:

@enumerate
@item
Press @kbd{M-<}@comma{} then @kbd{C-s}@comma{} type @code{ready} and press @kbd{@key{RET}} to finish the
search. Search moves point; the subsequent typing will occur there@comma{} not
in a separate search box. Press @kbd{C-e l} to confirm the sentence.
@item
Point is immediately after @code{ready}. Press @kbd{M-b} to move to its beginning@comma{}
then @kbd{M-d} to remove that word. Type @code{prepared}. The surrounding text
should now read @code{The draft is prepared for review.}.
@item
Read the whole line again before saving. If the edit is wrong@comma{} press
@kbd{C-g} in the document to deactivate any selection@comma{} then run
@kbd{M-x undo-only @key{RET}} and inspect the line. Repeat @code{undo-only} only as far
as needed; removing the old word and inserting the new one can be separate
steps. Unlike ordinary @kbd{C-/}@comma{} @code{undo-only} does not redo an earlier undo
merely because you paused to read between corrections. See
@ref{Recovering Text Edits,Recover a text edit before saving,,emacsvox,} for the full recovery procedure.
@item
Press @kbd{C-x C-s} only when the text is correct. Switch to another buffer
and back with @kbd{C-x b} to continue working; leaving the note does not
require closing its buffer.
@end enumerate

If a search fails@comma{} check the text and the starting position. @kbd{@key{RET}} ends the
search at its current position; @kbd{C-g} can remove a failing suffix before
aborting the search@comma{} so listen for whether it is still active. Finish or cancel
search before issuing editing commands. Keep the desired note selected when
saving: a browser page@comma{} Help buffer and document do not share Save semantics.

An edit made after a successful save needs another save to replace that file's
contents. Undo also changes the buffer; it does not by itself restore the file
on disk. For a separate review version@comma{} use @ref{Saving A Working Copy, , Save a separate working copy} rather
than renaming the buffer and assuming that changed its destination.

@node Structured Editing
@section Structured Editing And Templates

@cindex Outline mode
@cindex Tempo
@findex outline-mode
@findex outline-minor-mode

In Outline mode@comma{} heading navigation speaks the heading you reach and visibility
commands announce what was hidden or exposed. Start a dedicated outline with
@kbd{M-x outline-mode}@comma{} or enable @code{outline-minor-mode} in a suitable document.
See @ref{Outline Mode,Outline Mode,,emacs,} in @emph{The GNU Emacs Manual} for the editor and
@ref{emacsvox-outline,emacsvox-outline,,emacsvox-reference,} in the @emph{Emacsvox Reference Manual} for its speech support.

Tempo supplies template fields for modes and packages that use it. Emacsvox
speaks inserted text and the line reached when moving between template marks.
The package supplying the template also supplies its insertion commands.
Consult that package's mode help and
@ref{emacsvox-tempo,emacsvox-tempo,,emacsvox-reference,} in the @emph{Emacsvox Reference Manual}.

@node Browsing Structure
@section Browsing Structure

@cindex structured navigation

Use the active mode's structure when it matches the task: Info has nodes and
menus@comma{} Outline has headings and visibility@comma{} and EWW exposes HTML headings@comma{}
links@comma{} lists@comma{} tables@comma{} and other document elements. The following sections
explain Emacsvox's reading of headings@comma{} tables@comma{} and application lists. These
reading controls are distinct from commands that edit the underlying content.

@menu
* Reading Markdown and Org tables: Reading Markdown And Org Tables.  Read cells, rows and columns without editing the source table.
@end menu

@subsection Reading Org headings

Reading a whole Org heading omits its leading stars at punctuation levels
@code{some} and @code{none}. Heading voices and the optional heading-level announcement
still identify the structure. At @code{all} punctuation@comma{} the stars are counted@comma{}
for example@comma{} ``3 star'' before a level-three heading. An empty heading is
spoken as ``Empty heading'' when its stars are omitted.

Word reading and partial-line reading count the stars actually selected.
Starting at the second star of a level-three heading therefore says ``2 star''.
Word movement includes the star count when it reaches the first heading word@comma{}
in either direction@comma{} without changing the usual cursor position. Moving through
the remaining title words does not repeat that count or heading-level
announcement. Character reading and spelling retain the individual stars.
Collapsed headings have a collapse cue at every level; the large-movement cue
starts alongside heading speech during Org structure navigation@comma{} such as
@kbd{C-c C-n} and @kbd{C-c C-p}. Hold Control for the final key too. Ordinary line
movement with arrows or @kbd{C-n~/~C-p} does not request that movement cue.
To change its timing@comma{} capture the navigation in Recent Feedback and use
@ref{Guided Feedback Changes,Change sound timing,,emacsvox,}.

Counts preserve the configured character name without adding a plural ending.
At @code{all} punctuation@comma{} the speech server names @code{*}@comma{} so an active Omnivox
pronunciation of ``asterisk'' produces ``3 asterisk''. Saved punctuation-editor
changes take effect after restarting speech. Explicit word and partial-line
reads at @code{some} or @code{none} use @code{tts-character-to-speech-table}@comma{} the Emacs table
also used for character reading.

@subsection Use managed Muggles

@anchor{managed-muggles}

@cindex Muggles
@cindex Org, heading navigation menu
@findex emacsvox-muggles-enable
@findex emacsvox-muggles-disable
@findex emacsvox-muggles-save
@findex emacsvox-muggles-status
@findex emacsvox-muggles-org-nav/body
@findex emacsvox-muggles-org-table/body
@findex emacsvox-muggles-navigate/body
@findex emacsvox-muggles-outliner/body
@findex emacsvox-muggles-view/body
@findex emacsvox-muggles-yank-pop/body
@findex emacsvox-muggles-info-summary/body
@findex emacsvox-muggles-smartparens/body
@findex emacsvox-muggles-browse
@vindex emacsvox-muggles-preferences

A Muggle is a temporary menu of related commands@comma{} implemented with Hydra.
The managed list offers Org heading navigation@comma{} Org table editing@comma{} Text
navigation@comma{} Outline navigation@comma{} View navigation@comma{} Repeatable yank@comma{} Info summary
and Smartparens. All require the Hydra package. Enable installs a menu's entry
keys when available; Run enters it
and can move point or perform editing.

Press @kbd{C-e H m} to browse managed Muggles@comma{} or run
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-muggles-browse @key{RET}}. Select a row@comma{} then use @kbd{e} to enable its
entry keys or @kbd{d} to disable them for this session. @kbd{W} saves explicit session
choices through Customize. Settings and action search also find the Muggles
entry. Opening or browsing the list does not load or activate a Muggle.
The individual menus are described below@comma{} followed by the manager's conflict@comma{}
saving and restoration rules.

Org heading navigation lets you repeat movement and section reading with
single keys in an Org buffer.
Start it directly with @kbd{M-x emacsvox-muggles-org-nav/body @key{RET}}. Entry announces
the menu without moving point or changing the document.

Use @kbd{n} and @kbd{p} to move between visible headings and speak their sections.
@kbd{@key{SPC}} speaks the current section from point. @kbd{f} and @kbd{b} move between headings
at the same level@comma{} and @kbd{u} moves to the parent. @kbd{g} opens Org's heading chooser
and leaves the menu. @kbd{?} reads the available keys; @kbd{q} or @kbd{C-g} exits. Automatic
Hydra hints are quiet during this menu; exit restores their previous setting
unless it was changed during the visit.

Org table editing starts with @kbd{M-x emacsvox-muggles-org-table/body @key{RET}} at an
Org table cell. @kbd{j} and @kbd{k} move to the next or previous row; @kbd{h} and @kbd{l}
move to the previous or next field. These are native Org editing commands:
they may realign the table@comma{} recalculate formulas or create a row. @kbd{@key{SPC}} reads
the field@comma{} @kbd{.} gives coordinates@comma{} and @kbd{b}@comma{} @kbd{r} or @kbd{c} reads the value with
both@comma{} row or column headers. @kbd{?} explains the keys and @kbd{q} exits. For movement
that preserves the table text@comma{} use @ref{Reading Markdown And Org Tables, , the shared table reader}.

Text navigation starts with @kbd{M-x emacsvox-muggles-navigate/body @key{RET}} and
immediately attempts to move down one line. @kbd{n~/~p} or @kbd{j~/~k} repeat line
movement@comma{} @kbd{f~/~b} move by character@comma{} and @kbd{a~/~e} reach the line's beginning
or end. @kbd{v~/~V} scroll@comma{} @kbd{<~/~>} reach the buffer's beginning or end@comma{} and @kbd{l}
recentres. @kbd{?} reads the keys and @kbd{q} exits. Entry temporarily toggles Hydra
hints and exit restores them; pressing @kbd{s} makes an explicit hint choice
that is retained after exit. Movement uses the buffer's existing speech support.

Outline navigation starts with @kbd{M-x emacsvox-muggles-outliner/body @key{RET}}
in a document with headings recognised by Outline. Entry enables
@code{outline-minor-mode} and announces the menu without moving point. @kbd{n~/~p}
move between visible headings@comma{} @kbd{f~/~b} move at the same level@comma{} and @kbd{u} moves
to the parent. @kbd{d} hides a subtree and @kbd{a} shows all text. Heading movement
and folding use the Outline speech support described under
@ref{Structured Editing, , Structured editing}. @kbd{?} explains the keys. Use @kbd{z} or @kbd{C-g} to leave;
@kbd{q} hides sublevels. Exit and Disable retain Outline minor mode and completed
folds. Use the ordinary Outline commands to change visibility afterwards.

View navigation starts with @kbd{M-x emacsvox-muggles-view/body @key{RET}}. It offers
View commands without enabling View mode or making the buffer read-only.
@kbd{j~/~k} move by line@comma{} @kbd{h~/~l} by character@comma{} and @kbd{@key{SPC}~/~@key{DEL}} scroll by page.
@kbd{/} searches forward and @kbd{n~/~p} repeat the last search forward or backward.
@kbd{c} reads the character and @kbd{w} the word; movement retains existing speech
support. @kbd{?} explains further actions@comma{} including copying and registers;
@kbd{q} or @kbd{C-g} exits. Entry temporarily toggles Hydra hints. Exit restores
the previous value unless you choose hints explicitly with @kbd{s}.

Repeatable yank starts with @kbd{M-x emacsvox-muggles-yank-pop/body @key{RET}} without
pasting. @kbd{C-y} pastes from the kill ring; @kbd{M-y} replaces the previous yank.
@kbd{y~/~Y} cycle forward or backward. Native yank prefix arguments and undo
remain available. @kbd{i} or @kbd{s} opens an Ido chooser and leaves the menu;
cancelling that chooser preserves the previous yank. @kbd{l} opens the optional
@code{browse-kill-ring} package@comma{} or explains when it is unavailable. @kbd{?} explains
the keys; @kbd{C-g} leaves. Temporary quiet hints are restored on exit.

Info summary starts with @kbd{M-x emacsvox-muggles-info-summary/body @key{RET}} in an
Info buffer. Choose @kbd{n~/~p} for the next or previous node@comma{} @kbd{u} for the parent@comma{}
@kbd{m} for a menu item or @kbd{i} for the index. Most actions leave the Muggle after
running. @kbd{?} invokes the ordinary Info summary and @kbd{h} opens Info help.
@kbd{C-g} cancels the menu; @kbd{q} closes the Info window.

Smartparens starts with @kbd{M-x emacsvox-muggles-smartparens/body @key{RET}} after you
enable @kbd{M-x smartparens-mode @key{RET}} in the source buffer. It requires the
Smartparens package. @kbd{f~/~b} move by expression; @kbd{<right>~/~<left>} slurp or
barf forward@comma{} and @kbd{C-<right>~/~C-<left>} do so backward. @kbd{(} wraps with
parentheses@comma{} @kbd{R} splices an expression@comma{} and @kbd{k} kills it. @kbd{?} explains the
remaining keys; @kbd{C-g} leaves. Exit and Disable retain Smartparens mode and
completed edits. Temporary quiet hints are restored on exit.

In the Muggles manager@comma{} up/down selects a row and reads its purpose@comma{} current
choice@comma{} configured override and availability. Left/right inspects columns;
@kbd{@key{SPC}} reads the full row.
@kbd{@key{RET}} or @kbd{?} explains the selected Muggle and its actions. Returning from Help
with @kbd{q} restores the row and column. @kbd{g} refreshes the displayed state.

On the selected row@comma{} @kbd{e} requests its entry key for this session: @kbd{C-c C-@key{SPC}}
in Org for heading navigation@comma{} @kbd{C-c t} in Org for table editing@comma{} or global
@kbd{s-n} for text navigation@comma{} @kbd{C-. o} for Outline@comma{} @kbd{C-. v} for View and
@kbd{C-c @comma{}} for Smartparens. Repeatable yank manages global @kbd{C-y} and @kbd{M-y}
together; Info summary manages @kbd{?} in Info buffers.
@kbd{s-n} means Super-n. The two @kbd{C-.} sequences require an existing prefix map;
the manager leaves an unbound or conflicting prefix untouched. Use Run or the
direct @kbd{M-x} command when a key is blocked. Org and Info entries wait for
their corresponding application to load. The availability column names
conflicts and missing dependencies.

Explicit Enable may replace the standard @kbd{C-y} @code{yank} and @kbd{M-y} @code{yank-pop}
bindings@comma{} or Info's standard @kbd{?} @code{Info-summary}. A personal binding blocks
replacement; either blocked yank key prevents activation of the pair. These
ordinary commands remain available through @kbd{M-x}. Loading a legacy collection
alone does not replace these standard bindings. Other personal or package
bindings are preserved.

@kbd{d} restores adopted standard bindings@comma{} removes other managed entry keys and
closes this Muggle if active. It preserves completed actions@comma{} independently
configured bindings and personal replacements made after Enable. Global keys remain subject to local and minor-mode bindings.
These choices apply throughout the session@comma{} rather than to one buffer.

Enable and Disable retain the selected row and show the choice as not saved.
@kbd{W} saves all explicit session Muggle choices through Customize; the
@code{emacsvox-muggles} customisation group contains the configured overrides.
An absent override follows a legacy collection request@comma{} otherwise disabled;
explicit Disabled takes precedence over a legacy request. Session choices
take precedence until saved or changed. @kbd{q} closes the list and keeps session
choices; when opened through settings@comma{} it returns to the same menu item.

To try a Muggle@comma{} open the list from the intended source and press @kbd{r} on its
row. This leaves the list and enters that menu at the captured position@comma{} even
when the managed key is disabled or blocked. It does not enable keys or save
settings. Org heading navigation requires an Org buffer; Org table editing
also requires a table at the captured position. Info requires an Info buffer@comma{}
and Smartparens requires its mode already enabled. A closed buffer@comma{} changed source
item or unsuitable context requires reopening from the intended location.
Run is an explicit action; browsing and Help never perform it. Once the menu
starts@comma{} @kbd{C-g} leaves any managed menu. Outline also exits on @kbd{z}; Org and
Text/View navigation exit on @kbd{q}. In Info summary@comma{} @kbd{q} closes the Info window.

The named commands @code{emacsvox-muggles-enable}@comma{} @code{emacsvox-muggles-disable}@comma{}
@code{emacsvox-muggles-status} and @code{emacsvox-muggles-save} offer the same management
operations without the list. Each menu's direct @kbd{M-x} command also remains
available while disabled. Loading @code{extra-muggles} requests Org heading@comma{}
Outline and View navigation@comma{} Info summary and Smartparens; loading
@code{emacsvox-muggles} requests Org table editing@comma{} Text navigation and Repeatable
yank. The standard-binding adoption rule still requires an explicit Enabled
choice for yank and Info on their usual keys.
These managed requests respect explicit Disabled choices and protect
conflicting bindings. Unconverted collection members retain their legacy keys.

@node Reading Markdown And Org Tables
@subsection Reading Markdown and Org tables

In a Markdown or Org table@comma{} press a Control-Meta arrow to enter table reading
and move immediately. Left/right moves between columns; up/down moves between
logical rows@comma{} retaining the column. Every Control-Meta arrow reads the
destination cell. The first movement announces table reading and the destination
together. Table reading starts on demand by default in these source buffers;
you can choose automatic entry in the Tables settings described below.
Outside supported tables@comma{} these arrows retain their existing bindings@comma{}
including inside code examples.

@kbd{C-e C-t C-r} enters at the current cell with the default speech prefix.
It announces the dimensions and current cell. The equivalent named command is
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-table-reader-mode}. Press @kbd{q} or the same command to restore
ordinary editing keys without moving point. This suspends automatic entry for
the current visit: another explicit entry command@comma{} or leaving the table and
returning@comma{} can start reading again. From prose@comma{} @kbd{C-e C-t ]} and @kbd{C-e C-t [}
find and enter the next or previous table. Inside table reading@comma{} @kbd{t} and @kbd{T}
do the same; code examples are skipped.

While table reading is active:

@itemize
@item
Plain Up/down moves by logical row and reads the complete destination row
by default. You can select cell speech for these keys. Control-Meta-up/down
always reads the destination cell; both retain the column.
@item
Control-Meta-left/right moves between cells. Plain left/right retains
character movement. @kbd{@key{TAB}} and @kbd{S-@key{TAB}} move between cells without editing;
@kbd{@key{RET}} or @kbd{@key{SPC}} reads the current cell.
@item
@kbd{r} reads the row and @kbd{c} the column. @kbd{.} announces coordinates and @kbd{=}
gives dimensions.
@item
@kbd{a} opens Tables settings for titles@comma{} order@comma{} movement speech@comma{} activation
and optional detail. Browsing settings leaves the table untouched.
@item
@kbd{w} or @kbd{k k} copies the cell@comma{} @kbd{k r} copies the row with tabs between cells@comma{}
and @kbd{k c} copies the column with newlines between cells.
@item
Meta-up/down leaves directly for preceding/following prose. Up/down also
leaves across a vertical table edge when content exists beyond it by default.
Choose Stop at the edge to retain the cell instead. If no adjacent content
exists@comma{} point stays in the table.
@end itemize

A horizontal edge or a missing cell in a shorter row leaves point unchanged
and announces the boundary. Separators are skipped. A separator immediately
after the first row identifies column titles; otherwise that row is data.
Coordinates distinguish the header from one-based data rows. Empty cells are
spoken as “blank.” Wrapping never shortens a logical cell's speech or copy.

Reading does not align tables@comma{} create rows@comma{} or evaluate Org formulas. Copies
use cell values: ordinary links become their labels and inline code becomes
its literal text. Use the host mode to copy exact markup or formulas. This
reader covers simple pipe tables; HTML spans@comma{} nested tables@comma{} and
spreadsheet-specific structures have separate interfaces.

Other editing commands retain their normal meaning. An edit ends reading and
suspends automatic re-entry while you continue typing. Org's native table
editing feedback@comma{} including its table Muggle@comma{} remains independent; it can
speak after native cell movement without starting this reader. Markdown's
markup-stripping speech preference also remains independent.

Agent Shell uses the same reading preferences and movement units@comma{} with automatic
entry as its initial policy. It retains native Tab@comma{} Shift-Tab and Return
actions and handles character movement within rendered cells; see
@ref{Agent Shell, , Agent Shell}.

@subsection Reading application lists

In an application built on Tabulated List mode@comma{} use a Control-Meta arrow or
@kbd{C-e C-t C-r} to enter shared reading. Ordinary application navigation stays
unchanged until you enter@comma{} unless you select automatic entry. Within reading@comma{}
plain up/down reads complete logical rows by default@comma{} Control-Meta arrows read
cells@comma{} and plain left/right inspects characters.
@kbd{q} leaves reading and restores the application's usual navigation.

Application actions take precedence over other shared reader keys. Opening@comma{}
refreshing@comma{} sorting@comma{} searching@comma{} and editing keep their commands@comma{} including
@kbd{@key{RET}} on a button. When a shared key is occupied@comma{} use
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-table-reader-speak-cell}@comma{} @kbd{M-x emacsvox-table-reader-speak-row}@comma{}
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-table-reader-speak-column}@comma{} @kbd{M-x emacsvox-table-reader-speak-context}@comma{}
or @kbd{M-x emacsvox-table-reader-speak-dimensions}. The matching
@code{emacsvox-table-reader-copy-cell}@comma{} @code{emacsvox-table-reader-copy-row} and
@code{emacsvox-table-reader-copy-column} commands copy logical values.

Speech and copies use complete entry text even when a displayed column is
truncated. Column headings come from the application@comma{} not the first item.
Coordinates count visible data rows; hidden rows are excluded and empty cells
say “blank.” Movement stops at the first or last row. A refresh ends reading;
enter again to inspect the refreshed list. Custom table printers@comma{} image-only
cells@comma{} and duplicate column names are not supported by this reader.

@subsection Choose table reading preferences

Open @kbd{M-x emacsvox-table-settings} from the buffer you want to configure.
While reading a source or Agent Shell table@comma{} @kbd{a} opens the same page. The
speech settings menu provides @kbd{C-e H s T}@comma{} and the focused Org and Agent Shell
menus provide @kbd{T}. Settings search at @kbd{C-e H /} finds table reading@comma{} titles@comma{}
activation and inheritance. Each route captures the original buffer so moving
through the menu does not redirect your changes.

A control shows its pending choice@comma{} its effective value and the source of that
value. Open the control@comma{} browse with arrows@comma{} then press @kbd{@key{RET}} to stage a
choice and return to that control. Press @kbd{q} in the choice list to cancel.
Neither browsing nor selecting changes runtime behaviour yet. Press @kbd{?} and a
choice's key for Help; returning restores the browsed choice. The page's @kbd{h}
explains scopes@comma{} migration and host exceptions.

For example@comma{} open Titles with @kbd{t}@comma{} select Both titles@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{SPC}} to
preview a small sample. The sample includes a second row and a blank cell;
it uses the pending speech choices without moving or editing your source.
@kbd{s} stops it. Preview treats pending choices as buffer overrides@comma{} so inheritance
uses the current mode settings. Saving inheritance for the mode can resolve
to a different common value; the effective labels refresh after saving.
Try activation and edge behaviour in an actual table after
applying them@comma{} since the speech sample does not navigate the source.

Choose one explicit action to use the pending settings:

@itemize
@item
@kbd{A} applies only the changed choices temporarily to the captured buffer.
@item
@kbd{W} saves only those choices as common defaults through Customize.
@item
@kbd{M} saves only those choices for the captured major mode through Customize.
Agent Shell's transcript and viewport share one mode setting.
@end itemize

After Apply@comma{} choices are labelled applied here@comma{} not saved. You can then Save
without selecting them again. Closing keeps applied buffer choices but does
not persist them.

Buffer overrides take precedence over mode overrides@comma{} which take precedence
over common defaults. Saving a wider default preserves narrower overrides;
the effective labels show when an override still wins. No titles is an
explicit choice@comma{} distinct from inheritance. Select Inherit wider scope and
Apply to release a buffer choice@comma{} or Save mode to make that mode follow the
common default. @kbd{I} stages inheritance for all settings; it still requires
Apply or Save mode. Common defaults cannot inherit. @kbd{D} discards pending
choices. Closing the page discards choices that were neither applied nor saved.

The controls cover titles@comma{} data/title order@comma{} plain-arrow speech@comma{} activation@comma{}
coordinates during movement@comma{} entry detail@comma{} repeated titles and vertical edges.
Initial defaults retain whole-row plain arrows@comma{} full entry detail@comma{} repeated
titles@comma{} no extra movement coordinates and leaving at vertical edges when
adjacent prose exists. Brief entry still identifies a table and reads its cell.
Suppressing repeated titles affects consecutive cell moves; explicit cell
inspection remains complete. Application lists always stop at their edges.

Activation's Mode defaults choice means automatic entry in Agent Shell and
on-demand entry in Org@comma{} Markdown and application lists. A common Automatic or
On demand choice applies wherever there is no narrower override. Quitting
always provides a temporary escape from automatic reading. A redraw or
Agent Shell response update alone cannot undo that quit.

Existing shared title/order settings retain their scope. Older Agent Shell
title/order choices remain effective as Agent Shell overrides@comma{} including saved
choices equal to their original defaults. The page identifies their source;
Help lists retained older values even when a newer override takes precedence.
An older value whose origin cannot be determined is preserved and identified
as uncertain. Select a replacement or inheritance explicitly to resolve it.
Opening the page never rewrites configuration. Advanced configuration lives
in the @code{emacsvox-table-reader} customisation group.

@subsection Browsing CSV data

Use @code{emacsvox-table-find-csv-file} to open a CSV file in the table browser@comma{}
or @code{emacsvox-table-view-csv-buffer} for the current buffer's CSV data. Quoted
commas@comma{} doubled quotation marks@comma{} and multiline fields remain single logical
cells. Blank records outside quoted fields are skipped; empty fields remain
cells. Empty input or malformed quoting produces an error before creating a
view.

Browsing preserves source text. If the file is already open@comma{} the view uses
that buffer's current contents@comma{} including unsaved edits@comma{} without replacing the
source buffer. Shared table controls are active immediately:

@itemize
@item
Up/down and Control-Meta-up/down move by logical row and read the whole row.
@item
Control-Meta-left/right and @kbd{@key{TAB}~/~S-@key{TAB}} move by cell. Plain left/right
and @kbd{C-b~/~C-f} move by character; @kbd{C-p~/~C-n} move by logical row.
@item
@kbd{@key{RET}} or @kbd{@key{SPC}} reads the cell; @kbd{r} reads the row and @kbd{c} the column.
@item
@kbd{.} gives coordinates and @kbd{=} dimensions. The first row supplies column
titles and is announced separately from data rows.
@item
@kbd{w} or @kbd{k k} copies the cell@comma{} @kbd{k r} copies the row with tabs between cells@comma{}
and @kbd{k c} copies the column with newlines between cells. @kbd{K} copies the whole
table to the clipboard used by @code{emacsvox-table-paste-from-clipboard}.
@item
@kbd{a} changes column titles@comma{} row titles@comma{} or data order for this view using
shared reader preferences. @kbd{q} closes the view.
@end itemize

These replace the old browser cell-arrow@comma{} header-reading@comma{} automatic-speech@comma{}
and single-key table-copy bindings. The old local aliases @kbd{b}@comma{} @kbd{n}@comma{} @kbd{p}@comma{} @kbd{A}@comma{}
@kbd{B}@comma{} @kbd{E}@comma{} and @kbd{T} are removed; speech-prefix table commands remain available.

Search with @kbd{s}@comma{} @kbd{R} for a row@comma{} @kbd{C} for a column@comma{} or @kbd{h} for headers.
@kbd{#} sorts on the current column and @kbd{M-<~/~M->} jumps to the top or bottom.
At an edge@comma{} movement announces the boundary and leaves point in place.

Filters remain on @kbd{f} for a row and @kbd{g} for a column; a prefix argument
requests a new filter. Filter indexes and the @kbd{j} jump prompt retain
zero-based model coordinates@comma{} including the header@comma{} although spoken
coordinates count data rows and columns from one. For example@comma{} the row filter
@code{(0 "value" 2)} reads the first column@comma{} “value”@comma{} and the third column.
@kbd{M-l} and @kbd{M-s} load and save filters. Filters are explicit inspections;
shared row movement always reads the whole row. Sorting preserves filters and
speech preferences. Use @kbd{C-h m} for the full browser key map.

@node Web Browsing
@section Web Browsing With EWW

@cindex EWW
@cindex Web browsing
@findex eww

EWW is Emacs's built-in text-oriented web browser. Emacsvox adds speech and
navigation using the page's HTML structure. These commands act on the rendered
page@comma{} not on the manual you are reading now. Start with a speaking session and
use a public page for practice; a site requiring JavaScript may need your
ordinary external browser instead.

The keys below include Emacsvox's EWW bindings@comma{} which differ from unmodified
EWW@. In particular@comma{} lowercase @kbd{b} moves to the previous link@comma{} lowercase @kbd{s}
requests a readable view@comma{} and @kbd{?} is not the settings menu's Help key. Use
@kbd{C-h m} or @kbd{C-h k} to inspect the running buffer. Help can select its own
buffer and remove other windows. Return to the page with @kbd{C-x b} and check
its name with @kbd{C-e m} before using the browser keys below. The upstream
@ref{EWW,EWW guide,,emacs,} and @ref{emacsvox-eww,emacsvox-eww,,emacsvox-reference,} reference cover the rest of the interface.

@menu
* Open a page and establish your place: Opening A Web Page.  Enter an address, wait for rendering and identify the selected browser buffer.
* Read by heading@comma{} paragraph or search: Navigating A Web Page.  Use HTML structure when it is present and text search when it is not.
* Follow a link and return to the article: Following Web Links.  Inspect a link, follow it and distinguish browser history from buffer selection.
* Recover a cluttered or incomplete page: Recovering A Web Page.  Restore a reading view, handle missing content and keep browser settings distinct.
@end menu

@node Opening A Web Page
@subsection Open a page and establish your place

@cindex web page, opening
@cindex EWW, buffer names

@enumerate
@item
Run @kbd{M-x eww @key{RET}}. At its next prompt@comma{} enter a web address@comma{} for example
@code{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/}@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}. Text that EWW does
not recognise as an address is sent to its configured search service;
do not enter private document text as a practice search.
@item
Wait for loading and rendering to finish before navigating the page.
An opening cue means the command started@comma{} not that the server returned the
expected article. Read the page title and any error or login message.
Emacsvox normally names the buffer after the page title@comma{} so do not assume
the buffer is called @code{*eww*}. Press @kbd{C-e m} to identify it.
@item
Press @kbd{M-<} and use @kbd{C-n} or @kbd{C-e l} to read. Keep the browser buffer's
name in mind when switching to notes or Help. @kbd{C-x b} returns to that
existing buffer; running @code{eww} again starts another browsing operation.
@end enumerate

To request a separate browser buffer instead of reusing the default one@comma{}
start with @kbd{C-u M-x eww @key{RET}}. This is an intentional use of the prefix
argument explained in @ref{Emacs Key Notation, , Read the key instructions}. It lets you keep the earlier
page available while opening another. A title can change after navigation;
check the new buffer name before using it in a later switch.

@node Navigating A Web Page
@subsection Read by heading@comma{} paragraph or search

@cindex web headings
@cindex web page, finding text

In the EWW page buffer@comma{} period@comma{} @kbd{.}@comma{} moves to the next supported heading and
comma@comma{} @kbd{@comma{}}@comma{} to the previous one. @kbd{1} through @kbd{4} find the next heading at the
corresponding level; @kbd{M-1} through @kbd{M-4} go backwards. These are HTML heading
levels@comma{} not numbered paragraphs. A page without suitable heading markup
cannot be navigated as though that structure existed.

For Outline commands@comma{} @kbd{C-e S o} chooses SHR-aware outlining based on rendered
HTML structure or native outlining based on text list markers. Arrows browse@comma{}
@kbd{@key{RET}} applies to the captured EWW buffer@comma{} and @kbd{q} cancels. This temporary
choice preserves your text and position; the page's existing @kbd{0} toggle remains
available.

Use @kbd{]} and @kbd{[} to move between paragraphs. @kbd{M-@key{SPC}} requests the contents of
the current HTML element. Section-navigation autospeak can read more than a
heading or a line@comma{} depending on your setting; use explicit line or element
reading when you want to control the amount. Stop current speech with @kbd{C-e s}
without disabling later feedback.

For known words@comma{} press @kbd{C-s}@comma{} type the text@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{RET}} to finish at
the match. Repeat @kbd{C-s} while searching to find another occurrence. This is
Control-s@comma{} not the unmodified @kbd{s} key used for a readable view. Search covers
the current rendered buffer; it does not submit a web search or find text
that was never loaded into the page.

Use the original page when checking whether information is genuinely absent.
A readable or filtered view can omit menus@comma{} tables or other useful material.
The recovery procedure below explains restoring the page without treating a
filter as an edit to the website.

@node Following Web Links
@subsection Follow a link and return to the article

@cindex web links, inspecting
@cindex web history, going back
@cindex EWW, bookmarks

Use lowercase @kbd{f} to reach the next link and lowercase @kbd{b} for the previous
one. Emacsvox presents link navigation; @kbd{C-e l} reads its surrounding line.
The link's label is not necessarily its destination address.

On a link@comma{} @kbd{k} copies its address to Emacs's kill ring@comma{} the text store used by
@kbd{C-y}. To inspect that address as text@comma{} press @kbd{C-x b}@comma{} enter an unused buffer
name such as @code{evox-web-address}@comma{} and press @kbd{@key{RET}}. If that name already contains
work@comma{} choose another. Run @kbd{M-x text-mode @key{RET}}@comma{} press @kbd{C-y} to insert the
address@comma{} then @kbd{C-e l} to read it. Return to the browser by name with @kbd{C-x b}.
This temporary buffer need not be saved. Do not paste into an important document
or a web form merely to inspect a URL@. Between copying and pasting@comma{} avoid
@kbd{C-e a} and @kbd{C-e f}: repeating a message or reading a filename also adds text
to the kill ring and can replace the address as the next paste. Check the
pasted text; @ref{Choosing Earlier Copied Text,Choose text copied earlier,,emacsvox,} explains retrieving an older entry.

To copy the page's own address regardless of point@comma{} use
@kbd{M-x eww-copy-page-url @key{RET}} instead. Emacsvox's @kbd{k} is context-sensitive and
means the link address when on a link.

Press @kbd{@key{RET}} on the intended link to follow it. After the new page loads@comma{}
press lowercase @kbd{l} to go back in that EWW buffer's history and @kbd{r} to go
forward again. Emacsvox announces URL navigation through the page header.
Check your position with line reading; if necessary@comma{} search for a distinctive
phrase from the article. History is limited and a page can change@comma{} so do not
assume the exact passage is always restored.

To follow a link in a separate buffer@comma{} use @kbd{M-@key{RET}}. Return to the original
page with @kbd{C-x b}; that is a buffer change rather than a history step in the
new buffer. Use @kbd{q} in an ordinary EWW page to leave its view. Uppercase @kbd{Q}
is Emacsvox's buffer-killing command and is not required for this task.

To keep a website for a future session@comma{} run @kbd{M-x eww-add-bookmark @key{RET}} on the
wanted page. @kbd{M-x eww-list-bookmarks @key{RET}} opens the saved list; select a
bookmark and press @kbd{@key{RET}} to visit it. These are website bookmarks@comma{} not the
Emacsvox ebook-position marks on @kbd{m}@comma{} and they do not promise to preserve an
exact reading position on a changing page.

@node Recovering A Web Page
@subsection Recover a cluttered or incomplete page

@cindex EWW, readable view
@cindex web page, missing content

In a page buffer@comma{} lowercase @kbd{s} runs EWW's readable-view command. It tries to
isolate the main text; it is not the same action as @kbd{s} inside the focused
settings menu. To return from the filtered view@comma{} run
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-eww-restore @key{RET}}. This restores the retained canonical view
rather than changing the site. The command is also on @kbd{@key{DEL}}@comma{} but its name
avoids differences in how keyboards report Backspace and Delete.

If the retained view is unavailable or you need fresh content@comma{} @kbd{g} reloads
the current address. Reloading makes a new request and can lose form input or
change the page and reading position. Save needed notes and finish or abandon
forms deliberately before reloading. Search for a known phrase afterwards
rather than assuming the old screen position still identifies the passage.

For content that requires JavaScript or another unsupported browser feature@comma{}
use @kbd{M-x eww-browse-with-external-browser @key{RET}} to hand the page to the configured
external browser. The external browser and any screen reader then provide
its controls; Emacsvox's EWW keys do not operate there. Browser configuration@comma{}
login state and downloaded-content handling can differ. A failed or inaccessible
website is not in itself a reason to change speech engines or reset voices.

For speech preferences@comma{} open @kbd{C-e S} from EWW@. In that focused menu@comma{} @kbd{s}
toggles section-navigation autospeak and @kbd{i} changes image suppression for
subsequent rendering. The latter also changes the shared SHR image default
while preserving buffer-local overrides; it does not reload the current page.
Lowercase @kbd{w} saves those two Emacsvox defaults@comma{} @kbd{A} opens advanced settings@comma{}
and @kbd{q} returns. Returning does not undo changes you have already made.

In the same menu@comma{} @kbd{m} chooses the HTTP identity default for Emacs URL requests@comma{}
including requests outside EWW@. @emph{Browser} sends the configured browser header;
@emph{Emacs} identifies requests as @code{URL/Emacs}. EWW link following explicitly uses
browser identity regardless of this default. Arrows browse@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} applies@comma{}
and @kbd{q} cancels. Choosing an identity does not reload pages or change login
state or browser capabilities. Both HTTP identity and the outlining choice
are temporary and are excluded from @kbd{w}.

The Emacs/W3 and w3m survey is historical. Do not install either on the strength
of the Heritage manual; use EWW unless a separately maintained integration says
otherwise. The GNU Emacs Manual's @ref{EWW,EWW,,emacs,} entry provides its general introduction.

@node Messaging
@section Messaging

@cindex messaging

For indexed mail search@comma{} asynchronous results@comma{} threads@comma{} attachments@comma{} and
privacy controls@comma{} follow @ref{Notmuch Mail, , Notmuch Mail}. For another mail or messaging client@comma{}
start with its own manual and mode help@comma{} then find the corresponding Emacsvox
module in the generated reference. The presence of a module is not a substitute
for configuring the underlying account or service.

@node Editing Code
@section Editing Code

@cindex programming

Programming modes provide language-aware movement@comma{} indentation@comma{} compilation@comma{}
and navigation. Emacsvox speaks these operations and uses Voice Lock to
distinguish comments@comma{} strings@comma{} warnings@comma{} and language constructs where the mode
exposes them. Use the language mode's help for editing commands and its named
integration in the generated reference for speech details.

@node Development Environment
@section Development Environment

@cindex development tools

Compilation@comma{} diagnostics@comma{} project navigation@comma{} version control@comma{} debuggers@comma{} and
language servers can share the Emacs workspace. Configure each through its own
manual and verify its operation before combining it with other components.
Emacsvox supplies speech to many of them; their underlying operations remain
owned by those tools. @ref{Agent Shell, , Agent Shell} provides the separate guide to AI-agent
sessions and their permission@comma{} feedback@comma{} and review controls.

@subsection Magit

Magit navigation speaks the section or line you reach. Empty and
whitespace-only lines use the normal line tones. Commit hashes are silent by
default; changing the voice assigned to @code{magit-hash} affects navigation and
explicit reading consistently. Moving the section highlight preserves that
choice.

After successful staging or unstaging in a Magit view@comma{} speech reads where point
lands after the refresh. Failures remain explicit. Staging and unstaging from
an ordinary file buffer still confirm their outcome. Branch checkout@comma{} branch
and remote renaming@comma{} remote removal@comma{} and stash creation announce results.
Repository-list fetching reports partial failures. Background completion uses
the notification stream; entering status and navigating use the main stream.

Visibility commands describe collapsed@comma{} expanded@comma{} or partly expanded sections.
Copying a heading without a value reports that there is nothing to copy rather
than presenting an old kill-ring entry as a new copy.

@cindex Magit
@cindex Transient menus

Magit uses Transient menus for Push@comma{} Fetch@comma{} Commit@comma{} Branch@comma{} and other actions.
Opening one speaks a short introduction and navigation guidance. Its automatic
shortcut list remains visible without being read on the notification stream.

Up/down browses items and left/right moves between sections. The shared
navigation preferences apply to Magit as well as Emacsvox menus: wrapping
within a section is the default@comma{} and continuous navigation or stopping at
section edges can be selected. Announcement order can be item first@comma{} section
first@comma{} or dependent on the movement. Detailed command descriptions are enabled
by default; value adjustments still give brief feedback. Configure these
choices through @kbd{C-e H s n}@comma{} as described in
@ref{Aural Home,Getting started with settings menus,,emacsvox,}.

An item announcement identifies its key@comma{} relevant value@comma{} and description;
capital keys are named explicitly. Section announcements include the heading
and position according to the selected policy. Do not expect the same
section-name repetition after changing that policy.

Use @kbd{C-j} or @kbd{M-@key{RET}} to activate a selected Magit item@comma{} or type its shortcut.
@kbd{?} followed by the shortcut opens Help. @kbd{C-g} closes the current menu or
leaves its help mode. Unlike Emacsvox's own settings menus@comma{} @kbd{q} is not a
universal quit key in Transient applications. An unavailable key gives brief
recovery guidance on the main stream.

Browsing only selects an item. Activating an action can perform a Git
operation@comma{} while activating a switch changes an argument for the next action.
Listen to the description before activation.

@node Desktop Management
@section Desktop Management

@cindex buffers
@cindex windows
@cindex tabs

Buffers@comma{} windows@comma{} frames@comma{} and tabs organise the workspace. @kbd{C-x b} selects a
buffer; specialised buffer-list and tab interfaces provide their own mode
help. @ref{Audio Desktop, , Audio Desktop} explains the common navigation model so it need not be
learnt again for each application.

@node Personal Information Management
@section Personal Information Management

@cindex personal information management

Calendar@comma{} Org@comma{} and related packages define their data formats and workflows.
Emacsvox presents navigation@comma{} state changes@comma{} and structured content when those
modes are active. Begin with the application's manual and live help@comma{} then
consult its matching integration in the generated reference.

Org's focused menu at @kbd{C-e S} includes its table-editing feedback choices.
Those are separate from the shared reading mode above: the focused menu's
global default does not replace an existing buffer-local choice@comma{} and the
shared reader retains its own title and speaking preferences.

@node Desktop Applications
@section Desktop Applications

@subsection Terminal Applications

Use @kbd{M-x shell} for ordinary line-oriented shell commands. For a full terminal@comma{}
follow @ref{EAT Terminal Access, , EAT Terminal Access}@comma{} or use built-in
@ref{Running Terminal Based Applications, , Term} when no additional package is wanted. Their input@comma{} review@comma{} and
asynchronous feedback models differ; choose the appropriate chapter rather
than transferring terminal keys from one application to another.

In Shell/comint@comma{} @kbd{C-e S} opens focused speech settings. @kbd{s} toggles automatic
output speech and @kbd{b} toggles background monitoring. Background reading needs
automatic speech enabled. Both settings affect the captured buffer for its
lifetime; this menu has no Save action. @kbd{q} returns to the originating entry
or closes a directly opened focused menu.

@vindex emacsvox-shell-complete-windows-commands

In WSL Shell buffers@comma{} command-name completion skips Windows drive directories
by default. Scanning large directories such as @code{C:\Windows\System32} can
otherwise pause Emacs. Windows programs still run by name@comma{} and explicit
filename completion@comma{} including @code{/mnt/c/} paths@comma{} remains available. To include
Windows command names again@comma{} use
@kbd{M-x customize-option @key{RET} emacsvox-shell-complete-windows-commands @key{RET}}.
This option does not change native Windows or remote Shell buffers.

@subsection Forms Mode

@cindex Forms mode
@findex forms-find-file

Forms presents records from a structured text file one at a time. Emacsvox
speaks fields@comma{} record movement@comma{} searches@comma{} and state changes. Forms requires a
format specification; see @ref{Forms Mode,Forms Mode,,emacs,} in @emph{The GNU Emacs Manual} for
setup and @ref{emacsvox-forms,emacsvox-forms,,emacsvox-reference,} in the @emph{Emacsvox Reference Manual} for speech.

@subsection OCR And PDF-To-Text With PaddleOCR

@cindex OCR
@cindex PaddleOCR
@cindex PDF, converting to text
@cindex tables, OCR
@findex emacsvox-ocr

Emacsvox uses PaddleOCR PP-StructureV3 to turn an image or PDF into UTF-8
Markdown text. It recovers reading order and represents recognised tables as
Markdown or embedded HTML@. Keep @code{.md} output when structure matters; Markdown
is plain text and can be named @code{.txt} when its lightweight markup is acceptable.

This route is intended for scanned or image-only PDFs. For a born-digital PDF
with a trustworthy text layer@comma{} extracting that layer is faster and usually
more exact than OCR@. Recognition still depends on scan quality; upright@comma{}
high-contrast pages at 300 DPI are a useful starting point.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Prepare the isolated OCR environment}Prepare the isolated OCR environment


The supported baseline is CPU-only x86-64 WSL2 or GNU/Linux. Paddle's Linux
wheel requires an AVX-capable x86-64 processor and Python 3.9 through 3.13.
Do not use Ubuntu 26.04's Python 3.14 for this environment. The commands below
install Python 3.13 separately without altering Emacsvox@comma{} system Python@comma{} or
another project's packages. Installation uses about 1.7 GB; the first complete
PP-StructureV3 run downloads approximately another 1.8 GB of models@comma{} cached
for later runs.

Follow the @uref{https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/, uv installation guide} if @code{uv} is unavailable@comma{} then create the environment:

@example
uv python install 3.13
uv venv --python 3.13 --seed \
  "$HOME/.local/share/emacsvox/paddleocr-venv"
@end example

Install the tested CPU runtime and PaddleOCR's @code{all} variant@comma{} which includes
the document-parsing@comma{} table@comma{} and formula dependencies:

@example
"$HOME/.local/share/emacsvox/paddleocr-venv/bin/python" -m pip install \
  paddlepaddle==3.3.0 \
  -i https://www.paddlepaddle.org.cn/packages/stable/cpu/
"$HOME/.local/share/emacsvox/paddleocr-venv/bin/python" -m pip install \
  'paddleocr[all]==3.7.0'
@end example

From the checkout@comma{} verify that the launcher finds both packages:

@example
./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr --check
@end example

For a Debian installation@comma{} use the installed launcher@comma{} here and in the
recognition commands below:

@example
/usr/share/emacsvox/bin/emacsvox-paddleocr --check
@end example

The expected report names PaddlePaddle 3.3.0@comma{} PaddleOCR 3.7.0@comma{} and the CPU
device. A missing @code{ccache} warning is harmless for normal inference.

oneDNN@comma{} formerly MKL-DNN@comma{} is an optional CPU execution backend@comma{} not an OCR
model. The launcher leaves it disabled because PaddlePaddle 3.3.0 has an
@uref{https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/Paddle/issues/77340, upstream PIR-to-oneDNN conversion defect}. In the tested pipeline this aborts
initialisation at the formula model@comma{} although that model is not itself at
fault. Standard CPU kernels keep every recognition feature enabled@comma{} including
tables and formulas. The trade-off is speed@comma{} not capability.

@item
@anchor{Recognise and read a document}Recognise and read a document


For one image@comma{} redirect the clean Markdown result to a file. Model downloads
and progress go to standard error@comma{} not the recognised text:

@example
./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr "/path/to/page.png" > page.md
@end example

PDF input uses the same command. Paddle renders and recognises every page@comma{}
then joins them in reading order:

@example
./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr "/path/to/scanned-document.pdf" > document.md
@end example

A large PDF can require substantial CPU time: table@comma{} layout@comma{} formula@comma{}
orientation@comma{} and unwarping models are all active by default.

Inside Emacsvox@comma{} press @kbd{C-e C-o} or run @kbd{M-x emacsvox-ocr @key{RET}}. In the OCR
buffer@comma{} @kbd{o} selects an image or PDF@. Recognition runs asynchronously and adds
its result as a new page when complete. Listen for the completion cue@comma{} then
use @kbd{@key{SPC}} to read the current result@comma{} @kbd{s} to save it@comma{} or @kbd{w} to save the
whole OCR buffer. @kbd{[} and @kbd{]} move between results. @kbd{d} opens the per-user
OCR directory. Progress and errors are retained separately in
@code{*Emacsvox OCR Errors*}.

@item
@anchor{Configure recognition and diagnose the adapter}Configure recognition and diagnose the adapter


Use @kbd{M-x customize-group @key{RET} emacsvox-ocr @key{RET}} for ordinary Emacs configuration
rather than shell environment variables. The bundled adapter provides typed
options:

@itemize
@item
@code{emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-python} selects an exact Python executable; @code{nil}
uses the isolated installation above.
@item
@code{emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-language} and @code{emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-device} choose
recognition language and device.
@item
@code{emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-table-recognition} and
@code{emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-formula-recognition} control the structural
models; both default to enabled.
@item
@code{emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-document-orientation}@comma{}
@code{emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-document-unwarping}@comma{} and
@code{emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-use-text-line-orientation} control image correction.
@item
@code{emacsvox-ocr-paddleocr-enable-mkldnn} enables oneDNN/MKL-DNN acceleration@comma{}
not an OCR model. Keep it disabled for the tested PaddlePaddle 3.3.0 CPU
setup on WSL@.
@end itemize

These options apply only when @code{emacsvox-ocr-engine} names the bundled adapter.
A different engine retains the generic interface. @code{emacsvox-ocr-engine-options}
is an advanced list of literal arguments; for the bundled adapter it follows@comma{}
and can override@comma{} the typed options.

For standalone shell or automation use@comma{} @code{EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_VENV} changes the
environment directory@comma{} @code{EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_PYTHON} selects an executable@comma{} and
@code{EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_LANGUAGE} or @code{EMACSVOX_PADDLEOCR_DEVICE} changes defaults.
See @code{./bin/emacsvox-paddleocr --help} for switches. These environment settings
are not the primary Emacs configuration interface.

Direct SANE scanner-acquisition commands remain for compatibility but are not
part of this tested WSL baseline. Scan with a Windows application and select
the resulting image or PDF with @kbd{o} when that is the available acquisition
route. Scanner configuration is independent of PaddleOCR@.
@end enumerate

@node Notmuch Mail
@chapter Notmuch Mail

@cindex Notmuch mail
@cindex mail search, Notmuch
@cindex asynchronous search, Notmuch
@findex notmuch

Notmuch provides the mail index and Emacs interface. Install compatible
command-line and Emacs components@comma{} then configure the mail store and database
using the @uref{https://notmuchmail.org/doc/latest/notmuch-emacs.html, Notmuch Emacs manual}. Emacsvox does not fetch or send mail@comma{}
create the database@comma{} or replace Notmuch's commands. It adds structured speech
to Hello@comma{} Search@comma{} and Show; Tree has more limited support@comma{} described below.

Follow this chapter to search@comma{} read@comma{} and manage mail with speech@comma{} then adjust
which information is spoken automatically. The generated
@ref{emacsvox-notmuch,emacsvox-notmuch,,emacsvox-reference,} section in the @emph{Emacsvox Reference Manual} provides the
complete command and option reference.

@menu
* Start Notmuch: Starting Notmuch.  Open Hello or start a query with the speech integration loaded.
* Use Hello and start a search: Notmuch Hello And Searches.  Choose a search and understand asynchronous results and refreshes.
* Navigate and inspect Search results: Notmuch Search Results.  Read thread summaries, inspect full fields, and open a result.
* Read a thread and its MIME parts: Notmuch Messages And Attachments.  Read messages, inspect attachments, and choose explicit MIME actions.
* Change tags and archive state: Notmuch Tags And Archiving.  Hear the actual outcome of tag and archive commands.
* Choose fields@comma{} status@comma{} and privacy: Notmuch Speech And Privacy.  Set spoken fields, automatic limits, completion policy, and privacy.
* Know the support boundaries: Notmuch Support Boundaries.  Distinguish supported views, limited Tree support, and composition.
* Troubleshoot the live integration: Notmuch Troubleshooting.  Check live keys, package identity, and the speech diagnostics.
@end menu

@node Starting Notmuch
@section Start Notmuch

@cindex starting Notmuch
@cindex Notmuch Hello
@findex notmuch-search

Run @kbd{M-x notmuch} to open Hello. With the standard Emacsvox global maps
loaded@comma{} @kbd{C-' m} runs @code{notmuch-search} directly and asks for a query.
Emacsvox loads its integration when Notmuch loads and marks new Hello@comma{} Search@comma{}
Show@comma{} and Tree buffers with Notmuch semantic context.

Loading speech support does not alter mail@comma{} tags@comma{} files@comma{} or Notmuch
configuration. Notmuch owns database@comma{} tag@comma{} archive@comma{} attachment@comma{} and composition
operations; Emacsvox presents the result of the action you invoke.

@node Notmuch Hello And Searches
@section Use Hello and start a search

@cindex saved searches, Notmuch
@cindex widgets, Notmuch Hello

Moving among Hello widgets speaks each saved search's label and displayed
message count@comma{} or identifies the button or link reached. Use these common
Hello commands:

@table @kbd
@item TAB
@code{widget-forward} reaches the next search@comma{} field@comma{} button@comma{} or link.
@item <backtab>
@code{widget-backward} reaches the preceding widget.
@item RET
@code{widget-button-press} activates the widget; on a saved search it starts
the query.
@item j
@code{notmuch-jump-search} chooses a configured search.
@item g
@code{notmuch-refresh-this-buffer} refreshes Hello.
@item m
@code{notmuch-mua-new-mail} starts composition@comma{} handled by Message mode and
the separate @code{emacsvox-message} integration.
@end table

Press @kbd{?} in a Notmuch view or @kbd{C-h m} for the bindings supplied by your
installed Notmuch version. A search can display its first result before all
rows finish rendering@comma{} so an initial message announcement and a later count
need not arrive together.

@subsection Understand asynchronous search completion

@cindex search results, asynchronous rendering
@cindex notification stream, Notmuch
@vindex emacsvox-notmuch-search-completion-style

The default @code{adaptive} policy gives useful early feedback without rereading
content after you have moved on. For a search you start@comma{} other than a refresh@comma{}
a progress cue is given while its buffer remains selected. The first complete
result can then be spoken while the search continues@comma{} using the same summary@comma{}
voices@comma{} and status cues as thread navigation. This never moves point or reads
an empty row.

When the new search remains selected@comma{} you have issued no command in it@comma{} and
no input is pending@comma{} the first result is spoken once on the main stream.
Completion reports the final thread count on the notification stream without
repeating that result. If completion occurs before an early result could be
announced@comma{} it reports the count and result at point@comma{} or a zero-thread summary.

If you navigate@comma{} invoke another command@comma{} or leave before completion@comma{} the later
announcement is only a generic count on the notification stream. It does not
reread the selected row or disclose the query@comma{} sender@comma{} recipients@comma{} subject@comma{}
tags@comma{} or attachment metadata. Killing the owning search buffer silently
cancels that delivery.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Refresh an existing result}Refresh an existing result


An explicit refresh differs from a new search. It queues only the generic
count on the main stream@comma{} without interrupting speech already playing.
Navigation can interrupt this announcement@comma{} including after you leave the
search buffer before the refresh finishes. Refresh completion never rereads
the selected row.

@item
@anchor{Choose less automatic feedback}Choose less automatic feedback


@code{summary} gives a generic count and completion cue; @code{cue} gives only the cue;
@code{silent} suppresses successful completion. These policies use the same
main-stream selection for refreshes. Other search summaries use the
notification stream. Failures remain audible and generic with every policy;
refresh failures also use the main stream. The deliberate global refresh-all
command remains silent.

Use the focused settings described under @ref{Notmuch fields and privacy, , Notmuch fields and privacy} to choose
a policy. @ref{Aural Speech Output,Speech Output And Notifications,,emacsvox,} explains the general stream behaviour;
the refresh rules above are specific to this integration.
@end enumerate

@node Notmuch Search Results
@section Navigate and inspect Search results

@cindex Notmuch Search
@cindex search fields, Notmuch

Thread movement reads configured semantic fields and status cues. At a boundary@comma{}
speech identifies the beginning or end instead of repeating the current row.
Emacsvox makes the arrow keys equivalent to Notmuch's next and previous thread
commands.

@table @kbd
@item n
Run @code{notmuch-search-next-thread}.
@item <down>
Also run @code{notmuch-search-next-thread}.
@item p
Run @code{notmuch-search-previous-thread}.
@item <up>
Also run @code{notmuch-search-previous-thread}.
@item RET
Run @code{notmuch-search-show-thread}. Show lands on a useful visible body
line or leaf MIME part and presents the opened message.
@item SPC
Run @code{notmuch-search-scroll-up}. This scrolls the viewport rather than
selecting a semantic thread.
@item C-c C-p
Run @code{emacsvox-notmuch-speak-search-details} to read all configured fields
for the result at point without automatic content limits.
@item +
Run @code{notmuch-search-add-tag}.
@item -
Run @code{notmuch-search-remove-tag}.
@item *
Run @code{notmuch-search-tag-all} for the selected results.
@item a
Run @code{notmuch-search-archive-thread}.
@item g
Run @code{notmuch-refresh-this-buffer}; completion follows the interruptible
refresh policy above.
@end table

Search defaults to authors@comma{} subject@comma{} relative date@comma{} matched and total message
counts@comma{} and ordinary tags. Status tags assigned auditory cues are not repeated
as words. @ref{Notmuch fields and privacy, , Notmuch fields and privacy} explains omission and ordering of sensitive fields.

@node Notmuch Messages And Attachments
@section Read a thread and its MIME parts

@cindex Notmuch Show
@cindex MIME parts, Notmuch
@cindex attachments, Notmuch

Opening a thread selects its newest unread message among the open messages
matching the search. If there is none@comma{} Notmuch's initial selection is retained.
Emacsvox speaks the selected message's summary and first visible body line@comma{}
skipping hidden alternative bodies. When no body text is visible@comma{} point falls
back to a leaf MIME-part button where possible. That button announces a
bounded filename@comma{} content type@comma{} and size.

Line reading speaks a visible sender row even if the preceding message ends
with folded quoted text; it does not unfold the quote. In a short graphical
window@comma{} Down advances through ordinary body text before speaking the next
line@comma{} even when the current line is only partly visible. Images or text taller
than the window retain Emacs's partial scrolling.

Tab and Shift-Tab move through the same sequence of links and MIME controls
in either direction. HTML links retain their link voice without a navigation
earcon; MIME controls keep their part descriptions and cues. Link navigation
does not speak the destination URL on the notification stream.

When a row contains one visible MIME control and otherwise only whitespace@comma{}
you can use Enter or the part actions below from the indentation or trailing
space. Your reading position stays on that row. Enter shows or hides content
that Notmuch can display; otherwise it uses
@code{notmuch-show-part-button-default-action}@comma{} normally saving the part. Speech
confirms the visibility change or the save/view action once. Rows containing
other text or multiple controls require you to move onto the intended control.

@table @kbd
@item n
Run @code{notmuch-show-next-open-message}.
@item p
Run @code{notmuch-show-previous-open-message}.
@item N
Run @code{notmuch-show-next-message}@comma{} including closed messages.
@item P
Run @code{notmuch-show-previous-message}@comma{} including closed messages.
@item SPC
Run @code{notmuch-show-advance-and-archive}. Within a message@comma{} speech reads the
newly visible page; at a message transition it reads the new message;
at the thread end it reports the actual archive outcome and destination.
@item DEL
Run @code{notmuch-show-rewind} and read the resulting page or message.
@item RET
Activate the button or the single MIME control on its row. Elsewhere@comma{}
run @code{notmuch-show-toggle-message} and cue the resulting visibility.
@item M-RET
Run @code{notmuch-show-open-or-close-all} to open every message and report the
state and total@comma{} for example ``Opened all 5 messages.'' @kbd{C-u M-@key{RET}} closes
every message and reports the corresponding total.
@item C-c C-p
Run @code{emacsvox-notmuch-speak-show-position} for the message ordinal@comma{} total@comma{}
and full configured details. On a MIME-part button it instead reads the
complete part description@comma{} including its full filename.
@item TAB
Run @code{notmuch-show-next-button} and identify the button or MIME part.
@item <backtab>
Run @code{notmuch-show-previous-button} and identify the button or MIME part.
@item w
Run @code{notmuch-show-save-attachments}. Speech confirms when the command
returns@comma{} but Notmuch exposes no per-attachment result here. The
confirmation therefore cannot distinguish partial skips.
@item . s
Run @code{notmuch-show-save-part} on the current MIME part.
@item . v
Run @code{notmuch-show-view-part}.
@item . o
Run @code{notmuch-show-interactively-view-part} and choose an external action.
@item . m
Run @code{notmuch-show-choose-mime-of-part}.
@end table

Viewing@comma{} saving@comma{} overwriting@comma{} or launching a MIME part is an explicit Notmuch
operation. Navigation and detail inspection never open an attachment or an
external viewer by themselves.

@node Notmuch Tags And Archiving
@section Change tags and archive state

@cindex tags, Notmuch
@cindex archiving, Notmuch

Emacsvox compares displayed tags before and after an interactive change and
reports actual additions and removals. Adding an existing tag or removing an
absent tag reports ``Tags unchanged''. Errors and cancelled commands do not
produce success feedback.

These Show commands receive result-aware feedback:

@table @kbd
@item +
Run @code{notmuch-show-add-tag} on the current message.
@item -
Run @code{notmuch-show-remove-tag}.
@item *
Run @code{notmuch-show-tag-all} across the thread.
@item a
Run @code{notmuch-show-archive-message-then-next-or-next-thread}.
@item A
Run @code{notmuch-show-archive-thread-then-next}.
@end table

Archive feedback distinguishes a real change@comma{} unchanged archive tags@comma{} and an
unset @code{notmuch-archive-tags}. Movement wrappers combine the result instead of
duplicating the feedback of nested tag or archive commands.

@node Notmuch Speech And Privacy
@section Choose fields@comma{} status@comma{} and privacy

@anchor{Notmuch fields and privacy}

@cindex privacy, Notmuch speech
@cindex fields, Notmuch speech
@cindex status cues, Notmuch
@cindex automatic speech limits, Notmuch

From a Notmuch buffer@comma{} press @kbd{C-e S} for focused speech settings@comma{} or reach it
through @kbd{C-e H j c}. The same menu is available from Hello@comma{} Search@comma{} Show@comma{} and
Tree. Availability of settings in Tree does not add Tree-specific navigation
or message formatting.

@kbd{c} opens explained completion choices. Arrows browse without applying@comma{}
@kbd{@key{RET}} applies and returns@comma{} and @kbd{q} cancels. @kbd{?} followed by a choice's key
opens spoken Help; @kbd{q} returns to that browsed choice. In the parent menu@comma{}
@kbd{f} and @kbd{b} control per-field character and UTF-8 byte limits@comma{} while @kbd{t} and
@kbd{B} control total limits. Shift-left/right adjusts a selected limit by 100;
@kbd{@key{RET}} accepts an exact positive integer or @code{none}. Disabling one limit leaves
the others active. @kbd{w} saves these four limits and completion policy as the
five displayed global defaults@comma{} not every Notmuch option.

For ordered search/message fields@comma{} use @kbd{s} and @kbd{m}. Uppercase @kbd{S} and @kbd{M}
edit the text separating spoken search and message fields respectively;
punctuation policy affects how that text sounds. @kbd{i} and @kbd{I} open the
status-tag and fallback-cue maps. These linked Customize editors retain custom
formatter functions and use their own Apply@comma{} Save@comma{} and Reset actions. Returning
with @kbd{q} does not save them. @kbd{p} opens Presentation Options and @kbd{A} advanced
settings. Search at @kbd{C-e H /} finds both separators after Notmuch is loaded.
The full group is also available through
@kbd{M-x customize-group @key{RET} emacsvox-notmuch @key{RET}}. These speech settings do not
alter Notmuch's database or its own display configuration.

@subsection Choose the information in a spoken summary

@table @code
@item emacsvox-notmuch-search-result-fields
Defaults to @code{(authors subject date count tags)}. Remove sensitive fields@comma{}
reorder them@comma{} or add a formatter function.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-show-message-fields
Defaults to @code{(from date to cc tags attachments)}. Show omits the subject
but includes To and Cc; remove @code{to}@comma{} @code{cc}@comma{} or other fields when privacy
requires it.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-search-field-separator
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-show-field-separator
Set the text between configured fields. Both default to comma and space.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-search-status-icons
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-show-status-icons
Map unread@comma{} replied@comma{} forwarded@comma{} flagged@comma{} and personal tags to cues or
silence. Removing an entry makes that status available to ordinary tag
speech. When @code{mail-message-status-cues} is enabled in Presentation
Options@comma{} its rules own the shared semantic status cues; the maps still
suppress the corresponding spoken status tags.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-search-completion-style
Select @code{adaptive}@comma{} @code{summary}@comma{} @code{cue}@comma{} or @code{silent} using the completion
behaviour described earlier.
@end table

@subsection Understand limits and full inspection

Mail-controlled text is bounded before automatic speech and Aural history.
Defaults are 256 characters and 1@comma{}024 UTF-8 bytes per field@comma{} then 1@comma{}000
characters and 4@comma{}096 bytes for the complete automatic presentation.

@table @code
@item emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-field-character-limit
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-field-byte-limit
Bound each automatic field before formatting and submission.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-total-character-limit
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-automatic-total-byte-limit
Bound the final automatic Notmuch presentation.
@item emacsvox-notmuch-mime-node-limit
@itemx emacsvox-notmuch-mime-depth-limit
Default to 4@comma{}096 cons nodes and depth 64@comma{} limiting inspection of malformed@comma{}
cyclic@comma{} broad@comma{} or deeply nested MIME metadata. An incomplete scan reports
a conservative attachment lower bound@comma{} not a claim that no attachment exists.
@end table

A shortened Search or Show announcement explains what was omitted and points
to @kbd{C-c C-p} for full configured details. A shortened Hello item points to
@kbd{@key{RET}}. Explicit Search and Show inspection deliberately bypasses automatic
content limits; use it only when speaking the full configured fields is
appropriate. These limits are not a general privacy guarantee for every
explicit command or another application's output.

@subsection Understand retained and notification content

@cindex Aural history, mail privacy

Main-stream Notmuch presentations can enter bounded Aural history. By default@comma{}
Emacsvox retains at most 20 presentations and 4@comma{}096 UTF-8 bytes of speech
preview per record. Records contain frozen data@comma{} source names@comma{} and positions@comma{}
but never retain source buffers. Inspect them through @kbd{C-e H f h} or
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-aural-list-recent-feedback}. Set
@code{emacsvox-aural-presentation-history-limit} to zero to disable this history.

Generic asynchronous completion contains no query or message metadata@comma{} but a
notification-stream completion may be retained by the notification subsystem.
Choose @code{cue} or @code{silent} if even a generic thread count is unwanted. Review
notification-device and logging settings separately from mail field choices.

@node Notmuch Support Boundaries
@section Know the support boundaries

@cindex Notmuch Tree support
@cindex message composition, Notmuch

@table @asis
@item Hello
Widget navigation@comma{} view lifecycle@comma{} and searches have Notmuch-specific feedback.
@item Search
Results@comma{} asynchronous lifecycle@comma{} tags@comma{} archiving@comma{} opening@comma{} and configured
detail inspection have Notmuch-specific feedback.
@item Show
Messages@comma{} MIME parts@comma{} pages@comma{} visibility@comma{} tags@comma{} archiving@comma{} and explicit
inspection have Notmuch-specific feedback.
@item Tree
Receives @code{emacsvox-aural-module} context only. Core speech can operate@comma{}
but this module has no Tree-specific row formatter@comma{} navigation@comma{} mutation@comma{}
visibility@comma{} or asynchronous lifecycle.
@item Composition
Standard Message mode owns composition. Speech after new-mail@comma{} reply@comma{}
forward@comma{} or resume transitions belongs to @code{emacsvox-message}@comma{} not the
Notmuch integration.
@end table

There is no independent Notmuch speech-disable or unload command. Its advice@comma{}
hooks@comma{} and Search arrow bindings remain installed for the Emacs session after
loading. Repeated loading is idempotent and does not duplicate them.

@node Notmuch Troubleshooting
@section Troubleshoot the live integration

@cindex troubleshooting, Notmuch
@cindex byte-code, Notmuch integration

Use @kbd{?} or @kbd{C-h m} in the affected view to check its current keys and
@kbd{M-x notmuch-version} to identify the package. Consult Notmuch's own manual
for its behaviour before treating a database or display problem as a speech
problem.

For bindings@comma{} loaded source versus byte-code@comma{} presentation rules@comma{} sounds@comma{} or
backend issues@comma{} open Aural Doctor through @kbd{C-e H D d} or
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-aural-doctor}. Recent feedback at @kbd{C-e H f h} or
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-aural-list-recent-feedback} shows the bounded presentation
submitted for an action. These diagnostics do not query or modify mail.

For source-level work@comma{} @ref{Integration Maintenance, , Integration Maintenance} contains the build@comma{} test@comma{}
renderer@comma{} and asynchronous lifecycle contracts.

@node Agent Shell
@chapter Agent Shell

@cindex Agent Shell

@cindex artificial intelligence agents

Agent Shell is an Emacs package for interactive AI-agent sessions. Emacsvox
adds speech and structured navigation to its prompts@comma{} responses@comma{} permissions@comma{}
and other activity. Agent Shell remains responsible for providers@comma{} sessions@comma{}
prompt editing@comma{} and permission decisions. Install and configure Agent Shell
using @uref{https://github.com/xenodium/agent-shell, its own documentation}; Emacsvox requires version 0.85.3 or newer.

This chapter follows a session from writing a prompt through receiving and
reviewing an answer@comma{} then explains speech choices and recovery. The generated
@ref{emacsvox-agent-shell,emacsvox-agent-shell,,emacsvox-reference,} section in the @emph{Emacsvox Reference Manual} lists the
complete Emacsvox command and option interfaces.

@menu
* Start and control a session: Starting An Agent Shell Session.  Write prompts, follow responses, steer work, and manage queued input.
* Answer an agent's question: Answering Agent Questions.  Review and complete an optional structured question form.
* Choose automatic speech and privacy: Agent Shell Speech And Privacy.  Choose session feedback, background disclosure, and automatic limits.
* Respond to urgent permissions: Agent Shell Permissions.  Find and decide a permission request without confusing routine speech.
* Inspect a response on demand: Reading Agent Responses.  Read a completed response in full or request a structural overview.
* Navigate transcript blocks: Agent Shell Transcript Navigation.  Move among responses, prompts, headings, links, tools, and errors.
* Read and copy source blocks: Agent Shell Source Blocks.  Read logical code, copy it, and open a change review.
* Navigate and copy rendered tables: Agent Shell Tables.  Read table cells, rows, and columns, and understand local key conflicts.
* Recover from interruption or failure: Agent Shell Recovery.  Interrupt work, inspect the previous response, and recover the session.
@end menu

@node Starting An Agent Shell Session
@section Start and control a session

Load Emacsvox and start Agent Shell with @kbd{M-x agent-shell}.  Emacsvox loads
its integration after Agent Shell and activates both new and already existing
shell buffers.  Run @kbd{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable} only when manually
re-enabling support after it was disabled or while diagnosing a live reload.

@subsection Write and edit prompts

By default@comma{} reaching the live prompt lets you type immediately. The
@emph{Input activation} setting offers an explicit editing step if you prefer to
keep browsing at the end of the transcript. Open @kbd{C-e S i}@comma{} choose @emph{Automatic}
or @emph{Explicit}@comma{} and press @kbd{w} in the parent menu to save. Settings search also
finds @emph{Agent Shell input activation}. The choice applies to existing and new
shells; buffer-local overrides take precedence.

With @emph{Explicit} selected@comma{} @kbd{J}@comma{} @kbd{U} and the other navigation letters keep their
navigation meanings at the live prompt. Press @kbd{@key{RET}} on its label@comma{} on blank
padding immediately above it@comma{} or anywhere in the draft to activate editing@comma{}
or @kbd{C-c i} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-edit-input}) from anywhere in the shell to
focus the draft. From the transcript or prompt label@comma{} this moves to the end of the draft;
within input it preserves point. By default@comma{} Emacsvox says “Editing.”
Ordinary typing@comma{} paste and editing commands then work@comma{} and submission uses Agent Shell's normal
commands. @kbd{@key{RET}} on links@comma{} permission buttons and question controls retains its
existing action; only the inactive live prompt uses it to start editing.

The @kbd{C-c i} and @kbd{C-c e} bindings apply only to inline shells using Explicit
activation. Emacsvox's Org support can assign @kbd{C-c i} globally to link
insertion; Explicit mode uses it for input focus instead. Use
@kbd{M-x org-insert-link} for that action while editing@comma{} or choose Automatic to
restore the ordinary binding.

Press @kbd{C-c e} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-browse}) to return to browsing at point
without sending or discarding the draft. Emacsvox says “Browsing.”
Moving out of the live input also returns to browsing and keeps the destination's
navigation speech; moving between its lines keeps editing active.
While editing@comma{} @kbd{M-<} moves to the beginning of the draft and reads its first
line without leaving editing. To reach the top of the transcript@comma{} press
@kbd{C-c e} followed by @kbd{M-<}. Automatic input and viewport editors retain their
ordinary @kbd{M-<} binding.
A successful submission or queueing
returns to browsing@comma{} while a rejected submission keeps its draft editable.
The mode line shows @emph{Agent browse} or @emph{Agent edit}. Reading the first input
line says “Input” along with the agent's busy or waiting status.
Browsing protects the draft from ordinary typing@comma{} paste and deletion.

For sound-only feedback when activating editing or returning to browsing@comma{} open
@kbd{C-e S e} and choose @emph{Earcon}. @emph{Speech} is the default; @emph{Both} combines speech
and sound. Editing uses @code{08-pop-and-collapse-on} and browsing uses
@code{08-pop-and-collapse-off}. These recordings are bundled with Emacsvox as
the @code{agent-input-on} and @code{agent-input-off} cues@comma{} available with every sound
pack. You can assign different recordings to those cues in a personal pack.
Auditory icons must be enabled to hear these sounds.
With @emph{Earcon} or @emph{Both} selected@comma{} arrowing out of the input field also plays
the browsing sound without replacing the destination's speech or adding
“Browsing.” Moving between lines within the field does not play it.
The choice applies immediately; buffer-local overrides take precedence.
Press @kbd{w} in the parent menu to save the global default. Submission
confirmations remain separate from this setting.

The live prompt retains Emacs undo across these transitions. Interactive text
yanks speak the inserted text@comma{} including when a prefix argument selects a
different kill-ring entry.
Image attachment keeps Agent Shell's existing feedback. Submitted transcript
text is history rather than editable prompt input.  Press @kbd{C-c C-c} to ask Agent Shell to interrupt
an active request; its normal confirmation setting still applies.

With Agent Shell's persistent prompt enabled@comma{} you can keep typing while the
agent works. Brackets and navigation letters are ordinary text while editing
that prompt. Reading the live prompt says “Agent busy” while the agent works.
A pending permission is announced
as “Waiting for permission”; a pending question says “Waiting for an answer.”
These cues also precede the first line of a draft;
later lines keep ordinary reading feedback.  “Ready for input” is reserved for
an empty prompt when the agent is idle and input activation is Automatic.

Quoting a selected region@comma{} sending source context@comma{}
dropping files@comma{} and inserting shell-command output can add to that live draft
while the agent works@comma{} including in Explicit browse mode. These insertions do
not submit the draft or activate editing. If there is no live prompt while
busy@comma{} Agent Shell offers its queue editor instead.
Use @kbd{M-x yank-media} to attach a clipboard image on a display that supports
native clipboard access. Image previews survive queueing and steering.
The animated busy marker does not add repeated speech; reading the prompt
continues to give its current state.

The dedicated viewport prompt editor always opens ready for typing@comma{} regardless
of the inline input setting. @kbd{M-x agent-shell-prompt-compose} transfers the
inline draft to that editor. Continuing a list announces its bullet or next
number and indentation.  Indenting and dedenting announce the new indentation;
leaving an empty list item announces that the list ended.

@subsection Follow a response and steer a running turn

Emacsvox speaks routine content only at complete turn boundaries.  A network
pause does not make a partial answer sound complete.  A cancelled@comma{} refused@comma{} or
failed turn reports its outcome without replacing the last successfully
completed response.

In Explicit inline input and the viewport editor@comma{} Emacsvox announces whether a
prompt was submitted@comma{} queued@comma{} or sent for steering. Inline input returns to
browsing without another state announcement. The viewport also reports whether
its editor stays open.
The normal and override send commands follow Agent Shell's configured busy
routes@comma{} including queueing when the provider does not support steering.
“Steering sent” confirms dispatch; the agent's acceptance or refusal follows
separately.

When you use @kbd{M-x agent-shell-prompt-steer}@comma{} Emacsvox confirms whether the
agent accepted the steer@comma{} declined it@comma{} or started a separate turn.  A separate
turn is announced as still in progress; it is not reported as complete until
Agent Shell supplies a completion boundary.  Server-pushed turns@comma{} when
supported by the provider@comma{} follow the same completed-response speech policy.

@subsection Work with queued prompts

Queued prompts announce when they start and how many remain. Recent Agent Shell
versions merge queued prompts into one turn by default; Emacsvox announces how
many prompts started together. Set @code{agent-shell-prompt-queue-merge} to nil to
send them one turn at a time. After cancellation@comma{} Agent Shell asks whether to
continue with the queue. If you decline@comma{} or a failed turn leaves prompts waiting@comma{}
the queue announcement gives its count and the command
@kbd{M-x agent-shell-prompt-queue-resume}. Queue-start feedback
follows the session's speech level; paused-queue warnings follow
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-signal-processing}.
Agent Shell also asks before killing a shell or exiting Emacs when queued
prompts would be lost. Declining that question keeps the prompts available.

@subsection Check session settings and status

Model@comma{} mode@comma{} thought-level@comma{} and other session setting changes announce their
confirmed values.  Background setting announcements identify the session.
When grouped choices share a name@comma{} confirmation includes the group shown by
Agent Shell's completion menu.

Use @kbd{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-header} to hear the full session header.
When @code{agent-shell-show-cost-indicator} is enabled and the provider reports a
positive cost@comma{} this includes the cumulative cost with Agent Shell's currency
and rounding.  Brief announcements when entering a session stay concise.

@node Answering Agent Questions
@section Answer an agent's question

Some providers ask you to complete a structured form rather than answer in
ordinary prompt text. Agent Shell's form support is experimental and off by
default. To try it@comma{} set @code{agent-shell-elicitation--experimental-feature-enabled}
to non-nil after loading Agent Shell and start a new session so the agent learns
that forms are supported. This upstream experimental setting may change.

When a form arrives@comma{} Emacsvox announces that an answer is needed and reads the
question. Automatic question feedback follows the session's speech level;
@code{quiet} suppresses it. Explicit reading and actions still speak.

Use @kbd{@key{TAB}} and @kbd{S-@key{TAB}} to move between controls in the shell or viewport.
Emacsvox reads the field name@comma{} whether it is required@comma{} and its current value or
selection state. Press @kbd{@key{RET}} to select an option@comma{} toggle a checkbox@comma{} or edit a
text or number field. Changes are confirmed after they take effect. When an
option has a preview@comma{} @kbd{?} opens or closes it; opening it reads its contents.
The preview key applies only to controls that offer a preview.

Choose @emph{Submit} to send the answers or @emph{Decline} to decline the question.
Missing required answers are reported without announcing successful submission.
@kbd{C-c C-c} interrupts the running request@comma{} including any pending form. To return
to the latest question@comma{} use @kbd{M-x agent-shell-elicitation-jump-to-latest-form}.
The next-field and previous-field commands are also available through @kbd{M-x}.

@node Agent Shell Speech And Privacy
@section Choose automatic speech and privacy

The focused session defaults to @code{response}: completed answers and routine
completion feedback are presented@comma{} while thought@comma{} plan@comma{} and tool chatter is
suppressed.  An unfocused session defaults to @code{notify}: its notification
names the session but does not disclose response or tool content.

@table @code
@item full
Speak responses plus configured thought@comma{} plan@comma{} and tool detail.  Full tool
output can contain commands@comma{} file contents@comma{} or other sensitive provider data.
@item response
Speak completed agent responses while suppressing routine thought@comma{} plan@comma{} and
tool content.
@item notify
Present routine completion without reading the response body.
@item quiet
Suppress routine automatic feedback.  Explicit inspection remains available.
@item auto
Use @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-foreground-speech-level} or
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-background-speech-level} according to focus.
@end table

Press @kbd{C-c C-q} to run
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-select-speech-level} and choose the current
session's level@comma{} including @code{auto}.  Press @kbd{C-c C-S-q} to run
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-select-background-speech-level} and change the
shared background default.
@kbd{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-cycle-speech-level} cycles toward less routine
speech; use a prefix argument to restore @code{auto}.

@subsection Adjust speech in the settings menu

Press @kbd{C-e S} in an Agent Shell buffer or its viewport for focused Emacsvox
settings. The same menu is available through @kbd{C-e H j c} and the integration
chooser. @kbd{s} opens choices for the temporary session override. @kbd{f} and @kbd{b}
open foreground and background defaults; @kbd{t} and @kbd{o} open thought and tool-output
choices. Each list starts on the current value and explains its choices.
Browse with arrows@comma{} press @kbd{@key{RET}} to apply and return@comma{} or @kbd{q} to cancel. Press
@kbd{?} followed by a choice's key for its effect and scope; @kbd{q} returns from
Help. @kbd{? ?} opens the menu overview.

@kbd{i} chooses inline input activation; @kbd{e} chooses speech@comma{} an earcon or both for
editing and browsing feedback@comma{} described under
@ref{Starting An Agent Shell Session, , Start and control a session}. Both share this menu's explicit Save action.

The @kbd{p}@comma{} @kbd{c} and @kbd{l} controls toggle permission speech@comma{} tool-call announcements
and processing cues. Thought and tool content still depend on the effective
speech level.

@kbd{T} opens the shared Tables settings page described in
@ref{Reading Markdown And Org Tables, , Reading Markdown and Org tables}. It has separate buffer Apply@comma{} common Save
and mode Save actions.

The @kbd{a}@comma{} @kbd{n} and @kbd{k} controls set automatic-content@comma{} line-navigation and
block-navigation character limits. @kbd{@key{RET}} accepts an exact positive integer or
@code{none}; Shift-left/right adjusts by 100. For line navigation@comma{} @code{none} restores
the core long-line policy. For the other limits it allows complete content.
@kbd{w} saves the displayed global defaults through Customize@comma{} excluding the
session override. The @kbd{x} and @kbd{d} editors below have their own Save actions.
@kbd{A} opens advanced settings; @kbd{q} returns. From the direct
@kbd{C-e S} entry@comma{} @kbd{q} closes the menu and leaves you in the captured buffer.

Use @kbd{x} to edit the words spoken for rendered status icons without changing
their display. Use @kbd{d} to edit the delay@comma{} in seconds@comma{} for grouping unsolicited
out-of-turn messages. Normal submitted responses still follow turn completion;
this delay does not determine when they are spoken. These global options open
Customize with separate Apply and Save actions; @kbd{q} returns to the same menu
entry. Search at @kbd{C-e H /} finds status labels and out-of-turn speech delay
after Agent Shell is loaded.

Thought content defaults to an icon under
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-thought-process}.  Tool feedback is separately
controlled by @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-tool-calls} and
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-tool-output-verbosity}.  Selecting background
@code{response} or @code{full}@comma{} thought @code{speak}@comma{} or full tool output is an
explicit disclosure choice.

At @code{full} speech level@comma{} tool verbosity @code{full} reads the tool's rendered output@comma{}
including Codex command output and textual diffs.  Changed output can be read
even when the tool's status has not changed; identical updates are suppressed.
Tool verbosity @code{status} plays status cues as tools start and finish.
Tool updates@comma{} steering outcomes@comma{} queue changes@comma{} and confirmed setting changes
use the notification lane@comma{} leaving navigation speech independent.

@subsection Understand automatic speech limits

Automatic response@comma{} tool@comma{} error@comma{} permission@comma{} and similar text is limited by
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-automatic-content-max-characters}@comma{} which defaults to
2@comma{}000 characters.  A shortened presentation gives exact omitted character and
line counts and tells you where to inspect the full content.  The complete text
rendered by Agent Shell stays in its buffer; Emacsvox limits the automatic
announcement rather than editing that displayed text. Agent Shell separately shortens tool
output lines longer than 32@comma{}768 characters@comma{} keeping their beginning and end with
an omitted-character count. Full tool speech reads that notice; the removed
middle is not available by reading the buffer.

@node Agent Shell Permissions
@section Respond to urgent permissions

Permission requests are separate from routine speech levels because they can
block the agent and require a decision.  With
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-permissions} enabled@comma{} a request interrupts the
appropriate speech stream@comma{} identifies the session and requested operation@comma{} and
announces its choices.  Provider-controlled descriptions and choice labels are
bounded@comma{} but the actual permission controls remain in Agent Shell.

If the provider supplies new operation details after asking permission@comma{}
Emacsvox announces the changed description or input.  Identical updates and
routine output do not repeat the warning.  The permission controls remain the
place to inspect and decide the request.

If the request belongs to another session@comma{} switch to the named buffer.  Use
@kbd{M-x agent-shell-jump-to-latest-permission-button-row} to find its latest
permission row@comma{} or use Agent Shell's @kbd{@key{TAB}} and @kbd{S-@key{TAB}} item navigation.
Move among the buttons and press @kbd{@key{RET}}@comma{} or use a direct choice key displayed
by Agent Shell.  Choice keys are supplied by Agent Shell and the provider@comma{} so
the visible permission row is authoritative.

Error blocks remain exceptional feedback rather than routine transcript
chatter.  Lifecycle error announcements follow
@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-signal-processing}; rendered error content remains
available in the transcript even when routine response speech is quiet.

@node Reading Agent Responses
@section Inspect a response on demand

These commands work from the shell and its associated viewports:

@table @kbd
@item C-c r
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-last-response}.  It reads the latest
successfully completed response in full without moving point.  This deliberate
operation does not inherit the automatic 2@comma{}000-character limit.  While a new
turn is busy it explicitly identifies and reads the prior completed response.
@item C-c R
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-response-overview}.  It reports line@comma{}
heading@comma{} source-block@comma{} and table counts plus a bounded opening preview.
@end table

These commands remain usable at @code{notify} and @code{quiet}.  If no turn has
completed@comma{} they say that no completed response is available rather than
presenting a streaming partial or failed answer.

@node Agent Shell Transcript Navigation
@section Navigate transcript blocks

Agent Shell responses contain semantic prompt@comma{} response@comma{} thought@comma{} activity@comma{}
tool@comma{} plan@comma{} permission@comma{} error@comma{} source-block@comma{} and table locations.  Emacsvox
navigation reports the block type@comma{} visibility@comma{} and a bounded body preview.

Up and Down play the ellipses cue when you enter a collapsed heading@comma{} such as
Capabilities@comma{} Models@comma{} or Notices. Wrapped continuation rows do not repeat the
cue; leaving and returning to the heading plays it again. The optional block
visibility cue replaces this default cue. Reading the current line with
@kbd{C-e l} continues to indicate hidden content.

@table @kbd
@item h / H
Use @kbd{h} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-heading}) or
@kbd{H} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-heading}).
Move to the next or previous visible Markdown heading and speak its text.
@item k / K
Use @kbd{k} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-source-block}) or
@kbd{K} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-source-block}).
Move to the next or previous source block and announce its language and
line count. Inside a table@comma{} lowercase @kbd{k} remains the table
copy prefix; use @kbd{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-next-source-block} to jump forward
to code from there.
@item u / U
Use @kbd{u} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-user-prompt}) or
@kbd{U} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-user-prompt}).
Move to the next or previous submitted user prompt@comma{} skipping the live editor.
@item j / J
Use @kbd{j} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-response}) or
@kbd{J} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-response}).
Move to the next or previous agent response and speak its bounded body
preview. These keys skip intervening thoughts@comma{} tools@comma{} and other block types.
From code or a table inside an answer@comma{} they skip that enclosing answer.
When no later response exists@comma{} @kbd{j} moves to the end of the buffer and
announces the boundary. With Explicit input activation@comma{} browsing stays
active there@comma{} so @kbd{J} returns to the last response without changing your draft.
@item l / L
Use @kbd{l} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-link}) or
@kbd{L} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-link}).
Move to the next or previous visible rendered link and speak its label and
Return action together. The cursor hint remains visible without a second
voice repeating it@comma{} including when you reach links with ordinary arrows or
word movement.
Press @kbd{@key{RET}} to follow the link.
To inspect the full destination@comma{} press @kbd{C-h .} (@code{display-local-help}).
@kbd{M-x agent-shell-copy-link-url-at-point} copies it to the kill ring.
Both commands announce the URL when message speech is enabled.
@item e / E
Use @kbd{e} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-error}) or
@kbd{E} (@code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-error}).
Move to the next or previous error or failed tool call. Failed tools are
identified by Agent Shell's error styling in their status label@comma{} rather than
by words in their output.
@item ]
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-block-at-point} to find the next block of
the current semantic type.
@item [
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-block-at-point} to find the preceding
block of the current semantic type.
@item C-c ]
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-next-block-of-type}@comma{} choose a type@comma{} and move to
its next occurrence.
@item C-c [
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-previous-block-of-type}@comma{} choose a type@comma{} and move
to its preceding occurrence.
@end table

After a successful typed jump@comma{} @kbd{]} and @kbd{[} repeat in that direction.
At an editable live prompt or viewport editor@comma{} bare brackets and navigation
letters insert themselves instead of taking over prompt editing. With Explicit
input activation@comma{} the inline prompt remains navigable until you activate editing.

@node Agent Shell Source Blocks
@section Read and copy source blocks

Use @kbd{k} or @kbd{K}@comma{} or typed block navigation@comma{} to
reach a source block@comma{} then:

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-b
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-speak-source-block} to read the complete rendered
source block with its language and line count.
@item C-c C-y
Run @code{emacsvox-agent-shell-copy-source-block} to copy the logical source
through Agent Shell's public copy operation.
@end table

The source commands are explicit operations and do not use the automatic
content limit.  Ordinary line navigation retains its separate bounded speech
policy.

@subsection Review proposed changes

In an expanded editing tool@comma{} file headings speak as “File:” followed by the
filename. Up and Down skip the decorative lines above and below each filename;
continuous speech also omits those borders. Diff text retains its contents@comma{}
including literal box-drawing characters in code. Character movement and
copying still expose the original displayed text.

Opening an Agent Shell change review announces its file and hunk counts and@comma{}
for a single file@comma{} its name.  The normal Diff Mode hunk navigation remains
available; opening the review does not accept or reject changes.

These events can have their own presentation rules. You can customise speech@comma{}
voices and earcons for steering outcomes@comma{} queue changes@comma{} list editing@comma{} setting
changes@comma{} updated tool output and change-review entry. Use
@ref{Explaining Presentation,Feedback Details,,emacsvox,} to inspect a representative event before changing it.

@node Agent Shell Tables
@section Navigate and copy rendered tables

Agent Shell reads rendered Markdown tables through the shared table controls.
By default@comma{} moving into a table activates its contextual map and announces
the dimensions and current cell. Choose automatic or on-demand activation@comma{}
headings and speech detail in the common Tables page; see
@ref{Reading Markdown And Org Tables, , Reading Markdown and Org tables}. Older Agent Shell title and order choices
remain scoped to Agent Shell until you explicitly replace them or select
inheritance.

Control-Meta arrows enter reading on demand and move to the next cell.
@kbd{C-e C-t C-r} or @kbd{M-x emacsvox-table-reader-mode} enters without moving.
Press @kbd{q} to leave reading in place. Automatic entry stays suspended until
you explicitly enter again or navigate out of the table and return; rendering
updates and resizing do not cancel that suspension. Leaving restores ordinary
Agent Shell keys. Tab@comma{} Shift-Tab and Return retain Agent Shell's native actions
throughout reading.

Plain Left and Right@comma{} or @kbd{C-b} and @kbd{C-f}@comma{} move by character within the current
cell. @kbd{M-b} and @kbd{M-f}@comma{} or Control-Left and Control-Right@comma{} move by word. These
commands follow wrapped text within the same cell@comma{} skipping borders@comma{} padding@comma{}
and neighbouring columns. At the beginning or end of the cell@comma{} they stop and
announce the boundary. Use Control-Meta-Left and Control-Meta-Right to move
between columns. Control-Meta-Up and Control-Meta-Down move between logical
rows in the current column and read only the destination cell. Plain Up and
Down make the same move and read the entire destination row by default;
the Tables page can select cell speech for these plain arrows. Wrapped cells
are read in full@comma{} using your table title settings.

@kbd{C-n} and @kbd{C-p} retain ordinary line movement. They can move through wrapped
cell text@comma{} but do not stay within the logical cell or skip other columns.
Use character and word movement to follow the cell's text in order.

Outside editable input@comma{} press @kbd{t} for the next table or
@kbd{T} for the previous table. These keys skip the current table
and announce the destination table and cell. Like the bracket navigation keys@comma{}
they insert themselves in an active inline editor or viewport editor@comma{} including
while the agent is working.

@subsection Understand table key conflicts

In viewport view mode@comma{} @kbd{t} takes the place of Agent Shell's thought-level
shortcut; use @kbd{M-x agent-shell-viewport-set-session-thought-level} for that
action. Inside a table@comma{} @kbd{r}@comma{} @kbd{c}@comma{} and @kbd{a} read rows@comma{} read columns@comma{} and open
Tables settings. To invoke the displaced viewport actions there@comma{}
use @kbd{M-x agent-shell-viewport-reply}@comma{} @kbd{M-x agent-shell-viewport-reply-continue}@comma{}
or @kbd{M-x agent-shell-viewport-reply-again}. In the shell transcript@comma{} table @kbd{r}
replaces quoting; use @kbd{M-x agent-shell-quote-region} with a selected region.
Leaving reading restores these ordinary bindings. In a viewport@comma{} table @kbd{q}
leaves reading rather than closing the view; a second @kbd{q} runs @code{bury-buffer}.
@kbd{M-x bury-buffer} remains available directly.

Outside tables@comma{} @kbd{C-M-<up>} and @kbd{C-M-<down>} do nothing in Agent Shell@comma{}
including its viewport and prompt editor. Repeating a row key after leaving
a table therefore keeps point at the exit. These two keys also do nothing
in ordinary @kbd{shell-mode} buffers. For structural movement there@comma{} use
@kbd{M-x backward-up-list} or @kbd{M-x down-list}. Source-code buffers keep their
usual bindings.

Continuous speech and response-block reading omit rendered table borders and
keep the cell text. Decorative borders therefore do not force a switch to a
different speech engine. Literal box-drawing characters in prose or code remain
available to symbol naming and exact character inspection.

@subsection Table command reference

