@node Agent Shell Recovery
@section Recover from interruption or failure

If a request is taking the wrong path@comma{} press @kbd{C-c C-c} and confirm the
Agent Shell interrupt.  A failed or interrupted partial response is not cached
as complete.  Press @kbd{C-c r} for the preceding completed response@comma{} or
@kbd{C-c R} for its overview.  Read the error block in the transcript for the
provider's details and edit the live prompt before resubmitting.

Use @kbd{M-x agent-shell-restart} when Agent Shell itself needs a fresh process
for the same project.  Use @kbd{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable} to reinstall
speech hooks after deliberately disabling them.  Neither recovery command
discards transcript files or project work.

If you are changing the integration rather than using it@comma{} see
@ref{Integration Maintenance, , Integration Maintenance} for its public event@comma{} renderer@comma{} and
compatibility contracts.

@node EAT Terminal Access
@chapter Speech Access To EAT

@cindex EAT terminal emulator
@cindex terminal accessibility
@cindex speech access, terminal
@findex eat

EAT (Emulate A Terminal) is a third-party terminal emulator@comma{} commonly installed
from NonGNU ELPA@. Run @kbd{M-x eat} to start it; a prefix argument creates or
selects another terminal. Follow the @ref{Top,Eat User Manual,,eat,} for installation@comma{}
terminal setup@comma{} and EAT's complete command reference.

Three parts cooperate here. EAT emulates the terminal; the shell@comma{} router@comma{} or
other program handles terminal input and completion; Emacsvox presents the
rendered result. Emacsvox observes a bounded screen@comma{} not the program's private
state. It does not generate shell or router completion candidates.

First choose an input mode appropriate to the program. Then use automatic
feedback for current work and explicit review for content you need to examine
again. @kbd{C-e S} opens focused EAT speech settings; those menu keys apply only
while the menu is open@comma{} not to ordinary terminal input.

@menu
* Choose an input mode: EAT Input Modes.  Decide which keys reach Emacs and which reach the terminal program.
* Understand automatic terminal feedback: EAT Automatic Feedback.  Follow output, prompts, editing results, and application-screen changes.
* Hear terminal-side completion: EAT Terminal Completion.  Hear completed input and inspect retained candidate or help rows.
* Review retained terminal output: EAT Screen Review.  Read retained state or inspect an immutable screen without sending input.
* Adjust and save terminal feedback: EAT Feedback Preferences.  Choose verbosity, background monitoring, and focused speech defaults.
* Follow shell prompts and command status: EAT Shell Integration.  Understand the Bash or Zsh integration and its remote-shell limits.
* Enter passwords and protect sensitive content: EAT Passwords And Privacy.  Use protected password input and understand retained terminal data.
* Control terminal access to the clipboard: EAT Clipboard Trust.  Review OSC 52 trust and the two terminal selection-control options.
* Inspect full-screen applications and diagnose silence: EAT Full-screen Applications.  Use retained review and distinguish terminal rendering from speech.
* Use EAT in Eshell: EAT In Eshell.  Understand embedded terminals and visual-command window return.
@end menu

@node EAT Input Modes
@section Input Modes And Completion Responsibilities

@cindex EAT input modes
@cindex completion, EAT
@cindex Corfu, EAT
@findex eat-semi-char-mode
@findex eat-char-mode
@findex eat-emacs-mode
@findex eat-line-mode
@kindex C-c C-j (EAT)
@kindex C-c M-d (EAT)
@kindex C-c C-e (EAT)
@kindex C-c C-l (EAT)

EAT has four input modes. Emacsvox announces the mode name when you switch.
The mode determines whether a key edits text in Emacs or reaches the terminal
program.

@table @asis
@item Semi-character mode
The default. Most keys@comma{} including @kbd{@key{TAB}}@comma{} go to the terminal@comma{} while
prefixes such as @kbd{C-x}@comma{} @kbd{C-c}@comma{} and @kbd{M-x} remain available. Use @kbd{C-q}
before a normally reserved key to send it through. @kbd{C-c C-j} selects
this mode.
@item Character mode
Supported input keys go to the terminal@comma{} useful for programs needing
Emacs-like keys. Emacsvox's review prefix remains available as described
under @ref{EAT Screen Review,Review retained terminal output,,emacsvox,}. Enter character mode with @kbd{C-c M-d}
and return to semi-character mode with @kbd{C-M-m} or @kbd{M-@key{RET}} before using
ordinary Emacs command entry.
@item Emacs mode
Keys stay in Emacs and send no terminal input. Use it for ordinary buffer
motion and inspection. Enter with @kbd{C-c C-e}.
@item Line mode
Edit the pending line locally and send it with @kbd{@key{RET}}. @kbd{@key{TAB}} invokes
Emacs's @code{completion-at-point}@comma{} whose result may be displayed by a front
end such as Corfu. Enter from semi-character or Emacs mode with @kbd{C-c C-l}.
Line mode is not available in EAT's embedded Eshell terminal.
@end table

Line mode does not create unlimited history. @code{eat-term-scrollback-size} still
limits retained text by @emph{characters}@comma{} not lines; @code{nil} permits unlimited
retention. The editable draft follows the terminal extent@comma{} and changing input
modes does not change this limit.

In semi-character and character modes@comma{} @kbd{@key{TAB}} belongs to Bash Readline@comma{} a
remote shell@comma{} router CLI@comma{} or the running terminal program. Corfu is not involved.
In line mode@comma{} completion belongs to Emacs and does not query a remote CLI@.
Semi-character mode is therefore usually appropriate for SSH and network-device
sessions. If an Emacs completion backend blocks in line mode@comma{} @kbd{C-g} cancels
that Emacs operation; switch to semi-character mode when terminal completion
was intended.

The shell must have its own completion configuration. For example@comma{} completing
@code{git pul} to @code{git pull} in Bash requires suitable programmable definitions@comma{}
often supplied by @code{bash-completion}. Emacsvox neither installs nor substitutes
for those definitions.

@node EAT Automatic Feedback
@section Automatic Terminal Feedback

@cindex EAT automatic speech
@cindex terminal output, speech policy
@kindex C-e C-q (EAT)
@findex emacsvox-eat-toggle-autospeak
@vindex emacsvox-eat-autospeak

@kbd{C-e C-q} toggles automatic foreground feedback for this terminal; it starts
enabled. The same control is @kbd{s} in the focused settings menu. It covers
routine output@comma{} status@comma{} automatic metadata@comma{} application-screen boundaries@comma{}
and process lifecycle feedback. Explicit reading@comma{} input and completion
feedback@comma{} and the configured terminal bell remain available. Verbosity
(@kbd{C-e q v}) and background monitoring (@kbd{C-e q m}) are independent preferences.

Automatic speech requires the selected EAT buffer and Emacs keyboard focus.
The selected window must follow the live cursor or edit a line-mode draft
after terminal output. Reviewing scrollback prevents new output from
interrupting you. Another selected terminal or an active minibuffer also
suppresses foreground feedback. In terminal-only Emacs@comma{} or when keyboard
focus cannot be reported@comma{} selection within Emacs is the available boundary.

Turning autospeak off or losing eligibility discards locally pending automatic
feedback. Speech already sent to Omnivox may finish. Returning or re-enabling
speech starts with fresh output; it does not replay missed content. Use explicit
review instead. This policy also applies to an embedded Eshell terminal@comma{}
without changing ordinary Eshell output.

@subsection Read output and follow progress

On the main screen@comma{} Emacsvox collects a burst before classifying it. Complete
new output lines are spoken once@comma{} with automatic limits of eight lines and
1@comma{}000 characters. Continued input rows and empty terminal placeholders are
omitted without hiding output or rereading earlier rows. Output remains
eligible even if it scrolls beyond the screen@comma{} including repeated identical
lines; increasing scrollback is unnecessary for this feedback.

For progress rows@comma{} speech omits runs of three or more horizontal box-drawing
strokes separated from surrounding text by spaces@comma{} while retaining figures
and labels. A row containing only a horizontal rule@comma{} with optional box
corners@comma{} uses the separator cue even at @code{all} punctuation. This applies to
automatic output@comma{} explicit screen review@comma{} and line reading in EAT and frozen
review buffers. Table junctions@comma{} vertical borders@comma{} and shorter runs keep their
usual presentation. The buffer text remains intact@comma{} so you can inspect
individual characters. Adjust the separator sound through
@ref{Sounds And Space,Line and boundary cues,,emacsvox,}.

Wrapped output is joined and oversized bursts announce how much was omitted.
Fresh text without a newline also speaks@comma{} keeping password requests and
confirmation questions accessible. Carriage-return progress and status
rewrites use rate-limited@comma{} replaceable speech. Unchanged repaints and
resize-only updates remain silent. Bells@comma{} pastes@comma{} input-mode changes@comma{}
application-screen boundaries@comma{} and process exit are content-free semantic
events rather than arbitrary screen readings.

@subsection Recognise and inspect prompts

@vindex emacsvox-eat-prompt-feedback
@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-prompt
@kindex C-e q p (EAT)

When EAT shell integration identifies a ready prompt@comma{} Emacsvox gives a cue
after output instead of reading the prompt text. @code{emacsvox-eat-prompt-feedback}
defaults to @code{earcon}; choose @code{text} to hear the prompt or @code{silent} to suppress
it. This option can be buffer-local and is independent of verbosity@comma{} but
automatic prompt feedback still obeys foreground autospeak eligibility.

Split and multiline prompts are collected until ready. Redisplay and typing
do not repeat them. If several prompts arrive in one burst@comma{} feedback describes
the final ready prompt.

@kbd{C-e q p} explicitly reads the last identified prompt@comma{} even with autospeak off.
Recognition does not require margin annotations@comma{} but it does require shell
integration. An unintegrated remote prompt or question is ordinary output@comma{}
read as text; the prompt preference does not silence it. The last identified
prompt may still belong to the local shell. Use @kbd{C-e q l} for the current
retained cursor row in that situation.

@subsection Hear input and deletion results

When the terminal has keyboard focus@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} stops earlier main speech before
sending a newline in line@comma{} semi-character@comma{} or character mode. For example@comma{}
advancing to the next line in @code{ed} cuts off the preceding line. Output chunks
within one response still play in order@comma{} and notification speech continues
independently. @kbd{@key{RET}} itself adds no spoken text. Sending line input without a
newline or editing scrollback does not trigger this stop.

On the main screen in semi-character and character modes@comma{} Emacsvox names the
character reached by rendered left/right movement. After up/down it speaks
the resulting input or history row without the observed shell prompt.
@kbd{M-b}@comma{} @kbd{M-f}@comma{} and modified left/right word movements
speak the rendered text crossed. A boundary key that produces no movement
stays silent.

Line-mode history navigation speaks the recalled command once. EAT's
history-item counter and input-restored message remain visible in the echo
area without speaking over the command on the notification stream.

An echoed Space is named without rereading the prompt or line. For Backspace@comma{}
Delete@comma{} @kbd{M-d}@comma{} and @kbd{C-k}@comma{} Emacsvox compares bounded public snapshots before
and after the key. One unambiguous same-row removal is read as the actual
character or text removed. Otherwise only a deletion tone is given; adjacent
prompt or screen text is not guessed as deleted content. Padding and trailing
input blanks do not prevent an otherwise unambiguous word deletion.

A no-echo peer provides no visible deletion to announce. This input feedback
requires the selected window to follow the live cursor@comma{} and is silent during
protected password entry@comma{} in an unselected terminal@comma{} and on an alternate
screen where the application owns the keys.

@subsection Bells and application-screen changes

Bells retain EAT's configured sound without speaking "Terminal bell" by default.
An aural rule can add a label by matching module @code{eat}@comma{} role
@code{command-interaction}@comma{} event @code{command-terminal-bell}@comma{} and occasion
@code{notification}@comma{} with speech before or after the event. Bell notifications
remain rate-limited and obey selected-buffer@comma{} live-cursor@comma{} and secure-input
restrictions.

On full-screen or alternate-screen applications@comma{} Emacsvox does not read every
refresh. A cursor row reached by correlated arrow-style navigation@comma{} or a compact
high-confidence transfer of selection-like styling@comma{} may be announced.
Ambiguous repaints remain available to explicit review. Inverse video or a
coloured row alone is not proof of an active item.

@node EAT Terminal Completion
@section Terminal-side Completion

@cindex completion candidates, terminal
@cindex router completion
@kindex TAB (EAT terminal completion)

Before a terminal-side @kbd{@key{TAB}} on the main screen@comma{} Emacsvox captures the public
screen and waits briefly for output to settle. An inline completion speaks the
newly displayed field; for example@comma{} @code{~/sr<TAB>} can speak @code{src slash} after the
shell displays @code{~/src/}.

An ambiguous completion printed as columns or help rows is presented as one
transaction after the input line is redrawn. Small inferred lists speak up to
eight candidates. Router-style descriptions keep their layout rather than
being forced into a shell-token model. Large lists are counted and shortened
for automatic speech. If candidates have already scrolled beyond the bounded
screen@comma{} the count is a lower bound@comma{} such as ``at least N visible''@comma{} not an
invented total.

Identical repeated lists@comma{} split output@comma{} bells@comma{} wrapping@comma{} and resizing are
coalesced without repeating the candidate text. This works from rendered
results for local Readline@comma{} SSH@comma{} and unstructured router help. No visible
change means there is nothing reliable to announce. On an alternate screen@comma{}
@kbd{@key{TAB}} is navigation rather than shell completion.

Use @kbd{C-e q c} to read the retained candidate/help layout@comma{} or open frozen
review with @kbd{C-e q r} and press @kbd{c}. This is useful for large or multi-column
results and does not ask the terminal to complete again.

@node EAT Screen Review
@section Explicit Review Commands

@cindex EAT review commands
@cindex terminal screen review
@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-current-row
@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-last-change
@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-likely-focus
@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-completion-output
@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-visible-screen
@findex emacsvox-eat-review-screen
@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-retained-status
@findex emacsvox-eat-speak-retained-metadata
@findex emacsvox-eat-cycle-verbosity
@findex emacsvox-eat-toggle-background-monitoring
@kindex C-e q (EAT review)

With the default speech prefix@comma{} @kbd{C-e q} provides review commands in every
EAT input mode:

@table @kbd
@item C-e q l
Speak the cursor row from the latest retained screen.
@item C-e q d
Speak the last classified change.
@item C-e q h
Speak the conservative likely-focus inference and its confidence.
@item C-e q c
Speak retained completion candidates or help rows.
@item C-e q s
Speak a bounded copy of the retained visible screen.
@item C-e q r
Open a read-only frozen copy for line-by-line review.
@item C-e q t
Speak retained progress@comma{} application@comma{} or shell-prompt status.
@item C-e q p
Speak the last identified shell prompt.
@item C-e q i
Speak the retained@comma{} sanitised title or working-directory change.
@item C-e q v
Cycle terse@comma{} normal@comma{} and verbose automatic feedback for this terminal.
@item C-e q m
Toggle content-free background monitoring for this terminal.
@end table

The reading commands inspect retained snapshots with concealed content
redacted. The verbosity and monitoring commands instead change the source
terminal's preferences. Neither operation sends terminal input. Speech is
bounded: the retained visible screen is not arbitrary scrollback and need
not match a changing live screen.

@subsection Frozen Screen Review

@cindex frozen EAT review

@kbd{C-e q r} opens an immutable copy of the latest visible screen. It does not
refresh@comma{} move the live cursor@comma{} or send input. It preserves normalised styling@comma{}
cursor and likely-focus anchors@comma{} and retained completion rows. Opening and
closing play distinct object cues and announce the transition.

@table @kbd
@item n@comma{} p; C-n@comma{} C-p; down@comma{} up
Move by captured terminal row and speak the destination@comma{} interrupting the
previous row announcement.
@item SPC@comma{} RET@comma{} .
Speak the current captured row.
@item a
Speak a bounded form of the current screen or completion view.
@item s@comma{} c
Show captured screen rows or retained completion/help rows.
@item C
Go to the captured terminal cursor.
@item h
Go to retained likely focus when available.
@item [@comma{} ]
Move among inverse/background-styled@comma{} selection-like regions. They are not
labelled as confirmed focus.
@item t@comma{} i
Speak status or metadata copied when review opened.
@item v@comma{} m
Change verbosity or monitoring in the source terminal without sending
terminal input. The copied content stays frozen.
@item ?
Describe frozen review mode.
@item q
Kill the content-bearing review buffer and restore its window.
@end table

Secure input@comma{} terminal reset or restart@comma{} killing the source@comma{} or leaving EAT
mode also destroys its frozen review. These events bound the lifetime of
copied terminal content.

@node EAT Feedback Preferences
@section Verbosity And Background Monitoring

@vindex emacsvox-eat-verbosity
@vindex emacsvox-eat-monitor-background-output

@code{emacsvox-eat-verbosity} is buffer-local:

@table @code
@item terse
Retain routine output and metadata for explicit review without speaking
them automatically. Actionable failures and content-free lifecycle feedback
remain available.
@item normal
The default: bounded command output@comma{} progress/status@comma{} completion@comma{} and
actionable failures.
@item verbose
Also give bounded title and directory changes and successful shell-command
status when EAT shell integration supplies it.
@end table

Background monitoring starts off. With @kbd{C-e q m} enabled@comma{} an unselected
terminal gives only a rate-limited content-free cue. Returning reports the
unread burst count@comma{} never the terminal's identity or output. Cursor movement@comma{}
style changes@comma{} repaint@comma{} resize@comma{} and process-generation changes do not count.
Unlike Shell/comint background reading@comma{} EAT monitoring neither requires
foreground autospeak nor reads background content.

@subsection Adjust and save focused terminal settings

Use @kbd{C-e S} or @kbd{C-e H j c} from EAT or its frozen review. @kbd{C-e H j a} opens
the integration chooser@comma{} and @kbd{C-e H /} finds individual controls.

@itemize
@item
@kbd{s} toggles automatic foreground speech.
@item
@kbd{b} toggles background cues and unread counts independently.
@item
@kbd{p} chooses ready-prompt cue@comma{} text@comma{} or silence; recognition still requires
shell integration and automatic delivery obeys foreground autospeak.
@item
@kbd{d} chooses terse@comma{} normal@comma{} or verbose feedback.
@end itemize

Arrows announce values and @kbd{@key{RET}} activates a control. Choice lists start on
the current value; browsing does not apply a choice@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} applies and returns@comma{}
and @kbd{q} cancels. Confirmations interrupt older main speech. Changes belong to
the captured terminal@comma{} including embedded Eshell EAT; from frozen review they
belong to its source terminal.

@kbd{q} returns to the originating menu entry. From direct @kbd{C-e S}@comma{} it closes
the menu and announces the restored buffer and mode with a close cue. @kbd{w}
saves the four displayed values as defaults through Customize. Terminals with
local overrides keep them; others inherit the new defaults. Adjustment alone
does not save. @kbd{A} opens advanced EAT settings and @kbd{q} there returns to the
focused menu.

@node EAT Shell Integration
@section Shell Integration And Command Status

@cindex EAT shell integration
@cindex shell command status

EAT's Bash or Zsh integration supplies prompt annotation@comma{} directory tracking@comma{}
and per-command running/success/failure status. For Bash@comma{} EAT documents:

@example
[ -n "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR" ] && \
  source "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR/bash"
@end example

For Zsh@comma{} use its script instead:

@example
[ -n "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR" ] && \
  source "$EAT_SHELL_INTEGRATION_DIR/zsh"
@end example

@vindex eat-enable-shell-prompt-annotation

With integration and @code{eat-enable-shell-prompt-annotation} enabled@comma{} Emacsvox
retains running@comma{} successful@comma{} and failed command status. A failure is eligible
for automatic feedback at every verbosity@comma{} subject to foreground autospeak.
Success is automatic only at verbose level after observed command activity;
the initial successful prompt is not announced as a completed command.
Running state stays silent. @kbd{C-e q t} requests the retained current state.

Status is not guessed from prompt characters. A remote shell or router usually
does not provide this per-command integration. Its output and completion can
still be accessible while individual exit status remains unavailable.

@node EAT Passwords And Privacy
@section Passwords And Sensitive Content

@cindex password input, EAT
@cindex terminal privacy
@findex eat-send-password

Use @kbd{M-x eat-send-password @key{RET}} for a terminal password. In character mode@comma{}
first press @kbd{M-@key{RET}} to return to semi-character mode so command entry reaches
Emacs rather than the terminal program. Enter the secret only in the protected
password prompt. EAT uses Emacs's password reader rather than recording those
keystrokes in command lossage.
Emacsvox suppresses observation and bell speech during the command@comma{} clears
content-bearing retained state before and after it@comma{} and announces only whether
secure input was sent or cancelled.

Ordinary no-echo input does not establish that boundary. EAT exposes no public
echo-state signal@comma{} and a quiet repaint is not evidence of password entry.
Typing a secret through normal terminal input can leave keystrokes in Emacs
lossage. Backspace or Delete may give a content-free deletion tone@comma{} but speech
names content only when one unambiguous visible removal is exposed.

Selected foreground output can contain sensitive information and be spoken.
Frozen review is a real in-memory copy until killed@comma{} although visually concealed
cells are redacted when captured. Verbose title and directory announcements can
disclose host/path metadata. Background monitoring@comma{} by contrast@comma{} never reads
content or terminal identity.

@node EAT Clipboard Trust
@section Terminal-Controlled Clipboard Trust

@cindex clipboard security, EAT
@cindex OSC 52, EAT
@cindex remote terminal trust
@vindex eat-enable-kill-from-terminal
@vindex eat-enable-yank-to-terminal

EAT supports terminal selection-control sequences@comma{} commonly called OSC 52.
Trust therefore extends to any local program@comma{} SSH peer@comma{} router@comma{} or other output
source that reaches its parser. Displaying an untrusted file through a program
that does not sanitise control sequences can also exercise this interface.

In the audited EAT 0.9.4 release@comma{} @code{eat-enable-kill-from-terminal} defaults to
non-@code{nil}. Despite its name@comma{} this is the terminal-to-Emacs direction: a terminal
program can place text in the kill ring. @code{eat-enable-yank-to-terminal} defaults
to @code{nil} for security; enabling it lets a terminal program request current
kill-ring text. Check the installed defaults again after an EAT upgrade.

For output you do not fully trust@comma{} disable both directions globally through
Customize or locally in the EAT buffer:

@lisp
(setq-local eat-enable-kill-from-terminal nil
            eat-enable-yank-to-terminal nil)
@end lisp

Evaluate this with @kbd{M-:} in that buffer before processing untrusted output.
Reserve kill-ring read-back for programs and remote endpoints trusted with
whatever the kill ring currently contains.

Emacsvox changes neither option. A terminal-originated selection payload is not
an accessibility command: its contents are neither spoken nor retained as
terminal screen text. Content-free paste announcements apply to explicit user
paste commands@comma{} not these terminal-originated sequences.

@node EAT Full-screen Applications
@section Full-screen Applications And Limitations

@cindex curses applications, EAT
@cindex alternate screen, EAT

Pagers@comma{} editors@comma{} menus@comma{} and refreshing dashboards often rewrite cells instead
of printing lines. Emacsvox uses conservative cursor@comma{} style-change@comma{} input-
direction@comma{} and timing evidence. Arrow movement and clear paired highlight
movement work best. Printable keys such as @kbd{h}@comma{} @kbd{j}@comma{} @kbd{k}@comma{} and @kbd{l} are not
assumed to mean navigation: the application may treat them as text.

After a silent update@comma{} use @kbd{C-e q l}@comma{} @kbd{C-e q d}@comma{} or @kbd{C-e q h}. For systematic
inspection@comma{} open @kbd{C-e q r} and review rows or selection-like regions. Rapid
dashboards are deliberately not read on every refresh. EAT's current public
API does not expose hyperlink targets@comma{} sixel/image semantics@comma{} true application
accessibility focus@comma{} or exact update causes; pixels and faces are insufficient
to infer them reliably.

When ordinary output is unexpectedly silent@comma{} check the input mode@comma{} selected
buffer and live-cursor position@comma{} foreground autospeak@comma{} and verbosity before
changing recognition settings. Retained review may explain what was observed@comma{}
but it is not a replacement for live output or an unbounded transcript.

Remote programs also need compatible terminal information. Missing remote
terminfo can break cursor addressing and screen layout; speech classification
cannot repair that. Consult EAT's manual when the terminal itself renders
incorrectly.

@node EAT In Eshell
@section EAT In Eshell

@cindex Eshell, EAT accessibility
@findex eat-eshell-mode
@findex eat-eshell-visual-command-mode

With global @code{eat-eshell-mode} enabled@comma{} Emacsvox uses the same screen observer
for EAT's terminal region inside Eshell. Rendered command output follows one
speech path instead of also being repeated by generic Eshell feedback.

With @code{eat-eshell-visual-command-mode}@comma{} a visual command runs in a separate EAT
buffer. Emacsvox announces its start@comma{} delivers eligible final output before
process-exit feedback@comma{} and identifies the exit as an Eshell visual command.
EAT and Eshell still own window return. In particular@comma{}
@code{eshell-destroy-buffer-when-process-dies} determines whether a successful
visual-command buffer is killed and Eshell restored; Emacsvox does not change it.

@node Running Terminal Based Applications
@chapter Term Terminal Access

@cindex Term mode
@cindex terminal applications
@cindex Eterm, Emacsvox module name

Term is the terminal emulator built into Emacs. Its Emacsvox integration is
named @code{emacsvox-eterm} for historical reasons; no separate Eterm package is
required. Use Term when avoiding an extra package matters or a particular
application works better there.

For a new full-terminal workflow@comma{} @ref{EAT Terminal Access, , EAT Terminal Access} provides the richer
current model for bounded output@comma{} terminal completion@comma{} scrollback@comma{} alternate
screens@comma{} and asynchronous rendering. For ordinary line-oriented commands@comma{}
@kbd{M-x shell} is another option. These environments have different input and
review keys; do not transfer a key sequence merely because both are terminals.

@menu
* Starting Term::
* Choose Term's input mode: Term Input Modes.  Choose whether keys reach Emacs or the terminal program.
* Understand Term's automatic speech: Term Speech Feedback.  Control automatic feedback and inspect silent output.
* Reviewing The Term Screen::
* Help and recovery in Term: Term Help And Recovery.  Recover speech and send commands to the intended application.
@end menu

@node Starting Term
@section Starting Term

@findex term

Run @kbd{M-x term @key{RET}}. At @code{Run program:}@comma{} accept the proposed shell or enter
another executable and press @kbd{@key{RET}}. Emacsvox makes the selected Term window
fill the frame when @code{term} or @code{ansi-term} starts. This changes window layout@comma{}
not the existence of buffers previously shown in other windows.

Term emulates the terminal@comma{} the child program handles input and completion@comma{}
and Emacsvox presents the rendered buffer. See @ref{Term Mode,Term Mode,,emacs,} in @emph{The GNU
Emacs Manual} for the emulator and @ref{emacsvox-eterm,emacsvox-eterm,,emacsvox-reference,} in the @emph{Emacsvox Reference
Manual} for the complete integration reference.

@node Term Input Modes
@section Character And Line Input Modes

@cindex character mode, Term
@cindex line mode, Term
@findex term-char-mode
@findex term-line-mode

Term starts in character mode. Choose according to who should receive the keys:

@table @asis
@item Character mode
Most keys are sent immediately to the terminal program. This suits
full-screen or key-driven applications. @kbd{C-c C-j} enters line mode.
Normal Emacs prefixes may belong to the child@comma{} so use @kbd{C-c C-h m} for
mode help and @kbd{C-c M-x} to run an Emacs command.
@item Line mode
Ordinary Emacs editing commands edit pending input; @kbd{@key{RET}} sends it to
the program. Emacs Help keys such as @kbd{C-h m} work directly.
@kbd{C-c C-k} returns to character mode.
@end table

An interactive switch announces @code{Terminal line mode} or
@code{Terminal character mode}. In either mode@comma{} @kbd{C-t} introduces Term's Emacsvox
review commands. Send a literal @kbd{C-t} to the child in character mode with
@kbd{C-t C-t}.

Use the input-mode escape before trying ordinary Emacs settings or Help keys
that the child might otherwise receive. In line mode@comma{} @kbd{C-e H} opens the
Emacsvox settings menu. Its general settings do not replace Term's own
buffer-local automatic-speech control below. Likewise@comma{} @kbd{q} closes an Emacsvox
menu or dedicated review only while that interface owns the keys; it is not
a general command to leave a running terminal application.

@node Term Speech Feedback
@section Automatic Speech Feedback

@cindex automatic speech, Term
@vindex emacsvox-eterm-autospeak
@findex emacsvox-toggle-eterm-autospeak

@code{emacsvox-eterm-autospeak} controls automatic feedback in the current buffer
and defaults to enabled. @kbd{C-t C-q} toggles it. The older spelling
@code{eterm-autospeak} is not an Emacsvox variable.

A Term buffer must be displayed in a live window for automatic output speech.
A hidden buffer can keep running silently. This displayed-window condition is
not EAT's selected-buffer and keyboard-focus policy.

In line mode@comma{} speech reads newly inserted output as a range rather than
interrupting it for every character. In character mode@comma{} rendered cursor and
screen changes determine whether a character@comma{} word@comma{} movement@comma{} or reached line
is presented. New terminal activity can stop earlier speech.

This integration does not provide EAT's bounded screen transactions@comma{}
completion model@comma{} or alternate-screen policy. When a redraw is ambiguous or
an announcement too brief@comma{} use the explicit review commands.

@node Reviewing The Term Screen
@section Reviewing The Term Screen

@cindex review pointer, Term
@cindex review mode, Term

The review pointer is independent of the terminal cursor. Moving it does not
send keys or move the child application. It reviews the Term buffer@comma{} not the
immutable snapshot used by EAT's frozen review. The normal prefix is @kbd{C-t}:

@table @kbd
@item C-t .
Place the review pointer at the terminal cursor and announce its coordinates.
@item C-t p
@itemx C-t n
Move to the previous or next line and speak it. @kbd{C-t <up>} and
@kbd{C-t <down>} are equivalent.
@item C-t <left>
@itemx C-t <right>
Move by a character and speak the destination.
@item C-t M-b
@itemx C-t M-f
Move backward or forward by a word and speak it.
@item C-t l
@itemx C-t w
@itemx C-t c
Read the line@comma{} word@comma{} or character at the review pointer without moving.
@item C-t SPC
Read from the pointer to the current end of the Term buffer.
@item C-t s
Ask for text@comma{} search forward from the pointer@comma{} and read the matching line.
@end table

In character mode@comma{} @kbd{C-t q} enters dedicated review. Ordinary keys then stay
in review rather than reaching the child. Use @kbd{p}@comma{} @kbd{n}@comma{} arrows@comma{} @kbd{M-b}@comma{}
@kbd{M-f}@comma{} @kbd{l}@comma{} @kbd{w}@comma{} @kbd{c}@comma{} and @kbd{s} without the prefix. @kbd{q} returns to character
mode. To copy text@comma{} set one endpoint with @kbd{m}@comma{} move the pointer@comma{} then press
@kbd{y} to copy the region to the kill ring.

Named rectangular regions and focus/filter windows are advanced@comma{}
coordinate-based facilities. Consult the live mode Help and generated
reference before using them; remember the character-mode Help prefix when
ordinary Emacs keys would otherwise reach the child.

@node Term Help And Recovery
@section Help And Recovery

For visible but silent output@comma{} inspect @code{emacsvox-eterm-autospeak} in the
current buffer and ensure that buffer is displayed. Toggle with @kbd{C-t C-q}
when necessary@comma{} then test new output. An automatic-speech setting does not
prove that the program has produced a new readable screen change.

If an Emacs command goes to the child@comma{} check the input mode. In character
mode use @kbd{C-c C-h m}@comma{} @kbd{C-c M-x}@comma{} or switch to line mode with @kbd{C-c C-j}.
If dedicated review owns the keys@comma{} @kbd{q} returns to character mode first.

When review has moved away from current output@comma{} @kbd{C-t .} resynchronises the
pointer. Leave dedicated review with @kbd{q} before using the normal prefix.
For a full-screen redraw that Term cannot present reliably@comma{} try the workflow
in @ref{EAT Terminal Access, , EAT Terminal Access} and use that emulator's explicit review commands.

@node Speech Backends
@chapter Speech Backends

Emacsvox sends speech through a speech server. For a new installation@comma{}
use @emph{Omnivox}. The server communicates with the engine that produces speech:
normally eSpeak NG on GNU/Linux@comma{} AVSpeechSynthesizer on macOS@comma{} and WinRT
SpeechSynthesizer on Windows.

Follow @ref{Installation, , Installation} if you have not yet obtained speech. This chapter is for
choosing a supported platform route@comma{} installing or configuring a different
backend@comma{} and diagnosing problems. Omnivox's native build procedures@comma{} engine
behaviour@comma{} and versioned protocols belong to
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox, the Omnivox repository}.

Use the manual supplied with your checkout or installed package when following
menu routes; older releases may provide a different interface.

@menu
* Supported Platform Routes::    Choose the maintained route for this machine.
* Install Omnivox From A Release Binary: Installing An Omnivox Release Binary.  Install Omnivox through Homebrew or a verified release archive.
* Install An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2: Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2.  Use the matching verified Windows archive without a build toolchain.
* Installing Native Windows Emacsvox: Trying Native Windows Emacsvox.  Install and verify supported native Windows x64 Emacsvox.
* Installing Omnivox From Source:: Build when a release archive does not match the host.
* Building Omnivox For WSL2::    Stage the Windows runtime from WSL2.
* Selecting And Checking Omnivox:: Prove speech before starting Emacsvox.
* Compatibility Speech Servers:: Understand the retained direct adapters.
* Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities: Backend Diagnostics And Ownership.  Locate failures and authoritative detail.
@end menu

@node Supported Platform Routes
@section Supported Platform Routes

The following table describes Emacsvox's supported installation routes.
After installing Omnivox@comma{} run @code{omnivox --check} on the machine that will play
speech and confirm that you hear both its tone and spoken message.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .31 .51
@headitem Emacs platform
@tab Recommended route
@tab Requirements and limits
@item GNU/Linux x86-64 with an Ubuntu 24.04-compatible runtime
@tab Omnivox 1.18.0 release archive using eSpeak NG
@tab Requires compatible glibc@comma{} libstdc++@comma{} libgcc@comma{} and ALSA runtime libraries.
@item Other GNU/Linux systems
@tab Source-built Omnivox using eSpeak NG
@tab Use the source route for Linux ARM64@comma{} an older distribution ABI@comma{} or a host that cannot run the published x86-64 archive.
@item macOS on Apple silicon
@tab Omnivox through the official Homebrew tap@comma{} using AVSpeechSynthesizer
@tab Homebrew selects the ARM64 archive; install Emacs separately.
@item macOS on Intel
@tab Omnivox through the official Homebrew tap@comma{} using AVSpeechSynthesizer
@tab Homebrew selects the Intel archive; install Emacs separately.
@item Emacs in WSL2 on x86-64 Windows
@tab Omnivox 1.18.0 Windows x64 release archive using WinRT
@tab Keep its eSpeak data and notices beside the executable; the guided installer preserves this layout.
@item Emacs in WSL2 on Windows on ARM
@tab Omnivox 1.18.0 Windows ARM64 release archive using WinRT
@tab The guided installer selects ARM64 automatically. Speech plays through Windows.
@item Native Windows x64 Emacs 30.2 or newer
@tab Guided PowerShell installation with Omnivox 1.18.0 using WinRT
@tab Install on a local Windows drive. Native Windows ARM64 is not supported.
@end multitable

Use Homebrew on macOS@comma{} the guided installer under WSL2@comma{} or the matching release
archive on GNU/Linux. Manual Mac archive installation remains available.
A bare executable@comma{} repository tag@comma{} or unpackaged CI artifact is not a complete
installation: keep the release's runtime data and notices beside the executable.
Homebrew preserves that layout for you.

Do not set @code{OMNIVOX_ENGINE} during initial setup. Its empty value selects
the normal platform engine above. Engine routing and optional Piper@comma{}
Eloquence@comma{} or DECtalk support are later customisations@comma{} not prerequisites for
first speech.

For Emacs running on a remote SSH host@comma{} see
@ref{Remote workstation speech setup, , Remote Workstation Speech}.

@subsection Version and capability requirements

Omnivox 1.18.0 is the minimum supported release. It supplies named punctuation
profiles@comma{} palette-aware character speech and editable engine configuration@comma{}
alongside managed voice libraries@comma{} eSpeak variants@comma{} engine-specific voice
controls@comma{} individual fallback tuning and complete voice previews. A newer
compatible release may be used. The manual archive examples and guided
installers pin the 1.18.0 core and matching optional engines exactly; the
macOS Homebrew tap follows its maintained stable formula.

Updating Emacsvox source alone does not upgrade Omnivox. Rerunning a guided
installer can install its pinned Omnivox release; Homebrew and manual
installations use their own update procedures. Check which route owns the
selected installation before changing it. Voice-library management uses the
bundled local management route. Remote and direct-native launches do not
acquire the WSL launcher's file or release-management capabilities merely by
connecting to an Omnivox worker. The individual procedures identify their
required host and capabilities.

@node Installing An Omnivox Release Binary
@section Install Omnivox From A Release Binary

@subsection macOS with Homebrew

On macOS (formerly OS X)@comma{} install @uref{https://brew.sh, Homebrew} and follow its shell setup
instructions. Then install Omnivox from the
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/homebrew-omnivox, official Homebrew tap}:

@example
brew install bartbunting/omnivox/omnivox
omnivox_program="$(brew --prefix omnivox)/bin/omnivox"
"$omnivox_program" --version
"$omnivox_program" --check
"$omnivox_program" --list-voices
@end example

Homebrew selects the Apple silicon or Intel archive and verifies its checksum.
The version must be 1.18.0 or newer@comma{} and the check must produce both a tone and
spoken confirmation. Apple system speech and eSpeak are included. You do not
need a Rust build or Omnivox source checkout. Install Emacs 30.2 or newer
separately; this tap does not install Emacs or Emacsvox.

To select this Homebrew installation persistently for Emacsvox@comma{} record the
stable executable path:

@example
omnivox_config_home="$@{XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config@}"
omnivox_config_file="$@{EMACSVOX_OMNIVOX_CONFIG_FILE:-$omnivox_config_home/emacsvox/omnivox-program@}"
mkdir -p "$(dirname "$omnivox_config_file")"
printf '%s\n' "$omnivox_program" > "$omnivox_config_file"
@end example

This replaces any previous executable selection in that file. The path uses
Homebrew's current-version link and continues to work after upgrades. Do not
substitute a versioned @code{Cellar/omnivox/...} path. The bundled launcher reads
this file even when Emacs does not inherit the shell's Homebrew @code{PATH}@comma{} as can
happen when starting from Finder. An explicit @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} override
still takes precedence; remove or update an override selecting another
installation. If using @code{EMACSVOX_OMNIVOX_CONFIG_FILE} or @code{XDG_CONFIG_HOME}@comma{}
make sure the launching Emacs sees the same location.

Emacsvox uses its bundled Lisp adapter. Do not load Homebrew's
@code{libexec/omnivox-voices.el}@comma{} intended for upstream Emacspeak. Continue with
@ref{Quick Installation, , Quick Installation} to compile Emacsvox@comma{} run @code{./bin/emacsvox --diagnose}
and @code{./bin/emacsvox --check}@comma{} and start an isolated speaking session.
Confirm the selected server and audible output on your Mac.

To upgrade Omnivox later:

@example
brew update
brew upgrade omnivox
"$(brew --prefix omnivox)/bin/omnivox" --version
"$(brew --prefix omnivox)/bin/omnivox" --check
@end example

Restart Emacsvox after upgrading so both speech processes use the new
executable. Updating Emacsvox alone does not upgrade Homebrew's package.
@code{brew uninstall omnivox} removes it; select another server before restarting
Emacsvox because the saved executable path remains in your configuration.

Optional Piper@comma{} Flite@comma{} and RuTTS companions and voice models are separate.
The bundled RHVoice bridge still needs a compatible user-installed runtime
and data; its presence does not establish macOS runtime support. Keep optional
companions@comma{} downloaded voices@comma{} and configuration outside Homebrew's Cellar@comma{}
and use explicit paths as described in the
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/README.md, Omnivox engine guides}. Companions must match core release and architecture.
The WSL optional-engine installer is not a Homebrew package manager.

The tap installs published binaries without adding Apple Developer ID signing
or notarisation. If local policy refuses the verified binary@comma{} use
@ref{Installing Omnivox From Source, , Installing Omnivox From Source}.

@subsection Manual archive installation

Use this route for GNU/Linux x86-64 with an Ubuntu 24.04-compatible runtime@comma{}
macOS on Apple silicon@comma{} or macOS on Intel. The
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/tag/v1.18.0, Omnivox 1.18.0 release} supplies:

@multitable @columnfractions .28 .27 .45
@headitem Host
@tab Set @code{platform} to
@tab Archive
@item GNU/Linux x86-64
@tab @code{linux-x64}
@tab @code{omnivox-1.18.0-linux-x64.tar.gz}
@item macOS Apple silicon
@tab @code{macos-arm64}
@tab @code{omnivox-1.18.0-macos-arm64.tar.gz}
@item macOS Intel
@tab @code{macos-x64}
@tab @code{omnivox-1.18.0-macos-x64.tar.gz}
@end multitable

On a Mac@comma{} @code{uname -m} identifies @code{arm64} for Apple silicon or @code{x86_64} for Intel.
Create a download directory@comma{} select the platform@comma{} and fetch the archive and
published checksums:

@example
mkdir -p "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.18.0"
cd "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.18.0"
omnivox_version=1.18.0
platform=linux-x64
archive="omnivox-$@{omnivox_version@}-$@{platform@}.tar.gz"
release_url="https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/download/v$@{omnivox_version@}"
curl -fLO "$release_url/$archive"
curl -fLO "$release_url/sha256sums.txt"
@end example

Replace @code{linux-x64} with the appropriate Mac value when needed. Verify before
extraction. On GNU/Linux:

@example
grep "  $archive$" sha256sums.txt | sha256sum --check -
@end example

On macOS:

@example
grep "  $archive$" sha256sums.txt | shasum -a 256 --check
@end example

The command must name the archive and report @code{OK}. Otherwise remove the
download and do not run it. Extract the verified payload into a versioned
directory@comma{} keeping every supplied directory beside the executable:

@example
install_dir="$HOME/.local/opt/omnivox-$@{omnivox_version@}"
mkdir -p "$install_dir"
tar -xzf "$archive" -C "$install_dir"
chmod 755 "$install_dir/omnivox"
export PATH="$install_dir:$PATH"
@end example

Add that @code{PATH} entry to the shell startup used to launch Emacs for subsequent
sessions. The directory contains @code{omnivox}@comma{} @code{espeak-ng-data}@comma{}
@code{third-party-licenses}@comma{} and @code{omnivox-voices.el}. Do not load or copy the last
file into Emacsvox@comma{} which has its own adapter.

Check the binary before starting Emacsvox:

@example
omnivox --version
omnivox --check
omnivox --list-voices
@end example

The version must be at least 1.18.0 and the check must produce tone and speech.
Mac archives are neither code-signed nor notarised. If security policy refuses
the verified binary@comma{} use the source route rather than weakening policy. On
GNU/Linux@comma{} missing libraries or incompatible glibc also call for a source build.

For WSL2@comma{} use @ref{Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2, , Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2}@comma{} which verifies
a Windows archive without requiring a compiler or Docker.

@node Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2
@section Install An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2

This is the ordinary WSL2 route for x86-64 Windows and Windows on ARM@. From
the Emacsvox checkout@comma{} first inspect without changing anything:

@example
./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check
@end example

The doctor detects Windows architecture@comma{} selects its Omnivox 1.18.0 archive@comma{}
and reports Emacs and per-user paths. Missing Linux packages are reported with
an @code{apt} command for you to review and run. Working @code{powershell.exe} and
@code{wslpath} interoperation are required. Resolve prerequisites@comma{} then run:

@example
./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install
@end example

@code{etc/wsl-install.conf} pins the Omnivox version@comma{} release URL@comma{} x64/ARM64 archive
names@comma{} and both SHA-256 hashes. The installer queries Windows architecture and
verifies the matching published archive; it does not build Omnivox.

The full payload is installed below the Windows user's @code{LocalAppData}. Its
absolute WSL executable path is recorded in
@code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox-program}@comma{} normally
@code{~/.config/emacsvox/omnivox-program}. The installer does not invoke @code{sudo} or
edit shell startup. It compiles Emacsvox@comma{} runs a non-audible launcher diagnostic@comma{}
and finishes with the audible server check. Confirm both tone and speech.

No Omnivox checkout@comma{} Docker@comma{} Rust@comma{} MinGW@comma{} Visual Studio@comma{} Windows .NET Framework@comma{}
or optional/proprietary runtime is needed. WinRT speech is included in Windows.
Use @code{./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --no-audio-check} only for controlled/headless
installation with a separate audible check later.

@menu
* Optional Omnivox Engines: Optional Omnivox Engine Modules.  Install, check, remove, and reinstall optional engines from Emacs.
@end menu

@subsection Manual Release Installation

Use this equivalent manual procedure to inspect installer actions@comma{} work around
a restriction@comma{} or repair one layer. It needs @code{curl}@comma{} @code{unzip}@comma{} @code{sha256sum}@comma{}
@code{powershell.exe}@comma{} and @code{wslpath}. The example uses Windows x64. On Windows on
ARM@comma{} use @code{windows-arm64} in the archive and installation directory and verify
that archive's checksum.

Download from WSL:

@example
mkdir -p "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.18.0"
cd "$HOME/Downloads/omnivox-1.18.0"
omnivox_version=1.18.0
archive="omnivox-$@{omnivox_version@}-windows-x64.zip"
release_url="https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/download/v$@{omnivox_version@}"
curl -fLO "$release_url/$archive"
curl -fLO "$release_url/sha256sums.txt"
grep "  $archive$" sha256sums.txt | sha256sum --check -
@end example

The last command must name the archive and report @code{OK}. Otherwise remove it
and do not run it. Install all files on the Windows filesystem:

@example
windows_local_app_data="$(powershell.exe -NoProfile -NonInteractive \
  -Command '[Environment]::GetFolderPath("LocalApplicationData")' | tr -d '\r')"
install_dir="$(wslpath -u "$windows_local_app_data")/Emacsvox/Omnivox/releases/$omnivox_version-windows-x64"
mkdir -p "$install_dir"
unzip -q -o "$archive" -d "$install_dir"
chmod 755 "$install_dir/omnivox.exe"
config_home=$@{XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-"$HOME/.config"@}
mkdir -p "$config_home/emacsvox"
printf '%s\n' "$install_dir/omnivox.exe" > \
  "$config_home/emacsvox/omnivox-program"
@end example

Keep @code{omnivox.exe}@comma{} @code{espeak-ng-data}@comma{} and @code{third-party-licenses} together.
Do not load the archive's @code{omnivox-voices.el}; Emacsvox supplies its adapter.
This data-only file persists selection without shell initialisation. Respect
@code{XDG_CONFIG_HOME} when set. @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} remains an explicit temporary
override with precedence over the file.

Verify through the checkout's launcher:

@example
./servers/omnivox --version
./servers/omnivox --list-voices
./servers/omnivox --check
@end example

Confirm version 1.18.0 or newer and listen for both tone and speech.
The Windows archive is not
code-signed. Do not weaken Windows security or local policy to install it.

The generic 1.18.0 archive includes TGSpeechBox profiles/language data and a
portable RHVoice helper. RHVoice's runtime and voice/language packages are
separate. Optional Piper@comma{} Flite@comma{} and RuTTS companions are not required for
WinRT; matching 1.18.0 companions are published separately.

The clean source-build route verifies/stages the official Piper companion and
builds RHVoice@comma{} Flite@comma{} and RuTTS from matching source. Piper models and the
RHVoice runtime/data are not bundled by that build. A compatible local voice
management service can separately download the reviewed voice packages below;
this does not acquire the RHVoice runtime. Flite/RuTTS need no external runtime
in the source-built bundle. Eloquence/DECtalk runtimes remain separately
licensed and supplied.

@node Optional Omnivox Engine Modules
@subsection Optional Omnivox Engines

The ordinary WSL2 archive is the core@comma{} not a claim that every supported engine
is installed. Companion archives provide larger@comma{} experimental@comma{} or
architecture-specific engines. Some require a separately supplied runtime or
chosen voice data before they can speak.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Inspect running engines}Inspect running engines


Open @kbd{C-e H v b} to browse engines. The equivalent command is:

@example
M-x emacsvox-omnivox-manage-components RET
@end example

Opening uses the current
speech inventory without waiting for file checks or fresh discovery. Optional
engines missing from that inventory stay visible as @emph{not reported} so management
controls remain reachable. @kbd{v} browses voices; @kbd{@key{RET}} opens details.

Details begin with a short status and voice actions@comma{} with @emph{Browse voices}
selected. Matching main/notification evidence is combined and problems remain
near the top. @emph{Startup settings} and @emph{Diagnostics} start folded; @kbd{@key{RET}} toggles
sections. Selection and folding are remembered per engine.

@emph{Available} describes discovery from the current worker. An older check does
not alone make it unavailable. @emph{Previously available} belongs to a stopped or
replaced worker. Details announce the engine before requesting newer inventory
in the background when its check is over five minutes old or belongs to another
worker. File checks likewise run in the background where requested.
@emph{Streams differ} identifies different availability@comma{} policy@comma{} health@comma{} or voice
identities. A bridge's presence does not establish its runtime; @emph{Not checked}
does not mean missing. Diagnostics shows each worker's paths@comma{} inventory time@comma{}
runtime discovery@comma{} and health.

@emph{Required runtime} appears near the top when native libraries are needed.
Successful DECtalk discovery@comma{} for example@comma{} confirms DLL and dictionary loading.
Matching worker evidence is combined@comma{} differences shown separately@comma{} and failures
retain their reasons. Disabled does not mean missing@comma{} and retired-worker evidence
does not establish current status. Opening/refreshing details uses worker
discovery without invoking the separate release installer.

@item
@anchor{Browse voices and variants}Browse voices and variants


@emph{Browse voices} combines reported voices and the installed library@comma{} including
disabled downloads. This dedicated browser preserves Workbench's filters and
staged edits. Languages have expandable headings with counts; @kbd{@key{RET}} toggles.
A one-language list starts expanded and selection/folding are remembered.
@kbd{@key{RET}} or @kbd{P} on a voice auditions an exact sample@comma{} @kbd{x} shows details@comma{} and @kbd{t}
opens an unsaved experiment. Saving asks for a palette destination; browsing
or previewing changes no palette. @kbd{q} returns to selected engine details.

Library data loads asynchronously with quiet refresh. @emph{Installed} identifies
library entries versus supplied voices. @emph{Enabled} is the desired selection
for the next Apply. @emph{Active} describes eligibility on both streams@comma{} main only@comma{}
notification only@comma{} or neither. @emph{Unknown} means missing/inconsistent evidence;
@kbd{R} refreshes. Active does not measure model residency or prove listening.
Samples use main speech; inactive downloads need enablement and Apply first.

@emph{Voice variants} in eSpeak details lists the running worker's bundled variants.
Choose a base with @kbd{b}@comma{} browse with arrows or @kbd{C-n~/~C-p}@comma{} and audition with
@kbd{P} or @kbd{@key{RET}}. Sorting is alphabetical by displayed name@comma{} ignoring capitalisation.
The older @kbd{p} preview key also remains. Variants require no download@comma{} enablement@comma{}
saving@comma{} or speech restart.

@kbd{u}@comma{} @emph{Use in palette}@comma{} asks for palette and named entry@comma{} then opens the voice
editor with the variant preferred@comma{} retaining fallbacks and tuning. Preview with
@kbd{P}@comma{} then @kbd{w} for Save and apply@comma{} or save to the collection without selecting.
Editing a built-in creates a personal copy; @emph{Use at Startup} remains separate.
For Max as ordinary reading@comma{} choose the English base with @kbd{b}@comma{} select @code{Max}@comma{}
press @kbd{u}@comma{} and select the palette's @code{default}. Preview/save there. The same
@code{default} is alongside @code{indent} and other entries in normal palette editing.

@kbd{V} instead proposes the base/variant for every named voice. Create or update
a personal palette@comma{} compare@comma{} then save; shared adjustments and existing fallback
tuning survive. @ref{Editing Named Voices,Editing Named Voices,,emacsvox,} explains review and recovery. @kbd{q} returns to variants
with selection retained; @kbd{v} opens the base browser. Saved references remain
valid. Older workers lacking on-demand variants report the need for an update.

@item
@anchor{Install and activate voice packages}Install and activate voice packages


Installing files@comma{} enabling a voice@comma{} and activating it are separate steps.
@emph{Get more voices} in Piper@comma{} Flite@comma{} MBROLA@comma{} or RHVoice details opens the reviewed
catalogue. Piper groups language@comma{} voice name@comma{} and quality; Flite offers AWB/RMS@.
Single-quality voices appear directly under their language@comma{} with another group
only for multiple qualities. @kbd{@key{RET}} opens a group/model and @kbd{q} returns.
@kbd{/} searches all models by name or language beyond the current group.
Details show size@comma{} source@comma{} terms@comma{} and storage. @kbd{s} shows/hides speakers sharing
a model; it downloads once while individual speakers can be enabled separately.

@kbd{i} installs asynchronously without speaking each progress update. Omnivox
checks files and validates the voice before adding it disabled. In the catalogue@comma{}
@kbd{e} toggles enablement@comma{} asking which speaker for a shared model@comma{} and @kbd{a} reviews
Apply to both streams. Enablement alone does not restart. Model details also
show these actions near installed/enabled status.

Installation/Apply confirmations offer @code{yes} and @code{no} through completion:
@kbd{y @key{TAB} @key{RET}} confirms@comma{} empty @kbd{@key{RET}} declines@comma{} and @kbd{C-g} cancels. @kbd{c} requests
cancellation; wait for cleanup. The shipped catalogue contains reviewed@comma{} pinned@comma{}
natively validated models. Unresolved terms or unsupported configurations are
withheld; it is not a live catalogue of every upstream download.

MBROLA offers US1@comma{} US2@comma{} and US3@comma{} with British English en1 included. Downloads
need the updated separately configured development companion on Linux/Windows.
Older hosts keep Piper/Flite catalogues and report missing MBROLA support.
Each database carries its licence and README@. Initial installation retains
en1 enabled unless explicitly disabled. Multiple enabled databases can serve
different entries/streams; each request opens its selected database rather
than keeping all enabled databases resident.

RHVoice offers Alan (Scottish English)@comma{} BDL/CLB (American English)@comma{} and Ksp
(Indian English)@comma{} requiring an updated host and separately installed compatible
RHVoice runtime selected in engine settings. Each package contains its English
data and upstream notices. Enable and Apply combines managed voices with external
ones@comma{} including SLT@. A conflicting external copy is reported@comma{} not replaced.
Removal affects the managed package@comma{} not external voices or the runtime.

In @emph{Browse voices}@comma{} @kbd{+} changes desired enablement@comma{} and @kbd{a} reviews Apply.
The operation checks both replacement workers@comma{} restarts main and notification
speech@comma{} and verifies them before committing. Failure restores both previous
configurations. Disabling preserves files and palette references. @emph{Enabled}
changes immediately but @emph{Active} awaits Apply.

After installation@comma{} @kbd{l} browses@comma{} @kbd{+} enables@comma{} and @kbd{a} reviews managed engines
and both streams. Installation alone neither loads a voice nor changes speech.
Downloads belong to you in Omnivox's per-user storage across executable upgrades.
Failed/interrupted operations retain files for inspection; automatic resume
and package updates are not provided. Uninstall cleanup has its separate
explicit resume action below.

@item
@anchor{Remove or import managed voices}Remove or import managed voices


Disable every speaker in a downloaded Piper@comma{} Flite@comma{} MBROLA@comma{} or RHVoice package
and Apply before @kbd{C-c u} in its voice browser. Review voices@comma{} size@comma{} and retained
palette references before confirming. Files still needed by other sessions
remain. Built-in SLT/en1@comma{} imported originals@comma{} and engine runtimes cannot be
removed here. @kbd{C-c U} reviews incomplete cleanup for explicit resumption.

The voice browser's @kbd{d} opens downloads and @kbd{b} includes built-in Flite SLT@.
@kbd{i} imports a completed native-validation operation by ID@comma{} initially disabled.
Imported files remain at their validated paths and must stay there. This is
an advanced import@comma{} not the catalogue installation or a file-selection wizard.
@kbd{C-c r} shows the last Apply result@comma{} @kbd{R} refreshes@comma{} and @kbd{q} returns.

Apply needs separate main/notification streams from the updated local host.
Clear explicit Piper/Flite file overrides before applying those engines' library
voices. Uncertain native cleanup or commit blocks another Apply and stays
visible in its result.

@item
@anchor{Configure and check an engine}Configure and check an engine


Under @emph{Startup settings}@comma{} @emph{Edit file settings} opens the bundled local launcher's
engine option in Customize. Piper accepts one ONNX model and matching JSON;
Eloquence/DECtalk accept runtime DLLs; Flite accepts external @code{.flitevox} files
with individual @emph{Load file} switches. Use absolute paths as seen by Emacs;
WSL converts them for the selected Windows executable. Keep companion files
beside their runtime/model. Remote settings must be changed on the speech host.

These are explicit overrides@comma{} not the complete installed-voice inventory.
An empty file selection does not mean that no voices are available: the
library or launcher defaults may still supply them. A manual Flite file list
overrides its installed library.

@emph{Check configured engine} runs independent discovery using the local launcher's
current configuration@comma{} limited to 30 seconds. It retains target@comma{} voices@comma{} and
diagnostics but neither speaks a sample nor replaces live workers. A failed
Flite file can leave built-in SLT usable; incomplete discovery reports fewer
than the selected files plus SLT@. Duplicate external voice names are unsupported.

File settings affect newly started workers. Save in Customize@comma{} then explicitly
choose @emph{Restart local speech} to interrupt this session's two streams when ready.
Refresh live voices afterward. Disabling a Flite file preserves data/references;
new helpers omit it@comma{} while existing helpers keep it until restart. Built-in SLT
stays loaded and each worker loads its own enabled external voices. These
switches are not a general per-voice policy or memory measurement.

@code{OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL}@comma{} @code{OMNIVOX_FLITE_VOICES}@comma{} runtime environment settings@comma{}
and legacy @code{EMACSVOX_ECI_DLL=/=EMACSVOX_DECTALK_DLL} override file options and
are identified in details. Clearing an option returns to discovery/defaults.
Direct-native executable launches do not consume the bundled launcher's options.

@item
@anchor{Manage the installed Omnivox release}Manage the installed Omnivox release


This manager changes versioned release files@comma{} not the running workers or a
voice library's active configuration. On WSL@comma{} choose @emph{Manage installed release}
and check @emph{Installation being managed} and @emph{Location}. That target can differ
from a development build or remote speech server. @emph{Installed engine files}
reports presence; @emph{Check release engine} tests runtime initialisation and
voice discovery for that release.

Release installation and removal keep current speech running and do not select
another speech server. They do not automatically restart workers. A helper in
use causes removal to fail visibly; close sessions using that release before
retrying. This differs from the library's explicit Apply and the file editor's
@emph{Restart local speech}. Legacy direct-operation restart preferences do not
change the boundary of this explicit installed-release view.

Use @emph{Install engine} and confirm to verify and install a version-matched
component. Progress belongs to the release view while current-worker status
stays separate. @emph{Refresh release files} runs a bounded asynchronous file check;
@emph{Check release engine} discovers voices without auditioning. Refresh after a
changed target before retrying. Completion gives a short notice without
selecting another buffer; @emph{Read last result} opens retained complete output.

Within details@comma{} @kbd{@key{TAB}~/~S-@key{TAB}} moves among actions and sections@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} activates
or toggles@comma{} and arrows read visible fields. @kbd{q} returns to the selected engine
row@comma{} @kbd{h} to Emacsvox settings@comma{} and @kbd{?} to Help. Selection and expansion survive
background refresh. @emph{Refresh status} or @kbd{g} in current-worker details asynchronously
requests both live inventories@comma{} not a release-file check.

@emph{Uninstall engine} under @emph{Engine installation} removes only manager-installed
Flite@comma{} RuTTS@comma{} Piper@comma{} or TGSpeechBox components. It does not remove the core@comma{}
built-in engines@comma{} or runtime bridges. The whole managed component directory
is removed: move any personal files elsewhere before confirming. External
runtimes/models outside it are untouched and retain their supplier's removal
procedure. Verified downloads remain cached; @emph{Install engine} reuses a valid
cache for reinstallation.

Operations are asynchronous. The view shows @emph{uninstalling} or @emph{testing} and
refreshes on completion or failure. A failed listing retains its last records
and the error while live discovery remains independently available. If a helper
is locked@comma{} close the sessions using it and invoke the removal action again.
An interrupted removal with only the expected helper left can resume; directories
containing unrecognised files remain protected.

@code{etc/omnivox-components.conf} pins names@comma{} sizes@comma{} and SHA-256 hashes to the
installed core version. The WSL installer rejects mismatched cores@comma{} unsafe
archive layout@comma{} missing source provenance@comma{} and helpers that fail staged
discovery. Components are installed under the same versioned Windows
@code{LocalAppData} release. It does not invoke @code{sudo} or edit shell startup.

Component and data boundaries differ:

@itemize
@item
Flite includes compact English SLT without an external runtime.
@item
RuTTS includes compact Russian voices without an external runtime.
@item
Piper installs engine support; a usable voice also needs a reviewed ONNX
model and model card@comma{} configured separately or through supported library
management.
@item
TGSpeechBox is in the Windows x64/ARM64 core with multilingual formant voices@comma{}
calibrated rate@comma{} and exact anchors for capitalisation/timed presentation.
Matching standalone components allow reinstallation.
@item
RHVoice@comma{} Eloquence@comma{} and DECtalk bridges do not acquire their external runtimes.
Supply compatible runtimes under their distribution/licensing terms.
RHVoice's reviewed catalogue can separately download voice and language
packages on a supporting local host@comma{} as described above. This is distinct
from runtime acquisition and preserves external installations. Eloquence
and DECtalk runtime@comma{} dictionary@comma{} and licence material remain user-supplied.
@end itemize

For terminal management@comma{} use @code{./bin/emacsvox-omnivox-components --list}@comma{} then@comma{}
for example@comma{} @code{./bin/emacsvox-omnivox-components --install flite}. Multiple
engines can follow @code{--install} or @code{--uninstall}.
@code{./bin/emacsvox-omnivox-components --uninstall flite} removes the managed
directory while retaining its archive. Restart deliberately when the running
session needs newly installed configuration@comma{} and use @code{--test ENGINE} or the
release view's @emph{Check release engine} to check that target. Direct release
assets remain available for maintenance/recovery@comma{} but the manager is the
ordinary component-selection route.
@end enumerate

@node Trying Native Windows Emacsvox
@section Installing Native Windows Emacsvox

Native Windows x64 is supported with Emacs 30.2 or newer. A development Setup
executable supplies an installation wizard. Otherwise use the guided
PowerShell installer from a Windows checkout. The advanced manual route
requires an existing native Emacs. Neither Setup nor the guided installation
requires WSL@comma{} Docker@comma{} Rust@comma{} or a system MSYS2 installation.

@subsection Using a Windows Setup Build

Run the development Setup executable to install @code{Emacsvox Windows} for your
account. The wizard identifies Emacsvox and its included Emacs/Omnivox versions.
No administrator privileges or network connection are required. A desktop
shortcut is optional; a Start menu group is created unless disabled for an
unattended installation. The final page explains startup/removal and offers
Test speech for two announcements and Start Emacsvox. Finish runs selected
actions and closes Setup.

The default directory is @code{%LOCALAPPDATA%\Emacsvox\Desktop Dev}. Launch through
Start or its shortcut. Rerun Setup to repair missing files or upgrade a
development build. First close the Emacsvox session using that installation;
Setup does not close it. Locally edited application files prevent replacement so
they can be preserved.

Windows Settings or the Start uninstall entry removes the application while
retaining personal data by default. Optional cleanup separately lists sizes
for logs/cache@comma{} this Emacs profile (including welcome preference)@comma{} and unused
downloads in the shared Omnivox library. Leave choices unchecked for reinstallation.
Shared palettes/settings outside the profile stay. Unattended removal always
preserves personal data.

Active voices@comma{} voices referenced by another profile@comma{} and files retained by a
speech session stay with an explanation. Disable and Apply active downloads@comma{}
then close the sessions using them before cleanup. Imported originals and
system voices are never removed@comma{} and directory links are not followed.
Use the manual accompanying the Setup version you install.

@subsection Guided Windows x64 Installation

You need PowerShell 5.1 or newer@comma{} Git@comma{} network access@comma{} and working Windows audio
with a speech voice. Put the checkout on a local Windows drive@comma{} not a WSL or
network path:

@example
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox.git
Set-Location emacsvox
.\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -Role Desktop -Check
.\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -Role Desktop
@end example

Skip cloning for an existing checkout and inspect its branch/local changes
before updating it. The check reports selected Emacs and paths without downloads@comma{}
configuration writes@comma{} or audio.

The installer reuses native Emacs 30.2 or newer. For a checksum-verified complete
GNU Emacs 31.1 Windows release@comma{} use:

@example
.\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -DownloadEmacs -Check
.\bin\emacsvox-install.ps1 -DownloadEmacs
@end example

This includes runtime libraries without a compiler/MSYS2. Alternatively select
a separately installed @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/download.html, prebuilt Windows Emacs} with @code{-Emacs} and its
@code{bin/emacs.exe}. @code{-BuildEmacs} explicitly builds 31.1 through a private MSYS2
UCRT64 toolchain; it can require several gigabytes and substantial downloading.
No source build begins without that choice. The installer then prepares the
pinned Omnivox core and Emacsvox@comma{} reusing installed components and verified cache
on later runs.

@code{-Emacs "C:\path\to\Emacs\bin\emacs.exe"} selects an existing executable.
Explicit @code{-Emacs}@comma{} environment @code{EMACS}@comma{} and @code{local.mk} must agree and require
native 30.2 or newer. @code{-DownloadEmacs} and @code{-BuildEmacs} choose their managed
Emacs instead of saved/PATH selections@comma{} reuse a matching complete installation@comma{}
and cannot replace an explicit choice. Choose only one.

Per-user defaults are:

@itemize
@item
Emacs@comma{} Omnivox@comma{} isolated profile@comma{} and logs under @code{%LOCALAPPDATA%\Emacsvox\Native}.
@item
Private build tools under @code{%LOCALAPPDATA%\Emacsvox\Toolchains\msys2-20260611}.
@item
Verified archives under @code{%LOCALAPPDATA%\Emacsvox\Downloads}.
@item
Data-only @code{native-install.json} in the checkout@comma{} ignored by Git.
@end itemize

@code{-InstallRoot}@comma{} @code{-ToolchainRoot}@comma{} and @code{-CacheDirectory} choose other local
paths. The MSYS2 tool path must have no spaces; set @code{-ToolchainRoot} if needed.
Checkout and Emacs paths may contain spaces. Personal initialisation@comma{} system
PATH@comma{} Windows services@comma{} and JAWS settings remain unchanged.

@code{-Offline -CacheDirectory "C:\path\to\archives"} forbids downloads and verifies
required cached archives before changes. Missing/damaged archives stop it.
Offline supports prebuilt/existing Emacs@comma{} not @code{-BuildEmacs}. Read-only @code{-Check}
reports the plan without testing cached archives or playing audio.

Source archives are checksum-pinned. MSYS2 compiler packages use signed rolling
repositories@comma{} and installed versions are recorded. Managed source builds disable
Lisp native compilation and include runtime DLLs@comma{} so launch needs no MSYS2 on PATH@.

The final test opens a temporary native Emacs@comma{} verifies both streams@comma{} speaks
two announcements@comma{} and closes that test. Confirm both by listening. After
@code{-NoAudioCheck}@comma{} run the explicit check before using the installation:

@example
.\bin\emacsvox.ps1 -Diagnose
.\bin\emacsvox.ps1 -Check
.\bin\emacsvox.ps1
@end example

The last command opens the isolated desktop with Windows speech by default.
@code{OMNIVOX_ENGINE} remains an explicit selection for separately configured
engines. Native optional-engine installation@comma{} ARM64@comma{} and other native roles
are not supplied by this guided installer.

After source updates@comma{} rerun it before launching. It preserves source edits and
refreshes compiled files. Missing/stale compilation stops launch with that
instruction. Keep named build logs after failure. Inspect incomplete installations
before moving or choosing another directory; unknown partial trees are not
overwritten.

Muting JAWS speech does not disable its shortcuts. If @kbd{M-<} or @kbd{M->} is
intercepted@comma{} try @code{JAWSKey+3} before the key. Bart's @code{workstation-config}
repository supplies the separate Emacs-specific JAWS override used on the
test workstation.

@subsection Manual Native Installation

This route requires native Emacs 30.2 or newer already installed. It does not
acquire Emacs or replace the guided x64 checks. Set @code{$Emacsvox} to the absolute
checkout path and continue in one PowerShell window.

@subsection Download And Verify Omnivox

Set @code{$Platform} for the host. The normal Intel/AMD x64 example is:

@example
$Version = "1.18.0"
$Platform = "windows-x64" # Use windows-arm64 only on Windows on ARM.
$DownloadDir = Join-Path $env:USERPROFILE "Downloads\omnivox-$Version"
$Archive = "omnivox-$Version-$Platform.zip"
$ReleaseUrl = "https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/releases/download/v$Version"

New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $DownloadDir | Out-Null
$ArchivePath = Join-Path $DownloadDir $Archive
$ChecksumPath = Join-Path $DownloadDir "sha256sums.txt"
Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing "$ReleaseUrl/$Archive" -OutFile $ArchivePath
Invoke-WebRequest -UseBasicParsing "$ReleaseUrl/sha256sums.txt" -OutFile $ChecksumPath

$Pattern = '\s+\*?' + [regex]::Escape($Archive) + '$'
$ChecksumLine = Get-Content $ChecksumPath |
  Where-Object @{ $_ -match $Pattern @} |
  Select-Object -First 1
if (-not $ChecksumLine) @{ throw "No published checksum for $Archive" @}
$Expected = ($ChecksumLine -split '\s+')[0].ToLowerInvariant()
$Actual = (Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 $ArchivePath).Hash.ToLowerInvariant()
if ($Actual -ne $Expected) @{ throw "Checksum mismatch for $Archive" @}
Write-Host "Checksum OK: $Archive"
@end example

Do not extract/run unless the final line reports @code{Checksum OK}. This unsigned
Windows release may be rejected by policy; stop rather than weakening security.
The existence of an ARM64 Omnivox archive does not extend Emacsvox's supported
native desktop beyond x64.

@subsection Install And Check The Native Server

In the same PowerShell@comma{} extract everything beneath per-user @code{LocalAppData}:

@example
$InstallDir = Join-Path $env:LOCALAPPDATA "Emacsvox\Omnivox\releases\$Version-$Platform"
New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $InstallDir | Out-Null
Expand-Archive -LiteralPath $ArchivePath -DestinationPath $InstallDir -Force
$Omnivox = Join-Path $InstallDir "omnivox.exe"

foreach ($Required in @@(
  $Omnivox@comma{}
  (Join-Path $InstallDir "espeak-ng-data\phontab")@comma{}
  (Join-Path $InstallDir "third-party-licenses\THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md")
)) @{
  if (-not (Test-Path -LiteralPath $Required -PathType Leaf)) @{
    throw "Incomplete Omnivox installation: $Required"
  @}
@}

& $Omnivox --version
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) @{ throw "Omnivox version check failed" @}
& $Omnivox --list-voices
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) @{ throw "Omnivox voice discovery failed" @}
& $Omnivox --check
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) @{ throw "Omnivox audio check failed" @}
@end example

Require version 1.18.0 or newer@comma{} at least one Windows voice@comma{} and both tone and
speech. Keep executable@comma{} @code{espeak-ng-data}@comma{} and @code{third-party-licenses} together.
Do not copy/load @code{omnivox-voices.el} from the archive; that is the upstream
Emacspeak adapter@comma{} not Emacsvox's bundled one.

@subsection Start Native Emacsvox

Keep the window so @code{$Omnivox} still identifies the verified binary. Set @code{$Emacs}
to native @code{emacs.exe} and @code{$Emacsvox} if the checkout was not set above:

@example
$Emacs = "C:\path\to\emacs\bin\emacs.exe"
# If not set above@comma{} uncomment and edit the next line:
# $Emacsvox = "C:\path\to\emacsvox"

& $Emacs --version
if ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) @{ throw "Could not run native Emacs" @}
$env:TTS_PROGRAM = $Omnivox
& $Emacs -Q --no-splash --load "$Emacsvox\lisp\emacsvox-setup.el"
@end example

Require Emacs 30.2 or newer. Use @code{emacs.exe} instead of @code{runemacs.exe} for first
launch so startup errors remain in PowerShell. Direct setup loading is intentional:
@code{bin/emacsvox} is POSIX; @code{bin/emacsvox.ps1} is the guided native entry.

@subsection Verify Speech And Report Problems

Check the isolated native session:

@enumerate
@item
Confirm the startup introduction/ready checkpoint is audible. Note the
working buffer's name with @kbd{C-e m}.
@item
@kbd{C-h v tts-program @key{RET}} should identify the verified executable's absolute
path. Leave Help and return to the working buffer by name with @kbd{C-x b}.
@item
@kbd{M-x tts-speak-version @key{RET}} speaks the version; @kbd{M-x omnivox-list-voices @key{RET}}
displays Windows voices. Finish inspecting that list@comma{} then return to the
working buffer and confirm its name and mode.
@item
@kbd{C-n} and @kbd{C-p} should speak the lines reached. Press @kbd{C-e s} to stop@comma{}
then @kbd{C-e l} to read again: stopping does not disable later speech. To test
restarting separately@comma{} run @kbd{M-x tts-restart @key{RET}} and confirm that speech
returns without process errors. Restarting is not needed after every Stop.
@item
Toggle icons twice with @kbd{M-x emacsvox-toggle-icons @key{RET}}. In a session
starting with icons enabled@comma{} the second toggle restores them and plays an
icon in addition to speech. Preserve the initial preference when testing a
customised session.
@end enumerate

If direct @code{--check} fails@comma{} diagnose Omnivox/Windows audio first. If it passes
but the server cannot be found in Emacs@comma{} confirm the same PowerShell's
@code{$env:TTS_PROGRAM} is absolute and @code{Test-Path $env:TTS_PROGRAM} returns @code{True}.
For a process that exits@comma{} retain PowerShell errors@comma{} @code{*Messages*}@comma{} Windows
version/architecture@comma{} and full @code{emacs.exe --version} and @code{omnivox.exe --version}.

Once speech works@comma{} put the same selection before setup in your init file:

@lisp
(setenv "TTS_PROGRAM"
        "C:/Users/NAME/AppData/Local/Emacsvox/Omnivox/releases/1.18.0-windows-x64/omnivox.exe")
(load-file "C:/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")
@end lisp

Replace both paths. This deliberately starts with an absolute executable.
Adding its directory to @code{exec-path} is optional until selecting @code{omnivox} by
short name. Native desktop support remains x64; remote speech is a separate
preview.

@node Installing Omnivox From Source
@section Installing Omnivox From Source

On GNU/Linux/macOS@comma{} a source build needs network access for clone@comma{} toolchain@comma{}
and locked dependencies. Prepare:

@itemize
@item
@uref{https://rustup.rs/, rustup}@comma{} with the checked-in @code{rust-toolchain.toml} selecting Rust.
@item
Python 3@comma{} C/C++@comma{} CMake@comma{} and Clang/libclang for eSpeak NG and generated data.
@item
GNU/Linux @code{pkg-config} and ALSA development headers@comma{} commonly
@code{libasound2-dev} on Debian/Ubuntu or @code{alsa-lib-devel} on Fedora.
@item
macOS Xcode command-line tools@comma{} installed with @code{xcode-select --install}.
@end itemize

Keep Omnivox beside Emacsvox@comma{} also matching the WSL layout:

@example
cd /path/containing/emacsvox
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox
cd omnivox
make build
make install
@end example

@code{make build} runs the locked release build and stages matching eSpeak data and
notices. @code{make install} installs binary/data normally under @code{~/.cargo/bin}.
Set @code{OMNIVOX_INSTALL_BIN} only for another Cargo binary directory. A bare
@code{cargo build} does not produce the complete installation payload.

Make the installed directory visible on @code{PATH}. No proprietary runtime is
needed for the recommended Linux/Mac route.

@node Building Omnivox For WSL2
@section Building Omnivox For WSL2

This advanced path maintains the reproducible Windows bundle or optional
engine integrations. Ordinary WSL2 installations should use the
@ref{Installing An Omnivox Windows Release Under WSL2, , prebuilt Windows release}. This source target produces x86-64 Windows.

Requirements are:

@itemize
@item
WSL2 with working @code{powershell.exe} and @code{wslpath}@comma{} plus writable Windows
@code{LocalAppData}.
@item
A Docker client/daemon supporting Linux/AMD64 containers@comma{} runnable by the
WSL user without @code{sudo}. Docker Desktop's WSL integration is one option.
@item
Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or newer@comma{} not merely a modern @code{dotnet} SDK@.
Visual Studio is unnecessary.
@item
@code{git}@comma{} GNU @code{make}@comma{} @code{curl}@comma{} @code{unzip}@comma{} @code{sha256sum}@comma{} and standard GNU utilities
including @code{awk}@comma{} @code{cmp}@comma{} @code{find}@comma{} @code{readlink}@comma{} @code{sed}@comma{} @code{sort}@comma{} and @code{xargs}.
@item
Network for the initial Docker image@comma{} Rust dependencies@comma{} and two
checksum-pinned NuGet packages. Subsequent builds reuse caches.
@item
Sibling @code{emacsvox} and @code{omnivox} checkouts@comma{} or explicit @code{OMNIVOX_DIR}.
The clean release target requires the Omnivox source commit recorded for
the pinned Piper companion in @code{servers/omnivox-release/toolchain.lock};
mismatches fail rather than combining versions. The development route
accepts current source by omitting Piper. Allow disk space for Docker@comma{}
native/cross builds@comma{} and caches.
@end itemize

Rust@comma{} MinGW@comma{} CMake@comma{} Clang/libclang@comma{} eSpeak NG@comma{} Roslyn@comma{} Visual Studio@comma{} and .NET
reference assemblies need not be installed in WSL; the controlled toolchain
pins/builds them. Eloquence@comma{} DECtalk@comma{} and RHVoice runtime/data remain optional
and unnecessary for WinRT@.

Check prerequisites with:

@example
cd /path/to/emacsvox
make verify-windows-omnivox-toolchain
@end example

The first run downloads pinned .NET compiler packages@comma{} builds the pinned Docker
image@comma{} and checks exact Rust@comma{} MinGW@comma{} libclang@comma{} Roslyn@comma{} and Framework versions.
It is not a read-only presence check. Failures identify missing host tools or
unsupported Framework before the full build.

With clean Emacsvox and sibling Omnivox tracked files@comma{} run:

@example
cd /path/to/emacsvox
make windows-omnivox
@end example

This reproducible release path builds a pinned Windows-GNU executable and
stages a content-addressed runtime under @code{servers/omnivox-bin}. When the Piper
lock identifies the matching integrations@comma{} it includes the checksum-pinned
Piper companion@comma{} RHVoice helper without runtime@comma{} Flite with CMU US SLT@comma{} and
RuTTS with male/female Russian voices. It bundles no Piper model or RuLex.
The payload is also copied to Windows-local storage to avoid WSL filesystem
cost during discovery. It does not download Eloquence@comma{} DECtalk@comma{} or RHVoice
runtimes or require them for WinRT@.

The final live gate starts that exact runtime@comma{} discovers Flite SLT and both
RuTTS voices@comma{} and requires nonempty WAV synthesis from each helper. Repeat
without rebuilding using @code{make verify-windows-omnivox-live}.

To register Piper@comma{} review its model/card and keep the adjacent @code{.onnx.json} or
@code{.json}. Supply the absolute model path while staging:

@example
OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL=/absolute/path/to/voice.onnx make windows-omnivox
@end example

Model/configuration are copied to separate content-addressed Windows
@code{LocalAppData} storage with recorded hashes@comma{} not bundled into the runtime.
Omitting that variable installs the companion but leaves Piper unregistered.
After staging@comma{} check with a fresh process:

@example
./servers/omnivox --engine piper --list-voices
@end example

Restart both speech processes or Emacs@comma{} then inspect running engines with
@kbd{C-e H v b}. Piper requires the newly staged server and successfully loaded
model/helper. The direct @kbd{C-e d e} preference chooser is not a read-only
inventory command; use the browser to inspect without selecting a preference.

For tracked development changes@comma{} use the route in
@ref{Integration Maintenance, , Integration Maintenance} rather than bypassing the clean-release guard:

@example
OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_LIBRARY=/mnt/c/path/to/RHVoice.dll \
  OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_DATA=/mnt/c/path/to/RHVoice/data \
  make windows-omnivox-dev
./servers/omnivox --engine flite --list-voices
./servers/omnivox --engine rutts --list-voices
@end example

It builds current source@comma{} stages RHVoice's helper@comma{} Flite SLT@comma{} and RuTTS 6.3.3
male/female voices@comma{} deliberately omitting the older pinned Piper companion.
RuTTS needs no separate install; RuLex is excluded. RHVoice needs compatible
@code{RHVoice.dll} and language/voice data. Supplied library/data variables are
validated and Windows path pointers retained beside the local runtime; the
launcher restores them for fresh sessions. Also set @code{OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_CONFIG}
for a separate configuration directory. These external files are not copied
into the payload. Omnivox's @code{docs/RHVOICE.md} and @code{docs/RUTTS.md} give validation
details. Restart after restaging/changing inputs@comma{} then inspect with @kbd{C-e H v b}.

Piper tests against newer committed Omnivox use @code{windows-omnivox-piper-dev}
and a native Windows CI artifact. It requires the published SHA-256 and a
reviewed external model@comma{} rejecting dirty or different source. See
@ref{Integration Maintenance, , Integration Maintenance} for provenance and the complete command. This does
not alter the reproducible release's pinned companion.

@node Selecting And Checking Omnivox
@section Selecting And Checking Omnivox

Test the installed server outside Emacs on GNU/Linux/macOS:

@example
omnivox --version
omnivox --check
omnivox --list-voices
@end example

In WSL2@comma{} the launcher selects @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} first@comma{} the per-user recorded
installation next@comma{} then a content-addressed source runtime@comma{} then @code{omnivox} on
@code{PATH}:

@example
./servers/omnivox --check
@end example

The check initialises engine/output and plays tone then speech. Its success
message asks you to confirm that you hear both. If either is absent@comma{} follow
@ref{Backend Diagnostics And Ownership, , Backend Diagnostics} before starting Emacsvox.

Select before loading Emacsvox:

@example
export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox
@end example

An export affects that shell and children; a shell-startup entry persists it.
On POSIX@comma{} @code{servers/omnivox} finds @code{omnivox} on @code{PATH}; @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM}
can explicitly override it. The WSL per-user selection persists across shells
and outranks staged source runtimes. Native Windows resolves @code{omnivox.exe}
through @code{exec-path} or the absolute @code{TTS_PROGRAM}. Follow
@ref{Trying Native Windows Emacsvox, , Installing Native Windows Emacsvox} for the supported native x64 route.

In a running session@comma{} @kbd{M-x omnivox} temporarily selects that server.
@kbd{M-x tts-speak-version} identifies the live adapter and @kbd{M-x tts-restart}
restarts after a deliberate configuration change. The complete isolated start@comma{}
expected speech@comma{} and recovery remain in @ref{Installation, , Installation}.

@subsection Remote Workstation Speech (Preview)

@anchor{Remote workstation speech setup}

Emacs can run on an SSH host while Omnivox@comma{} installed voices@comma{} and playback stay
on your workstation. This uses Omnivox 1.18.0's @code{--serve} and Emacsvox's remote
transport. Remote access remains a preview feature.

Create a private token on the workstation. The tool refuses to overwrite or
print an existing token:

@example
mkdir -p "$HOME/.config/omnivox"
python3 /path/to/omnivox/tools/create_remote_token.py \
    "$HOME/.config/omnivox/remote-token"
@end example

Leave the installed server running in a terminal@comma{} with the matching Emacsvox
sound-pack directory:

@example
omnivox --serve --token-file "$HOME/.config/omnivox/remote-token" \
    --sound-root /path/to/emacsvox/sounds
@end example

For a Windows service through the WSL launcher@comma{} translate both paths:

@example
/path/to/emacsvox/servers/omnivox --serve \
    --token-file "$(wslpath -w "$HOME/.config/omnivox/remote-token")" \
    --sound-root "$(wslpath -w /path/to/emacsvox/sounds)"
@end example

In another workstation terminal@comma{} copy the token through verified SSH and open
the reverse forward@comma{} replacing @code{user@@emacs-host}:

@example
ssh user@@emacs-host 'mkdir -p ~/.config/omnivox; chmod 700 ~/.config/omnivox'
scp "$HOME/.config/omnivox/remote-token" user@@emacs-host:.config/omnivox/remote-token
ssh user@@emacs-host 'chmod 600 ~/.config/omnivox/remote-token'
ssh -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes -o ServerAliveInterval=10 \
    -o ServerAliveCountMax=2 \
    -R 127.0.0.1:6417:127.0.0.1:6417 user@@emacs-host
@end example

When Omnivox runs on Windows@comma{} use Windows OpenSSH for the final connection:
@code{ssh.exe} from WSL or @code{ssh} from PowerShell. It must reach Windows loopback;
a WSL client under NAT reaches separate WSL loopback instead. Windows SSH uses
its Windows keys and host-trust configuration.

@ref{Installing Remote Emacsvox, , Installing Remote Emacsvox} provides the remote-only installation and isolated
launcher. @code{--remote} configures transport before speech starts and needs no
local Omnivox binary. For personal configuration@comma{} set before startup:

@lisp
(setq tts-program "omnivox"
      omnivox-remote-host "127.0.0.1"
      omnivox-remote-port 6417
      omnivox-remote-token-file "~/.config/omnivox/remote-token")
@end lisp

@kbd{M-x omnivox-remote-connect} connects/retries@comma{} @kbd{M-x omnivox-remote-status}
inspects@comma{} and @kbd{M-x omnivox-remote-disconnect} stops and suspends retries.
Reconnection refreshes voices/routing and discards unfinished speech. Main and
notification retain separate workers. Only one Emacs session owns a service
at a time.

If main connects but notification fails@comma{} main remains available while the
missing connection retries. Recovery does not replace working main speech.
@code{omnivox-remote-auto-reconnect} set to nil disables automatic retry; explicit
disconnect suspends it until another connection. Status retains retry and error
information.

Keep service and forwarded endpoint on loopback; do not enable SSH
@code{GatewayPorts}. SSH encrypts the transport and the token excludes other remote
host users. To rotate@comma{} stop the service@comma{} replace both private token files@comma{} and
restart. Typing @code{quit} or interrupting the service terminal stops workers.

Inventory belongs to the workstation; install/manage engines there. Bundled
icons resolve by pack-relative name. Personal sounds outside that directory
and sound uploads are unsupported; no @code{--sound-root} means no file-based icons.
For an occupied remote port@comma{} change the left side of @code{-R} and match
@code{omnivox-remote-port}. Authentication failure indicates differing tokens or
excessively broad file permissions; a busy service belongs to another connection.

@node Compatibility Speech Servers
@section Compatibility Speech Servers

These retained direct servers support existing installations@comma{} not the recommended
first setup or platform matrix:

@table @code
@item espeak
Direct Linux Tcl server. @code{make espeak} needs Tclx at runtime@comma{} Tcl 8.6
headers@comma{} eSpeak NG development libraries@comma{} and C++. Useful for compatibility
testing; new installations should use Omnivox.
@item swiftmac
Direct Swift macOS server@comma{} needing Swift 5.9 or newer and @code{make swiftmac}.
See @code{servers/mac-swiftmac/Readme.org} for this alternative to Omnivox's
AVSpeechSynthesizer engine.
@item mac
Legacy Python/PyObjC server. The unset Darwin selector still names it as
a compatibility default@comma{} not an initial-user route. It needs Foundation@comma{}
AppKit@comma{} and PyObjCTools.
@item outloud
Direct Linux IBM TTS/Eloquence adapter with separately licensed compatible
runtime. Emacsvox does not distribute it. Voxin availability is described
at @uref{https://voxin.oralux.net}.
@item dtk-soft
Direct Software DECtalk adapter with separately installed runtime. See
@code{servers/software-dtk/Readme.org}. Licensed runtimes are not distributed
by Emacsvox.
@end table

The native Windows audio@comma{} standalone Eloquence@comma{} and standalone DECtalk bridges
under @code{servers/} are advanced WSL compatibility paths. Piper is an optional
Omnivox engine/model workflow@comma{} not a primary Emacsvox server.

@node Backend Diagnostics And Ownership
@section Backend Diagnostics And Responsibilities

Local server messages/errors remain in diagnostics buffers@comma{} hidden from the
ordinary buffer list by default. Use @kbd{C-e H D m} for main or @kbd{C-e H D n} for
notification diagnostics@comma{} or @kbd{M-x tts-show-diagnostics} with @code{Speaker} or
@code{Notify}. Missing local diagnostics are reported rather than invented.

Set @code{tts-hide-diagnostic-buffers} to nil in Customize and restart speech to
include them in the ordinary list. Existing diagnostics are retained; custom
buffer switchers may show hidden buffers independently.

Choose the diagnostic at the failed layer:

@table @asis
@item @code{omnivox} is not found
In WSL@comma{} run @code{./bin/emacsvox-wsl-install --check} and inspect its saved path.
On other POSIX systems check @code{PATH} or an absolute @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM}.
Keep @code{espeak-ng-data} and @code{third-party-licenses} beside the executable.
@item @code{omnivox --check} errors or is inaudible
Diagnose below Emacsvox using Omnivox's
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/README.md, build guide}@comma{}
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/STATUS.md, platform status}@comma{} and
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/guides/diagnostics.md, diagnostics guide}. Do not change Emacs configuration before tone/speech work.
@item The check passes but Emacsvox is silent
Confirm @code{TTS_PROGRAM} was @code{omnivox} before setup loaded@comma{} then use
@kbd{M-x tts-speak-version}. Rerun the WSL installer check for a release
installation. For development source staging@comma{} run
@code{make verify-windows-omnivox-runtime} and restart after restaging.
@item A process starts and later fails
The POSIX/WSL launcher retains private bounded stderr logs at
@code{$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox}@comma{} normally
@code{~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox}. Spoken text is excluded unless
@code{OMNIVOX_LOG_SYNTHESIS_TEXT} is explicitly enabled; that can expose
passwords@comma{} mail@comma{} and documents.
@item Speech works but is delayed
Note whether diagnostic logging is already enabled. Use @kbd{C-e d D} to enable
it only if needed@comma{} reproduce one short non-confidential presentation@comma{} then
restore its previous on/off state. The default log path is
@code{~/.emacsvox-aural-diagnostics.log} and can be changed
with @kbd{C-u C-e d D} when enabling. Records @code{dispatch-sent}@comma{}
@code{mixer-source-observed}@comma{} and @code{playback-terminal-observed} share a dispatch ID@.
Match it in Omnivox logs to separate submission@comma{} queueing@comma{} synthesis@comma{} and
mixer-source delay. This Emacs log is off by default because submitted
speech text is complete. A mixer observation is not physical output;
device/hardware buffering can add delay. The diagnostics menu's @kbd{l} also
exposes the explicit session logging control with its privacy warning.
@end table

Emacsvox owns selection@comma{} launcher@comma{} Windows staging@comma{} and its bundled Lisp adapter.
Omnivox owns native builds@comma{} engines@comma{} CLI@comma{} runtime layout@comma{} and protocols.
Backend-local READMEs document compatibility adapters and licensed runtimes.
Use those owning references for versioned details instead of duplicating their
contracts in first-installation instructions.

