@node Everyday Reading Commands
@section Reading reminders

@cindex reading commands, quick reference
@cindex read from here, command

These commands apply to the selected buffer's accessible text. In Info that
means the current node@comma{} not the whole manual. Use the application's own reader
for a table@comma{} terminal or other specialised view.

@table @asis
@item Read from here (@kbd{C-e i})
Read towards the accessible buffer end with
tracked position. Requires a supporting speech server; point advances by
chunks@comma{} and another command stops reading before performing its action.
See @ref{Reading From Point,Stopping and restarting tracked reading,,emacsvox,}.
@item Stop current speech (@kbd{C-e s})
Stop main and notification speech without
disabling future feedback. During tracked reading this retains a chunk
boundary@comma{} not an exact spoken word. See @ref{Reading From Point,Where stopping leaves point,,emacsvox,}.
@item Repeat the current line (@kbd{C-e l})
Inspect the line without moving point.
@kbd{C-u C-e l} reads from point to the line's end. See
@ref{Reading Without Moving Point,Short reading and prefix variants,,emacsvox,}.
@item Read or spell the word (@kbd{C-e w})
Read the word at point; repeat at the
same position to spell it. See @ref{Reading Without Moving Point,Inspecting short passages,,emacsvox,}.
@item Inspect the character (@kbd{C-e c})
Read the character at point@comma{} using its
phonetic name where available. Point does not move. See
@ref{Reading,Character and word reading,,emacsvox,}.
@item Read the paragraph (@kbd{C-e [})
Read the paragraph around point without
moving it. The mode determines its boundaries. Sentence reading is also
available by name. See @ref{Reading Without Moving Point,Sentence and paragraph inspection,,emacsvox,}.
@item Read a checked selection (@kbd{C-e r})
Read between point and mark. A short
region read preserves its endpoints and activation; it does not establish a
new selection for you. See @ref{Selecting Text With Speech,Selecting and checking text,,emacsvox,}.
@item Read the buffer (@kbd{C-e b})
Read its accessible contents without tracked
interruption position. Requests of 40@comma{}000 characters or more fall back to
windowful reading at the current default limit@comma{} removing other windows.
The same limit applies to region reading. See
@ref{Reading Without Moving Point,Buffer-reading limits and alternatives,,emacsvox,}.
@end table

Page reading and the complete reading-key list remain under
@ref{Reading,Reading and navigating,,emacsvox,}. Page reading changes the mark; it is not a
substitute for inspecting an existing selection.

@node Everyday Editing Commands
@section Editing reminders

@cindex editing commands, quick reference
@cindex cut and paste, command reference

Use these in ordinary text@comma{} after leaving any prompt or special reader. Check
the buffer and the affected text first. @ref{Editing Terms,Selection and editing terms,,emacsvox,}
defines Emacs's names for copying@comma{} cutting and pasting.

@table @asis
@item Start a selection (@kbd{C-@key{SPC}})
Set the mark@comma{} then move point to its other
end. Extend it by moving rather than repeatedly setting the mark. See
@ref{Selecting Text With Speech,Selection boundaries and newline checks,,emacsvox,}.
@item Inspect the other end (@kbd{C-x C-x})
Exchange point and mark. The selected
text is unchanged until you move an endpoint. See
@ref{Selecting Text With Speech,Adjusting a checked selection,,emacsvox,}.
@item Copy (@kbd{M-w})
Copy the checked region to the kill ring without removing
the source text. See @ref{Copying Text Between Buffers,Copying into another buffer,,emacsvox,}.
@item Cut (@kbd{C-w})
Remove the checked region and retain it in the kill ring.
This edits the buffer@comma{} not the saved file. See
@ref{Moving Text Safely,Moving text and verifying both locations,,emacsvox,}.
@item Paste (@kbd{C-y})
Insert the current kill-ring entry at point; clipboard
integration can supply a more recent external copy instead. Point ends
after the inserted text. See @ref{Copying Text Between Buffers,Checking the destination and pasted text,,emacsvox,}.
@item Use an earlier copy (@kbd{M-y})
Immediately after a yank@comma{} replace that
insertion with an earlier entry. After a separate command@comma{} including a
reading command@comma{} choose an entry for another insertion instead. See
@ref{Choosing Earlier Copied Text,Earlier copies and yank sequencing,,emacsvox,}.
@item Undo while listening (@kbd{M-x undo-only @key{RET}})
Undo a change in the selected
buffer@comma{} inspect it@comma{} and repeat only as needed. Deactivate an unrelated
selection first. Unlike ordinary @kbd{C-/}@comma{} this avoids undoing an earlier undo
after an intervening reading command. See
@ref{Recovering Text Edits,Undo@comma{} inspection and recovery limits,,emacsvox,}.
@item Find text (@kbd{C-s} or @kbd{C-r})
Search forwards or backwards. Finish with
@kbd{@key{RET}} before typing an edit; text entered during search changes the query.
See @ref{Searching And Replacing Text,Search direction and failure recovery,,emacsvox,}.
@item Review replacements (@kbd{M-%})
Query-replace in the checked active region@comma{}
or ordinarily from point onwards when no region is active. The replacement
questions have their own keys; stopping does not undo accepted changes.
See @ref{Reviewing Text Replacements,Scoped replacement and match-by-match decisions,,emacsvox,}.
@end table

Copying is not a permanent backup. Message repetition and filename speech can
also change the kill ring; @ref{Copying Text Between Buffers,the transfer guide,,emacsvox,} explains how to inspect it
without accidentally replacing the next paste.

@node Everyday Workspace Commands
@section Files@comma{} buffers and windows

@cindex files and windows, quick reference
@cindex close a view, command

A buffer contains work; a window displays it. Identify the selected buffer with
@kbd{C-e m} before editing@comma{} saving or closing anything. For definitions@comma{} see
@ref{Working Context Terms,Buffers@comma{} windows and frames,,emacsvox,}.

@table @asis
@item Open a file (@kbd{C-x C-f})
Visit a pathname@comma{} creating a new unsaved buffer
if the file does not exist. @kbd{C-x C-r} visits read-only instead. See
@ref{Opening A Document,Checking the document and its destination,,emacsvox,}.
@item Save the current file (@kbd{C-x C-s})
Write this buffer to its file; a new
buffer may need a filename. An error is not a completed save. See
@ref{Saving A Working Copy,Saving destinations and failure recovery,,emacsvox,}.
@item Save under another filename (@kbd{C-x C-w})
Write the current text@comma{} including
unsaved edits@comma{} and change where this buffer's later saves go. Cancel an
unexpected overwrite prompt. See @ref{Saving A Working Copy,Working copies versus copies of saved files,,emacsvox,}.
@item Switch to another buffer (@kbd{C-x b})
Select content by buffer name in the
current window. The buffer you leave stays available. See
@ref{Desktop Objects,Buffer selection,,emacsvox,}.
@item Display a reference (@kbd{C-x 4 b})
Display and select an existing buffer
in another window@comma{} creating or reusing a view. See
@ref{Reference And Draft Windows,Keeping a reference beside a draft,,emacsvox,}.
@item Select another window (@kbd{C-x o})
Select the next view; lowercase o is not
zero. With more than two windows@comma{} the next one need not be the draft. See
@ref{Working With Windows,Identifying the selected view,,emacsvox,}.
@item Remove a window (@kbd{C-x 0})
Remove this view@comma{} not its buffer. The last
ordinary window cannot be removed this way. @kbd{C-x 1} instead keeps only the
selected view. See @ref{Closing Windows Safely,Closing windows without losing documents,,emacsvox,}.
@item Close a buffer (@kbd{C-x k})
Kill the named buffer@comma{} checking any unsaved-work
prompt. This is not the same as removing its window or deleting its file.
See @ref{Opening A Document,Leaving document buffers safely,,emacsvox,}.
@end table

@node Everyday Help Commands
@section Help and orientation

@cindex Help commands, quick reference
@cindex where am I, command

Help can change the selected buffer and window arrangement. Before your next
editing command@comma{} check that you have returned to the intended document.

@table @asis
@item Identify your context (@kbd{C-e m})
Speak the mode line@comma{} including the
current buffer and major mode. See @ref{Status Information,Position and status commands,,emacsvox,}.
@item Describe the windows (@kbd{C-e C-w})
Describe the current arrangement.
This does not select a different view. See
@ref{Working With Windows,Working with windows through speech,,emacsvox,}.
@item Explain a key (@kbd{C-h k} followed by the key sequence)
Describe its
effective binding without invoking it as the requested action. See
@ref{Using Online Help,Discovering a key@comma{} command or setting,,emacsvox,}.
@item Discover local commands (@kbd{C-h m})
Describe the current modes. @kbd{C-h b}
lists effective bindings; invoke these from the application you are asking
about. See @ref{Using Online Help,Help for the current buffer,,emacsvox,}.
@item Read Help again (@kbd{C-e h})
Select and read Help using a windowful helper
that removes other windows without killing their buffers. Lowercase h is
different from capital H for settings. See
@ref{Using Online Help,Reading Help and returning to work,,emacsvox,}.
@item Open the manual (@kbd{C-e C-i})
Open this manual directly in Info. Use @kbd{i}
in Info to look up a subject and @kbd{l} to retrace a link. See
@ref{Finding Help In Info,Manual lookup and return navigation,,emacsvox,}.
@end table

@kbd{C-e f} speaks a filename or default directory@comma{} and @kbd{C-e a} repeats a message.
Both also put text in the kill ring and can affect the next paste. See
@ref{Status Information,Status commands and their side effects,,emacsvox,}.

@node Everyday Speech Commands
@section Speech and settings

@cindex speech settings, command reference
@cindex cancel versus undo

These routes use the current settings interface. A live adjustment@comma{} a saved
default and a retained draft are distinct; follow the linked scope and saving
instructions before making a persistent change.

@table @asis
@item Open speech controls (@kbd{C-e H s})
Browse rate@comma{} punctuation and related
controls for the captured context. Inspect the displayed scope; global
defaults need not override local values. See @ref{Speech System,Speech controls and scope,,emacsvox,}.
@item Open settings for this task (@kbd{C-e S})
Use the integration's focused menu
when available@comma{} otherwise general buffer settings. @kbd{C-e H .} always opens
the latter. See @ref{Using Online Help,Finding the appropriate settings menu,,emacsvox,}.
@item Find a setting (@kbd{C-e H /})
Choose a matching name; accepting the search
selects its control without changing the value. Activate it separately.
See @ref{Finding Settings And Actions,Settings search and action search,,emacsvox,}.
@item Explain this item's feedback (@kbd{C-e E})
Inspect the presentation chosen
for the object at point. The report is not itself a repair. See
@ref{Explaining Presentation,Understanding voices@comma{} sounds and rules,,emacsvox,}.
@end table

In an Emacsvox settings menu@comma{} @kbd{q} goes back and @kbd{C-q} closes the sequence.
In an ordinary unfinished prompt@comma{} @kbd{C-g} cancels. None of these reverses an
already applied change. Editors@comma{} tables and terminals have their own return
commands; see @ref{Returning From Settings,Returning from settings,,emacsvox,} and
@ref{Recovering Aural Changes,Recovering an unfinished edit,,emacsvox,}.

@node Emacsvox Key Prefixes
@section How prefixes and menus fit together

@table @kbd
@item C-e
The main Emacsvox keymap@comma{} including reading@comma{} status@comma{} and navigation commands.
@item C-e d
The text-to-speech keymap@comma{} including direct rate@comma{} punctuation@comma{} and echo controls.
@item C-e H
Open the general settings menu. Its displayed keys and contextual Help
provide discoverable routes to speech@comma{} voices@comma{} sounds@comma{} rules@comma{} and diagnostics.
@item C-e S
Open focused settings for the captured integration when available@comma{} otherwise
general buffer settings. @kbd{C-e H .} always opens general buffer settings.
@end table

A prefix such as @kbd{C-e} waits for another key; @kbd{C-e H} is a complete
command that opens a settings menu. Release Control-e before typing capital H@.
Once the menu is open@comma{} its own displayed letters choose actions. They are not
additional keys to hold with Control.

Inside an Emacsvox settings menu@comma{} arrows browse and @kbd{@key{RET}} activates a choice.
@kbd{?} followed by an item's key explains it; @kbd{q} goes back and @kbd{C-q} closes
the sequence. Shift-left/right adjusts a selected numeric control where offered.
To inspect the selected item@comma{} use @kbd{C-c l} to repeat it@comma{} @kbd{M-f~/~M-b} for words
or @kbd{C-f~/~C-b} for characters. In this menu context @kbd{C-a~/~C-e} reaches the
start/end of item review@comma{} so @kbd{C-e} is not the speech prefix. Menu-specific
bindings take precedence; @ref{settings-menu-review, , menu review} explains the full set and its limits.

A voice editor@comma{} terminal@comma{} table reader or another Transient
application retains its own keys. The same letter can mean Save in an editor@comma{}
Export in a palette manager or ordinary input in a terminal. Check which view
has focus before applying a command from another view's instructions.

Integration-specific and convenience maps depend on major/minor modes@comma{} window
system or terminal@comma{} and personal configuration. Live Help resolves those
contexts. The manual uses the default speech prefix; a customised prefix changes
its entry sequences without changing a menu's own displayed shortcuts.

@section Choose a different speech prefix

Set @code{emacsvox-prefix} before loading Emacsvox. For example:

@lisp
(setq emacsvox-prefix (kbd "C-c e"))
@end lisp

Then @kbd{C-c e l} reads the line and @kbd{C-c e e} provides end-of-line recovery.
Evil@comma{} EXWM@comma{} widgets@comma{} and Term use the prefix followed by @kbd{e} for their
corresponding recovery command when it contains multiple events. Repeating
such a prefix is also bound if unused or already assigned to that recovery.
Existing commands and submaps take precedence: @kbd{C-c e C-c} retains selective
display@comma{} so @kbd{C-c e C-c e} is not installed as recovery.

Single-event prefixes keep their repeated-key behaviour@comma{} including default
@kbd{C-e C-e}. Mode-local bindings can still shadow the prefix; inspect them before
assuming a key is unavailable or broken.

@section Inspect the effective bindings

@kbd{C-h m} describes the current modes and their commands. @kbd{C-h b} lists effective
bindings@comma{} and @kbd{C-h k} followed by a key identifies its command. After typing
a prefix such as @kbd{C-e}@comma{} @kbd{C-h} shows that prefix's current bindings.

When live Help differs from this manual@comma{} check personal configuration and
installed package versions@comma{} then compare the generated defaults. For speech
feedback problems rather than binding discovery@comma{} see
@ref{Presentation Troubleshooting,Troubleshooting And Recovery,,emacsvox,}.

