@node Working Context Terms
@section Your working context

@cindex buffer, meaning
@cindex point, meaning
@cindex window, meaning
@cindex frame, meaning
@cindex read-only, meaning
@cindex narrowing, meaning
@cindex captured source
@cindex scope, meaning

@table @asis
@item Buffer
An Emacs object containing a document or application view. It is
not necessarily a file@comma{} and switching buffers does not close the one you
leave. A window displays a buffer. See @ref{Desktop Objects,Buffers hold your work,,emacsvox,}.
@item Window
A view of a buffer inside an Emacs frame. Removing a window does
not kill its buffer. Two windows showing the same buffer are views of the
same text@comma{} not independent copies. See @ref{Working With Windows,Working with windows,,emacsvox,}.
@item Frame
An outer Emacs application window@comma{} or the corresponding display in
a terminal session. It contains Emacs windows. Selecting another window
within a frame differs from switching applications in the operating system.
See @ref{Working With Windows,Windows and frames,,emacsvox,}.
@item Point
The current editing position@comma{} between characters. Text is normally
inserted there. Short reading often leaves it unchanged; tracked reading
advances it. See @ref{Reading From Point,Reading position and interruption,,emacsvox,}.
@item Mode
The commands and behaviour associated with a buffer. Its major mode
supplies its principal tools; minor modes add other behaviour. See
@ref{Object Oriented Desktop,Let modes give each buffer its tools,,emacsvox,}.
@item Read-only
A buffer state that prevents ordinary text edits. It does not
mean the buffer has no unsaved changes@comma{} and making a buffer writable does
not grant permission to overwrite a protected file. Special applications
may still provide their own operations. See @ref{Opening A Document,Opening a document safely,,emacsvox,}.
@item Narrowing
Restricting access to part of a buffer without deleting the
rest. Reading@comma{} movement and many editing commands stay within that portion;
widening removes the restriction. Ordinary file saving still saves the
entire buffer. See @ref{Reading Without Moving Point,Reading accessible text,,emacsvox,} and
@ref{Narrowing,Narrowing,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual.
@item Captured source
The buffer and position retained when a settings workflow
starts. Contextual actions continue to refer to that source rather than
whichever menu or editor is currently selected. A changed or closed source
may need to be captured again. See @ref{Aural Home,Getting started with settings menus,,emacsvox,}.
@item Scope
Where a setting or rule applies@comma{} such as one buffer@comma{} a session@comma{}
or global defaults. Scope is not the same as persistence: a global change
need not be saved@comma{} and a saved resource need not be active. See
@ref{Saving And Startup,Choose what persists,,emacsvox,}.
@end table

@node Editing Terms
@section Selection@comma{} copying and editing

@cindex mark, meaning
@cindex region, meaning
@cindex selection, meaning
@cindex active region, meaning
@cindex Transient Mark mode, meaning
@cindex copy, meaning
@cindex cut, meaning
@cindex kill, meaning
@cindex paste, meaning
@cindex yank, meaning
@cindex kill ring, meaning
@cindex clipboard, meaning
@cindex undo, meaning

@table @asis
@item Mark
A remembered position in a buffer. Point and mark delimit the
region@comma{} whichever comes first. The mark can remain after the selection is
deactivated; it is not copied text. See @ref{Selecting Text With Speech,Selecting and checking text,,emacsvox,}.
@item Region or selection
The text between point and mark@comma{} including any
selected spaces and newlines. The character at its end is excluded. The
active region is the current selection; some commands can also use an
inactive mark. Establish a fresh region before cutting. See
@ref{Selecting Text With Speech,Selection boundaries and activation,,emacsvox,}.
@item Transient Mark mode
Emacs's usual active-selection behaviour@comma{} used by
the editing examples. It is unrelated to the Transient package that supplies
settings menus. See @ref{Selecting Text With Speech,Using the region through speech,,emacsvox,} and
@ref{Mark,The mark and the region,,emacs,} in the GNU Emacs Manual.
@item Copy
Store a passage in the kill ring while leaving its source text
unchanged. The destination becomes an independent text copy when you paste
it. See @ref{Copying Text Between Buffers,Copying between buffers,,emacsvox,}.
@item Kill or cut
Remove text and keep it in the kill ring for later insertion.
Some deletion commands do not keep that copy. Killing a buffer is a different
operation: it closes the buffer rather than copying its contents. See
@ref{Moving Text Safely,Moving text safely,,emacsvox,}.
@item Yank or paste
Insert text from the kill ring@comma{} or through configured
clipboard integration@comma{} at point. It changes the destination buffer@comma{} not
automatically its saved file. See @ref{Copying Text Between Buffers,Checking a paste,,emacsvox,}.
@item Kill ring
Emacs's shared history of copied and cut text. It can contain
more than the latest passage@comma{} including text placed there by inspection
commands. It is not a permanent backup or the same store as the system
clipboard. See @ref{Choosing Earlier Copied Text,Choosing an earlier copy,,emacsvox,}.
@item Clipboard
The operating system's text-transfer facility. Its connection
to Emacs depends on the display session and configuration; a new external
copy can affect the next paste. See @ref{Copying Through The Clipboard,Testing clipboard exchange,,emacsvox,}.
@item Undo
Reverse recorded edits in the selected buffer. It does not reverse
a settings save or an action already sent to another application. When
listening between successive corrections@comma{} use the recovery guide's
@code{undo-only} procedure. See @ref{Recovering Text Edits,Undo and spoken inspection,,emacsvox,}.
@end table

@node Presentation Resource Terms
@section Voices and presentation resources

@cindex physical voice, meaning
@cindex named voice, meaning
@cindex palette, meaning
@cindex profile, meaning
@cindex rule, meaning
@cindex auditory icon, meaning

@table @asis
@item Physical voice
A voice reported by a speech engine@comma{} such as an installed
system voice or a downloaded model's voice. Installation alone does not
prove that it is enabled@comma{} active@comma{} or audible. See
@ref{Installed Voices And Engines,Installed voices and engines,,emacsvox,}.
@item Named voice
A presentation name such as @code{default} or @code{bolden}@comma{} resolved
through a voice palette to physical choices and adjustments. Editing a
shared named voice can affect several kinds of text that use it. See
@ref{Editing Named Voices,Edit a named voice,,emacsvox,}.
@item Voice palette
A collection defining named voices@comma{} including their
physical choices@comma{} fallbacks@comma{} and adjustments. Palettes can inherit values;
editing one is different from changing an engine preference. See
@ref{Voices And Routing,Voices and routing,,emacsvox,}.
@item Presentation rule
A match between application meaning or context and
the feedback to produce@comma{} such as a voice@comma{} cue@comma{} or silence. A rule chooses
presentation; it does not install a physical voice. See
@ref{Managing Feedback Rules,Manage feedback rules,,emacsvox,}.
@item Auditory icon or cue
A short sound conveying an event or state. A sound
pack supplies cues@comma{} while presentation rules determine when to use them.
Tones can convey information without a recorded sound file. See
@ref{Sounds And Space,Sounds and space,,emacsvox,}.
@item Presentation profile
A saved selection of presentation resources and
options. It refers to resources rather than copying everything into a full
configuration backup. Applying a profile also retains its selection for
startup; individual palette operations have different rules. See
@ref{Presentation Profiles,Presentation profiles,,emacsvox,}.
@end table

@node Speech And Settings Terms
@section Changes and speech output

@cindex preview, meaning
@cindex retained draft, meaning
@cindex Save, meaning
@cindex Apply, meaning
@cindex startup selection
@cindex speech worker, meaning

@table @asis
@item Preview
An audition or comparison of a proposed result. Previewing does
not by itself save the draft or make it the ordinary live configuration.
@item Retained draft
An unfinished edit kept in the current Emacs session for
return to its editor. Retaining it is not a promise of recovery after Emacs
exits@comma{} and discarding it does not undo a save already completed.
@item Save
Record data in the store owned by that editor or setting. Read the
action's label: @emph{Save and apply} and @emph{Save to collection without selecting}
intentionally have different effects.
@item Apply
Put a chosen configuration into use. The operation may require
confirmation@comma{} validation@comma{} or a worker restart. Its exact effect depends on
the resource; it is not a universal synonym for saving.
@item Startup selection
The configuration to select when a later session
starts. Selecting something for this session does not always select it for
the next one. @ref{Saving And Startup,Choose what persists,,emacsvox,} compares these states and their owners.
@item Speech server@comma{} engine@comma{} and worker
Emacsvox talks to a speech server;
the server uses engines to synthesise speech. The manual uses @emph{worker} for
a running speech instance whose configuration and reported voices can
differ from another instance or from installed files. See
@ref{Speech Backends,Speech Backends,,emacsvox,}.
@item Main and notification speech
Separate output paths for foreground
reading and other announcements. An integration decides which path to use
for an event; their existence is not a guarantee that all background text
is private. See @ref{Aural Speech Output,Speech output and notifications,,emacsvox,}.
@end table

