@node Developer Guide
@chapter Developer And Maintainer Guide

This guide is for people changing Emacsvox source@comma{} documentation@comma{} integrations@comma{}
speech-server protocols@comma{} build machinery@comma{} or release staging.  It is included
at the end of the published manual so that an installed Info manual and an
offline checkout remain complete@comma{} but it is not part of the ordinary
installation and use path.

Before making a governed change@comma{} read @code{AGENTS.md} and every accepted record in
@code{docs/adr/}.  Use the Emacs selected by @code{local.mk} for repository checks.  The
generated @ref{Top,Emacsvox Reference Manual,,emacsvox-reference,}
is the exact inventory of Lisp commands@comma{} options@comma{} and keymaps; the chapters
that follow explain contracts and procedures that cannot be generated from
those interfaces.

The stable entry and bootstrap files remain directly under @code{lisp/}. Load
@code{emacsvox-setup.el} there as before. Related modules occupy six directories:
@code{core/} for shared reading and navigation@comma{} @code{aural/} for presentation services@comma{}
@code{speech/} for engine and transport code@comma{} @code{voices/} for logical voice data@comma{}
@code{ui/} for settings and editors@comma{} and @code{integrations/} for package adapters.
Filenames@comma{} provided features and commands are unchanged.

When adding a module@comma{} use its owning directory and update @code{lisp/Makefile}.
That file remains the compilation and dependency inventory. The data-only
@code{etc/lisp-directories} declares the directories used by bootstrap@comma{} builds@comma{}
autoload discovery and distribution checks; do not add a recursive scan or
a second module list. Keep optional Muggles targets separate from the ordinary
compiled inventory. The source template remains @code{lisp/.skeleton.el}.

After changing source locations@comma{} rebuild with @code{make bytecode-rebuild} and run
@code{make bytecode-check} before starting a fresh Emacs. The rebuild removes owned
obsolete byte-code; copying new sources over old compiled files is insufficient.
A directory does not establish an independent dependency layer: preserve
feature names@comma{} initialization order and deferred integration loading.

@menu
* Documentation Maintenance::    Author, validate, and publish Emacsvox documentation.
* Speech Server Protocols::      Understand speech-server protocols.
* Integration Maintenance::      Maintain optional integrations and release staging.
@end menu

@node Documentation Maintenance
@section Documentation Maintenance

This chapter is for contributors changing the maintained manuals@comma{} generated
reference@comma{} or public browser documentation.  Reports and internal notes do not
require an Emacsvox build; use the smallest documentation target that covers
the source being changed.

@subsection Choose the canonical source

The maintained Org master is @code{docs/manual/emacsvox.org}.  Current-user chapters
live under @code{docs/manual/chapters/}@comma{} while developer and maintainer chapters
live under @code{docs/developer/}.  The source layout and fast authoring loop are
summarized in @code{docs/manual/README.org}.

The current project overview lives in @code{README.org}@comma{} and the complete user
installation path lives in @code{docs/manual/chapters/installation.org}.  Source
checkout@comma{} byte-code@comma{} and release procedures live in
@code{docs/developer/integration-maintenance.org}.  Submit changes through the
Emacsvox repository at @uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/emacsvox}.  Before
changing a governed workflow or architecture@comma{} read @code{AGENTS.md} and every
accepted record under @code{docs/adr/}.  Repository-local @code{local.mk} selects the
Emacs used by supported targets; Emacsvox requires Emacs 30.2 or newer.

Do not hand-edit generated references.  @code{info/docs.texi} and @code{info/keys.texi}
come from the source documentation generator@comma{} and
@code{etc/aural-presentation-reference.org} comes from
@code{utils/emacsvox-aural-audit.el}.  The first two sources form the separately
installed @code{emacsvox-reference.info} manual.  Selected historical chapters form
the separately installed @code{emacsvox-heritage.info} manual.

@subsection Use the fast authoring workflow

Keep current operating instructions separate from historical accounts.  State
which backend or platform a procedure applies to@comma{} avoid duplicating detailed
protocol contracts across repositories@comma{} and link to the owning document.
Apply the user-versus-developer audience test in ADR 0004 before choosing a
source directory.

While editing Org prose@comma{} preview the complete manual without inspecting or
rebuilding Lisp byte-code:

@example
make docs-org-preview
@end example

Use ordinary Org code markup for keys (@kbd{C-x C-f} or @kbd{M-x term @key{RET}}) and
verbatim markup for other literals (@code{local.mk} or @code{make docs-update}).
The Texinfo exporter preserves keyboard and named-key formatting; standard
Org text and HTML exports retain the key text. Within the manual@comma{} link to a
named Info destination or an explicit Org anchor. A structural heading marked
@code{TEXINFO_NODE: no} cannot be used as an Info destination merely because HTML
can link to it. Use the containing named node when appropriate@comma{} and described
@code{info:} links to other manuals. Check the result in both Info and HTML@.

Run @code{make docs-org-text} for a complete UTF-8 text copy at
@code{.docs-preview/org-manual/emacsvox.txt}. Set @code{DOCS_ORG_TEXT} to choose another
output path. This also uses the standard Org exporter without loading Emacsvox.

The ignored Info@comma{} Texinfo@comma{} and HTML previews appear under
@code{.docs-preview/org-manual/}.  Once the text settles@comma{} run @code{make docs-update} to
update the tracked generated Texinfo body and checked Info files.  Do not edit
those generated files directly.

When public Lisp documentation or keys change@comma{} first bring byte-code current
with @code{make bytecode}.  Use @code{make bytecode-rebuild} instead after changing
macros or public interfaces@comma{} as described in @code{AGENTS.md}.  Then run
@code{make docs-reference}.  @code{make docs-generate} remains the comprehensive
reference and manual update.

Before review@comma{} merge@comma{} or deployment@comma{} run the non-mutating
@code{make docs-release-check}; @code{make docs-check} is its compatibility alias.
Commit the regenerated Info artifacts with their source changes.

@subsection Publish the browser manual

Org on @code{master} is the canonical maintained manual source.  GitHub Pages serves
the generated static files committed on the separate @code{gh-pages} branch; never
edit those HTML files as source.  Publishing is deliberately explicit:

@enumerate
@item
Finish the manual change@comma{} update its checked Info output@comma{} run the release
validation target above@comma{} and commit the complete source-side change on
@code{master}.

@item
Check out that exact commit in a clean source worktree.  Build its local@comma{}
ignored byte-code with the Emacs selected by @code{local.mk} or an @code{EMACS=}
override.  Use a separate sibling worktree checked out on @code{gh-pages} as the
publication directory.

@item
From the clean source worktree@comma{} run:

@example
make docs-publish-pages DOCS_PUBLISH_DIR=/path/to/emacsvox-pages
@end example

The target validates the manual and publishes that same staged render@comma{}
replaces only its managed HTML files@comma{} writes @code{.nojekyll}@comma{} and records the
source commit@comma{} Emacs version@comma{} and Texinfo version in
@code{emacsvox-source.txt}.  It refuses an uncommitted source tree.

@item
Inspect the @code{gh-pages} worktree@comma{} including the provenance file and the main
browser entry points.  Commit the generated site with the source commit in
the commit message@comma{} then push @code{master} and @code{gh-pages}.  The GitHub Pages
source setting must name the root of @code{gh-pages}.
@end enumerate

No documentation target creates a commit or pushes a branch.  This keeps the
published bytes reviewable and makes publication a distinct maintainer action.

@node Speech Server Protocols
@section Speech Server Protocols

Emacsvox produces spoken output by communicating with one of several speech
servers.  Two protocol families coexist:

@itemize
@item
The legacy command-and-queue line protocol is implemented by standalone Tcl
servers and retained by Omnivox for compatibility.

@item
Capability-negotiated Omnivox control@comma{} marker@comma{} logical-voice@comma{} and structured
presentation protocols carry bounded JSON records beside that legacy stream.
@end itemize

This chapter is primarily for speech-server and client developers.  The
detailed Omnivox contracts are owned by the Omnivox repository; this manual
summarizes their relationship to Emacsvox and then documents the legacy line
protocol.

For additional notes on how to log and view TTS server
commands when developing a speech server@comma{} see
the historical Emacspeak article at
@uref{http://emacspeak.blogspot.com/2015/04/howto-log-speech-server-output-to-aid.html}.

@subsection Omnivox Structured Protocol

At process startup the Emacsvox adapter requests capabilities and live engine
inventory.  Features are used only after negotiation; an older server
continues on the legacy path.  Current Emacsvox requires
@code{presentation_timeline_v3} before it submits an Aural Presentation as a
structured timeline.

Concrete Aural plans retain a bounded compatibility lowering when those
features are absent.  Speech@comma{} voice commands@comma{} silence@comma{} tones@comma{} dispatch@comma{} and
interruption use the established legacy queue commands.  A legacy tone cannot
preserve the explicit overlay clock: both insert and overlay modes become an
ordered @code{t} command@comma{} and a fractional frequency is rounded to the nearest
hertz.  The surrounding speech continues rather than being rejected for that
loss of presentation precision.

Guarantee-bearing operations do not invent success on a legacy server.
Playback completion@comma{} source markers@comma{} and domain-scoped replacement remain
unavailable when the selected server cannot acknowledge them truthfully.

Ordinary control requests belong to their speech process. They have a
30-second response deadline and an admission limit of 64 outstanding requests
per process. Background inventory refreshes share one pending request per
process. Capability negotiation uses @code{omnivox-control-negotiation-timeout}.
Previews@comma{} parameter queries@comma{} palette application@comma{} and synchronous library
operations retain their own deadlines and cancellation rules.

The client detaches a request and cancels its timer before invoking its
callback. A response deadline or process exit produces a local error response
with the original request ID@comma{} @code{:origin client}@comma{} and code @code{timeout} or
@code{process_closed}. This does not prove that a submitted server operation was
cancelled or had no effect. Late replies cannot change the completed outcome.
Ordinary navigation Stop leaves metadata requests pending; each speech lane
owns its requests independently.

When adding an ordinary control caller@comma{} use @code{omnivox--send-control-request}.
Its callback may run during the send. Use the internal @code{managed} timeout only
when the enclosing operation already enforces a deadline and calls
@code{omnivox--take-control-request} on every cancellation or nonlocal exit. Private
preview operations keep their existing playback ownership and reserved
callbacks. No control wire version changes for this client lifecycle handling.

A complete timeline contains its delivery policy@comma{} replacement domain@comma{}
generation@comma{} dispatch ID@comma{} speech spans@comma{} and positioned actions.  Small
timelines use one bounded frame; large timelines use a bounded multipart
envelope that is validated and admitted atomically.  Decodable rejected or
stale direct timelines receive terminal status so Emacs callbacks can retire
their dispatch records.

@code{ordered} and @code{urgent} timelines are admitted immediately.  Only
matching @code{replaceable} work uses the short server-side coalescing window.
Receipt of a newer valid replaceable generation immediately cancels queued@comma{}
synthesizing@comma{} and buffered work in the same replacement domain.  It does not
globally stop ordered@comma{} urgent@comma{} legacy@comma{} or differently keyed output.

Marker events report playback start and positioned playback boundaries;
tracked status reports terminal completion.  With opt-in Aural diagnostics@comma{}
Emacs records successful dispatch send@comma{} first received @code{utterance_started}@comma{}
and terminal observation under the same dispatch ID@.  Omnivox's normal
session log records protocol admission@comma{} worker queue wait@comma{} individual engine
attempts@comma{} audio queueing@comma{} first mixer-source consumption@comma{} and terminal
playback under that ID@.  This correlation uses existing marker and terminal
records rather than adding a wire-protocol version.  Playback markers track
mixer-source consumption and may precede physical sound by the output device's
buffer latency; a protocol write or terminal callback is therefore not proof
of acoustic onset.

Recent Feedback retains a separate@comma{} data-only playback snapshot. Owned@comma{}
validated start and voice-choice events associate actual physical voices with
the frozen registry layers and retained run ranges. A presentation shares a
64 KiB evidence budget@comma{} with at most 128 dispatches and 128 spans per dispatch;
speech text remains subject to the existing 4096-byte history limit. Exhausted
or incomplete evidence disables original replay without blocking delivery.
Completed snapshots drive exact private voice previews with fallback disabled.
They never rewrite the named-voice registry or borrow another lane's latest
logical-voice realization. Ordinary history navigation retains the requested
annotations; explicit original playback uses this observed snapshot.

The canonical protocol documents are:

@itemize
@item
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/protocols/control.md, Omnivox Control Protocol}

@item
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/protocols/presentation-timeline.md, Omnivox Presentation Timeline Protocol}

@item
@uref{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/protocols/helper.md, Omnivox Helper Protocol}
@end itemize

@subsection Legacy Line Protocol

The TTS server reads commands from standard input.
Script @strong{speech-server} can be used to cause a TTS
server to communicate via a TCP socket. Speech server
commands are used by the client application to make specific requests
of the server; the server listens for these requests in a non-blocking
read-eval-print (REPL)  loop and executes requests as they become available. Requests can
be classified as follows:

@itemize
@item
Commands that send text to be spoken.

@item
Commands that set @strong{state} of the TTS server.
@end itemize

All commands are of the form

@example
commandWord @{arguments@}
@end example

The braces are optional when the command argument contains no whitespace.
The speech server's current state determines speech rate@comma{} punctuation mode
and other output characteristics. The state-setting commands below describe
those controls. The client queues text and non-speech audio@comma{} then asks the
server to dispatch the queued requests and begin producing output.

Once the server has been asked to produce output@comma{} it removes items
from the front of the queue@comma{} sends the requisite commands to the
underlying TTS engine@comma{} and waits for the engine to
acknowledge that the request has been completely processed. This is a
non-blocking operation@comma{} i.e.@comma{} if the client application generates
additional requests@comma{} these are processed @strong{immediately}.

This design allows a legacy Emacsvox TTS server to be
@strong{highly} responsive; Client applications can queue large
amounts of text (typically queued a clause at a time to achieve the
best prosody)@comma{} ask the TTS server to start speaking@comma{} and
interrupt the spoken output at any time.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Testing A Direct Legacy Server}Testing A Direct Legacy Server


This protocol-level test is only for a retained direct Tcl-compatible server.
Run its launcher from the Emacsvox repository root@comma{} for example:

@example
./servers/espeak
@end example

Then enter:

@example
q @{This is a test.@}
d
s
@end example

The @code{q} command queues text@comma{} @code{d} dispatches it@comma{} and @code{s} stops speech.  Type
@kbd{C-d} to close the server.  A backend-specific README may provide a
stronger diagnostic and should take precedence over this generic protocol
test.

@item
@anchor{Commands That Queue Output}Commands That Queue Output.


This section documents commands that either produce spoken output@comma{} or
queue output to be produced on demand.  Commands that place the
request on the queue are clearly marked.

@example
version
@end example

Speaks the @strong{version} of the TTS engine. Produces
output immediately.

@example
tts_say text
@end example

Speaks the specified @strong{text} immediately. The text is not
pre-processed in any way@comma{} contrast this with the primary way of
speaking text which is to queue text before asking the server to
process the queue.

Note that this command needs to handle the special syntax for morpheme
boundaries @code{[*]}.  The @code{[*]} syntax is specific to the
Dectalk family of synthesizers; servers for other TTS
engines need to map this pattern to the engine-specific code for each
engine. As an example@comma{} see @code{servers/outloud} A morpheme boundary
results in synthesizing compound words such as @strong{left bracket}
with the right intonation; using a space would result in that phrase
being synthesized as two separate words.

@example
l c
@end example

Speak @strong{c} a single character@comma{} as a letter.  The character is
spoken immediately. This command uses the TTS engine's
capability to speak a single character with the ability to flush
speech @strong{immediately}.  Client applications wishing to produce
character-at-a-time output@comma{} e.g.@comma{} when providing character echo during
keyboard input should use this command.

Omnivox clients that negotiate @code{palette_letter_v1} and its complete layered
voice bundle use @code{emacsvox_letter @{BASE64_UTF8_JSON@}} instead. Its version 4
or 5 timeline envelope has one layered span containing one visible Unicode
scalar@comma{} ordered delivery@comma{} no replacement key@comma{} and no actions. Registration and
validation precede interruption. The command retains isolated-character rate@comma{}
uppercase pitch@comma{} pronunciation and short streaming startup while applying the
palette's @code{default} voice@comma{} actual fallback tuning and native parameters.
Playback receipts use the existing layered timeline contracts. Legacy servers
continue to receive @code{l}; ordinary phonetic words and punctuation names use the
normal palette speech path.

@example
d
@end example

This command is used to @strong{dispatch} all queued requests.  It was
renamed to a single character command (like many of the commonly used
TTS server commands) to work more effectively over slow
(9600) dialup lines.  The effect of calling this command is for the
TTS server to start processing items that have been queued
via earlier requests.

@example
s
@end example

Stop speech @strong{immediately}.  Spoken output is interrupted@comma{} and
all pending requests are flushed from the queue.

@example
q text
@end example

Queues text to be spoken. No spoken output is produced until a
@strong{dispatch} request is received via execution of command
@strong{d}.

@example
c codes
@end example

Queues synthesis codes to be sent to the TTS engine.  Codes
are sent to the engine with no further transformation or processing.
The codes are inserted into the output queue and will be dispatched to
the TTS engine at the appropriate point in the output
stream.

@example
a filename
@end example

Cues the audio file identified by filename for playing@comma{}
an @code{ogg} file.

@example
p filename
@end example

@strong{dispatch} play the audio file identified by filename for
playing  — @code{ogg} file.

@example
t freq length
@end example

Queues a tone to be played at the specified frequency and having the
specified length.  Frequency is specified in hertz and length is
specified in milliseconds.

@example
sh duration
@end example

Queues the specified duration of silence. Silence is specified in
milliseconds.

@item
@anchor{Commands That Set State}Commands That Set State


@example
tts_reset
@end example

@strong{Immediately} reset TTS engine to default settings.
Stops all speech and clears the queue.

@example
tts_set_punctuations mode
@end example

Queues setting TTS engine to the specified punctuation
mode. Typically@comma{} TTS servers provide at least three modes:

@itemize
@item
None: Do not speak punctuation characters.

@item
Some: Speak some punctuation characters. Used for English prose.

@item
All: Speak out @strong{all} punctuation characters; useful in
programming modes.
@end itemize

@example
tts_set_speech_rate rate
@end example

@strong{Immediately} change speech rate. The interpretation of this
value is typically engine specific.

@example
tts_set_character_scale factor
@end example

Queues a change to the scale factor used for individual-character speech.
For a speech rate of 500 and a character scale of 1.2@comma{} command @code{l} uses a
rate of 600: the base rate multiplied by the character scale.

@example
tts_split_caps flag
@end example

Queues changing of the state of @strong{split caps} processing.  Turn
this on to speak mixed-case (AKA Camel Case) identifiers.

@example
tts_sync_state punct splitcaps caps rate
@end example

@strong{Immediately} apply the passed settings.

This ensures @strong{atomicity} i.e.@comma{} all state settings in the TTS
engine happen at one shot. Note that failure to do this might result
in some utterances being spoken with a partially set state.

@itemize
@item
punct: see tts@math{_set}@math{_punctuations}

@item
splitcaps: see tts@math{_split}@math{_caps}

@item
caps: engine specific implementation of capital letter handling

@item
rate: see tts@math{_set}@math{_speech}@math{_rate}
@end itemize

@example
set_next_lang say_it
@end example

@strong{Immediately} switch to the next language on the server@comma{}
maintained internally on the TTS server. If say@math{_it} is non-nil say the
language change via the TTS@.

@example
set_previous_lang say_it
@end example

@strong{Immediately} switch to the previous language on the server@comma{}
maintained internally on the TTS server. If say@math{_it} is non-nil say the
language change via the TTS@.

@example
set_lang language:voice say_it
@end example

@strong{Immediately} switch to the requested language and/or voice@comma{}
separated by a colon. Examples

@itemize
@item
set@math{_lang} "en"

@item
set@math{_lang} "en:whisper"

@item
set@math{_lang} ":whisper"
@end itemize

If say@math{_it} is non-nil@comma{} speak via the TTS the language and voice
selected.

@example
set_preferred_lang alias lang
@end example

@strong{Immediately} set an alias in the TTS server mapping for
example "en" to "en@math{_GB}". This can later be used by set@math{_lang}.
@end enumerate

@node Integration Maintenance
@section Integration Maintenance

This chapter collects source-level contracts that are deliberately omitted
from the user task guides.  Optional package APIs and renderers evolve@comma{} so
recheck the upstream interfaces and keep compatibility behavior bounded when
changing an integration.

@menu
* Authoring Sound Packs::        Prepare a personal resource directory and validate its cue metadata.
@end menu

@subsection Logical table readers

@code{emacsvox-table-reader.el} supplies common cell/row/column formatting@comma{} copying@comma{}
and movement decisions. Agent Shell retains its rendered source-property
adapter@comma{} contextual map@comma{} public commands@comma{} and preferences. Markdown and Org
register source adapters and use an explicit reader minor mode. The table
browser uses a buffer-local adapter for shared inspection@comma{} copying and speech
preferences. Its own movement commands keep the standalone view at vertical
boundaries. Tabulated lists register an explicit reader adapter while retaining
their ordinary application navigation outside that context.

An adapter supplies a fresh cell snapshot and a native Aural submission
function. Snapshots contain complete logical rows@comma{} flat source positions in
row order@comma{} table bounds@comma{} optional header information@comma{} and zero-based internal
coordinates. Movement rejects missing cells and handles boundaries before
changing point. Source adapters rebuild snapshots for each command; source
edits end explicit reading. No native table editing command is used for
navigation. Presentation remains under each integration's Aural
module and role@comma{} with one main-lane submission per reader action.

The tabulated-list adapter reads printed entry properties in display order@comma{}
without calling entry producers. It uses full string/button labels and column
metadata@comma{} skips invisible rows@comma{} and maps an empty final cell to its newline.
Refreshes retire the reader before positions can become stale. An optional
adapter map preserves application and button actions; only shared navigation
and @kbd{q} deliberately override them during reading. Other occupied keys retain
their application action@comma{} with shared commands accessible through @kbd{M-x}.
Standalone list bounds include the buffer@comma{} so vertical edges stay put.

The read-only table browser caches logical string rows when rendering. Text
properties map every character@comma{} including embedded line breaks and delimiters@comma{}
to a model cell. Pre/post-command hooks synchronize the model after ordinary
character movement; snapshot extraction also synchronizes it. Browser movement
uses the model and its rendered position index. New reader actions submit under
module @code{table-ui}@comma{} with role @code{field} for cells and explicit main-lane delivery.
The local browser map is independent of the legacy speech-prefix map. CSV
parsing preserves quoted fields and visited source buffers before constructing
the view. Sorting constructs a new view and carries over both filters and the
shared speech preferences.

The shared prefix adds only previously unused keys: @kbd{C-r} toggles source-table
reading@comma{} and @kbd{[} / @kbd{]} discover tables. Printable reader keys are contextual.
Control-Meta arrows also enter reading and move within supported source tables.
A conditional minor-mode map uses menu-item filters: returning nil outside
these tables preserves the effective binding in lower maps@comma{} including personal
bindings. The filter preserves point and match data@comma{} excludes code examples@comma{}
and defers to the active reader map after entry. First-arrow entry and movement
produce one submission. Structural movement remains available outside tables;
inside tables@comma{} its usual @kbd{C-M-f/b/u/d} keys remain available.
Org's native @kbd{@key{TAB}}@comma{} @kbd{@key{RET}} and Meta-arrow editing bindings remain available
outside reading. Agent Shell's existing scoped overrides and prompt behaviour
remain as documented in its integration contract.

Regression coverage lives in @code{test/emacsvox-table-reader-tests.el} and the
Agent Shell table cases. The graphical Agent Shell integration gate includes
real wrapped-table movement and copying. The source-reader graphical case
@code{emacsvox-table-reader-graphical-wrapped-source} must also run in a fresh
graphical Emacs with Markdown Mode installed; its batch skip is not a pass.
Check source text and modified state@comma{} effective keys@comma{} complete wrapped values@comma{}
source edits@comma{} code exclusion@comma{} title preferences@comma{} and native Aural submissions.
Browser coverage lives in @code{test/emacsvox-table-ui-tests.el}; use
@code{EMACSVOX_TABLE_UI_TEST_LOAD=compiled} in a fresh Emacs after building to verify
the compiled implementation. Its @code{emacsvox-table-ui-graphical-multiline-navigation}
case requires a graphical display and checks actual wrapping and logical cell
movement.
Tabulated-list coverage lives in @code{test/emacsvox-tabulated-list-tests.el}@comma{}
including full values@comma{} blanks@comma{} buttons@comma{} native action keys@comma{} sorting@comma{} filtering@comma{}
refresh and hidden rows. Its graphical navigation case must run on a real
graphical Emacs; a batch skip does not establish display acceptance.

@subsection Ordinary line movement in short windows

Core @code{next-line} advice dynamically disables @code{auto-window-vscroll} for an
interactive one-line forward move when the displayed row fits in the selected
window. Native partial scrolling can otherwise scroll without moving point@comma{}
causing the existing after advice to read the same line again. This reproduced
while reading Notmuch HTML with a header line and mixed font heights.

Preserve the interactive marker for the after advice@comma{} programmatic movement@comma{}
prefix arguments and scrolling within genuinely tall rows. Do not change the
buffer's scrolling preference or suppress repeated text as a substitute for
correct movement. The graphical integration gate exercises real rendering@comma{}
cursor movement and speech@comma{} plus a tall-image case@comma{} in
@code{test/emacsvox-vertical-navigation-tests.el}.

@subsection EAT rendered output and prompt observation

EAT output is collected around @code{eat-term-process-output} before redisplay can
trim it@comma{} including a bounded unfinished row. Never retain raw process bytes or
unredacted concealed text. Read fresh public terminal bounds@comma{} preserve soft-wrap
continuity@comma{} and reject uncertain clear/reset/alternate-screen replacements.
Check selected-window following after EAT synchronizes windows@comma{} not between
chunks inside its renderer. Editable line-mode text lies after @code{eat-term-end};
it can share the final physical row with the terminal cursor.

Prompt observation wraps the public @code{ui-command-function} terminal parameter
and preserves its original callback. EAT has already parsed the protocol:
@code{e;B} and @code{e;C} bracket a prompt@comma{} and @code{e;J} identifies a new shell prompt cycle.
The adapter reads bounded rendered ranges@comma{} excludes those ranges from ordinary
output@comma{} and deduplicates same-position prompt repaint. It does not infer a
prompt from punctuation or require annotation overlays. Keep this protocol seam
covered against the pinned EAT source and its real Bash integration when
upgrading. Unmarked unfinished text remains ordinary output; otherwise remote
questions can disappear indefinitely while waiting for a newline.

Foreground loss discards Emacs-local automatic work only. It must not call a
process-wide speech stop. Already dispatched speech may finish; scoped Omnivox
cancellation remains a separate backlog direction. The graphical gate covers
real X11 focus loss@comma{} minibuffers@comma{} selection@comma{} line drafts and scrolling. Integration
runners force the silent @code{log-null} backend before loading Emacsvox so a failed
test cannot start your configured speech engine.

When aural diagnostics are enabled@comma{} @code{eat-output-decision} records explain
collection rejection@comma{} pending-work cancellation@comma{} retained output@comma{} and selection
for presentation. Each contains fixed decision/reason symbols plus terminal@comma{}
generation and update identifiers@comma{} without terminal text. Selection for
presentation is not proof of transport@comma{} playback or audible output; correlate
the ordinary aural submission and Omnivox records for those later stages.

@subsection Source checkout and byte-code lifecycle

Use the Emacs selected by the ignored repository-local @code{local.mk}.  When Emacs
30.2 or newer is not the system @code{emacs}@comma{} set its absolute path there:

@example
EMACS=/path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs
@end example

Run @code{make check-emacs} before diagnosing compilation.  After an ordinary Lisp
edit@comma{} use the incremental build:

@example
make bytecode
@end example

After switching branches@comma{} pulling changes@comma{} or changing a macro or public
interface@comma{} use:

@example
make bytecode-rebuild
@end example

That target removes ignored generated @code{.elc} files and recompiles them with
the selected Emacs; it does not clean or discard source changes.
@code{make bytecode-check} is the non-mutating preflight for startup or deployment.
It reports missing@comma{} stale@comma{} orphaned@comma{} or wrong-Emacs byte-code.

Each @code{make config} fully regenerates @code{lisp/emacsvox-loaddefs.el} from the
current sources@comma{} including edited autoloads@comma{} new modules@comma{} and deleted modules.
The build targets @code{make bytecode}@comma{} @code{make bytecode-rebuild}@comma{} @code{make emacsvox}@comma{}
and @code{make all} run configuration before compiling; @code{make compat-test} runs it
before testing.  The
portable command @code{emacs -Q --batch -l etc/configure-emacsvox.el} uses the same
full regeneration.  Saving a source file or starting Emacs does not regenerate
autoloads.  Neither @code{make bytecode-check} nor @code{make docs-release-check} does so.
Regenerating the index alone does not force all modules to be recompiled.

Tests deliberately prefer source and therefore do not prove that the
byte-code used by a live profile is current.  Verify rebuilt byte-code in a
fresh Emacs rather than relying on a file reload when compiled dependants may
already be resident.

During development@comma{} load the changing setup source explicitly:

@lisp
(load-file "/path/to/emacsvox/lisp/emacsvox-setup.el")
@end lisp

The setup entry point warns and prefers source when newer Lisp would otherwise
be hidden by stale local byte-code.  In a stable checkout with current @code{.elc}
files@comma{} @code{(load-library "emacsvox-setup")} may be used.  Do not use
@code{load-library} as the first load after editing the source tree: Emacs can
select an old @code{emacsvox-setup.elc} before the newer source checker runs.

@enumerate
@item
@anchor{Background startup ownership and failure recovery}Background startup ownership and failure recovery


@code{emacsvox} starts two named background tasks: @code{core-advice} loads core command
feedback and @code{package-preparation} registers optional package adapters.  The
private task registry retains each thread@comma{} state@comma{} start/end times@comma{} and original
error for the Emacs session.  Repeated startup reuses these records@comma{} including
completed and failed tasks@comma{} instead of starting duplicate background work.
Other startup effects@comma{} including speech-server initialization@comma{} still run when
@code{emacsvox} is called again; this is not a general-purpose retry command.

Registration completion is separate from adapter loading.  An already-loaded
package triggers its adapter immediately; another package gets a deferred load
callback.  Each package/adapter pair is registered once.  Both load paths record
failures without aborting later registrations.  Failed or completed adapters
are not automatically retried on another package load.  User quit is recorded
and still propagates.  Source fallback from the setup guard is preserved in
both background tasks and deferred callbacks.

@kbd{M-x emacsvox-startup-status} speaks a summary and selects a Help report with
task durations and original adapter errors.  Calling @code{emacsvox-startup-status}
from Lisp returns the summary without speech or a window.  Failures also issue
a warning and attempt a notification in interactive Emacs; retained records
remain available if either feedback path fails.  Successful completion adds no
new unconditional announcement.  The protocol and routing checkpoints defined
in the startup ADRs retain their separate meanings; task completion does not
declare the speech server ready@comma{} or establish that every adapter has loaded.

Correct the reported cause and verify in a fresh session@comma{} rebuilding changed
Lisp first.  A failed adapter can leave partial advice or hooks: error isolation
does not roll those effects back.  Do not clear private ownership tables as a
retry mechanism@comma{} since deferred callbacks can still refer to their records.
Retain error details@comma{} selected Emacs version@comma{} and phase durations when debugging.
Background scheduling is preserved; these records do not guarantee that core
advice finishes before the first input command@comma{} and batch timings do not measure
interactive startup latency.

The focused tests exercise real deferred loads@comma{} failure isolation@comma{} task reuse@comma{}
source fallback@comma{} and spoken status with speech intercepted.  Check source with
the selected Emacs using @code{test/emacsvox-startup-tests.el}.  For fresh compiled
parity@comma{} first run @code{make bytecode-check}@comma{} then set
@code{EMACSVOX_STARTUP_TEST_LOAD=compiled} when loading the same test file in a new
batch Emacs.  This selects @code{emacsvox.elc} explicitly; it does not make every
test dependency compiled.  Audible diagnosis and real startup latency still
need an isolated interactive session.

