No compiler is needed on the destination machine.

Run setup-helper checks and real lifecycle acceptance on Windows:

@example
.\test\emacsvox-windows-setup-helper-tests.ps1
.\test\emacsvox-windows-setup-cleanup-tests.ps1
.\test\emacsvox-windows-setup-tests.ps1 -Setup "C:\path\to\first-setup.exe" -Upgrade "C:\path\to\next-setup.exe" -AudioCheck
.\test\emacsvox-windows-setup-wizard-tests.ps1 -Setup "C:\path\to\setup.exe"
@end example

The lifecycle test refuses to replace an existing development Setup
registration or matching shortcuts. It tests an isolated installation@comma{}
shortcut launch@comma{} running-process refusal@comma{} repair@comma{} an optional second-build
upgrade@comma{} and uninstall while retaining profile and voice fixtures. It keeps
logs for diagnosis. Use a separate test account when a development Setup
installation is already registered. Test interactive wizard navigation with
screen readers separately; silent acceptance does not establish that UI@.
The wizard test inspects native accessibility controls@comma{} reaches the review
page and cancels without installing. It does not replace listening with a
screen reader or keyboard-only navigation.

For rollback acceptance@comma{} stage an intentionally failing fixture with
@code{python3 test/prepare_windows_setup_failure.py --bundle /path/to/bundle.zip
--staging-directory /path/to/failure-stage} and compile it using the same
build command. Pass its executable as @code{-FailedUpgrade} to the lifecycle test.
This fixture fails during configuration@comma{} before activation; the test checks
that the previous selection and launcher still work. Keep the failure
fixture separate from user-facing artifacts.

The main GitHub Actions CI workflow builds the installer on Windows x64 for
pushes and pull requests to @code{master}@comma{} and can also be started manually. Its
Linux packaging fixtures run first. The Windows job checks native ownership
and cleanup helpers@comma{} verifies the cached Emacs and Omnivox downloads@comma{} and
uses the pinned Inno compiler. It tests installation@comma{} shortcut launch@comma{}
running-process refusal@comma{} repair@comma{} rollback of a deliberately failed upgrade@comma{}
and uninstall with profile and voice fixtures preserved. Destination checks
run with only Windows tools on PATH@.
The core speech CI job also exercises startup and welcome-screen preferences
on each supported Emacs version without needing an audio device.

The @code{emacsvox-windows-installer-evidence} artifact retains build manifests@comma{}
checksums@comma{} compiler provenance and lifecycle logs for 14 days@comma{} including
