directory to @code{emacsvox-windows-setup-tests.ps1}. Its @code{result.json} records
whether acceptance passed and which optional checks were requested.

CI does not run audio or interactive screen-reader acceptance. It does not
yet exercise a successful second-build upgrade; use @code{-Upgrade} locally for
that check.

Successful runs offer two separate downloads in the job summary:
@code{emacsvox-windows-installer} contains the tested setup executable@comma{} its checksums@comma{}
provenance@comma{} launch instructions and @code{SOURCE-DOWNLOADS.md}@comma{} linking directly to
matching third-party sources on their upstream hosts. @code{emacsvox-windows-sources}
contains our exact Emacsvox code@comma{} build scripts@comma{} runtime assets and the same
source index. Unshipped historical soundscapes and talk recordings are omitted. It does not
mirror the large upstream archives. Sources are optional and are not installed on users'
machines. CI uploads them first and retains them for 90 days@comma{} compared with
14 days for the installer. Keep paired sources available if redistributing
an installer@comma{} and remove binaries before their sources when deleting artifacts.
Keep the upstream locations accessible; repair broken source links or withdraw
the affected installer.

@code{etc/windows-sources.json} binds the reviewed Emacs/Omnivox runtime archives
to source archives and licence notices. It records exact DLL package matches@comma{}
additional MSYS2 sources absent from the GNU dependency ZIP@comma{} and locked Rust
dependencies. @code{etc/windows-sources.txt} explains the archive contents and build
recipes. The application includes @code{etc/WINDOWS-THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt};
Emacs and Omnivox also retain their own notices. Runtime updates require a
source and notice review; link the exact versions with their checksums.

The packaging gate requires a clean committed checkout matching the staged
installer@comma{} intact payloads and compiler provenance. It verifies the pinned
upstream archives in a private build cache and publishes their links beside
the installer. It checks both downloads before making either available.
Only the main tested executable is uploaded. The failed-upgrade fixture and
offline bundle remain build inputs. Run @code{make windows-sources-test} for the
isolated source/publication boundary checks.

The setup executable is distributed separately from stable release artifacts@comma{}
as specified in @uref{../adr/0024-native-windows-installer.org, ADR 0024}. Public preview promotion is described below and in
@uref{../adr/0025-windows-preview-publication.org, ADR 0025}.
@end enumerate

@item
@anchor{Public Windows installer previews}Public Windows installer previews


Use a Windows preview when testers need public downloads without Actions
sign-in or artifact expiry. Keep @code{VERSION} and the @code{Unreleased} NEWS heading
unchanged. Do not use the stable release targets or a stable version tag for
this operation. The tag identifies the installer source commit; the clean
publication tooling checkout may be newer and records its own commit.

Start with a successful complete @code{CI} run from @code{master} in the repository
selected by @code{RELEASE_REMOTE} (default @code{origin}). The installer@comma{} matching sources
and evidence artifacts must still be available for preparation. For example:

@example
make windows-preview-test
make windows-preview-prepare WINDOWS_PREVIEW_RUN=RUN_ID \
  WINDOWS_PREVIEW_TAG=windows-preview-2026-09-20 \
  WINDOWS_PREVIEW_DIR=/path/to/new-preview-directory
make windows-preview-check WINDOWS_PREVIEW_DIR=/path/to/new-preview-directory
@end example

Preparation downloads and verifies the exact CI assets. It checks all archived
Emacsvox source files against Git@comma{} the installer and setup provenance@comma{} source
pairing@comma{} upstream source index@comma{} and native lifecycle result. It writes the
publishable files under @code{assets/} and a local @code{preview.json} receipt. It creates
no tag or GitHub release and refuses to overwrite an existing output directory.
Review @code{assets/README-preview.md}@comma{} the executable@comma{} sources and checksums.
These instructions include permanent source links; the old CI-only README is
not published. Upstream sources remain upstream links@comma{} not mirrored archives.

Only after explicit maintainer approval naming the preview@comma{} run:

@example
make windows-preview-tag WINDOWS_PREVIEW_DIR=/path/to/new-preview-directory
make windows-preview-publish WINDOWS_PREVIEW_DIR=/path/to/new-preview-directory
@end example

Both commands repeat validation. The first creates only a local annotated@comma{}
unsigned tag at the tested source commit. The second pushes that exact tag@comma{}
creates a private GitHub draft@comma{} attaches sources and installer@comma{} verifies all
remote SHA256 values@comma{} then publishes as a prerelease with Latest disabled.
It always selects the repository explicitly from @code{RELEASE_REMOTE}'s push URL@.
It does not edit the installer or source ZIP@. If uploading fails@comma{} rerun the
publish target against the same prepared directory; it resumes only a matching
draft with intact assets. A mismatched draft@comma{} conflicting tag or published
release is an error@comma{} never an invitation to force-push or overwrite it.

Changing the publication tooling commit or prepared files requires preparing
a new directory. Keep the original evidence for review. The preview is for
native Windows x64@comma{} remains unsigned@comma{} and does not establish stable installer
readiness. Its notes identify the remaining interactive and lifecycle checks.

@item
@anchor{Remote preview validation}Remote preview validation


Keep remote installer fixtures separate from connected acceptance. In the
Omnivox checkout@comma{} run @code{make dev} followed by @code{make remote-test}; set
@code{OMNIVOX_REMOTE_TEST_SLOW=1} to cover heartbeat expiry. Set
@code{OMNIVOX_REMOTE_TEST_EMACS} to the selected Emacs and
@code{OMNIVOX_REMOTE_TEST_EMACSVOX} to the matching Emacsvox checkout for client
