@end enumerate

@subsection Debian package builds

@code{make deb} builds a local development package in @code{dist/} using Python 3@comma{}
@code{dpkg-deb}@comma{} Git and the selected Emacs. It stages runtime sources and generates
autoloads in a private directory@comma{} preserving checkout byte-code and personal
configuration. The deb has architecture @code{all}@comma{} an Emacs 30.2 dependency
(including terminal and graphical alternatives)@comma{} and an Omnivox recommendation.
It loads source and does not install emacsen-common hooks or compile on install.
The ownership and release boundaries are recorded in
@uref{../adr/0013-debian-package.org, ADR 0013}.

@code{make deb-test} checks the actual archive@comma{} reproducibility@comma{} source startup@comma{}
remote diagnosis@comma{} dependency metadata and refusal of an unprepared release.
CI additionally installs@comma{} reinstalls and purges the package in a disposable
Ubuntu 26.10 container using @code{test/debian-install-smoke.sh}. This target supplies
Transient 0.13.3 or newer through apt; Ubuntu 26.04's package is too old.
The check loads settings through the isolated launcher and reports the actual
Transient version and path. CI uploads the development deb and checksum as
artifacts@comma{} without publishing a release.

After preparing the checked source archive using the release procedure below@comma{}
@code{make release-deb} produces @code{dist/emacsvox_VERSION-1_all.deb} and its checksum.
The script itself verifies the existing clean-source artifact gate before and
after packaging. Local versions instead contain @code{+git} with the source commit
and source hash@comma{} and revision @code{-0local1}. The included @code{BUILD-INFO.json} records
the exact source identity and autoload Emacs version. Neither package target
creates a tag@comma{} uploads release assets@comma{} adds an apt repository or changes the
manual release publication approval boundary.

After a maintainer publishes a stable GitHub release@comma{} the Release Debian
package workflow checks out its exact annotated tag and downloads the source
archive@comma{} checksum and provenance from that release. It checks the source
identity@comma{} installs from the archive@comma{} builds the release deb@comma{} and tests install@comma{}
startup@comma{} reinstall and removal in Ubuntu 26.10 before attaching the deb and
checksum. Build jobs have read-only repository access; only the attachment job
can write release assets. A rerun accepts identical existing assets and refuses
to replace different files. CI does not create tags or releases.

This workflow uses Emacs 31.1 for source-archive installation and autoload
generation@comma{} with its supported bundled Transient. The Ubuntu installation
check exercises the packaged Emacs 30.2 and external Transient route. The
recorded build toolchain can differ from a locally prepared package. Let CI
attach the release deb instead of uploading a differently built local package
with the same name.

@subsection Emacsvox release staging and publication

The repository-level @code{VERSION} file is the canonical stable release
identifier.  Calendar-versioning policy@comma{} including delayed patches and
backports@comma{} is recorded in
@uref{../adr/0002-calendar-versioning.org, ADR 0002}.  When advancing a release@comma{} update @code{VERSION}@comma{} the literal
package header in @code{lisp/emacsvox.el}@comma{} and the current heading in @code{etc/NEWS}
together@comma{} then run:

@example
make version-check
@end example

Between releases@comma{} @code{etc/NEWS} uses the @code{Emacsvox Unreleased} heading@comma{} so changes
can be recorded before deciding whether the next publication is a patch or a
new feature-release series.  After publishing@comma{} preserve those notes verbatim
as @code{etc/NEWS-VERSION} and start a new unreleased file.  Ordinary
@code{version-check} accepts that state; @code{release-check}@comma{} @code{release-tag}@comma{} and
@code{release-publish} require the current NEWS heading to match @code{VERSION} exactly.

Prepare an artifact only from a separate clean@comma{} committed worktree.  Build
fresh byte-code and run the guarded artifact target:

@example
make bytecode-rebuild
make release-artifact
@end example

@code{release-artifact} runs the complete test and documentation gates before
creating @code{dist/emacsvox-YYYY.MONTH.PATCH.tar.bz2}@comma{} its SHA-256 checksum@comma{} and a
provenance record naming the checked source commit.  For a patch-zero feature
release@comma{} the series must match the current month.  A corrective or backport
release retains its original non-future series as required by ADR 0002.
The source archive includes the maintained @code{utils/} tools needed by the
installers@comma{} launchers and build. Before recording checked provenance@comma{} the
artifact target extracts the actual archive into a temporary directory and
runs the remote-only installer with the selected Emacs in a private home. It
checks fresh compiled startup@comma{} the offline manual and the remote helper's
credential validation without opening a workstation connection or playing
speech. @code{make source-archive-check} repeats this check on an existing archive;
@code{make source-archive-test} checks export contents and rejection of missing
helpers@comma{} local configuration and byte-code. CI runs both checks before a
release as well. On native Windows@comma{} run
@code{test/emacsvox-source-archive-tests.ps1 -Archive PATH -Emacs PATH} to check the
actual archive's installer doctor@comma{} complete native compilation and fresh
byte-code validation in a temporary Windows directory.

Inspect the archive@comma{} checksum@comma{} and provenance before proceeding.

Stop after artifact inspection unless fresh explicit permission names the
release to be tagged and published.  Preparing an artifact@comma{} committing or
pushing a branch@comma{} publishing documentation@comma{} or receiving a general instruction
to continue is not permission to create or push a release tag.  Once that
separate authorization has been given@comma{} the guarded commands are:

@example
make release-tag
make release-publish
@end example

@code{release-tag} validates the artifact against the still-clean @code{HEAD} and creates
a local annotated@comma{} unsigned tag; it does not push the tag.  The unsigned-tag
policy and its trust boundary are recorded in
@uref{../adr/0006-annotated-unsigned-release-tags.org, ADR 0006}.  @code{release-publish}
repeats the artifact and source-commit validation@comma{} pushes only that exact tag@comma{}
and creates the GitHub release in the repository identified by
@code{RELEASE_REMOTE}.  This explicit selection prevents the GitHub CLI from
choosing an @code{upstream} remote when releasing from a fork.  No release target
pushes @code{master} or the documentation branch.  Use @code{make release-check} alone
when a non-mutating preflight is wanted without creating an artifact.

@subsection Aural extension boundaries

The following extensions require an explicit architecture decision before
implementation:

@itemize
@item
whether a critical alert may be completely suppressed or must retain an
alternative presentation;
@item
which deterministic ordering policy would apply before minor-mode selectors
can participate in the presentation cascade; and
@item
whether a new presentation occasion belongs to the core semantic contract
or to a module-owned extension.
@end itemize

Do not infer contracts from archived proposals. Current behavior is defined
by the Aural implementation@comma{} its generated reference@comma{} and accepted ADRs.

@subsection Voice-style metadata ownership

The private @code{emacsvox-aural--voice-style-fields} table in
@code{lisp/aural/emacsvox-aural-rules.el} owns accepted field names@comma{} groups@comma{} value types@comma{}
and integer bounds.  The existing public dimension lists and private accepted
key list are derived from it; validation traverses the descriptors directly.
Composition and provenance continue to consume the accepted-key inventory.

Keep the two established orders explicit.  Validation checks preset@comma{} family@comma{}
the four numeric ACSS fields@comma{} post-synthesis fields@comma{} relative rate@comma{} and legacy
rate.  The accepted-key inventory places relative and legacy rate before the
post-synthesis fields.  ACSS dimension order also determines positional legacy
personality settings@comma{} and rich dimension order determines adjustment rows.

Every accepted field allows @code{nil}.  Validation preserves the original plist@comma{}
including duplicate fields@comma{} for which the first value is used.  Field absence@comma{}
explicit @code{nil}@comma{} and zero remain distinct during composition and persistence.
Legacy @code{:rate} is accepted for loading saved data but is not applied as a new
rate; nonzero legacy values produce the existing retuning degradation.
The table supplies no implicit defaults and does not declare engine support.

An explicit @code{nil} over a preset is a compatibility behavior to characterize@comma{}
not an authoring decision settled by the metadata table.  For example@comma{} the
current compiler clears the reported average-pitch value for
@code{(:preset base :average-pitch nil)} while retaining the base voice command and
emitting no pitch-reset command.  The tests preserve this distinction.  A
future inheritance@comma{} omission@comma{} or reset policy needs a separate decision.

Compiler conversion@comma{} backend wire names@comma{} capability discovery@comma{} normalization@comma{}
and delivery stay in their existing owners.  When adding a field@comma{} update those
consumers explicitly and test final serialized output or an explicit
degradation. The common voice editor uses the shared field metadata for bounds
and neutral values. It covers portable ACSS and optional rate and post-synthesis
adjustments in the same retained draft.

Input grammar remains UI policy. In the common voice editor@comma{} blank numeric
entry requests the adapter default; zero is explicit. Individual choice fields
distinguish shared inheritance@comma{} custom values and explicit adapter defaults.
Rule-editor input has its own scoped inheritance semantics; do not substitute
one editor's blank-input behavior for the other's.

The common editor displays low-pass and high-pass as amounts from zero through
nine@comma{} increasing with stronger filtering. Low-pass converts between amount and
stored cutoff with @code{9 - value}; high-pass retains its numeric direction. Zero
is labelled neutral. Stored cutoffs and adapter-default nil retain their
meaning. Invalid boundary steps must fail before history@comma{} dirty state@comma{} refresh
or audition changes.

Delivery preparation has the helper boundaries described below; metadata
validation does not transfer ownership of asynchronous operations.

The rule tests state field inventories@comma{} bounds@comma{} error precedence@comma{} and
composition expectations independently of the metadata.  Scheme tests cover
saved rich palettes and explicit-nil rules; transport tests check distinct
ACSS mappings@comma{} zero and omitted wire fields@comma{} effects@comma{} and preset behavior.
@code{make compiled-aural-test} also runs these style contracts with the isolated
compiled subsystem loaded@comma{} checking that the implementations stay in that
build. The @code{voice-style-ui} tests exercise common-editor entry@comma{} inherited
reset and draft retention in source and compiled runs. Editor tests also cover
numeric bounds@comma{} explicit defaults@comma{} undo and comparison. They capture auditions
and do not start audio.
Run @code{make graphical-voice-test} for the voice editor's link@comma{} return navigation@comma{}
and save-button checks in a fresh graphical Emacs with current byte-code.
It requires Xvfb@comma{} xvfb-run and xauth@comma{} and uses a separate virtual display so
test windows cannot take desktop focus. The launcher prints the graphical
ERT log and exits unsuccessfully for failures@comma{} skips@comma{} startup errors or a
45-second timeout. A missing virtual-display tool fails before starting Emacs.
The graphical redisplay test is reported as skipped in ordinary batch runs.
The connected saved-style regression edits a custom @code{bolden}@comma{} reads its saved
data back@comma{} and compares common-editor and Workbench previews with the final
normal-speech wire for @code{voice-bolden}.  Workbench uses the compiler's palette
resolution and the shared effect normalizer: gain and pan level five must
remain exactly neutral at 0.5.  Preserve both this path and the independently
specified Workbench scale cases when changing preview or palette lookup.
Preserve final-write@comma{} timeout@comma{} reentrancy@comma{} and trace regressions when
working on later delivery slices; metadata validation alone cannot establish
process lifecycle or audible behavior.

@subsection Timeline preparation boundaries

Two private helpers in @code{lisp/aural/emacsvox-aural-transport.el} make preparation
inputs explicit.  @code{emacsvox-aural--timeline-effect-transition} takes previous
effects@comma{} normalized next effects@comma{} and a span ID@comma{} returning a directive and the
next state.  Replacement copies the saved state separately from the directive;
retention preserves the previous state.  The builder still owns the state
accumulator@comma{} span numbering@comma{} and when normalization runs.  Suppressed or empty
content does not advance effects@comma{} while spoken boundary actions do.

@code{emacsvox-aural--encode-timeline-delivery-fields} takes a policy and replacement
key.  The existing @code{emacsvox-aural--timeline-delivery-fields} wrapper reads the
dynamic submission variables at the end of timeline construction.  Preserve
that capture point: resource resolution earlier in construction can re-enter
Lisp and change the submission state.  The serializer preserves ordered
defaults@comma{} key conversion@comma{} circular printing@comma{} diagnostics@comma{} and byte bounds.

The complete builder is not pure.  Cue resolution through
@code{omnivox-remote-resource} may perform I/O; relative-rate support comes from the
live speech process.  Those operations@comma{} action placement@comma{} dispatch
registration@comma{} framing@comma{} fallback@comma{} and process writes retain their existing
owners and sequence.  Separating a complete delivery candidate from those
effects requires further design and failure-path characterization.

The @code{delivery-preparation} tests in @code{test/emacsvox-aural-transport-tests.el}
check complete serialized timelines and final writes@comma{} semantic bindings@comma{}
state ownership@comma{} policy inputs@comma{} and capture timing.  The compiled Aural gate
also runs these contracts and existing stop and send-failure regressions with
the compiled implementation resident.  Keep the full failure@comma{} reentrancy@comma{}
and golden-trace suites as regression checks; captured writes establish
protocol behavior@comma{} not audible playback.

@subsection Aural interface source ownership

Ordinary-buffer entry captures a source buffer@comma{} a marker@comma{} and a guarded source
line.  Child interfaces and Help inherit that context through the inspection
and shared UI helpers.  Use @code{emacsvox-aural-inspection-call-in-source} for
contextual commands so that moving the source cursor does not retarget an
open interface.  Re-entry from an ordinary buffer deliberately captures a new
target.  A dead or replaced source is reported rather than resolved through
an unrelated recent-buffer fallback.

Current-item lookup may reuse a frozen history record only when the captured
source identity@comma{} position@comma{} and modification tick still match.  Source identity
is a buffer-local@comma{} data-only token that distinguishes buffers sharing a name;
history continues to retain no buffer objects or markers.  Explicit historical
selection remains valid after the source changes and is explained from its
frozen plan.  Prepared current-item rules carry a source guard checked before
their saved or live rule layer changes.

Find rule editors by scope and target identity@comma{} not by a formatted buffer
name.  Buffer scopes belong to buffer objects@comma{} fragment scopes to fragment
identifiers@comma{} and personal/session scopes each share one working editor.
Reopening a dirty editor preserves its draft and source association.

@subsection Remote speech recovery

Ordinary @code{tts-initialize} startup schedules the existing remote retry when
main speech starts but a configured notification connection fails.  The
scheduler respects @code{omnivox-remote-auto-reconnect}@comma{} explicit suspension@comma{} and
its single pending timer.  The retry restores only missing lanes; it keeps a
working main process and negotiates fresh configuration for the new notifier.
@code{omnivox-remote-disconnect} cancels the timer and suspends even a retry callback
that has already been dispatched.  Local speech and disabled notification
streams do not start remote recovery.  Exercise these boundaries in
@code{test/omnivox-remote-tests.el} with controlled connection failures and timers.

@subsection Omnivox voice configuration applies

@code{omnivox-apply-voice-configuration} captures one generation and its definitions
for the original speech processes.  Applies may overlap.  Each owns its request
identifiers and retires only those callbacks when a process result becomes
terminal.  Acknowledging routing precedes logical registration; one aggregate
reports applied@comma{} partial@comma{} or failed after all participating processes finish.

The timeout closes pending results as failed with phase @code{timeout}@comma{} cancels
the timer@comma{} and releases the apply's pending callbacks.  A late acknowledgement
cannot start another phase@comma{} update acknowledged configuration@comma{} or publish a
second terminal result.  A reply from a stopped or replaced process also
cannot advance its old apply.  Request ownership must remain correct when
replies are dispatched while a send is still returning.

This closes client work; it cannot undo a change already sent to the server.
The Workbench therefore describes a timeout as an unconfirmed server outcome
and offers reapplication of the committed profile.  No late-reply history is
retained for expired applies.  Keep request-table@comma{} late-reply@comma{} overlapping-apply@comma{}
and replacement regressions in @code{test/emacsvox-tts-tests.el} and spoken timeout
feedback coverage in @code{test/emacsvox-aural-voice-workbench-tests.el}.

@subsection Notmuch contracts

The live module is registered as an optional package integration.  Its stable
extension points are the Hello@comma{} Search@comma{} Show@comma{} and Tree mode hooks; Search and
Show mode maps; public interactive commands; and public Search/Show accessors.
Advice is installed only when a target exists and is target-aware@comma{} so a nested
or programmatic upstream call does not claim the outer user's feedback.

The Show landing and visibility paths also read renderer metadata such as
@code{:headers-overlay}@comma{} @code{:message-visible}@comma{} message extents@comma{} @code{:notmuch-part}@comma{}
part-hidden state@comma{} and Notmuch part-button types.  Missing optional metadata
should reduce presentation detail rather than authorize an operation.  Review
these adapters whenever the Notmuch renderer changes.

Each user-owned asynchronous Search process carries its own completion state
as a process property.  The upstream sentinel renders first; Emacsvox then
clears that property before delivering exactly one terminal result.  The
owning buffer and process status are authoritative.  A killed owner remains
silent@comma{} concurrent buffers cannot overwrite one another@comma{} and the global
refresh-all path intentionally acquires no ownership.

Run @code{make notmuch-test} for the explicit source suite.  After current byte-code
exists@comma{} run @code{make compiled-notmuch-test}; it verifies the exact loaded
@code{emacsvox-notmuch.elc}@comma{} compiler version@comma{} and the same focused selector@comma{}
including real displayed-window reading for both advance paths.

@subsection Agent Shell contracts

Use @kbd{M-x emacsvox-agent-shell-enable} to reinstall speech hooks after
deliberately disabling them or while testing live integration changes.

The minimum supported Agent Shell is 0.85.3. The integration pins target
Agent Shell 0.89.1 at @code{78a2c94}@comma{} Shell Maker 0.98.2 at @code{f57ad5e}@comma{} and
ACP 0.18.2 at @code{2959da5}. A personal
package upgrade does not refresh @code{test/integration-dependencies.json} or its
prepared tree. When the preparer rejects a changed lock@comma{} choose a fresh
@code{TEST_DEPS_DIR} and pass the same directory to preparation and both integration
gates; preserve the old directory until it is no longer needed.

For every pin refresh@comma{} compare all Emacsvox navigation@comma{} speech-control@comma{} table@comma{}
and repeat keys against shell and viewport maps@comma{} inherited Shell Maker maps@comma{}
list editing@comma{} inline actions@comma{} permission and question controls@comma{} and transients. Verify
effective keys in real prompt and transcript contexts. The 0.89.1 refresh keeps
the existing scoped overrides: viewport @kbd{t} discovers tables; table @kbd{r} reads
rows instead of quoting or replying; viewport table @kbd{c} reads columns and @kbd{a}
selects table speech instead of continuing or repeating a reply. The table @kbd{k}
copy prefix takes precedence over Emacsvox's source-block jump. These preserve
consistent reading keys; the displaced commands remain available through
@kbd{M-x} as documented in the Agent Shell user chapter. Prompt letters remain
literal input@comma{} and repeat navigation must release unrelated keys.

