The lifecycle subscription currently consumes @code{init-started}@comma{}
@code{init-finished}@comma{} @code{session-selected}@comma{} @code{session-restored}@comma{} @code{input-submitted}@comma{}
@code{turn-complete}@comma{} @code{error}@comma{} @code{elicitation-request}@comma{} and @code{elicitation-response}.
Dedicated subscriptions consume
@code{permission-request}@comma{} @code{permission-response}@comma{} and @code{tool-call-update}.  Add new
semantic behavior at the public event layer when Agent Shell exposes it; keep
renderer and private fallbacks isolated and covered by compatibility tests.

Question feedback resolves control identity from renderer properties and values
from the owning shell's @code{:elicitations} state. Navigation and actions work in
both shell and viewport buffers. Public question events govern automatic
request and response announcements; action advice confirms edits and previews.
No new key overrides are installed. The upstream feature stays opt-in.

Configuration access uses @code{agent-shell-config-options} and
@code{agent-shell-initialized-p} when available. The 0.85.3 fallback reads normalized
private options and checks the initialization pipeline flags. Startup defaults
must not be announced as user changes. Verify the minimum at @code{f44c96b} as well
as the pinned version@comma{} including source and compiled speech and graphical form
controls. Use separate prepared dependency directories for each version.

The lifecycle subscription also observes @code{config-option-update}.  Configuration
snapshots suppress initial inventory and duplicate callback/event confirmation.
Recent Agent Shell versions emit this event on successful setting requests too;
the callback fallback remains for older versions and shares the same snapshot
cache. Confirmations reuse upstream completion labels when available so grouped
choices with identical names remain distinguishable.
Late tool details are compared only for permissions established by a public
@code{permission-request} event@comma{} and their snapshots are removed on resolution.

Current Agent Shell omits rendered Codex @code{rawOutput} and textual diffs from
@code{tool-call-update}.  A narrow @code{agent-shell--on-notification} adapter joins that
public event to the body rendered during the same call@comma{} matching both the
owning buffer and qualified fragment ID@.  Full tool speech is submitted after
rendering returns.  Output digests and lifecycle statuses are tracked separately
so late output is spoken without repeating identical updates.  Renderer IDs
without a matching public event do not authorize tool feedback.

Until upstream exposes public steering and push lifecycle events@comma{} small
adapters wrap the structured steering request's callbacks and the accepted
@code{session/push} lifecycle.  Push speech finishes before the original completion
callback can drain the next queued prompt.  Callback generations invalidate
pending speech feedback on session change or integration cleanup; original
transport callbacks still run.  No transcript text is interpreted as a
steering outcome or push completion.

The queue adapter captures the pending count before draining and lets
@code{input-submitted} confirm how many prompts actually left the queue. This covers
both one-at-a-time and merged submission without duplicating upstream policy.
Prompt list editing and diff entry have
native command feedback.  New asynchronous tool@comma{} queue@comma{} steering@comma{} setting@comma{} and
permission-detail submissions explicitly select the notification lane; list
editing and diff entry remain foreground interactions.  All new events and
their outcome@comma{} count@comma{} or setting attributes are registered by the workflow
semantic provider so Aural rules can customize their presentation.
@end enumerate

@subsection Magit contracts

Staging and selected synchronous operations collect Git outcomes at
@code{magit-process-finish}@comma{} before Magit can raise an error.  One interactive
command owns the aggregate result across nested calls.  A process that Magit
starts and waits for contributes only to the collector attached when it was
started; unrelated process callbacks cannot change that result.  Uncollected
asynchronous completions explicitly select the notification lane.  Neither a
command's return value nor the absence of an Emacs error proves Git succeeded.

Successful staging and unstaging in a Magit view present the refreshed line
with destination section facts and a @code{focus-entered} event.  The destination
does not inherit the original selection's staged or unstaged state.  Failures@comma{}
commands that run no Git operation@comma{} and commands outside Magit views retain
outcome messages.  Regression coverage enables real refreshes for file and hunk
staging and unstaging@comma{} including removal of the last file in a section.

Magit's positional highlight overlays must not become source personalities.
The integration suppresses legacy overlay voice mirroring for those highlights
and excludes their faces from source capture.  It preserves text faces@comma{}
deliberate personalities@comma{} and customized hash voices.  Navigation still emits
section facts; empty lines supply the core line-condition facts without a
competing selection icon.

Regression tests use isolated repositories for real Git failures and real
section trees for highlighting and visibility.  Run the Magit tests from
source@comma{} then again in a fresh Emacs with @code{EMACSVOX_MAGIT_TEST_LOAD=compiled}
after bringing byte-code current.  A live buffer created with the former
highlight mirroring needs a Magit refresh to rebuild its source properties.

@subsection WSL2 Omnivox development staging

The ordinary WSL2 user route is implemented by
@code{bin/emacsvox-wsl-install} and pinned by @code{etc/wsl-install.conf}.  Treat either
file as release tooling.  When advancing GNU Emacs or Omnivox@comma{} update the
version@comma{} archive name@comma{} URL@comma{} and SHA-256 together; exercise x64 and ARM64
selection in the isolated installer tests; and perform one real installation
of the newly pinned archive before updating the user manual.  The installer
must remain per-user@comma{} must not invoke @code{sudo} or edit shell startup files@comma{} and
must preserve an explicit @code{EMACS} or @code{local.mk} selection rather than silently
replacing it.

The installed Omnivox executable is recorded as an absolute WSL path in the
data-only @code{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox-program} file.  Launcher selection
is explicit @code{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM}@comma{} that per-user file@comma{} developer staging@comma{} then
@code{PATH}.  Keep the selection tests synchronized with this order.  A configured
but invalid path is a visible configuration error; silently falling through
would hide a stale or damaged user installation.

This section describes the reproducible source-build path@comma{} not the ordinary
pre-built WSL2 installation.  The complete source-build prerequisite checklist
is in
@ref{Building Omnivox For WSL2, , Building Omnivox For WSL2}.  Dependency ownership is deliberately
split across three boundaries:

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .30 .52
@headitem Boundary
@tab Supplied by
@tab Dependencies
@item Windows and WSL host
@tab Maintainer
@tab WSL interoperation@comma{} @code{powershell.exe}@comma{} @code{wslpath}@comma{} Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or newer@comma{} Docker with Linux/AMD64 support@comma{} Git@comma{} Make@comma{} download and checksum tools@comma{} standard GNU shell utilities@comma{} network access@comma{} and writable Windows @code{LocalAppData}.
@item Pinned release toolchain
@tab Emacsvox target
@tab The immutable Rust/Debian container@comma{} MinGW cross tools and runtime DLLs@comma{} CMake@comma{} Clang and libclang@comma{} eSpeak build dependencies@comma{} Roslyn@comma{} .NET 4.0 reference assemblies@comma{} and official Windows Piper companion.  The exact versions@comma{} source commit@comma{} and downloaded-package hashes live in @code{servers/omnivox-release/toolchain.lock}.
@item Build source
@tab Git checkouts
@tab The current Emacsvox checkout and the Omnivox checkout selected by @code{OMNIVOX_DIR}@comma{} including Omnivox's locked Rust dependencies and checksum-locked Flite and RuTTS source inputs.
@end multitable

@code{make verify-windows-omnivox-toolchain} prepares the cached Roslyn inputs@comma{}
builds the Docker image@comma{} and validates this boundary before compilation.  The
full target then builds Omnivox's two 32-bit helper sources deterministically@comma{}
builds native Windows Omnivox plus its RHVoice@comma{} Flite@comma{} and RuTTS helpers in the
container@comma{} and stages their licenses@comma{} provenance@comma{} and checksums.  The clean
target also builds Piper support and verifies that the pinned companion and
main executable identify the same Omnivox source commit.  A host Rust
installation@comma{} MinGW installation@comma{} Visual Studio@comma{} .NET SDK@comma{} or system eSpeak
installation is not an input to this release path.  RHVoice's native library@comma{}
languages@comma{} and voices remain external user-supplied runtime inputs.  Flite's
CMU US SLT voice is compiled into its checksum-locked helper.  RuTTS's built-in
male and female Russian voices are compiled from the checksum-locked 6.3.3
source; RuLex is not included.

After staging@comma{} the static verifier rechecks manifests@comma{} provenance@comma{} the
Windows-local copy@comma{} and all configured external inputs.  The separate live
verifier executes that exact Windows-local runtime@comma{} prewarms the bounded eSpeak
voice cache tied to the verified content-addressed data@comma{} discovers the compiled
Flite and RuTTS voices@comma{} and requires real WAV synthesis from both helpers.
Use @code{make verify-windows-omnivox-live} to repeat only the executable smoke gate.

@code{OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL} is an optional staging input@comma{} not a release payload.
When supplied@comma{} the target validates the adjacent configuration@comma{} copies both
files into a content-addressed Windows-local model cache@comma{} records their hashes@comma{}
and writes the Windows path used by the launcher.  The recursive runtime
manifest deliberately excludes model files.  A different model therefore
creates a distinct local build identity without changing the pinned companion.
The public-domain engineering voice used in Omnivox CI is approved only for
that bounded test purpose and is not a model-distribution default.

Maintainers testing tracked Emacsvox or Omnivox changes may use
@code{make windows-omnivox-dev}.  That target records both worktree-diff hashes and
labels the result as a development build.  It builds the RHVoice@comma{} Flite@comma{} and
RuTTS companions but omits Piper@comma{} because the currently pinned Piper companion
must not be mixed with later Omnivox source.  It also rejects
@code{OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL}.  Advance the checked Piper companion and source commit
together before these integrations enter the clean release path.  See
@code{servers/omnivox-release/README.org} for the pinned toolchain@comma{} licensing@comma{}
provenance@comma{} and restaging contract.

To test Piper before that release-pin advance@comma{} commit and push a clean Omnivox
revision and run its @code{Piper Native Validation} workflow.  Download the
@code{omnivox-piper-windows-x64} artifact@comma{} which contains the companion archive and
@code{piper-sha256sums.txt}@comma{} then stage it with a reviewed model:

@example
OMNIVOX_PIPER_DEVELOPMENT_ARCHIVE=/absolute/path/to/omnivox-1.18.0-piper-windows-x64.zip \
  OMNIVOX_PIPER_DEVELOPMENT_ARCHIVE_SHA256=64-character-sha256 \
  OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL=/absolute/path/to/voice.onnx \
  make windows-omnivox-piper-dev
@end example

This is a development-only bridge@comma{} not a release-pin bypass.  It verifies the
outer archive hash@comma{} rejects unsafe archive members and symbolic links@comma{} checks
the companion's internal manifest@comma{} and requires its source provenance to name
the exact clean Omnivox @code{HEAD} and the Windows MSVC target.  The staged runtime
records the artifact hash and companion tree hash and labels the companion
@code{github-actions-native-development-build} with version @code{development-ci}.  The
version-looking archive filename comes from Omnivox's current package metadata
and does not assert that post-tag code was released.  The final live gate loads
the separately cached model and requires Piper to enumerate that voice and
synthesize a non-empty WAV@.

The ordinary @code{windows-omnivox-dev} target remains appropriate for uncommitted
Omnivox work because it rebuilds all included companions locally and omits
Piper.  The CI-artifact target deliberately rejects a dirty Omnivox checkout:
commit and push the change@comma{} let the native workflow build that exact commit@comma{}
then use its new artifact.  Emacsvox itself may contain tracked changes; their
diff hash remains part of the local runtime identity.

After one complete development runtime has been staged@comma{} use
@code{make windows-omnivox-main-dev} for changes confined to the main Rust server or
its main-only audio output.  This guarded path uses the same pinned toolchain
to rebuild only @code{omnivox.exe}@comma{} derives a new content-addressed runtime from the
current hash-verified development payload@comma{} records the original complete build
and reused manifest in provenance@comma{} hard-links the unchanged payload@comma{} and runs a
focused live check of the new Windows main executable.  It refuses reuse when
helper@comma{} helper-protocol@comma{} dependency@comma{} toolchain@comma{} or companion inputs changed; in
that case rerun @code{make windows-omnivox-dev}.  Run
@code{make verify-windows-omnivox-runtime verify-windows-omnivox-live} when a
complete development audit is useful.  The clean @code{make windows-omnivox} release
path always rebuilds and verifies every payload and never uses incremental
reuse.

The development target accepts @code{OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_LIBRARY} and
@code{OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_DATA} together@comma{} plus an optional
@code{OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_CONFIG} directory.  It resolves WSL or Windows absolute paths@comma{}
requires the C API DLL and the language/voice data layout@comma{} records content
digests in provenance and machine-local Windows path pointers beside the
selected runtime@comma{} and leaves all RHVoice files external.  Those pointers let
the launcher restore the variables for every fresh Emacsvox process.  The
runtime verifier rejects missing or changed external inputs.  Omitting all
three variables leaves RHVoice unconfigured without affecting Flite@comma{} RuTTS@comma{}
or the other engines.  RuTTS requires no external installation; the staged
helper always provides its built-in male and female Russian voices without
RuLex.

Do not bypass the Omnivox release target manually or clean either repository
to make its release guard pass.  Use @code{make windows-omnivox} only for the
reproducible clean-release path documented in the user-facing WSL2 route.

@subsection Windows staging implementation and fixtures

Make owns option and clean-source guards@comma{} toolchain verification@comma{} companion
