or source lock fails this handoff.  Clean builds require TGSpeechBox;
development builds can explicitly omit it.

The script owns host-side payload staging: input checks@comma{} content hashes@comma{}
optional voice-data caches@comma{} diagnostics@comma{} provenance@comma{} path files@comma{} and selection
of the completed runtime.  Make passes the Emacsvox root as
@code{EMACSVOX_STAGE_ROOT}@comma{} the resolved @code{OMNIVOX_*} source/build/runtime paths and
feature switches@comma{} and the two pinned Roslyn/reference-assembly archive hashes.
The assignments at the call site and the script's initial parameter checks
define the complete internal input list.  Optional user inputs retain their
@code{OMNIVOX_PIPER_MODEL}@comma{} @code{OMNIVOX_RHVOICE_*}@comma{} and @code{OMNIVOX_ECI_DLL} environment
variables.  Main-only development staging remains separately owned by
@code{stage-main-dev.sh} with its existing reuse restrictions.

Run @code{make windows-staging-test} on a GNU/Linux host with Python 3@comma{} Git@comma{} Make@comma{}
and the standard GNU file/checksum tools.  The tests use private repositories@comma{}
prepared payloads@comma{} runtime directories@comma{} and a simulated LocalAppData.  Docker@comma{}
Windows commands@comma{} and compiler/verifier boundaries are replaced by fixtures;
Git guards@comma{} copying@comma{} hashing@comma{} provenance@comma{} and link activation execute normally.
The suite is also included in @code{make test} and its own CI job.  It exercises
clean/development variants@comma{} optional companions and external voice inputs@comma{}
paths containing spaces@comma{} corrupt caches@comma{} missing inputs@comma{} rejected main-only
reuse@comma{} interruption@comma{} and activation failure.

Preserve the current activation sequence during maintenance.  The script
selects the staged runtime before Make's final runtime and live checks; a
failure in those final checks does not restore the previous selection.
Interruption before activation can leave temporary files in a new version
directory while preserving the current link.  The fixture tests characterize
these existing behaviors and successful retry.  Changing cleanup or rollback
requires a separate fix and acceptance decision.  Fixture success does not
establish native compiler reproducibility@comma{} real Windows path conversion@comma{}
engine startup@comma{} or audible playback; run the existing native gates for that
acceptance in an appropriate staging environment.

@subsection Aural experiments and guided changes

Physical experiments use the common voice editor with an exact engine/voice
selector@comma{} an immutable opening snapshot@comma{} a mutable working style and undo
history. Navigation uses the ordinary voice. Experiments write no settings;
Keep prepares a named-voice draft@comma{} including any reviewed row placement and
adjustment scope. The destination still requires an explicit save.

The unified palette contract is recorded in ADR 0018. Every nonroot palette
uses complete schema 3 entries and an explicit parent chain to @code{acss-default}.
Palette definitions and immutable local-choice snapshots are saved through the
common coordinator. Frozen proposals retain separate persistence and apply
completion@comma{} and retry does not repeat a successful write. Save failures@comma{}
acknowledged application and actual playback evidence remain separate.
Workstation routing profiles contain global engine policy@comma{} not per-name routes.

Labelled comparisons use one controlled preview sequence@comma{} with identical
sample text and separately configurable labels.  Each sequence belongs to
its speech lane.  Stopping that lane cancels remaining entries and invalidates
late callbacks; stopping foreground speech must not cancel notifications.
Requested parameters and advertised capabilities are distinct from playback
reports.  Do not fabricate exact effective numeric values when the adapter
reports only support@comma{} acceptance@comma{} or degradation.

Guided feedback changes use existing declarative rules and registered facts.
The draft selects a component@comma{} matching criteria@comma{} and lifetime separately;
preview dynamically overlays the intended layer without mutating live rules.
Explicit buffer-rule snapshots in captured context must also be overlaid.
Generated IDs remain stable for the matched component; new rules follow
existing explicit order within the layer.  Apply checks a Current-item source
guard and refuses to overwrite another unfinished editor for the same scope.
Historical selection chooses a frozen part when a transaction contains
several runs.  Original replay uses that part; proposed preview deliberately
resolves it with current rules and the captured buffer context.

Shared Help and offline task nodes preserve the original source and exact
return position.  New interfaces should use the shared parent modes and add
a task-node mapping to @code{emacsvox-aural-ui-manual-nodes}.  Row action predicates
should hide operations that cannot apply to that row; this includes editing
built-in options and reordering items outside the active-order view.

@subsection Repeatable voice performance measurements

Use @code{utils/voice_benchmark.py} to retain comparable measurements of ordinary
speech@comma{} native voice settings and the voice editor. It starts private speech
workers and fresh Emacs processes; it does not connect to a running Emacs or
save editor drafts. The default null audio output avoids speaking the workload.
Reports remain below the ignored @code{.benchmarks/} directory.

The runner requires Python 3@comma{} the selected Emacs toolchain@comma{} and an Omnivox
checkout containing @code{tools/benchmark_server.py} and @code{tools/process_metrics.py}.
It reuses that checkout's lifecycle workloads. On Linux@comma{} @code{xvfb-run} provides
a private graphical display when installed. The plan can explicitly select
@code{native} for a visible graphical Emacs or @code{batch} for a nongraphical run.
Batch measurements do not establish graphical responsiveness. Build current
byte-code before running; the runner refuses stale byte-code.

Create a local plan once@comma{} using the same runtime overrides as the development
speech launcher. List the runtime libraries@comma{} dictionaries and voice data that
affect the selected engines. For example:

@example
python3 utils/voice_benchmark.py init .benchmarks/voices.json \
  --program /path/to/omnivox \
  --emacs /path/to/emacs-31/bin/emacs \
  --omnivox-root /path/to/omnivox-checkout \
  --runtime-input voices=/path/to/voice-data
@end example

Initialization captures supported runtime overrides from the environment@comma{}
including @code{OMNIVOX_DECTALK_DLL}@comma{} @code{OMNIVOX_ECI_DLL} and @code{ESPEAK_NG_DATA}.
Inspect the resulting JSON plan. Its initial routes are Eloquence @code{v1}@comma{}
DECtalk @code{paul} and eSpeak's British English voice (@code{espeak:gmw/en}@comma{} with a
backslash on Windows); remove unavailable engines or change exact
voice IDs. Native routes also specify one parameter and test value. A missing
voice@comma{} fallback voice@comma{} unsupported parameter or failed native readback fails
the workload. It must not become a fast but invalid result.

If a separately retained failure prevents one case from producing timings@comma{}
a route may declare @code{exclude_cases} as an object mapping case names to reasons.
Exclusions appear at the top of the report and must match on both sides of a
comparison. An excluded case supplies no evidence about its performance or
correctness; do not treat a completed timing run as clearing that failure.

The plan pins the executable path. When staging a new development build@comma{}
update @code{targets[].program} before measuring it. Retain the earlier executable
and complete helper payload if an old-build comparison is wanted.
@code{--baseline-program} at initialization adds a second target for ordinary
speech@comma{} with native controls and editor tests disabled for that older worker.
An editor comparison against older Lisp needs a target with its own
@code{emacsvox_root} and current byte-code for that checkout. Keep runtime inputs@comma{}
rate@comma{} display mode@comma{} machine and benchmark driver identical between runs.

Run a short setup check@comma{} then collect the repeatable baseline:

@example
make voice-benchmark BENCHMARK_PRESET=quick
make voice-benchmark BENCHMARK_OUTPUT=.benchmarks/before
@end example

The full preset runs three shuffled blocks@comma{} discards warm-up samples@comma{} and
collects 90 observations per warm speech case and 60 per repeated editor
operation. Cold speech and first editor openings have fewer observations and
are reported separately. Allow several minutes for a full run; engine startup
and timeout costs vary. Keep unrelated workload low. Output directories must
be new; existing evidence is never overwritten. An interrupted or failed run
retains its partial report and logs but cannot be compared as successful.

The speech cases cover a character@comma{} word@comma{} line@comma{} dense text@comma{} multipart delivery@comma{}
rapid replacement@comma{} and simultaneous main and notification workers. For engines
with native controls@comma{} the runner distinguishes ordinary timeline delivery@comma{}
layered delivery with no native overrides@comma{} and an explicit native override.
The editor workload measures opening@comma{} navigation@comma{} control readiness@comma{} common
preview completion and automatic previews after native adjustments. It keeps
synchronous command time separate from waiting for asynchronous replies.

Compare a subsequent full run against the baseline:

@example
make voice-benchmark BENCHMARK_OUTPUT=.benchmarks/after
python3 utils/voice_benchmark.py compare \
  .benchmarks/before .benchmarks/after --output .benchmarks/comparison.md
@end example

Use @code{--flavour legacy --mode warm} to examine ordinary speech@comma{} or
@code{--flavour editor --mode ui} for the editor. A plan containing both builds can
be compared against itself with @code{--before-target baseline --after-target
current}. Every report contains medians@comma{} 95th percentiles@comma{} maxima and raw
observations. The default comparison flags a series only when its 95th
percentile rises by more than both 5 milliseconds and 15 percent@comma{} with at least
20 observations on each side. Short setup checks and low-count cold openings
are explicitly inconclusive. Repeat flagged runs before attributing a change
to code. With @code{--fail-on-regression}@comma{} exit status is 1 for a flagged slowdown@comma{}
3 for insufficient evidence@comma{} and 2 for an invalid run or comparison.

The index records executable and helper hashes@comma{} runtime input hashes@comma{} driver
hashes@comma{} source commit and dirty-tree identity@comma{} toolchain and host information.
Comparison validates the retained raw-report hashes and recomputes summaries.
Changed runtime data or incompatible test settings are rejected. Retain the
whole run directory@comma{} not just @code{report.md}. The source identity may differ
between runs: that is the change under investigation.

Add @code{BENCHMARK_RESOURCES=1} for a separate process-tree CPU and memory phase.
Sampling runs after the latency jobs so process enumeration does not distort
those timings. Resource reports identify their observation provider and its
limitations; unavailable memory evidence is not zero memory use. Editor
reports also retain Emacs CPU@comma{} resident memory and garbage-collection counters.
Check retained processes and memory across repeated work before calling an
increase a leak.

These measurements observe software source consumption and completion@comma{} not
sound reaching the listener. Editor previews currently expose completion but
no onset event. Null audio bypasses the physical device@comma{} so device latency or
audible clipping needs a separate listening or loopback measurement. Results
from null and real audio are not comparable. Run
@code{make voice-benchmark-test} to check evidence integrity and comparison logic.

@node Authoring Sound Packs
@subsection Authoring Sound Packs

Ordinary selection@comma{} preview@comma{} and cue remapping are covered in
@ref{Sounds And Space,Sounds@comma{} Tones@comma{} and Spatial Cues,,emacsvox,}. Preparing a new pack requires audio files
and resource metadata. A pack supplies recordings for registered cue names;
the presentation rules decide when those cues occur.

The standard personal discovery root is @code{~/.emacsvox/sounds/packs/}@comma{} controlled
by @code{emacsvox-aural-personal-sound-packs-directory}. Each immediate child is a
candidate pack@comma{} and its directory basename becomes its identifier. Choose an
unused identifier. A directory containing @code{button.ogg} is discovered without
a manifest. A partial pack can instead supply the data-only manifest
@code{emacsvox-sound-pack.el} and inherit missing cues from another pack.

For example@comma{} create @code{my-cues/} below the personal root and put this single
Lisp data form in @code{my-cues/emacsvox-sound-pack.el}:

@lisp
(:schema-version 1
 :summary "My personal cues@comma{} inheriting the remaining chimes"
 :parent chimes
 :default-spatialization neutral)
@end lisp

The file contains data@comma{} not executable initialization: do not wrap the form in
@code{setq}@comma{} quote it@comma{} or add a second form. Put a replacement recording such as
@code{button.ogg} in the same directory. Its basename names the registered cue.
Unknown cue names fail the resource audit; introducing a new semantic cue
requires an integration to register its intent and use it. Every standalone
sound pack must resolve @code{button}@comma{} directly or through its declared parent.

The recording library writes version 2 manifests when saving assignments.
These retain the same metadata and add @code{:assignments}: an alist mapping cue
symbols to @code{inherit} or @code{(:recording SHA256 :file RELATIVE-PATH :name LABEL)}.
Audio is copied below @code{recordings/assigned/} inside the pack; paths and byte
hashes are checked before publication. Copy the complete pack directory when
relocating it. Version 1 manifests and flat cue filenames remain supported.

Explicit assignments override flat and module-specific files at their own
inheritance level. @code{inherit} skips both local sources without deleting them.
The browser shows recorded labels@comma{} while cue registration still owns semantic
intent. Candidate files belong in the separate library@comma{} not at the pack root;
see @ref{Personal Sound Packs,Personal Sound Packs,,emacsvox,} for the assignment workflow.

The optional @code{:profiles} field lists registered cue-coverage requirements.
These are resource validation contracts@comma{} distinct from Presentation Profiles
that select a user's setup. Keep the coverage appropriate to the intended
uses rather than treating a successful single audition as complete coverage.
Default spatialization is @code{neutral}@comma{} @code{stereo}@comma{} or @code{pre-spatialized}. Use the
last for recordings that already encode their intended spatial placement@comma{}
so runtime balance does not position them a second time.

Open @emph{Sound packs} with @kbd{C-e H o p} and press @kbd{g} to rescan.
Inspect the new pack with @kbd{@key{RET}}: the replacement should be
native@comma{} while unchanged cues identify their parent or fallback provider.
Audition individual cues with @kbd{P} and validate with
@kbd{v} before activating the pack. Missing or malformed assets
are errors to resolve@comma{} not cues to copy from an unrelated installed pack.
No activation follows from writing the directory or previewing it.

The manager's @kbd{e} edits metadata for discovered packs only.
It replaces the manifest atomically@comma{} retains the previous file with a @code{~}
suffix@comma{} and restores the old metadata if validation of the refreshed pack
fails. It does not edit recordings. After changing files externally@comma{} refresh
the pack; @code{emacsvox-aural-refresh-resource-pack} refreshes an already registered
pack@comma{} and @code{emacsvox-aural-refresh-discovered-resource-packs} rescans discovery
roots. Engine installation@comma{} remote sound-directory placement@comma{} and user
startup selection remain separate tasks.
